/* OCR A level Geography (H481) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: "OCR-H481", KITS: {"4":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Investigative geography (04/05) is worth 60 marks — internally assessed by your centre and externally moderated by OCR — and it sits entirely within AO3, which carries 28% of the whole qualification. Unlike the three exam papers, it rewards a report you plan, collect and write over weeks rather than in an exam room, marked 'best fit' against six sections: planning, purpose and introduction; data collection and sampling; presentation; analysis; conclusions and evaluation; and overall quality and communication. Knowing exactly what each section's top band asks for — in the specification's own words — is what turns a competent report into a high-scoring one.","idea":[{"code":"1","h":"Planning, purpose and introduction (8 marks)","t":"The investigation must be based on a question or issue defined and developed by the learner individually, addressing aims, questions and/or hypotheses relating to any part of the specification; finalising the title, focus and purpose is independent work, justified and contextualised in the introduction. The top level (6-8 marks) requires a clear, well-focused plan with aims, questions or hypotheses linked to the geographic purpose; a topic accurately and appropriately defined within a research framework; justification and valid contextualisation of fieldwork and research; a precise, geo-located location at appropriately different scales; and clear evidence of valid, individual literature research through wider geographical links, comparisons, models and theory. A top-level answer never leaves the reader to guess why the location or topic was chosen."},{"code":"2","h":"Data, information collection methods and sampling framework (7 marks)","t":"The investigation must incorporate data from field investigations, collected individually or in groups, drawing on the learner's own field data and, where relevant, secondary data the learner sources; devising methodology and sampling, and primary data collection, allow collaboration, but secondary data collection must be independent. The top level (5-7 marks) requires good knowledge and understanding of a range of quantitative and/or qualitative methodologies, justified with limitations outlined and explained in detail; clear evidence of personalised methods for primary and secondary data; clear evidence of collecting and using digital, geo-located data; a sampling framework — sampling, frequency, range and location choice — that is appropriate, coherent and justified; and an understanding of the ethical and socio-political dimensions of the methods chosen. A top-level answer justifies every method, not just names it."},{"code":"3","h":"Data presentation techniques (9 marks)","t":"Data and information presentation is independent work: learners select and use appropriate presentation methods on their own. The top level (7-9 marks) requires appropriate and selective presentation of the most influential data collected, directly related to the investigation; a range of techniques that is appropriate and well selected, with good knowledge and understanding of how to represent results clearly; and an appropriate balance of simple and more sophisticated presentation methods relevant to the topic. A top-level answer chooses each technique to fit the type of data — spatial, temporal, categorical or qualitative — rather than defaulting to the same chart for everything."},{"code":"4","h":"Data analysis and explanation (14 marks)","t":"Data analysis and explanation is independent work: learners select and use appropriate analysis techniques and independently interpret, contextualise, analyse and summarise the findings and data. The top level (11-14 marks) requires data analysed and interpreted in an effective and coherent manner, with evidence of independence; statistical analysis and significance testing used accurately and proficiently where appropriate; qualitative and non-numerical techniques successfully and individually developed where appropriate; analysis and explanation that link effectively to the stated aims, questions or hypotheses; and effective use of knowledge, theory and geographical concepts to explain findings. A top-level answer always finishes a statistical result with what it means for the hypothesis, not just the number itself."},{"code":"5","h":"Conclusions and investigation evaluation (12 marks)","t":"Conclusions and evaluation is independent work: learners evaluate the findings of their investigation and reach a balanced, supported conclusion on their own, drawing conclusions individually and communicating them through extended writing and relevant data. The top level (10-12 marks) requires clear, accurate, thorough conclusions linked to the aims, questions or hypotheses, communicated by extended writing; effective use of primary and secondary evidence and theory for a very well-argued case; convincing evidence the investigation extended geographical understanding with reference to the wider geographical context; a strong evaluation of overall success covering the reliability of data sources, the data collection methods including sampling, the accuracy and representativeness of the data, and the validity of the analysis and conclusions; and a thorough understanding of the ethical and socio-political dimensions of the research. A top-level answer evaluates the method and the sample, not just the result."},{"code":"6","h":"Overall quality and communication of written work (10 marks)","t":"The report must contain a clear structure and continuous prose, with any digital material referenced through weblinks and/or screenshots, and a recommended length of between 3,000 and 4,000 words. The top level (7-10 marks) requires a high standard of communication relevant to the investigation's geographic purpose; clear arguments showing a strong degree of individuality; work that is very well structured, logical and concise, with text and figures appropriately integrated; sources and literature references clearly stated and accurately referenced throughout; and technical geographical terminology used appropriately, with rare written-language errors. A top-level answer integrates every figure into the argument rather than leaving it to speak for itself in an appendix."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sharpening a vague title into a focused one","setup":"A student's NEA begins as 'Investigating coastal erosion'. Their centre can reach the soft glacial-till cliffs between Happisburgh and Walcott in north Norfolk, and the Shoreline Management Plan covering that frontage is published online. Sharpen the starting idea into one focused, testable title and justify it as the introduction would.","steps":["The opening idea fails on two counts: 'coastal erosion' is not something that can be recorded with an instrument, and no stretch of shoreline is identified, so nothing about it could be tested in the field.","Fix the place first: the study is geo-located to the frontage between Happisburgh and Walcott, shown at two scales — a regional map placing north Norfolk, and a large-scale extract marking each profile point by grid reference.","Fix the variable next: rate of cliff retreat, in metres per year, taken from repeat profiles at fixed GPS-located points, is a single quantity the student can measure and compare along that frontage.","Contextualise it for the introduction: the title is set against coastal process theory — wave energy, sub-aerial weathering and the weak glacial-till lithology — and against the policy for this frontage in the Shoreline Management Plan.","Final title: 'How does the rate of cliff retreat vary along the coastline between Happisburgh and Walcott, Norfolk?' — a precise geo-located place, one measurable variable and a named framework, which is what the top band of Section 1 rewards."],"cues":["What two faults stop this idea being tested?","How is the vague 'coast' made precise?","What can actually be measured here, in what units?","What should the introduction set the title against?","What is the finished title?"]},{"title":"Working out a stratified sample","setup":"Quality-of-life questionnaires are to be shared between three wards: Ward A with 4,200 residents, Ward B with 2,800 and Ward C with 3,000. A stratified sample of 50 questionnaires in total is to be split in proportion to each ward's population — how many should be given out in each ward?","steps":["Total population of the study area = 4,200 + 2,800 + 3,000 = 10,000 residents.","Ward A's share = 4,200 ÷ 10,000 = 0.42, so Ward A takes 0.42 × 50 = 21 questionnaires.","Ward B's share = 2,800 ÷ 10,000 = 0.28, so Ward B takes 0.28 × 50 = 14 questionnaires.","Ward C's share = 3,000 ÷ 10,000 = 0.30, so Ward C takes 0.30 × 50 = 15 questionnaires.","Quotas: 21 in Ward A, 14 in Ward B and 15 in Ward C; 21 + 14 + 15 = 50, so every ward is represented in proportion to its population."],"cues":["How many residents live in the three wards together?","What share of the total is Ward A, and its quota?","Now Ward B — what share, and how many?","And Ward C's share and quota?","Do the three quotas add back to the target?"]},{"title":"Calculating Spearman's rank correlation coefficient","setup":"At six beach sites 0, 20, 40, 60, 80 and 100 m from a groyne, mean sediment size is measured as 2, 8, 5, 12, 20 and 15 mm respectively. Calculate Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient (rs) between distance from the groyne and sediment size, and say what it shows.","steps":["Rank the distances from 1 (nearest the groyne) upwards: 0 m = 1, 20 m = 2, 40 m = 3, 60 m = 4, 80 m = 5, 100 m = 6.","Rank the sediment sizes from 1 (smallest) upwards: 2 mm = 1, 5 mm = 2, 8 mm = 3, 12 mm = 4, 15 mm = 5, 20 mm = 6, so the sites in order out from the groyne carry sediment ranks 1, 3, 2, 4, 6, 5.","Subtract at each site, d = distance rank − sediment rank: 0 m gives 1 − 1 = 0; 20 m gives 2 − 3 = −1; 40 m gives 3 − 2 = +1; 60 m gives 4 − 4 = 0; 80 m gives 5 − 6 = −1; 100 m gives 6 − 5 = +1.","Square each difference and add them: d² = 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1 across the six sites, so Σd² = 0 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 = 4.","rs = 1 − (6 × Σd²) ÷ (n(n² − 1)) = 1 − (6 × 4) ÷ (6 × 35) = 1 − 24 ÷ 210 = 0.886, or rs = 0.89 to 2 d.p.: on a scale reaching +1.0 that is a strong positive correlation, so sediment size broadly increases with distance from the groyne even though two pairs of sites swap places."],"cues":["How are the six distances ranked?","Now rank the sediment sizes — what order results?","What is d at each of the six sites?","How do those six d values become Σd²?","Which formula turns Σd² into rs, and what is it?"]},{"title":"Reading a significance test and stating confidence","setup":"A student's NEA scores a site quality index at eight survey sites at increasing distance from the CBD and obtains Spearman's rs = 0.83; the critical value for n = 8 at the 0.05 level (two-tailed) is 0.738. Is the relationship significant at the 5% level, and how much confidence does the conclusion deserve?","steps":["Set out the null hypothesis the test judges: there is no relationship between site quality index and distance from the CBD, and any pattern across the surveyed sites is the product of chance.","Compare the test statistic with the table value: rs = 0.83 is larger than the 0.738 required at n = 8 for the 0.05 level, two-tailed.","Because the statistic passes that threshold, the null hypothesis is rejected: a correlation this strong would arise by chance in fewer than 5 cases in 100, so site quality genuinely changes with distance from the CBD.","Answer: the relationship is significant at the 5% level, but the conclusion deserves only moderate confidence — with n = 8 one atypical site could pull rs below 0.738, so the evaluation should flag the small sample and argue for more sites."],"cues":["What is the null hypothesis being tested?","What must rs be measured against?","So what happens to the null hypothesis?","How safe is this with only eight sites?"]},{"title":"Choosing between two presentation techniques for paired data","setup":"Pedestrian counts have been taken at two competing retail sites at the same five times across one day, testing the hypothesis that the gap in footfall between the sites is widest at midday. Choose between a divided bar chart and a dual line graph for the report, and justify the choice.","steps":["A divided bar chart would give one bar per site, split into its time slices: each site's daily total and the share contributed by each part of the day read off clearly, but the gap between the sites at any one time has to be judged across two separate bars.","A dual line graph plots both sites' counts against one shared time axis, so the vertical distance between the lines at each time is the gap itself, and the time at which that gap is widest can be read straight off.","Test each against the hypothesis, which is about when the difference between the sites is greatest — not about how large either site's daily total is.","Choice: the dual line graph, both lines drawn on a common y-axis scale with the five survey times labelled; the divided bar chart is rejected because it presents totals and their composition rather than the changing gap the hypothesis concerns."],"cues":["What would a divided bar chart show, and hide?","What does one shared time axis let you read?","What exactly does the hypothesis claim?","So which technique, and why not the other?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between 'collaboration allowed' and 'independent work' in the NEA's independence table?","a":"Collaboration allowed means learners may work as a group or pair at that stage (e.g. devising methodology, primary data collection); independent work means learners must work alone (e.g. finalising the title, secondary data collection, presentation, analysis, conclusions and evaluation)."},{"q":"How many marks in total is the NEA worth, and how are they split across the six sections?","a":"60 marks: planning/purpose/introduction 8, data collection/sampling 7, presentation 9, analysis 14, conclusions/evaluation 12, communication 10."},{"q":"What does 'best fit' mean when a teacher marks the NEA?","a":"A response is placed in the level its work matches overall; it does not have to meet every bullet point of the level to be placed in it, and the mark within the level depends on how fully it meets the descriptor."},{"q":"What is the recommended word count for the NEA report?","a":"Between 3,000 and 4,000 words."}]},"room":{"facts":["The NEA (Investigative geography, 04/05) carries 60 marks in six sections: planning, purpose and introduction (8); data collection methods and sampling framework (7); data presentation techniques (9); data analysis and explanation (14); conclusions and investigation evaluation (12); overall quality and communication (10).","The title, sub-questions and purpose of the investigation must be finalised independently; collaboration is allowed only at the 'Exploring focus' stage (discussing ideas and research with peers and the teacher), when devising methodology/sampling, and during primary data collection.","Secondary data collection, data presentation, data analysis and conclusions/evaluation must all be carried out as independent work.","The recommended length is 3,000-4,000 words of continuous prose; a response that differs significantly from this is self-penalising through lacking coherence and concision, or not meeting the criteria to the required level.","Marking is 'best fit': a response does not need to meet every bullet in a level descriptor to be placed in it — the extent to which it does decides the mark within the level.","Both the learner and the teacher must sign an authentication declaration on the Geography Independent Investigation Form confirming the work is the learner's own, apart from permitted collaboration and general guidance."],"questions":[{"q":"NEA practice: This is a candidate's first attempt at a title: 'Investigating coastal erosion.' Rewrite it as a focused, geographical, answerable investigation title, naming a specific location and variable. (4 marks)","a":"A rewrite naming a specific stretch of coastline, stating the variable being investigated (e.g. rate of cliff retreat), phrased so it can be answered with a realistic method, e.g. 'How does the rate of cliff retreat vary along the coastline between [named point] and [named point]?'","sol":"Rewrite: 'How does the rate of cliff retreat vary along the 2 km stretch of coastline between Happisburgh and Walcott, Norfolk?' This meets the requirements of a focused title because it names a precise, geo-located stretch of coastline at an appropriate scale, states a single measurable variable (rate of cliff retreat) rather than the vague 'coastal erosion', and is phrased so a clear method and set of results can answer it. It also opens onto a research framework: the answer can be contextualised against coastal process theory and against published shoreline management information for the area.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what makes a title 'geographic' and 'answerable' rather than just a broad topic area.","Add a precise, named location at an appropriate scale, and a single variable you could actually measure.","Give one rewritten title, then one short line saying what it now allows you to research."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner wants to record river velocity at ten points across a channel. State an appropriate sampling frame for choosing where across the channel to take readings, and name the sampling strategy this is. (4 marks)","a":"Systematic sampling across the channel width, e.g. ten readings taken at equal intervals from bank to bank.","sol":"The sampling frame is the full width of the channel at the chosen cross-section. An appropriate strategy is systematic sampling: divide the channel width by ten to give an equal interval, and take one velocity reading at each interval point moving from one bank to the other. This is systematic sampling because points are chosen at fixed, regular intervals rather than at random or by category, which suits velocity here because it varies continuously and predictably across a channel (fastest near the centre, slowest near the banks and bed), so equal-interval sampling captures this pattern without bias in where points are chosen.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how a variable that changes continuously across a fixed distance is best sampled.","Divide the total width by the number of readings you want, and use that as your interval.","Name the sampling strategy, then describe where the ten points fall across the channel."],"codes":["2"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A candidate's report is 5,600 words long, has no figure captions, and lists only two references at the very end with no in-text citations. Identify two ways this falls short of the top mark band for overall quality and communication. (4 marks)","a":"Two of: the length significantly exceeds the recommended 3,000-4,000 words, risking a lack of concision; figures are not properly integrated/captioned; sources are not referenced/cited clearly throughout the text.","sol":"Two clear shortfalls against the top band: first, at 5,600 words the report significantly exceeds the recommended 3,000-4,000 words, which the specification says will be self-penalising through a lack of coherence and concision rather than depth. Second, references are not clearly stated and accurately referenced throughout the investigation — two sources listed at the end with no in-text citations mean a reader cannot tell which evidence or data came from where, which the top band requires to be traceable throughout the text, not only listed at the end.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Read the extract as a moderator would: ask what a top-band Section 6 requires and check the extract against each requirement in turn.","First check the word count against the recommended range; then, separately, check whether every source used in the text can be traced back to a reference.","Give two separate, clearly stated shortfalls, each tied to a different part of the top-band wording."],"codes":["6"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner has collected paired quadrat data (% vegetation cover) at ten points along a single sand dune transect. Suggest one appropriate presentation technique for this data and justify the choice. (4 marks)","a":"A line graph or annotated transect diagram plotting % cover against distance along the transect, because it shows the continuous change over distance.","sol":"An appropriate technique is a line graph, or an annotated transect diagram, with distance along the transect on the x-axis and % vegetation cover on the y-axis. This suits the data because it is a single continuous variable measured at regular, ordered points along one spatial line, so a line graph shows the trend — and any change in the rate of change, such as cover rising steeply near the embryo dunes and levelling off further inland — more clearly than a table or a set of separate bar charts, which would not show the spatial sequence as directly.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what kind of variable this is — a single measurement changing along one line.","Consider which chart type shows a trend over a spatial sequence rather than just separate totals.","Name one technique and give one reason tied to the type of data collected."],"codes":["3"]},{"q":"NEA practice: Justify why a title investigating 'How does footfall and retail character vary along the high street of [a named town], and how does this relate to the Burgess model?' meets the requirements for a well-focused plan. (6 marks)","a":"Names a precise, geo-located location; states a measurable variable and comparison; explicitly links to a geographical model, giving a research framework.","sol":"This title meets the top-band requirement for a well-focused plan in several ways. It names a precise, geo-located location — a specific named high street — so the investigation sits at an appropriate local scale. It states clear variables to measure and compare (footfall counts and retail character, e.g. via a land-use survey), so the plan is testable rather than a general description. It links the investigation explicitly to a named geographical model, the Burgess concentric model, which gives it a research framework: results can be interpreted against a theoretical prediction rather than described in isolation, and the introduction can justify the location by explaining what the model predicts there and why testing it in this real place is worthwhile.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Look at what the top band asks for beyond just a clear location — think about scale, testability and theory.","Check the title against each separate requirement in turn: location, variable, and a named model or framework.","Structure your answer as three short justified points, one for each requirement it meets."],"codes":["1"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner is investigating perceived quality of life across three residential areas of a town with populations of 3,600, 2,400 and 6,000. They plan 60 questionnaires in total. Justify a stratified sampling strategy for this question, showing the number of questionnaires allocated to each area. (6 marks)","a":"Total population 12,000; allocate proportionally: Area 1 = 3,600/12,000 × 60 = 18; Area 2 = 2,400/12,000 × 60 = 12; Area 3 = 6,000/12,000 × 60 = 30.","sol":"Stratified sampling is justified because the three areas differ in population size, so an equal split of 20 each would over-represent the smallest area and under-represent the largest, biasing the quality-of-life results. Proportional allocation: total population = 3,600 + 2,400 + 6,000 = 12,000. Area 1: 3,600 ÷ 12,000 × 60 = 18 questionnaires. Area 2: 2,400 ÷ 12,000 × 60 = 12 questionnaires. Area 3: 6,000 ÷ 12,000 × 60 = 30 questionnaires (18 + 12 + 30 = 60). Within each area, respondents should then be chosen at random or systematically (e.g. every nth household along a set route) to avoid the researcher's own bias in who is approached, meeting the requirement for a sampling framework that is appropriate, coherent and justified.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about why an equal split of respondents across areas of different sizes would misrepresent the population.","Work out each area's share of the total population before applying it to the sample size.","Show your numbers for each area, then add one sentence justifying stratification over an equal split."],"codes":["2"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner has three types of data: (i) wind speed measured every hour for 24 hours at one site, (ii) pedestrian counts at eight different locations across a town centre, and (iii) transcribed comments from five resident interviews. Select and justify one presentation technique for each. (6 marks)","a":"(i) A line graph over time; (ii) proportional symbols or a located bar chart at each of the eight points across the town centre; (iii) a coded qualitative summary table or word cloud of themes.","sol":"(i) Wind speed over 24 hours is a single continuous variable changing through time, so a line graph with time on the x-axis is appropriate — it shows the diurnal pattern directly. (ii) Pedestrian counts are absolute totals recorded at eight discrete points, not values that apply across an area, so proportional symbols or a located bar chart plotted at each point on a base map of the town centre is appropriate, letting the reader compare footfall site by site. A choropleth map would not suit this data: choropleths need areal units (a value applying evenly across a zone) and normalised data such as a density or a rate, whereas these are absolute counts tied to precise points, so shading zones by them would misrepresent the data and obscure exactly where each count was taken. (iii) Interview comments are qualitative, so a graph does not suit them; an appropriate technique is a coded summary table grouping quotations under recurring themes, or a word cloud showing which ideas recur most often — both let the reader see patterns in non-numerical evidence without reducing it to a single misleading number.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["For each dataset, ask whether the variable changes over time, over space, or isn't numerical at all.","Match each data type to a technique built for that kind of change — temporal, spatial or qualitative.","Give one technique and one justification for each of the three datasets in turn."],"codes":["3"]},{"q":"NEA practice: Six sites along a river were ranked by distance downstream (1 = nearest source) and by measured channel width (1 = narrowest). The rank differences (d) were 0, 1, -1, 0, 1, -1, giving d² values of 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1. Calculate Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (rs) for n = 6 and state what it shows. (6 marks)","a":"Σd² = 4; rs = 1 − (6×4)/(6×35) = 1 − 24/210 ≈ 0.89, showing a strong positive correlation between distance downstream and channel width.","sol":"Σd² = 0 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 = 4. rs = 1 − (6Σd² ÷ n(n²−1)) = 1 − (6 × 4) ÷ (6 × 35) = 1 − 24 ÷ 210 = 1 − 0.114 = 0.886, rounded to 0.89. This is close to +1, so it shows a strong positive correlation: channel width tends to increase with distance downstream across these six sites, consistent with the Bradshaw model's prediction that channel width increases towards the mouth as discharge and erosion increase.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the formula that converts ranked differences into a correlation coefficient.","Sum the d² values first, then substitute n = 6 into the formula before simplifying.","State the numerical value you calculate, then describe what the result shows about the relationship."],"codes":["4"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner's data shows a Spearman's rs of 0.94 (n = 6) between distance from a town centre and house price, well above the 0.05 critical value of 0.886 for n = 6. Draw a conclusion from this and say how confident the learner can be in it. (6 marks)","a":"Conclusion: house price is significantly positively correlated with distance from the centre, supporting the hypothesis; confidence should be limited because n = 6 is a small sample, so it should be treated as indicative rather than definitive.","sol":"Because 0.94 exceeds the critical value of 0.886 for n = 6 at the 5% significance level, the correlation is statistically significant, so the null hypothesis (no relationship) can be rejected: house price rises significantly with distance from the town centre. This outward rise is better explained by residential structure models such as Burgess's concentric zone model or Hoyt's sector model, and by housing age and quality — newer, larger, lower-density housing further from the centre commands higher prices — rather than by bid-rent theory, which predicts the opposite pattern: land values and willingness to pay fall with distance from the centre as accessibility declines. However, confidence in this conclusion should be limited: with only six sites, a single unusual site — for example, one large new-build estate close to the centre — could shift rs considerably, so the strength of the relationship found here may not hold if a larger, more representative sample of sites were used across the whole town.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the calculated value against the critical value to decide whether the relationship is significant.","Once you've decided on significance, think about what a small sample size does to how far you can trust the result.","Give one sentence stating the conclusion and one sentence qualifying your confidence in it."],"codes":["5"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner used an online questionnaire, shared only via one social media group, to measure perceived quality of life, and got 45 responses, mostly from people aged 18-30. Evaluate the reliability of this data-collection method and suggest one specific improvement. (8 marks)","a":"Evaluation should identify sampling bias (self-selecting, age-skewed, only social-media users) limiting representativeness; improvement e.g. stratified/systematic on-street sampling across age groups and locations.","sol":"This method has a clear reliability problem: sharing the questionnaire through a single social media group produces a self-selecting sample, so only people who saw the post and chose to respond are included, and these were disproportionately aged 18-30, meaning older residents' perceptions of quality of life are under-represented. This limits how representative the results are of the whole population being investigated, and any conclusions about 'perceived quality of life' in the area really only describe the views of a narrow, digitally engaged age group — a limitation that must be stated explicitly, along with its likely effect on the results. A specific improvement is to move to an in-person, systematic street survey stratified by age band, using census data on the area's age structure to set a quota for each band and then approaching every nth passer-by in each location until each quota is filled, which would collect data from a spread of ages and reduce the self-selection bias, at the cost of taking longer to administer.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Think about who was actually able to see and choose to answer this questionnaire, and who wasn't.","Identify the specific type of sampling bias this method produces before suggesting a fix.","Give one evaluative point on reliability and one concrete, different method that would address it."],"codes":["2","5"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner found that footpath erosion width was greater on steeper sections of a moorland trail (Spearman's rs = 0.81, n = 9, significant at 0.05). Analyse this finding using relevant geographical theory to explain why steeper sections might show more erosion. (8 marks)","a":"Link to footpath erosion theory: steeper gradients concentrate walker impact and surface run-off, increasing shear stress on the surface and encouraging braiding/shortcutting, both widening the eroded area.","sol":"The strong, significant positive correlation (rs = 0.81) supports the hypothesis that steeper sections of the trail experience greater erosion width. This can be explained through footpath erosion theory: on steeper gradients, both walkers and surface water are concentrated onto the path itself, since walkers tend to avoid slipping on the loose surface at the path edge and instead compact the centre, while rainwater run-off is channelled down the path line rather than infiltrating, increasing the shear stress the surface must withstand. Steeper sections also encourage 'braiding' and shortcutting, where walkers spread sideways to find firmer ground once the original line becomes rutted, widening the eroded area rather than only deepening it. Vegetation cover, which would otherwise bind the soil, is removed fastest where trampling pressure is highest, creating a feedback loop that further accelerates erosion — linking the statistical pattern found back to established process-based explanation rather than leaving it as a bare correlation.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what happens to walkers and to water on a steeper surface, not just to the soil itself.","Connect the statistical pattern to a process — what physically changes as gradient increases.","Explain the mechanism as a short chain of cause and effect leading to more erosion."],"codes":["4"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A learner's conclusion states confidently that 'flood risk perception is directly caused by proximity to the river' based on 20 questionnaires from one street flooded in 2021. Write an evaluation paragraph assessing the validity of this conclusion given the sampling limitations described. (8 marks)","a":"Should flag: small, non-random, single-street/single-event sample cannot support a causal claim; correlation-not-causation issue; confounding factors not controlled; recommend a qualified, cautious conclusion instead.","sol":"This conclusion overstates what the evidence can support. A sample of 20 questionnaires drawn from a single street that has itself experienced flooding is neither large nor representative of the wider area's population, so the finding cannot safely be generalised beyond that one street, let alone establish a general rule linking proximity and risk perception. More importantly, the claim of direct causation is not justified by a correlation between two variables measured at one point in time: perceptions could equally be shaped by confounding factors the investigation did not control for, such as recent media coverage of the 2021 flood, personal experience of damage, or insurance costs, any of which could raise perceived risk independently of physical proximity. A valid evaluation would qualify the conclusion — stating that proximity is associated with, rather than proven to cause, higher perceived risk in this small sample — and recommend a larger, more geographically spread sample, ideally including streets both affected and unaffected by the 2021 event, before any causal claim could be supported.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Consider what a sample drawn only from one flooded street, after the event, can and can't tell you.","Distinguish between a correlation the data shows and the causal claim the learner has made.","State the problem with the claim, then say what the conclusion should say instead."],"codes":["5"]},{"q":"NEA practice: A candidate's introduction states the title, then immediately describes the physical geography of the area for two pages without saying why the investigation matters or referring to any wider theory. Assess whether this introduction meets the top mark band for planning, purpose and introduction, and identify what is missing. (8 marks)","a":"Does not meet the top band: lacks justification/contextualisation of the investigation and lacks evidence of individual literature research linking to wider geographical links, comparisons, models or theory — description alone is not enough.","sol":"This introduction does not meet the top mark band. The specification requires a justification for the investigation provided in the introduction and valid contextualisation of fieldwork and research, and clear evidence of valid, individual literature research that defines and contextualises the investigation through wider geographical links, comparisons, models and theory. Two pages of purely descriptive physical background does not, by itself, justify why this investigation is worth doing, what gap or question it addresses, or how it connects to a wider geographical model or comparable study — it describes the place rather than framing the enquiry. To reach the top band, the introduction needs to state explicitly why the topic and location were chosen, reference relevant theory or a model the investigation will test, and show individual research, such as citing a comparable published study, that the physical description alone cannot provide; without this, the response is more likely to sit in the level below, where contextualisation is only attempted rather than achieved.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Check the introduction against each separate requirement for the top band, not just its length.","Distinguish between describing the place and justifying why the investigation is worth doing.","Say which requirement is missing, and what that means for which level the response sits in."],"codes":["1","6"]},{"q":"NEA practice: An investigation into microclimate variation found urban sites were on average 2.3°C warmer than rural sites (paired t-test, significant at 0.05), but three individual readings did not fit the pattern. Explain how these three anomalies should be treated in the analysis and conclusion. (8 marks)","a":"Anomalies should be identified, investigated for a specific cause (e.g. shading, a nearby heat source, equipment error, time of reading) rather than simply discarded, and their effect on confidence discussed in the conclusion/evaluation.","sol":"The three anomalous readings should not simply be dropped from the dataset without explanation; a top-level analysis identifies them explicitly, for example by site, and investigates a plausible physical cause for each, such as local shading from a building, a nearby heat source, unusual cloud cover at the time of reading, or a possible equipment or recording error. If a specific cause can be identified and justified, the anomaly can be noted and reasonably set aside from the general trend, while still being shown in the data. If no clear cause can be found, it should be reported honestly as an unexplained outlier rather than hidden, because the statistical test result already shows the overall pattern is robust despite these three cases. The conclusion and evaluation should then discuss what the anomalies mean for confidence in the finding — acknowledging that three sites did not fit the general urban heat island pattern shows the investigation has engaged critically with its own data, which is exactly what a strong evaluation of the validity of the analysis and conclusions rewards, rather than presenting the 2.3°C mean difference as if it applied uniformly everywhere.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what a researcher should do with a data point that doesn't fit, before deciding to use or discard it.","Consider what specific, checkable causes could explain an individual anomalous reading.","Explain how the anomalies should be handled in the write-up and what discussing them shows about the investigation."],"codes":["4","5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks are available for the NEA overall?","a":"60 marks."},{"q":"Name two stages of the investigation that must always be carried out independently.","a":"Any two of: finalising the title/focus/purpose, secondary data collection, data presentation, data analysis, conclusions and evaluation."},{"q":"What word count is recommended for the NEA report?","a":"3,000-4,000 words."},{"q":"What marking principle does OCR use for the NEA, where an answer need not meet every bullet point of a level to be placed in it?","a":"Best-fit marking."}]},"cards":[{"front":"How many marks for Section 1: Planning, purpose and introduction?","back":"8 marks.","code":"1"},{"front":"What must be finalised as independent work before methodology is planned?","back":"The title, sub-questions and purpose of the investigation.","code":"1"},{"front":"What does the top band require of the investigation's location?","back":"A precise, geo-located location, using geo-spatial techniques, at appropriately different scales.","code":"1"},{"front":"How many marks for Section 2: data collection methods and sampling framework?","back":"7 marks.","code":"2"},{"front":"Which stage of data collection must be independent, unlike primary data collection?","back":"Secondary data collection — learners must select secondary sources on their own.","code":"2"},{"front":"What must the sampling framework show at the top level?","back":"That sampling, frequency, range and location choice are appropriate, coherent and justified.","code":"2"},{"front":"How many marks for Section 3: data presentation techniques?","back":"9 marks.","code":"3"},{"front":"What balance does the top band for presentation reward?","back":"An appropriate balance of simple and more sophisticated presentation methods relevant to the topic.","code":"3"},{"front":"How many marks for Section 4: data analysis and explanation?","back":"14 marks — the single largest section.","code":"4"},{"front":"What must top-level analysis do with statistical tests, where appropriate?","back":"Use statistical analysis and significance testing accurately and proficiently.","code":"4"},{"front":"How many marks for Section 5: conclusions and investigation evaluation?","back":"12 marks.","code":"5"},{"front":"What four things must a strong evaluation cover?","back":"Reliability of data sources, data collection methods including sampling, accuracy/representativeness of data, and validity of the analysis and conclusions.","code":"5"},{"front":"How many marks for Section 6: overall quality and communication?","back":"10 marks.","code":"6"},{"front":"What is the recommended word count for the NEA report?","back":"3,000-4,000 words of continuous prose.","code":"6"},{"front":"What marking principle does OCR apply to the NEA?","back":"'Best fit': the mark reflects how far the work matches a level's descriptors overall, not whether every bullet point is met.","code":"6"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model section — top mark band","items":["Scenario: an evaluation section for an investigation into how perceived quality of life varies across three residential areas of a town, using stratified questionnaire sampling (n = 60).","Overall, this investigation succeeded in showing that perceived quality of life is significantly higher in Area 3 than in Areas 1 and 2, but several aspects of the methodology limit how far this conclusion can be generalised. The stratified sample of 60 questionnaires, proportionally allocated across the three areas' populations (18, 12 and 30 respectively), avoided the bias an equal split would have introduced, and a Mann-Whitney U test confirming a significant difference between Area 3 and Area 1 (U = 112, p < 0.05) gives reasonable confidence that the pattern is not due to chance. However, the reliability of the underlying data is more limited: questionnaires were administered only on weekday afternoons, which may have under-represented working residents and over-represented retired or unemployed respondents, a source of bias that should be weighed against the significant result rather than ignored. The accuracy of the perception scores is also open to question, since a 1-5 Likert scale asks respondents to convert a complex, personal judgement into a single number, and different respondents may have interpreted the scale's midpoint differently. Ethically, consent was obtained and respondents were not identifiable in the write-up, but the investigation did not consider whether repeatedly surveying the same lower-income street risked over-researching a community that receives little benefit from the findings. Taken together, the significant result gives good grounds for the conclusion that quality of life varies by area, extending understanding of intra-urban inequality by connecting it to a wider welfare-approach framework; but the timing bias in sampling and the subjectivity of the Likert scale mean the precise size of the difference, rather than its direction, should be treated with caution."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model section — middle mark band","items":["Scenario: the same evaluation task — an investigation into how perceived quality of life varies across three residential areas of a town, using stratified questionnaire sampling (n = 60).","This investigation found that quality of life was higher in Area 3. The questionnaires showed higher scores there than in Area 1 and Area 2. This could be because Area 3 has better housing and more green space. Some of the questionnaires might not have been very reliable because not everyone answered every question fully, and some people might have said what they thought I wanted to hear. If I did the investigation again I would give out more questionnaires and maybe use a different method as well. Overall the data was mostly correct and the results are useful for showing the differences in quality of life across the three areas.","What it lacks against the top band: it names only isolated problems, such as incomplete questionnaires, rather than evaluating the data sources, sampling and analytical method systematically; it draws no statistical evidence into the argument to show how confident the conclusion should be; it makes no reference to wider theory or the wider geographical context; and it does not address the ethical or socio-political dimensions of the research at all."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Purpose and question — the NEA guide","items":["Top band (6-8 marks) needs: a clear, well-focused plan with aims, questions or hypotheses linked to the geographic purpose of the investigation.","The topic or issue must be an individual one, accurately and appropriately defined and set within a research framework.","The introduction must justify the investigation and give valid contextualisation of the fieldwork and research.","The location must be precise and geo-located, using geo-spatial techniques, at appropriately different scales.","There must be clear evidence of valid, individual literature research that defines and contextualises the investigation through wider geographical links, comparisons, models and theory.","Common pitfall: choosing a title so broad it cannot be tested with a realistic data set.","Common pitfall: describing the location's physical or human geography at length without saying why the investigation is worth doing there.","Common pitfall: naming no model, theory or comparable study, so the plan has no research framework to sit within.","Checklist: title names a precise, geo-located place at a stated scale; states a testable aim, question or hypothesis; is justified with a reason the investigation matters; is linked to at least one named model, theory or comparable study; the title is finalised independently, not chosen from a list the centre provides."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Planning and data collection — the NEA guide","items":["Top band (5-7 marks) needs good knowledge and understanding of a range of data collection methodologies, quantitative and/or qualitative, justified with limitations outlined and explained in detail.","There must be clear evidence of personalised methodologies to observe and record primary data, and to incorporate secondary data.","There must be clear evidence of collecting and using digital, geo-located data.","The data design framework — sampling, frequency, range and location choice — must be appropriate, coherent and justified.","The methodology must address the ethical and socio-political dimensions of the methods chosen.","Devising methodology and sampling framework, and primary data collection, allow collaboration; secondary data collection must be carried out independently.","Common pitfall: listing methods without justifying why each one was chosen or naming its limitations.","Common pitfall: a sampling strategy with no stated frame, size or justification.","Checklist: each method is named, justified and its limitations stated; the sampling strategy names the frame, the size, the type and why it suits the variable; at least one source of digital, geo-located data is used; ethical issues are addressed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Presentation and analysis — the NEA guide","items":["Presentation (9 marks, top band 7-9): selective presentation of the most influential data, a well-selected range of techniques, and a balance of simple and more sophisticated methods relevant to the topic.","Data presentation is independent work — learners select and use the techniques on their own.","Analysis (14 marks, top band 11-14): data analysed and interpreted effectively and coherently, with evidence of independence.","Where appropriate, statistical analysis and significance testing must be used accurately and proficiently.","Where appropriate, qualitative and non-numerical analysis techniques must be successfully and individually developed.","Analysis and explanation must link effectively to the stated aims, questions or hypotheses, using appropriate knowledge, theory and geographical concepts.","Common pitfall: presenting every piece of data collected rather than selecting the most influential for the investigation's purpose.","Common pitfall: running a statistical test but not interpreting what the result means for the hypothesis, or not checking it against a critical value.","Checklist: presentation techniques are matched to the type of data; at least one more sophisticated technique is used alongside simpler ones; any statistical test states its result and compares it with the correct critical value; every analysis point is linked back to an aim, question or hypothesis."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Conclusions — the NEA guide","items":["Top band (10-12 marks) needs clear, accurate and thorough conclusions linked to the aims, questions or hypotheses, communicated by extended writing.","Conclusions must draw effectively on primary and secondary evidence, and theory where appropriate, to provide a very well-argued case.","There must be convincing evidence that the investigation extended the learner's geographical understanding, with clear reference to the wider geographical context.","Conclusions and their communication are independent work — learners reach and communicate them on their own.","Common pitfall: restating the results rather than answering the original aims, questions or hypotheses directly.","Common pitfall: a conclusion with no reference to wider geography, theory or comparable places beyond the investigation's own data.","Checklist: each aim, question or hypothesis from the introduction is answered explicitly; primary and secondary evidence, and relevant theory, are drawn together to support the argument; the conclusion states what the investigation adds to understanding of the wider geographical context, not just the local case."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Evaluation — the NEA guide","items":["Top band evaluation needs a strong evaluation of the investigation's overall success, covering: the reliability of data sources; the data collection methods, including sampling; the accuracy of data collected and how representative it is; and the validity of the analysis and conclusions.","It also needs a thorough understanding of the ethical and socio-political dimensions of the field research and data presentation.","Common pitfall: an evaluation that lists a few isolated errors rather than evaluating the method, sample and analysis systematically.","Common pitfall: no discussion of how confident the reader should be in the conclusions given the limitations described.","Common pitfall: no mention of ethics — consent, safety, sensitivity of the topic or location, or the impact of the research on those studied.","Checklist: reliability of each main data source is assessed; the data collection method and sampling strategy are evaluated, not just described; the accuracy and representativeness of the data collected are discussed; the validity of the analysis and conclusions is assessed, including the effect of sample size; ethical and socio-political dimensions are addressed explicitly."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Communication — the NEA guide","items":["Top band (7-10 marks) needs a high standard of communication relevant to the investigation's geographic purpose.","Arguments must be clear and show a strong degree of individuality.","The work must be very well structured, logical and concise, with text and figures appropriately integrated.","Sources and literature references must be clearly stated and accurately referenced throughout the investigation, not only listed at the end.","Geographical terminology must be technical and used appropriately, with rare written-language errors.","The recommended length is 3,000-4,000 words of continuous prose; digital material must be referenced through weblinks and/or screenshots.","Common pitfall: figures placed in an appendix and never referred to in the text.","Common pitfall: referencing given as a single bibliography with no in-text citations linking specific claims to specific sources.","Checklist: word count sits within or close to the 3,000-4,000 word guidance; every figure is numbered, captioned and referred to in the text; every source used is cited at the point it is used, not only listed at the end; technical geographical terms are used accurately throughout."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"The rules of the NEA","items":["Investigative geography (04/05) is internally assessed by the centre and externally moderated by OCR.","The investigation must be based on a question or issue the learner defines and develops individually, though data collection can be carried out in a group.","Independence table: collaboration is allowed for exploring the initial focus, devising methodology/sampling, and primary data collection; everything else — finalising the title/sub-questions/purpose, secondary data collection, presentation, analysis, and conclusions/evaluation — must be independent work.","Teachers can give general guidance, such as confirming a provisional title could meet the assessment criteria or explaining what independence means, but must not provide a choice of titles to pick from, give feedback on how to improve work to meet the criteria, or mark work provisionally and share the mark.","Any assistance beyond general guidance, such as feedback on how to improve a draft or data not collected by the learner, must be recorded on the Geography Independent Investigation Form and taken into account when marking; failing to record it is treated as malpractice.","A recommended length of 3,000-4,000 words is given; exceeding it is not penalised directly, but a response that differs significantly from this length is self-penalising through a lack of coherence and concision, or by not meeting the marking criteria to the required level.","The Geography Independent Investigation Form has three sections: a learner/teacher authentication declaration, an investigation proposal (title, hypotheses/questions/sub-questions, enquiry route, suggested data collection methods), and a mark sheet completed by the teacher.","The learner's declaration confirms the submitted work is their own unaided work, apart from permitted collaboration and general guidance; the teacher's declaration confirms any assistance beyond general guidance was recorded and accounted for in marking, and that signed learner declarations are kept on file.","Fieldwork must be undertaken for at least four days across the A level course, including in relation to processes in both physical and human geography; fieldwork carried out for the independent investigation may count towards this four-day requirement. Centres must submit a written fieldwork statement confirming this to OCR by 15 May in the year of entry, and failure to do so in good time is treated as malpractice or maladministration."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Investigation plan — How does perceived quality of life vary across a named town?","items":["Title/question: How does perceived quality of life vary between three contrasting residential areas of Guildford, Surrey?","Location at two scales: Guildford, Surrey (borough scale, centred approximately grid reference SU 9991 4954) within the London/South East commuter belt; and, at neighbourhood scale, three contrasting residential areas within it — Area 1, inner terraced housing near the town centre (centred SU 9987 4958); Area 2, interwar semi-detached housing (centred SU 9840 5050); and Area 3, a post-1980s suburban estate adjoining green space (centred SU 9720 5120).","Theoretical and comparative context: Burgess's concentric zone model and Hoyt's sector model, used to predict how housing age, density and environmental quality — and so perceived quality of life — should vary with distance and direction from the centre; Index of Multiple Deprivation (IMD) scores for the three areas' Lower Super Output Areas, used as an independent, published comparator against the questionnaire results; and a named published quality-of-life study (e.g. the ONS's Measuring National Wellbeing indicators) used as a comparison framework for the indicators chosen.","Sub-questions: (a) How does perceived quality of life differ between the three chosen areas? (b) Which environmental and social factors do residents associate most strongly with a higher perceived quality of life? (c) How closely does perceived quality of life relate to an objective indicator, such as access to green space?","Hypotheses: H1 — perceived quality of life is significantly higher in the area with greater access to green space and lower traffic flow. H0 — there is no significant difference in perceived quality of life between the three areas.","Primary data: a Likert-scale questionnaire (1-5) covering perceived safety, green space access, noise and overall satisfaction, administered face-to-face in each area; an environmental quality survey (bipolar matrix, scored 1-5 across indicators such as litter, traffic noise and building condition) recorded at ten systematic points in each area, each point's location logged by GPS grid reference (or what3words) so results can be mapped precisely and compared against secondary GIS layers.","Secondary data: Ordnance Survey Open Data (e.g. OS Open Greenspace) to identify green space provision, and Index of Multiple Deprivation (IMD) data for the three areas' Lower Super Output Areas, both mapped as digital, geo-located layers in a GIS (e.g. QGIS) alongside the primary sample points, sourced independently by the learner.","Sampling: stratified by area, proportional to each area's population — 18, 12 and 30 questionnaires respectively (60 total); within each area, a systematic point sample every fifth house along a pre-planned route for the environmental quality survey, with each sample point's grid reference recorded for mapping in GIS.","Analysis: mean Likert scores and mean environmental quality scores compared between the three areas using Mann-Whitney U tests as pairwise comparisons (Area 1 vs Area 2, Area 1 vs Area 3, Area 2 vs Area 3) — or a Kruskal-Wallis test to compare all three areas at once; Spearman's rank correlation between mean perceived quality of life score and mean environmental quality score per sample point to test H1.","Presentation: a choropleth map of mean environmental quality score by area (not an isoline map: with only ten points in each of three separate neighbourhoods there is no continuous surface to interpolate); divided bar charts comparing mean Likert responses by area; a scattergraph with a Spearman's rank line for the correlation between perceived and environmental quality.","Evaluation: assess the reliability of the timing and location of questionnaire administration, the representativeness of a 60-respondent sample against each area's population, the accuracy of a subjective 1-5 environmental scoring matrix, and the validity of using this small sample to generalise about the whole town; address ethical issues such as consent and not identifying individual respondents.","Conclusion: answer each sub-question in turn from the analysed data, state how far the hypothesis is supported and with what confidence (significance results, sample size), and return to the models and comparators named at the start.","Communication: 3,000–4,000 words, sections in the order of the six marking criteria, every figure numbered and referred to in the text, sources referenced in one consistent style, and the proportional allocation shown with the three areas' populations (from the 2021 Census output areas) so the 18/12/30 split can be checked."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.945Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All seven faults from the first verdict are genuinely fixed and re-checked against the document: room.facts now names 'Exploring focus' alongside devising methodology/sampling and primary data collection (p.62-63 table); the plan's sampling reads 18/12/30 in line with the model section; the plan now carries two geo-located scales with grid references, Burgess/Hoyt, IMD and an ONS comparator for Section 1, and GPS/what3words logging of its own primary points plus OS Open Greenspace and IMD GIS layers for Section 2's digital geo-located requirement; lesson.examples item 4 now uses 0.738 (two-tailed, n = 8), matching question 8's 0.886 (two-tailed, n = 6); question 6 now explicitly rules the choropleth out for point counts; question 8 now attributes the outward rise in house prices to Burgess/Hoyt and housing age and expressly says bid rent predicts the opposite; and the rules guide carries the four-day fieldwork requirement, that NEA fieldwork may count towards it, and the 15 May fieldwork statement with its malpractice consequence. Everything about the marking checks out exactly — 60 marks in six sections (8/7/9/14/12/10), every top-band boundary (6-8, 5-7, 7-9, 11-14, 10-12, 7-10), all-AO3, best fit, 3,000-4,000 words and the self-penalising wording, and both declarations — the lesson has one faithful section per key idea each closing on what a top-level answer does, all 14 answer keys are sound (Spearman Sigma-d-squared = 4, rs = 0.886; stratified 18/12/30; Mann-Whitney U = 112 for 18 v 30 is indeed significant), the two model sections sit clearly at different bands with the weaker one's shortfalls itemised, and the extras are the required six stage guides plus a rules guide and a plan. Polish only: the plan stops at evaluation, so it says nothing about what the conclusion must argue (the other half of Section 5) or about structure, referencing and word count (Section 6) — both are covered by the Conclusions and Communication stage guides, but a bullet each would make the plan a complete Section 1-6 template; it also asserts proportional allocation of 18/12/30 without giving the three Guildford areas' populations, so a student cannot check the arithmetic as they can in question 6; question 3's first hint still names both of the two shortfalls the answer needs (length and traceable referencing) before the student has thought; question 5 frames retail character on a high street against Burgess, where bid rent/PLVI is the closer fit (harmless, since the point being justified is that any named model supplies a research framework); and question 14's paired t-test is parametric, so a line on its assumptions would help a student who has only met Mann-Whitney."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:06:25.187Z"}},"1.1.1":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"1.1.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Coastal landscapes is the compulsory entry point into Topic 1.1 for centres choosing Option A, and it underpins every higher-tariff question on Papers 01 and 03: examiners expect systems thinking (inputs, processes, outputs, sediment cells), named case-study evidence for high and low energy coastlines, and an evaluative grip on how physical processes and human intervention interact to change the coast, from a single storm to millennia of sea level change.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Coastal landscapes as systems","t":"A coastline can be modelled as an open system: energy (wave, wind, tidal) and material (sediment) enter as inputs, are transformed by geomorphic processes such as erosion, transport and deposition, and leave as outputs when sediment exits a sediment cell or energy is dissipated on the shore. Examiners reward candidates who frame answers around this input-process-output structure rather than listing landforms in isolation. A top-level answer explicitly uses systems vocabulary - inputs, stores, flows, sediment cell, dynamic equilibrium - to explain why a coastline behaves the way it does, rather than simply describing what is there."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Physical factors: wind, waves, tides, geology and currents","t":"Wind speed, duration and fetch determine wave height and energy; wave type (constructive versus destructive) then controls whether a beach builds up or is stripped back. Tidal range widens or narrows the zone over which wave energy acts each day, while geology - rock type (lithology) and the arrangement of rock layers (structure, including dip and jointing) - governs how fast and in what shapes a coast erodes. Global ocean currents redistribute heat and can influence the storms reaching a coast. A top-level answer links two or more of these factors together, for example showing how a long fetch combined with steeply dipping weak rock produces rapid headland retreat, rather than treating each factor separately."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Sources of coastal sediment","t":"Sediment reaching the coast comes from terrestrial sources (rivers depositing fluvial sediment, weathering and mass movement supplying cliff material, marine erosion of the coast itself, wind-blown aeolian deposition, and longshore drift moving sediment along the shore), from offshore marine deposition, and from human sources such as beach nourishment. A top-level answer treats these sources as the inputs of the sediment budget for a named cell, and uses them to explain whether that cell is likely to be in surplus or deficit, rather than simply listing where sand comes from."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Interconnected processes forming coastal landforms","t":"Weathering, mass movement, and wave, fluvial and aeolian erosion break down and remove material; transportation (including longshore drift) then moves it before deposition builds new forms. These flows of energy and material create the classic erosional sequence - headland, cave, geo, blow hole, arch, stack, stump - on resistant, high energy coasts, and depositional forms such as beaches, spits, bars, tombolos and salt marshes on lower energy coasts. A top-level answer explains a landform as the product of a specific process, or sequence of processes, acting on a specific rock type or sediment supply, rather than simply naming it."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Coastal landforms as inter-related characteristic landscapes","t":"Individual landforms do not exist in isolation: on a high energy coastline such as Holderness, retreating till cliffs supply the sediment that longshore drift then builds into features such as Spurn Head; on a low energy coastline such as the Mississippi delta, river-supplied sediment builds distributary channels, natural levees and marshland that shift over centuries as delta lobes switch. Change operates across vastly different timescales, from a cliff collapse in seconds to spit growth over millennia. A top-level answer uses named, located evidence from both required case studies to show how the landforms of a landscape connect into one system, and how and why that system changes over different timescales."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Emergent coastal landscapes as sea level falls","t":"Where a cooling climate has locked water into ice sheets, or where land has rebounded after ice unloading, relative sea level falls and former wave-cut platforms are stranded above the reach of the sea as raised beaches and marine terraces, with the former cliff line left as an abandoned (relict) cliff. These landforms are then reshaped by present-day processes - chiefly subaerial weathering rather than marine erosion - because the sea no longer reaches them directly. A top-level answer clearly distinguishes the past process that created the landform (falling sea level) from the different, present-day process now modifying it."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Submergent coastal landscapes as sea level rises","t":"Where a warming climate melts ice sheets and causes thermal expansion of the ocean, relative sea level rises and drowns existing landscapes: river valleys become rias, glacially over-deepened valleys become fjords, and low-lying coastal plains can be reworked into shingle beaches as sediment is pushed landward. As with emergent landscapes, present and future climate and sea level change continue to modify these inherited landforms. A top-level answer names the specific landform produced (ria, fjord or shingle beach) and explains both its original submergence and how it is being altered today."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Intentional human change: managing a coastal landscape","t":"Where a coastline is managed - for example by building groynes or carrying out off-shore dredging - the strategy is chosen deliberately to alter flows of energy and material through the system, typically to trap or add sediment and reduce wave energy reaching the shore. This intentional change to the sediment budget then alters coastal landforms, such as beach profile, which in turn changes the wider landscape, for example by extending the shoreline seawards where sediment accumulates. A top-level answer follows this whole chain - strategy, intended impact on flows, resulting landform change, landscape-scale consequence - using a named, located management scheme, rather than describing the scheme alone."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Unintentional human change: economic development","t":"Economic development such as port construction, trade-route dredging or tourist resort building is not undertaken to change the coast, but it can still disturb flows of material and energy through the system, for example by disturbing the balance of a sediment cell. The resulting effect on landforms (such as an altered beach profile) then has a consequence for the landscape, such as coastal retreat where sediment supply is reduced. A top-level answer treats the human activity as an unintended input into the system, and traces its knock-on effect through processes, landforms and landscape exactly as it would for an intentional scheme."}],"examples":[{"title":"Sediment budget calculation for a stretch of coastline","setup":"In one year a sediment cell receives 12,000 m3 of sediment from cliff erosion and 3,000 m3 from river input, and in the same year loses 9,000 m3 to longshore drift leaving the cell and 4,000 m3 carried offshore. Calculate the cell's net sediment budget for the year and say what it means for its beaches.","steps":["Add the two flows that put sediment into the cell: cliff erosion contributes 12,000 m3 and the river 3,000 m3, giving total inputs of 15,000 m3.","Add the two flows that remove it: 9,000 m3 drifts out of the cell alongshore and 4,000 m3 is carried offshore, giving total outputs of 13,000 m3.","Net sediment budget = inputs − outputs = 15,000 − 13,000 = +2,000 m3 over the year.","A surplus of +2,000 m3 means the cell gains more sediment than it loses, so its beaches should thicken and build seaward over the year — the store within the cell is growing, not being drawn down."],"cues":["Which two flows add sediment, and what do they total?","Which two flows remove it, and what do they total?","How are the two totals combined?","What does that balance mean for the beaches?"]},{"title":"Calculating a rate of erosion from map evidence","setup":"On a 1990 map and a 2020 map of the same stretch of coast, the cliff top has moved 38 m further inland. Calculate the average annual rate of erosion between the two survey dates, to two decimal places.","steps":["Time elapsed between the two surveys = 2020 − 1990 = 30 years.","A rate is distance divided by time, so rate of retreat = 38 m ÷ 30 years.","38 ÷ 30 = 1.27 m per year (2 d.p.): the average rate of cliff-top retreat over the period, which smooths together the sudden falls and the quiet years inside it."],"cues":["How many years separate the two maps?","What is divided by what to give a rate?","So what is the annual rate, to 2 d.p.?"]},{"title":"Explaining why Mappleton was protected but Great Cowden was not","setup":"Mappleton and Great Cowden are neighbouring villages on the Holderness coast, East Yorkshire, where soft glacial till cliffs face the North Sea and longshore drift moves sediment north to south; Great Cowden lies immediately south of Mappleton. In 1991-92 the council defended Mappleton with a rock revetment and two rock groynes, and left Great Cowden undefended. Explain why Mappleton was defended and what the scheme has done to Great Cowden.","steps":["What was at stake at Mappleton: the village's homes and the coast road serving the settlements along this stretch stood on a retreating till cliff, and the council judged those fixed assets worth the cost of a scheme.","What the two defences do: the rock revetment absorbs and dissipates wave energy at the cliff foot, while the rock groynes trap sediment travelling south, so a wider, higher beach builds up in front of Mappleton.","The cost to the sediment cell: the sediment held behind the groynes no longer passes on down the coast, so the beach at Great Cowden is starved and thins, leaving more wave energy to reach the till cliff there.","So Mappleton was defended because its road and property justified the outlay, and the scheme has displaced the problem rather than solved it: cliff retreat at undefended Great Cowden has been reported to accelerate since the groynes went in, a classic terminal-groyne effect."],"cues":["What at Mappleton was worth the cost of defending?","How does each defence change waves or sediment?","If drift is trapped, what happens south of Mappleton?","So why defended — and at whose expense?"]},{"title":"Converting an erosion rate for use in a written answer","setup":"A source quotes a cliff retreat rate of 1.8 m per year for a stretch of coast. Work out what that rate gives over a century and what it is in centimetres per year, and state the assumption that both conversions make.","steps":["Over a century at a constant rate: 1.8 × 100 = 180 m of cliff-top retreat.","Changing the unit: 1 m = 100 cm, so 1.8 m per year = 180 cm per year — the same scale used for seasonal beach-profile change, so the two can be set side by side.","The assumption behind both: that retreat happens steadily, when a till cliff in fact fails episodically, standing for years and then losing metres in a single collapse.","So the rate converts to 180 cm per year and projects to 180 m per century, but that century figure should be quoted as an average only: it understates individual cliff falls and overstates the quiet years between them."],"cues":["What is 1.8 m/yr over 100 years, if constant?","How many centimetres in a metre — so cm/yr?","What does treating the rate as constant assume?","How should the two figures be qualified in an answer?"]},{"title":"Building a Level 4 point on emergent landscapes","setup":"An essay must build one Level 4 point about an emergent coastline where a raised beach and its shore platform now sit several metres above the present high-tide line, backed by an abandoned cliff. Set out the point, the evidence for it, and the reasoning that explains both what shaped the landform then and what modifies it now.","steps":["Point: the raised beach is a relict landform — it records a fall in relative sea level that has already happened, not a coast still emerging under today's processes.","Evidence: the beach deposits and the platform beneath them were cut at a former high-tide line, so their height above today's high tide measures how far relative sea level has fallen since; the cliff behind is abandoned precisely because waves no longer reach its foot.","Explanation of how it formed, first the loading: through the last glacial period the weight of the ice sheet resting on this coast depressed the crust isostatically, so the land was held down while so much water was locked up in ice.","Then deglaciation worked at two speeds: meltwater returning to the oceans raised eustatic sea level, and waves cut the shore platform and built the beach at that high relative sea level; as the eustatic rise slowed, post-glacial isostatic rebound of the now unloaded crust outpaced it and carried platform and beach up clear of wave attack.","Reasoning that closes the point: with marine erosion no longer operating at the cliff foot, sub-aerial weathering, mass movement and vegetation growth now dominate the landform's modification — so the raised beach is evidence of a past process regime preserved in the present landscape."],"cues":["Is a raised beach a present or a past record?","What does its height above high tide measure?","What did the ice sheet do to the crust beneath it?","After the ice went, which rose faster — land or sea?","Which processes shape it now that waves cannot?"]},{"title":"Weighing a 'to what extent' judgement","setup":"An essay asks: 'To what extent is human intervention, rather than geology, the main control on erosion rates along the Holderness coast?' The evidence available is that the whole coast is soft glacial till facing a long North Sea fetch and retreats at roughly 1.8 m a year where it is left undefended; that at Mappleton, protected by rock groynes and a revetment, the cliff line has been held; and that immediately downdrift at Great Cowden retreat has been reported to speed up. Weigh the two factors and reach a judgement.","steps":["The case for geology as the control: the till is unconsolidated glacial deposit with no resistant strata, and waves crossing a long North Sea fetch arrive with high energy, so rapid retreat is what this coast does everywhere it is left alone.","The case for human intervention: the same till at Mappleton barely retreats behind the revetment and groynes, so at that point what sets the rate is the engineering, not the lithology.","Great Cowden is the decisive comparison: its geology is identical to Mappleton's, yet its retreat has speeded up since the groynes began intercepting the southward drift, so the difference between the two sites cannot be put down to the rock.","Judgement: geology sets the coast's underlying vulnerability — unconsolidated till on a high-energy coast can only retreat quickly — but over the short term human intervention is the decisive control, because it redistributes where that vulnerability is expressed, holding the line at Mappleton at the price of faster loss at Great Cowden."],"cues":["What in the rock and the waves favours fast retreat?","What does Mappleton show about the same till?","Why is Great Cowden the decisive comparison?","Which control decides the rate, and over what timescale?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the input-process-output structure of a coastal system called?","a":"A systems approach (or systems framework)."},{"q":"Which kind of landform would you expect to dominate a low energy, low relief coastline rather than a high energy rocky one?","a":"Depositional landforms such as beaches, spits or salt marshes, rather than erosional landforms such as cliffs and stacks."},{"q":"If sediment outputs from a cell exceed inputs, what happens to the coastline within that cell?","a":"It is in deficit and the coastline is likely to erode or retreat."},{"q":"Which changes sea level over millennia, and which changes a beach profile in seconds?","a":"Climate-driven sea level change (emergence or submergence) operates over millennia; storm wave impact reshaping a beach profile can happen in seconds to hours."}]},"room":{"facts":["Rate of erosion = total retreat distance divided by the number of years elapsed (e.g. m/yr); multiply or divide to convert between m, cm and km.","The coastline of England and Wales is divided into 11 major sediment cells, each acting as a largely self-contained budget of sediment inputs, transfers and outputs.","Longshore drift moves sediment along a beach in the direction set by the dominant angle of wave approach: swash pushes material up the beach at that angle, backwash pulls it straight back down under gravity.","A sediment budget balances inputs (fluvial, cliff erosion, offshore, aeolian, human nourishment) against outputs (longshore drift out of the cell, offshore transport, aeolian loss); if outputs exceed inputs, the cell is in deficit and the coastline tends to erode.","Erosional landform sequence on a headland: crack/joint, exploited by hydraulic action and abrasion, widens into a cave, may break through to form a geo or blow hole, enlarges into an arch, whose collapsing roof leaves a stack, which erodes further to a stump."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify two outputs of a coastal landscape system.","a":"Any two valid outputs, e.g. sediment leaving the sediment cell (via longshore drift or offshore transport) and wave/tidal energy dissipated on the shore.","sol":"Two valid outputs of a coastal system are: (1) sediment removed from the cell by longshore drift into a neighbouring cell, and (2) wave energy that is dissipated as waves break and run up the beach, having been transformed from open-water wave energy into swash and backwash.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a system releases once material and energy have moved through it, not what enters it.","Match each output to the opposite of an input you already know for this system.","Two short, separately labelled points are enough at this tariff, with no explanation required."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify two landforms that are predominantly formed by erosion on a high energy coastline.","a":"Any two from: bays, headlands, cliffs, shore platforms, geos, blow holes, caves, arches, stacks, stumps.","sol":"Two erosional landforms are a headland, formed where a band of more resistant rock protrudes as weaker rock either side is cut back, and a wave-cut (shore) platform, the gently sloping rock surface left behind as a cliff retreats under repeated wave attack.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the sequence of shapes cut into resistant rock as waves attack a coastline.","Choose from the specification's own list of erosional forms rather than inventing new terms.","Name each landform in a single word or short phrase; no process explanation is needed here."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between swash and backwash.","a":"Swash is the forward movement of water and sediment up the beach as a wave breaks, driven by the wave's forward momentum; backwash is the return flow of that water back down the beach under gravity. Which one dominates determines whether a beach gains or loses sediment.","sol":"Swash is the surge of water, and any sediment it carries, moving up the beach face as a wave breaks, driven by the wave's forward momentum. Backwash is the water draining back down the beach face afterwards, pulled by gravity rather than by any forward wave force. The balance between the two matters because it controls net sediment movement: on a constructive wave, swash is stronger than backwash, so more material is carried up the beach than is dragged back down, and the beach builds up; on a destructive wave, backwash is stronger than swash, so net sediment is pulled back down the beach, and the beach is eroded.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the two halves of a single wave's journey up and back down the beach.","Consider which direction each half moves in, what causes that movement, and why the balance between them matters for the beach.","Explain both halves, with a reason for the direction of movement, rather than giving a single bare definition of each."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Explain how geology influences the formation of erosional landforms on a rocky coastline.","a":"Lithology (rock strength/resistance) controls the rate of erosion; structure (dip, jointing, alternating resistant/weak bands) controls where and in what shapes erosion is concentrated, e.g. discordant coastlines producing headlands and bays.","sol":"Lithology determines how quickly a rock erodes: a resistant rock such as granite withstands hydraulic action and abrasion far longer than a weak, unconsolidated rock such as boulder clay, which can retreat metres in a single storm. Structure then determines where that erosion is concentrated: where bands of resistant and weaker rock lie at right angles to the coast (a discordant coastline), the weaker rock is cut back into bays while the resistant rock survives as headlands; where joints or faults create lines of weakness, waves exploit these to open geos, and to cut caves that may progress to arches, stacks and stumps as the roof collapses. Geology therefore explains both the rate and the exact location and shape of erosional landforms.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the rock properties waves are working against, beyond simply 'hard' or 'soft'.","Remember that 'explain' means giving the reasons behind erosion patterns, not just naming or describing landforms you would find on a rocky coastline.","Structure your answer in two linked parts, each explaining a different aspect of the geology."],"codes":["1.b","2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how the construction of a harbour wall, built to shelter a port rather than to manage erosion, can unintentionally disrupt the sediment budget of a coastal landscape system.","a":"The wall blocks the longshore movement of sediment, so sand accumulates on the updrift side of the structure while the downdrift beach stops receiving new sediment, pushing that stretch's budget into deficit and causing it to erode, even though the wall was not built with the coast in mind.","sol":"A harbour wall is built to shelter a port entrance from waves, not to manage the coastline, but because it projects out into the surf zone it interrupts the longshore drift carrying sediment along the beach. On the updrift side of the wall, sediment that would otherwise have continued past is trapped and builds up, widening the beach there. On the downdrift side, the beach can no longer be resupplied by drift from updrift, so its sediment budget swings into deficit as wave action keeps removing material with nothing arriving to replace it, and the coastline there narrows and retreats. This mirrors the effect of a deliberately built groyne, but here the interruption to the sediment budget is an unintended by-product of a structure built for an entirely different, non-coastal purpose.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about a structure built to shelter shipping, not to manage the coast, that still sticks out into the surf zone.","Consider what happens to sediment travelling along the beach when it meets a solid barrier it cannot pass.","Explain the effect on one side of the structure, then the different effect on the other side, before linking both to a budget change."],"codes":["1.c","4.b"]},{"q":"Using a named example, explain how a coastal landscape has been managed to reduce erosion.","a":"Named UK scheme, e.g. Mappleton, Holderness: rock groynes/revetment built to trap sediment and absorb wave energy, protecting the village but altering longshore drift and sediment supply further along the coast.","sol":"At Mappleton on the Holderness coast, two rock groynes and a rock revetment were built in 1991-92 to protect the village and the coast road from an eroding till cliff. The groynes trap sediment moving south by longshore drift, building up a wider protective beach in front of Mappleton, while the revetment absorbs and reflects wave energy before it reaches the cliff foot. This intentional interruption of the drift has starved beaches further south, such as at Great Cowden, of sediment they previously received, so erosion there has been reported to accelerate even as Mappleton itself has been stabilised.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall a UK case where a specific structure, not just a policy name, was built to hold a village's coastline.","Explain what the structure physically does to wave energy or to sediment moving along the shore.","End by describing the effect the scheme had beyond the immediate site it was built to protect."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"A cliff top was measured 54 m further inland in 2005 than it had been in 1975. Calculate the average annual rate of erosion in cm per year, and comment on what this rate suggests about the coastline.","a":"54 m divided by 30 years = 1.8 m/yr = 180 cm/yr; a rate this high suggests a soft, easily eroded coastline (e.g. till) rather than a resistant rock coast.","sol":"Time elapsed = 2005 - 1975 = 30 years. Rate of erosion = 54 m divided by 30 years = 1.8 m per year. Converting to centimetres: 1.8 m/yr x 100 = 180 cm per year. A retreat rate of this size is high for a UK coastline, close to the coast's long-term average for Holderness, with hotspots nearer 4 m per year, and points to a weak, unconsolidated lithology such as glacial till being attacked by a long-fetch, high-energy wave climate, rather than a resistant rock coastline, which would typically retreat at a small fraction of this rate.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Work out how much time separates the two survey dates before doing anything else.","Find the rate in metres per year first, then convert to the unit the question actually asks for.","Give your numerical answer, then relate the size of that number to the kind of coastline that would produce it."],"codes":["2.a","2.b"]},{"q":"Using a named example, explain how economic development has unintentionally changed a coastal landscape.","a":"Named beyond-UK example, e.g. Nile delta and the Aswan High Dam: development for irrigation/power reduces sediment supply, altering landforms and causing coastal retreat.","sol":"On the Nile delta, large-scale irrigation and hydroelectric development were the goals of the Aswan High Dam, completed in 1970, not coastal change; but by trapping the great majority of the Nile's sediment load in Lake Nasser, the dam removed the sediment that had built and maintained the delta coast for millennia. Longshore currents continue to erode the Rosetta and Damietta promontories, but with far less new sediment arriving, the beach profile along these promontories has steepened and narrowed, and the shoreline has retreated, threatening farmland, fish farms and low-lying settlement close to the coast.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Choose a beyond-UK example where the development's original purpose had nothing to do with the coastline itself.","Identify the specific flow of material the development interrupted before describing the coastal effect.","Trace your answer through from the development, to the landform change, to the wider landscape consequence."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"A beach profile survey recorded a beach width of 42 m in summer and 27 m in winter. Calculate the percentage decrease in beach width between the two surveys.","a":"Decrease = 15 m; 15 divided by 42, times 100, is approximately 35.7%.","sol":"Decrease in width = 42 m - 27 m = 15 m. Percentage decrease = (15 divided by 42) x 100 = approximately 35.7%. This roughly one-third narrowing is typical of the seasonal switch from gentler, constructive summer waves to steeper, destructive winter waves, which pull sediment offshore into a bar rather than leaving it on the visible beach.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the actual change in width in metres before turning it into a percentage.","Divide that change by the original (summer) width, then convert to a percentage.","Give the percentage to one decimal place with the working that produced it shown."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"To what extent do physical factors alone explain the differences between high energy and low energy coastlines?","a":"Should weigh wave energy/fetch and geology against human influences (management, altered sediment supply), using named high- and low-energy case studies, reaching a qualified judgement.","sol":"Physical factors go a long way towards explaining the contrast between high energy and low energy coastlines. Wave energy, driven by fetch, wind speed and duration, is the most direct physical control: Holderness faces a long North Sea fetch generating powerful, destructive waves that attack weak glacial till, producing a rapidly retreating, erosional, high energy landscape of crumbling till cliffs. By contrast, the Mississippi delta sits in the low wave energy, microtidal Gulf of Mexico, where river-supplied sediment can accumulate faster than the weak waves remove it, producing a low energy, depositional landscape of distributary channels, levees and marsh. Geology reinforces this physical contrast, since Holderness's unconsolidated till could not withstand even a moderate wave climate, whereas a resistant rock coastline exposed to the same North Sea fetch would retreat far more slowly, showing that lithology and wave energy interact rather than acting alone. However, physical factors are not the whole story. At Holderness, human management at Mappleton, where rock groynes and a revetment were built in 1991-92, has locally reduced erosion to a fraction of the unmanaged rate despite the wave climate and geology being unchanged, while accelerating erosion at undefended Great Cowden further along the same coast; the difference between these two sites cannot be explained by physical factors at all. At the Mississippi delta, the logic runs in reverse: extensive levee building for flood control and navigation, and upstream damming, has cut the sediment supply that the delta's naturally low wave energy alone would not have removed, so wetland and land loss have accelerated regardless of the wave climate remaining calm. On balance, physical factors, principally wave energy/fetch and geology, set the fundamental potential for a coastline to be high or low energy, and explain most of the contrast between an undefended, high energy Holderness and a naturally low energy Mississippi delta. But the case studies show that human intervention, intentional at Mappleton and unintentional at the Mississippi delta, can measurably override that physical potential at specific, well-documented sites, so physical factors alone do not fully explain outcomes everywhere.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Consider both the case for physical control and for human control across your two case studies.","Use your high energy and low energy examples as the direct evidence for each side of the argument.","Build towards a conclusion that states how far you agree, rather than describing each factor in turn."],"codes":["1.b","2.b"]},{"q":"'Human intervention has a greater impact on coastal landscape systems than physical processes.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"16-mark essay: define terms, develop paragraphs contrasting physical process evidence with human intervention evidence, intentional and unintentional, from named case studies, evaluate throughout, reach a qualified judgement.","sol":"Physical processes remain the foundation of every coastal system: wave energy generated by wind and fetch, and the lithology and structure of the coast, set the underlying rate and style of change, as shown by the rapid natural retreat of the weak till cliffs of Holderness under a powerful North Sea wave climate. Human intervention, however, can locally overwhelm this physical control. At Mappleton, the rock groynes and revetment built in 1991-92 intentionally interrupted longshore drift and wave energy, reducing local erosion to a small fraction of the unmanaged rate, while accelerating erosion further along the coast at Great Cowden, a change in the sediment budget with no equivalent shift in the physical wave or geological regime. Unintentional intervention can be equally decisive: the Aswan High Dam was never built to affect the coast, yet by trapping the Nile's sediment load it converted a naturally maintained delta coastline into a retreating one, a reversal that physical processes alone cannot explain. Taken together, physical processes explain the underlying potential and pace of change a coastline would show left alone, but the case studies show that human intervention, whether intentional or not, can now match or exceed that physical control at specific, well-documented sites, so the statement holds to a considerable extent, though not everywhere, since many undefended stretches of coast still change overwhelmingly under physical control alone.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Set the physical baseline before bringing in the human cases that complicate it.","Draw on both an intentional and an unintentional example of human impact for your strongest answer.","Reach a conclusion that states how far you agree, acknowledging where physical control still dominates."],"codes":["1.b","4.a","4.b"]},{"q":"How far do the landforms of an emergent coastline reflect present-day processes rather than past sea level change?","a":"Explains raised beaches, marine terraces and abandoned cliffs as products of past sea level fall, then evaluates the extent to which present weathering/modification changes them, reaching a qualified judgement that origin is past and modification is present.","sol":"The defining landforms of an emergent coastline, raised beaches, marine terraces and abandoned cliffs, owe their existence overwhelmingly to a past episode of sea level fall relative to the land, during a previous cooling climate, when a wave-cut platform and its beach deposits were left stranded above the sea's current reach. In this sense, present-day marine processes cannot be said to explain these landforms at all, since the sea no longer reaches them directly to erode or nourish them. However, once formed, these relict landforms are not frozen: present and future climate and sea level change continue to modify them, chiefly through subaerial weathering, mass movement and soil development acting on the abandoned cliff face and the surface of the raised beach, and, where sea level rise now brings the sea back towards a low raised beach, renewed marine reworking is possible. Overall, present-day processes explain the ongoing modification and slow degradation of emergent landforms, but not their origin or their fundamental location and form, which are inherited entirely from a past, different sea level; the landforms are therefore relict features being gradually altered by the present, not features created by it.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the process that first made each landform from any process acting on it today.","Consider what type of process, marine or subaerial, can now reach a landform that used to be at sea level.","Conclude with a clear statement of how far 'present-day' can explain what you see, not just yes or no."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Identify two landforms associated with a submergent coastline.","a":"Any two from: rias, fjords, shingle beaches.","sol":"Two submergent landforms are a ria, a drowned river valley flooded by rising sea level, and a fjord, a drowned, glacially over-deepened valley flooded in the same way but typically steeper-sided with a shallower rock lip near its mouth.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think of landforms created when the sea rises to flood an existing valley shape.","There is more than one shape of valley that can be drowned by rising sea level - make sure your two answers are genuinely different landforms, not two names for the same one.","Name each landform in a word or short phrase; no explanation is required at this tariff."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how sea level change has influenced the landforms of an emergent coastline.","a":"Falling sea level (from a cooling climate or isostatic rebound) strands a former wave-cut platform and beach above current sea level as a raised beach/marine terrace, and the former cliff becomes an abandoned cliff.","sol":"During a previous cooler climatic period, falling sea level, whether from more of the world's water being locked into growing ice sheets or from the land rebounding as ice sheets melted from it, left the wave-cut platform and beach deposits that had formed at the old shoreline stranded above the reach of the present-day sea. These stranded surfaces are preserved as raised beaches and marine terraces, while the cliff that once backed the old shoreline now stands inland of the modern coast as an abandoned cliff, no longer attacked by waves. Sea level change is therefore the direct cause of both the position and the form of these landforms, even though the geomorphic processes that originally cut them, marine erosion, were identical to those shaping an active coastline today.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what causes relative sea level to fall in the first place.","Consider what happens to a shoreline's landforms once the sea that shaped them retreats from them.","Name the resulting landform types as you explain the mechanism that produced them."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Identify two ways in which wind direction and frequency, and global ocean currents, act as inputs into a coastal landscape system.","a":"Wind direction and frequency determine the dominant angle of wave approach, which sets the direction of longshore drift; ocean currents redistribute heat around the globe and can influence the frequency or intensity of storms reaching a coast, adding to its wave energy input.","sol":"Wind is not just a source of wave energy through its speed, duration and fetch: the direction it blows from, and how frequently it blows from that direction, sets the dominant angle at which waves approach a coastline, which in turn fixes the direction of longshore drift along that shore. Ocean currents are a second, larger-scale input: by redistributing heat around the globe, they influence the atmospheric and sea-surface conditions that generate storms, so a coast lying in the path of a current-linked storm track receives more frequent or more energetic wave inputs than one that does not. Both are therefore inputs into the system in the same sense as fetch or tidal range: external energy and directional information entering the coast from beyond the shore itself.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about wind as more than just a source of wave height - consider what else about the wind matters to a coastline.","Consider a second physical input operating at a much larger, ocean-basin scale than local wind and waves.","Give one point about wind and one point about currents, in a sentence or two each; no full explanation is required at this tariff."],"codes":["1.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is a sediment cell?","a":"A length of coastline, usually bounded by prominent headlands, within which inputs, transfers and outputs of sediment form a largely self-contained system."},{"q":"Name the process that moves sediment along a beach in the direction of the dominant wave approach.","a":"Longshore drift."},{"q":"Give one landform typical of a submergent coastline.","a":"A ria, a fjord, or a shingle beach (any one)."},{"q":"What do you need to know, besides the distance a cliff has retreated, to calculate a rate of erosion?","a":"The length of time, in years, over which that retreat occurred."}]},"cards":[{"front":"What three elements make up a coastal system?","back":"Inputs (e.g. wave and wind energy, sediment), processes (erosion, transport, deposition) and outputs (e.g. sediment leaving the cell, energy dissipated on the shore).","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Constructive vs destructive wave - what's the key difference?","back":"Constructive waves have a stronger swash than backwash, building the beach up; destructive waves have a stronger backwash than swash, eroding the beach.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Name the three broad sources of coastal sediment.","back":"Terrestrial (rivers, weathering, mass movement, longshore drift), offshore (marine deposition), and human (beach nourishment).","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Name three erosional processes that shape coastal landforms.","back":"Hydraulic action — waves compressing air into joints in the cliff; abrasion (corrasion) — sediment hurled at the cliff face by waves; attrition — clasts colliding and rounding in the surf zone; solution (corrosion) — seawater slowly dissolving chalk or limestone. Any three are creditworthy.","code":"2.a","open":true},{"front":"Put in order: cave, arch, stack, stump.","back":"Cave, then arch, then stack, then stump - each stage forms as continued erosion collapses the landform before it.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Which UK coastline is the high-energy case study here?","back":"The Holderness coast, East Yorkshire - soft glacial till retreating rapidly under a powerful North Sea wave climate.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Which beyond-UK coastline is the low-energy case study here?","back":"The Mississippi delta, Louisiana, USA - a low wave-energy, river-dominated depositional coastline.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"What is a raised beach?","back":"A former wave-cut platform and beach stranded above current sea level after relative sea level fell, now modified mainly by weathering rather than waves.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"What is a ria?","back":"A drowned river valley, flooded by rising sea level, widening gradually towards the sea.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"What is a fjord?","back":"A drowned, glacially over-deepened valley flooded by rising sea level - steep-sided, often with a shallower rock lip near its mouth.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"What management was used at Mappleton, Holderness?","back":"Rock groynes and a rock revetment, built in 1991-92, to protect the village and coast road from erosion.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Why did the Aswan High Dam unintentionally damage the Nile delta coast?","back":"It trapped most of the Nile's sediment in Lake Nasser, starving the delta coast of replenishment, so waves now erode faster than the coast rebuilds.","code":"4.b"},{"front":"How do you calculate a rate of coastal erosion?","back":"Divide the total distance the coastline has retreated by the number of years over which that retreat happened.","code":"2.a"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 4","items":["Human intervention has been the decisive control on the Holderness coast wherever it has been applied, even though the underlying geology remains constant along the whole coastline.","At Mappleton, two rock groynes and a rock revetment built in 1991-92 trap longshore drift and absorb wave energy, so the village and its coast road have been stabilised despite standing on the same weak glacial till that retreats rapidly everywhere else along Holderness.","This works because the scheme directly interrupts the sediment budget: by holding sediment that would otherwise continue south, it locally reverses the deficit that drives erosion, while starving beaches further along the coast, such as Great Cowden, of the sediment they previously received, and so accelerating erosion there instead.","The contrast between Mappleton's stability and Cowden's faster retreat, both cut from identical till under an identical wave climate, shows that human management, not geology or wave energy, is the factor separating the two outcomes.","To a considerable extent, then, human intervention now controls how the Holderness coast evolves at any specific, defended site, even though geology and wave energy still set the pace of change everywhere intervention has not reached."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2","items":["Human intervention has affected the Holderness coast a lot.","For example, at Mappleton they built some rock defences to stop erosion, which helped protect the village.","This shows that management can change how much a coastline erodes, because putting in defences means the waves cannot reach the cliff as easily.","So human intervention is quite important for the coast."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Holderness coast (high energy)","items":["Location: East Yorkshire coast, England, running roughly 61 km from Flamborough Head south to Spurn Head.","Geology: soft, unconsolidated glacial till (boulder clay), highly vulnerable to both marine erosion and rainfall-triggered mass movement.","Erosion rate: among the fastest-eroding coastlines in Europe, with an average long-term retreat commonly cited at approximately 1.5-2 m per year, though individual storms can remove several metres in a single event.","Sediment fate: eroded till supplies sand and shingle that longshore drift carries south, feeding the growth of Spurn Head spit at the southern end of the coast.","Historic loss: numerous villages recorded in medieval sources have been lost to the sea along this coast as the cliff line has retreated over the centuries."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Mississippi delta (low energy)","items":["Location: mouth of the Mississippi River, Louisiana, south-eastern USA, extending into the low wave-energy, microtidal Gulf of Mexico.","Landform type: a bird's-foot (digitate) delta, built by fluvial deposition through branching distributary channels bordered by natural levees.","Timescale of change: the delta has built a series of distinct lobes over approximately the last 7,000 years as the river has periodically switched course, abandoning older lobes to subside and erode.","Coastal wetland loss: coastal Louisiana's marshes and wetlands have shrunk substantially over the twentieth century, a change widely attributed to reduced sediment and freshwater reaching the delta plain.","Energy setting: low wave energy and a small tidal range allow river-supplied sediment to accumulate faster than it is removed, producing the delta's depositional, low-relief landforms."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Mappleton coastal management, Holderness","items":["Location: the village of Mappleton, on the actively eroding Holderness coast, East Yorkshire.","Strategy: two rock groynes and a rock revetment, constructed in 1991-92 by the local council.","Reason: to protect the village, the B1242 coast road and surrounding farmland from an eroding till cliff that threatened to cut the road within a few years.","Intentional impact: the groynes trap sediment moving south by longshore drift, widening the beach and reducing the wave energy reaching the cliff foot in front of the village.","Consequence beyond the site: with less sediment passing south of Mappleton, erosion has been reported to accelerate along the undefended coast further south, such as at Great Cowden, illustrating how defending one point on a coastline can increase erosion downdrift."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study factfile - Nile delta economic development, Egypt","items":["Location: the Nile delta, northern Egypt, where the Nile splits into the Rosetta and Damietta distributaries before reaching the Mediterranean Sea.","Economic development: intensive agriculture (notably cotton and rice) on the delta's fertile silts, extensive fishing and aquaculture (including Lake Manzala and Lake Burullus), and major ports such as Alexandria, Damietta and Port Said.","Key intervention: the Aswan High Dam, completed in 1970, built for irrigation control and hydroelectric power rather than to change the coast.","Unintentional impact: the dam traps the great majority of the Nile's sediment load in the reservoir (Lake Nasser) behind it, sharply reducing the sediment reaching the delta coast.","Consequence: with far less sediment to replace what waves remove, the Rosetta and Damietta promontories have retreated significantly in the decades since the dam was completed, threatening farmland, fish farms and low-lying settlement close to the coast."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - 'The evolution of coastal landscapes owes more to human activity than to physical processes.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? (16 marks)","items":["Decoded question: weigh physical controls (wave energy, geology, sea level history) against human controls (intentional management and unintentional development) on landform and landscape change, and reach a stated, qualified judgement on which matters more.","Paragraph 1 - establish the physical baseline: use wave energy/fetch and geology to explain the underlying difference between a high energy coastline (Holderness) and a low energy coastline (Mississippi delta), showing what physical processes alone would produce.","Paragraph 2 - intentional human change: use the Mappleton rock groynes and revetment to show management directly overriding the physical baseline at a specific, defended site, while shifting the problem to an undefended site nearby.","Paragraph 3 - unintentional human change: use the Aswan High Dam's effect on the Nile delta's sediment supply to show that even development with no coastal purpose can reverse a coastline's natural behaviour.","Evaluation thread: in each paragraph, weigh the human factor against the physical baseline it is acting on, rather than treating the two as separate lists - keep returning to whether the human change could have happened without the physical setting (weak till, sediment-starved delta) that made it effective.","Conclusion must state: a qualified judgement that human activity now exerts the stronger control at specific, well-documented sites, but that physical processes still set the underlying potential and dominate wherever no intervention has occurred, so the statement is true to a considerable but not total extent."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.936Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Content, case studies and answer keys are sound - the fact files are all coastal, the required set is complete and correctly balanced (Holderness UK high-energy, Mississippi delta beyond-UK low-energy, Mappleton UK managed, Nile delta beyond-UK used-by-people, giving two beyond-UK plus UK), all nine key ideas are covered in the board's terms and each lesson section closes with what a top-level answer does, both calculations re-solve exactly (54/30 = 1.8 m/yr = 180 cm/yr; 15/42 = 35.7%) as do all four worked examples, and the Level 4 / Level 2 model paragraphs are genuinely and visibly different; minor polish only beyond the listed problems - questions[2] uses 'State', which is not among the board's command words ('Explain the difference' or 'Define' would fit), questions[6].sol calls 1.8 m/yr 'comparable to the fastest-eroding sections of the Holderness coast' when that is nearer its long-term average than its 4 m/yr hotspots, the two calculation questions are coded 2.a/2.b rather than to the topic-skills strand, and the specification's wind 'direction and frequency' and the global pattern of ocean currents get one thin sentence in lesson idea 1.b and no question at all."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:06:45.829Z"}},"1.1.2":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"1.1.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because OCR's Physical Systems paper (Component 01) can set Option B Glaciated Landscapes in its low-tariff short-answer questions, its 6- to 10-mark medium questions and its 16-mark extended responses, and every one of those questions draws on the same conceptual model that opens the topic: inputs, processes, outputs and mass balance. Examiners reward answers that use this systems vocabulary precisely, connect physical factors and glacier type to the actual landforms produced, and back every extended answer with named, located evidence from the case studies the specification requires. Without a secure grip on mass balance, glacier movement, the erosional and depositional landform set, and the required case studies, the 12- and 16-mark extended-response questions on this topic cannot reach the top level of the mark scheme.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Glaciated landscapes as systems","t":"A glacier is a system with inputs (precipitation as snow, solar and geothermal energy), processes that transfer and transform mass (compaction of snow into ice, glacier flow, erosion, transport, deposition) and outputs (meltwater runoff, calving, sublimation and evaporation). Its health is tracked through mass balance: accumulation (snowfall building the glacier up) minus ablation (melting, calving and sublimation wearing it down) gives the net balance for the year. The equilibrium line altitude (ELA) is where accumulation equals ablation; above it lies the accumulation zone, below it the ablation zone. A top-level answer opens a question by defining the system in this vocabulary and links a positive or negative balance directly to whether the glacier is likely to advance, thicken, retreat or thin, rather than describing melting and snowfall in vague terms."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Physical factors influencing the system","t":"Climate sets the raw material: total precipitation controls how much snow can accumulate, and its seasonal pattern decides how much falls as snow rather than rain. Geology matters at a finer scale — resistant lithologies such as granite hold up arêtes and pyramidal peaks, while joints, bedding planes and faults are exploited by plucking, controlling where erosion is concentrated. Latitude and altitude together fix how high the ELA sits: nearer the poles or higher above sea level, temperatures stay low enough for snow to persist lower down. Relief and aspect shape the microclimate: in the Northern Hemisphere, north- and east-facing hollows are sheltered from the sun and from the prevailing south-westerly wind, so snow survives through summer; steep relief also encourages basal sliding by increasing gravitational stress on the ice. A top-level answer names the specific factor being tested and explains the mechanism by which it changes accumulation, ablation or movement, rather than just listing the four factors."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Types of glacier and how they move","t":"Glacier ice forms as fresh snow is buried, compacted and recrystallised over years into firn and then dense, air-poor glacier ice. Valley glaciers are constrained within existing valley sides, flowing downhill under gravity, while ice sheets are unconstrained, continental-scale masses that bury the landscape beneath them and flow outward under their own weight. Warm-based (temperate) glaciers have a base at the pressure-melting point, so meltwater lubricates the bed and the ice moves by both basal sliding and internal deformation; cold-based (polar) glaciers are frozen to the bedrock and move almost entirely by internal deformation, the slow creep of ice crystals under pressure. A top-level answer uses this distinction to explain why warm-based glaciers erode far more effectively than cold-based ones: sliding drags rock debris across the bed, while a frozen base cannot."},{"code":"2.a","h":"How interconnected processes build glacial landforms","t":"Weathering (chiefly freeze-thaw) and mass movement (rockfall, avalanches) supply debris onto and into the ice; nivation slowly enlarges a snow-filled hollow through repeated freeze-thaw and meltwater erosion; glacial erosion by abrasion (embedded debris scratching bedrock) and plucking (meltwater freezing onto and tearing away jointed rock) does the rest. Erosional landforms build from this: a nivation hollow deepened by rotational sliding and plucking becomes a corrie; two corries back to back leave a knife-edge arête, three or more a pyramidal peak; a valley glacier straightens, widens, deepens and over-steepens a river valley into a U-shaped trough with truncated spurs and hanging tributary valleys; a roche moutonnée is abraded smooth on its up-glacier side and plucked jagged on its down-glacier side; striations record the direction of ice flow. Deposition, once the ice can no longer carry its load, produces terminal, lateral and recessional moraines marking former ice margins, erratics carried far from their source rock, streamlined drumlins moulded beneath moving ice, and unsorted till sheets. A top-level answer names the specific process behind each landform rather than describing its shape alone."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Landforms as an inter-related, evolving landscape","t":"In a real landscape the erosional and depositional landforms above do not occur in isolation: a corrie basin feeds an arête, a trough valley carries moraines and drumlins downstream of it, and the whole assemblage reflects the physical factors — climate, geology, altitude, relief and aspect — that shaped it. The specification requires one case study of a landscape shaped by valley glaciers and one shaped by an ice sheet, at least one of which must be from beyond the UK, so that this inter-relationship and the physical controls behind it can be shown with named, located evidence. The same landscape also changes at very different speeds: a rockfall can alter a slope in seconds, deposition rates shift with the seasons, and the erosion of a deep corrie basin takes millennia. A top-level answer always names the specific case study and uses its actual features and figures as evidence, and explicitly addresses the timescale the question asks about rather than treating the landscape as static."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Glacio-fluvial landforms after the ice retreats","t":"As a glacial period ends, rising temperatures increase meltwater discharge, and this meltwater reworks and sorts the sediment the ice leaves behind. Kames are mounds of sorted sand and gravel deposited where meltwater ponds against or within stagnant ice; eskers are long, sinuous ridges left by sediment deposited in a meltwater tunnel running under or through the ice; outwash plains (sandar) are broad, gently sloping spreads of sorted, stratified sediment laid down by braided meltwater streams beyond the retreating ice margin. Present and future climate change continues to modify these landforms — further meltwater reworks outwash surfaces, and vegetation colonises stabilising kames and eskers. A top-level answer explains the sorting these landforms show as the direct fingerprint of flowing meltwater, in contrast to the unsorted till produced by ice alone."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Periglacial landforms from climate change around a glacial period","t":"Periglacial conditions — seasonal or permanent ground freezing without an overlying glacier — occur before and/or after a glacial period, wherever the climate is cold enough to sustain permafrost but too dry or too warm for ice to accumulate. Repeated freeze-thaw and frost heave sort stones from finer sediment to produce patterned ground (circles, polygons or stripes), while groundwater freezing and expanding beneath the surface domes the ground upward into an ice-cored pingo. As present and future climate warms, permafrost thaws, ice cores melt and frost sorting weakens, degrading these landforms and, in extreme cases, collapsing them into thermokarst. A top-level answer keeps periglacial landforms clearly distinct from glacial ones — the ice never overrides the ground in a periglacial environment — and explains the freeze-thaw or groundwater mechanism behind the specific landform named in the question."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Human activity changing periglacial landscape systems","t":"People use periglacial landscapes chiefly for resource extraction — oil and gas fields and the roads, pipelines and buildings that support them. This infrastructure adds heat to the ground and removes the insulating surface vegetation, increasing the depth of the active layer that thaws each summer. Where permafrost thaws, ice within the ground melts and the surface subsides unevenly, producing thermokarst: collapsed, hummocky ground pitted with thaw ponds, which in turn damages the very buildings, roads and pipelines that caused the thaw. A top-level answer follows this full chain — activity, its effect on heat or vegetation, the resulting change in ground ice, and the landform consequence — using the specification's required named case study rather than a generic description of permafrost."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Human activity changing glaciated landscape systems","t":"People use glaciated valleys chiefly for their steep sides and reliable glacial meltwater discharge, most often for hydroelectric power, damming a trough to create a reservoir. The dam traps the glacier's sediment load (rock flour and coarser debris) behind it, so the river leaving the dam carries far less sediment than before. Starved of its load, that river can erode its own bed more aggressively immediately below the dam (increased channel scour), while sediment that would once have reached the valley floor downstream now builds up in the reservoir instead, changing the shape and ecology of the valley floor on both sides of the dam. A top-level answer traces this same activity-to-impact-to-landform-change-to-landscape-consequence chain, naming the specification's required case study rather than describing dam-building in general."}],"examples":[{"title":"Calculating a glacier's net mass balance","setup":"A glacier's winter accumulation is measured as +2.8 m water equivalent (w.e.) and its summer ablation — melting, calving and sublimation combined — as −3.4 m w.e. Calculate the glacier's net mass balance for the year and state what the result means for the glacier.","steps":["Compare the two terms first: the loss of −3.4 m w.e. over the summer is greater in size than the gain of +2.8 m w.e. over the winter, so the year must end with a deficit.","Net mass balance = accumulation + ablation = 2.8 + (−3.4) = −0.6 m w.e.","Read what that figure represents: averaged over the whole glacier surface, the ice lost in the year is equivalent to a layer of water 0.6 m deep.","Answer: the net mass balance is −0.6 m w.e.; more mass was lost than gained, so the glacier is thinning and its terminus will retreat up-valley until years of positive balance return."],"cues":["Which is the bigger figure — the gain or the loss?","How are the two figures combined?","What does that figure represent on the ice?","So what happens to the glacier and its snout?"]},{"title":"Locating the equilibrium line altitude (ELA)","setup":"Over a decade of warmer summers on one valley glacier, the equilibrium line altitude (ELA) has risen from approximately 2,000 m to approximately 2,150 m. Explain what the ELA marks on a glacier and what this rise means for the glacier's mass balance.","steps":["High on the glacier, more snow falls each winter than the following summer can melt, so mass is added year on year: this is the accumulation zone, still snow-covered when the melt season ends.","Lower down, each summer removes more ice than the winter's snowfall replaces, so mass is lost: this is the ablation zone, where bare, debris-streaked ice is exposed by late summer.","The ELA is the altitude at which the two exactly cancel — net mass balance = 0 — which is why it can be mapped in the field as the snowline at the end of the melt season.","A rise of 150 m, from approximately 2,000 m to approximately 2,150 m, pushes that dividing line further up the glacier, so a larger share of the ice surface now sits below it in the ablation zone.","Consequence for mass balance: with more of the glacier melting each summer and less of it gaining snow, the annual balance becomes steadily more negative, and the glacier thins and its snout retreats."],"cues":["What happens to snow high on the glacier each year?","And lower down, by the end of summer?","What is the net balance exactly at the ELA?","How far has the line moved, and which zone grows?","So what happens to the annual balance?"]},{"title":"Converting a stake's movement into an annual glacier velocity","setup":"A stake fixed into a glacier's ice surface moves 45 cm down-glacier over 3 days. Estimate the glacier's velocity in metres per year, and say whether that figure is typical for a valley glacier.","steps":["Daily rate of movement = 45 cm ÷ 3 days = 15 cm per day.","Scale the daily rate up to a full year: 15 cm per day × 365 days = 5,475 cm per year.","Convert to metres: 5,475 ÷ 100 = 54.75 m per year.","Answer: the glacier is flowing at about 54.75 m per year, which sits comfortably inside the tens to low hundreds of metres a year usual for valley glaciers — an ordinary flow rate, not a surge."],"cues":["How far does the stake move in one day?","How do you scale a daily rate to a year?","How many centimetres make a metre?","Is that fast or slow for a valley glacier?"]},{"title":"Explaining why UK corries face north-east","setup":"Most former corries in the British Isles have a back wall facing north or east. Explain why this aspect favoured corrie formation in the Northern Hemisphere.","steps":["Insolation: in the Northern Hemisphere the sun stays in the southern half of the sky, so a north-facing hollow lies in shadow for much of the day and the snow lying in it melts most slowly.","Time of day: an east-facing wall takes only the weak morning sun and is shaded through the hottest part of the afternoon, so it too loses less snow to melting.","Wind: the prevailing winds over the British Isles are south-westerly, so north- and east-facing hollows lie in the lee, and snow scoured off the plateau above is redeposited into them as drifts and cornices.","Result: snow here survives the summer and is buried by the next winter's fall, compacting through firn into glacier ice, so nivation, freeze-thaw and plucking of the back wall and rotational sliding have the centuries they need to hollow out a corrie — which is why so many British corries open to the north-east."],"cues":["Where does the sun sit in a Northern Hemisphere sky?","When does an east-facing wall lie in shade?","Which way do the prevailing winds blow here?","What can snow that survives the summer become?"]},{"title":"Working out a simple sediment budget for a glacier","setup":"A glacier gains approximately 1,200 tonnes of sediment a year from rockfall and valley-side weathering, while its meltwater streams carry approximately 950 tonnes of sediment a year away beyond the snout. Work out the glacier's net sediment budget and say what it means for the system.","steps":["Subtract to find the balance: total inputs 1,200 tonnes minus total outputs 950 tonnes leaves a net sediment budget of +250 tonnes per year.","The sign is positive, so debris enters the glacier faster than meltwater can flush it out: the difference has to stay somewhere in the system.","Where it stays: the surplus is held as supraglacial, englacial and subglacial debris and is dumped at the ice margins, building lateral, terminal and recessional moraine.","Answer: the net sediment budget is +250 tonnes per year — the glacier is accumulating its sediment store rather than exporting it, so moraine and till deposits grow year on year for as long as the surplus lasts."],"cues":["How is a net budget worked out from the two flows?","What does the sign of that figure tell you?","Where does the surplus debris actually end up?","So what is happening to the glacier's store?"]},{"title":"Distinguishing basal sliding from internal deformation","setup":"A glacier can move by internal deformation, by basal sliding, or by both at once. Explain the difference between the two mechanisms, and how the mix of them differs between cold-based and warm-based glaciers.","steps":["Internal deformation: under the weight of the ice above, individual ice crystals creep and shear past one another, so the ice mass changes shape from within — this happens in every glacier, warm-based or cold-based.","Basal sliding: the whole ice body slips over the rock beneath it, which is only possible where the basal ice reaches the pressure-melting point and a film of meltwater lubricates the contact with the bedrock.","A cold-based glacier's base stays below the pressure-melting point and is frozen on to the bedrock, so basal sliding is ruled out: it creeps forward by internal deformation alone, often only a few metres a year, and erodes very little.","A warm-based glacier has both mechanisms running together, so it moves far faster and does much more work on the landscape: sliding drags debris-laden basal ice across the bedrock to abrade and striate it, while meltwater refreezing into joints allows plucking."],"cues":["What makes ice crystals creep inside the glacier?","What must be present at the bed for slipping?","If the base is frozen on, what movement is left?","Why does a warm-based glacier erode so much more?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between accumulation and ablation?","a":"Accumulation is mass gained by the glacier (mainly snowfall); ablation is mass lost (melting, calving, sublimation)."},{"q":"Why does a warm-based glacier erode more than a cold-based one?","a":"Its meltwater-lubricated base allows basal sliding, dragging debris across bedrock, whereas a cold-based glacier is frozen to its bed and cannot slide."},{"q":"What separates the accumulation zone from the ablation zone?","a":"The equilibrium line altitude (ELA), where net mass balance equals zero."},{"q":"How is a pingo different from a corrie?","a":"A pingo is an ice-cored mound formed by freezing groundwater in a periglacial landscape with no glacier present; a corrie is an erosional hollow carved directly by glacier ice."}]},"room":{"facts":["Net mass balance = total accumulation − total ablation, measured in metres water equivalent (m w.e.); a positive balance means the glacier is gaining mass overall.","The equilibrium line altitude (ELA) is the elevation on a glacier where accumulation equals ablation (net balance = 0); above it lies the accumulation zone, below it the ablation zone.","Warm-based (temperate) glaciers move by basal sliding and internal deformation; cold-based (polar) glaciers move almost entirely by internal deformation because their base is frozen to the bedrock.","To convert a measured rate of ice movement to an annual rate: (distance ÷ number of days) × 365.","A sediment budget = inputs (e.g. rockfall, weathering) − outputs (e.g. fluvioglacial transport); a positive value means net storage/deposition, a negative value means net erosion/export.","At least one of the two case studies of a valley-glacier landscape and an ice-sheet landscape required by the specification must be located beyond the UK."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify two outputs from a glaciated landscape system.","a":"Any two of: meltwater/runoff, calving of icebergs, sublimation or evaporation of ice/snow.","sol":"Two valid outputs are meltwater runoff, which leaves the glacier as streams and rivers, and calving, where blocks of ice break away at a glacier's margin (often where it meets the sea or a lake) and leave the system as icebergs. Sublimation, ice turning directly to water vapour, is also acceptable.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about where mass actually leaves the glacier, rather than where it enters.","Run through the system in order — inputs, processes, then outputs — and stop at the last stage.","Give a short list of two named features; no explanation is needed for 3 marks."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Define the term 'basal sliding'.","a":"The whole glacier moving by sliding over its bed, made possible by meltwater lubricating the base.","sol":"Basal sliding is the process by which an entire glacier moves as a body over the bedrock beneath it. It occurs where the base of the glacier is at the pressure-melting point, so a thin film of meltwater lubricates the contact between ice and bedrock, letting the ice slide.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what happens at the very base of the ice, not within the ice itself.","Recall what condition at the bed has to be true before this kind of movement is possible.","Write one sentence: state what moves and how, not why it matters."],"codes":["1.c"]},{"q":"Identify two landforms produced by glacial deposition.","a":"Any two of: moraine (terminal/lateral/recessional), erratic, drumlin, till sheet.","sol":"Two valid depositional landforms are a moraine — a ridge of till dumped at a former ice margin — and a drumlin, a streamlined hill of till moulded beneath moving ice. Erratics and till sheets are also acceptable.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what is left behind once ice can no longer carry its load, not what the ice has carved away.","Separate this list mentally from the erosional landforms studied alongside it.","A short list of two named landforms is enough for 3 marks — no description required."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"State what is meant by the 'equilibrium line altitude' (ELA) of a glacier.","a":"The elevation on a glacier at which net mass balance is zero — accumulation equals ablation.","sol":"The equilibrium line altitude is the elevation on a glacier's surface where, over a year, the amount of accumulation exactly balances the amount of ablation, so the net mass balance at that point is zero. Above it lies the accumulation zone; below it lies the ablation zone.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the boundary between the two zones of a glacier's mass balance.","Recall what balances exactly at this particular elevation.","State the definition in one sentence, naming the two processes either side of that boundary."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify one landform typically found in a periglacial landscape.","a":"Patterned ground or a pingo (or an ice wedge).","sol":"A pingo is a valid answer: an ice-cored, dome-shaped mound formed where groundwater freezes and expands beneath the surface in a periglacial environment. Patterned ground (sorted circles, polygons or stripes produced by frost heave) is equally acceptable.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think of a landform formed by seasonal or permanent ground freezing, not by moving ice.","Recall the named periglacial landforms in the specification content.","Name one landform only — no description is required for 3 marks."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how nivation and glacial erosion combine to form a corrie.","a":"Nivation slowly enlarges a snow hollow through freeze-thaw and meltwater erosion; once a glacier forms in it, plucking steepens the back wall, abrasion deepens the floor, and rotational sliding over-deepens the basin.","sol":"A corrie begins as a hollow on a shaded, sheltered slope where a patch of snow survives through summer. Nivation — repeated freeze-thaw weathering at the edges of the snow patch plus meltwater erosion beneath it — slowly enlarges this hollow. Once enough snow has compacted into glacier ice, the ice rotates as it moves, a process called rotational sliding, which erodes the floor of the hollow more than its lip. Plucking, as meltwater refreezes onto jointed rock at the back wall and tears blocks away, steepens that wall into a near-vertical cliff, while abrasion by debris embedded in the ice deepens and smooths the basin floor. Together these processes over-deepen the basin below the level of its rock lip, so that when the ice eventually melts, meltwater and rainwater collect there as a tarn.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate processes are being asked for here — one that starts the hollow, one that deepens it once ice is present.","Work through the sequence in order: what enlarges the hollow first, then what the moving ice itself does to it.","Structure your answer in two developed parts, each naming and explaining one process, finishing with what shape is left behind."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how relief and aspect influence the microclimate of a potential glacier basin.","a":"Steep, sheltered, shaded (north/east-facing in the Northern Hemisphere) hollows receive less solar radiation and are sheltered from prevailing winds, so temperatures stay lower and snow accumulates rather than melting.","sol":"Aspect controls how much direct solar radiation a hollow receives: in the Northern Hemisphere, a north- or east-facing slope is turned away from the sun for more of the day than a south- or west-facing one, so it stays colder and any snow that falls there melts more slowly. The same aspects are also usually in the lee of the prevailing south-westerly wind, so rather than being scoured bare they collect wind-blown snow blown in from more exposed slopes. Relief adds to this: a steep-sided hollow shades its own floor for more of the day and can trap cold, dense air. Together, lower solar radiation, wind-deposited snow and self-shading keep the microclimate of the basin colder and snowier than the surrounding landscape, allowing snow to survive through summer and begin to accumulate into glacier ice.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Consider two separate influences on the local climate of a hollow — the direction it faces, and its shape.","For each, think about what it does to incoming sunlight or to the wind carrying snow.","Explain both influences in turn and end by linking them to why snow can persist there."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Explain the formation of an esker.","a":"Meltwater flowing in a tunnel under or within stagnant/retreating ice deposits sorted sand and gravel; when the ice melts, this infill is left as a long, sinuous ridge on the landscape.","sol":"As a glacier stagnates or retreats, meltwater increasingly flows in channels and tunnels beneath or within the ice rather than over its surface. These fast-flowing, confined streams carry a heavy sediment load and, because the water is channelised rather than spreading out, it deposits sorted, stratified sand and gravel along the floor of the tunnel. When the surrounding ice eventually melts away completely, this channel-fill of sorted sediment is left standing proud of the surrounding land as a long, sinuous ridge — an esker — whose winding shape traces the former course of the meltwater tunnel.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about where meltwater flows once a glacier has stopped advancing — not over the surface.","Consider what a confined, fast-flowing channel does to the sediment it carries, compared with water spreading freely.","Describe the deposit first, then what happens to the surrounding ice, in that order."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"A glacier records winter accumulation of 2.8 m water equivalent (w.e.) and summer ablation of 3.4 m w.e. Calculate the glacier's net mass balance for the year and state what this means for the glacier.","a":"Net balance = 2.8 − 3.4 = −0.6 m w.e.; a negative balance means the glacier lost more mass than it gained, so it is thinning/retreating.","sol":"Net mass balance = accumulation − ablation = 2.8 − 3.4 = −0.6 m w.e. Because the result is negative, the glacier lost more mass over the year than it gained: its overall volume and thickness decreased. A sustained run of years like this would be expected to cause the glacier's terminus to retreat up-valley and its equilibrium line altitude to rise.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Remember that ablation reduces the glacier's mass, so it is subtracted from accumulation.","Combine the two figures to find a single net value.","Show the calculation as one line of working, then state in a sentence what a negative result means for the glacier."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Using a named example, explain how human activity has changed processes within a periglacial landscape.","a":"For example, Alaska's North Slope: infrastructure supporting oil extraction adds heat and removes insulating vegetation, deepening the active layer and thawing permafrost.","sol":"On Alaska's North Slope, oil extraction at Prudhoe Bay and the buildings, roads and the Trans-Alaska Pipeline that support it have changed the periglacial system. Heat from buildings, vehicles and pipelines, and the loss of insulating surface vegetation where it has been cleared or compacted, both raise the temperature of the ground. This deepens the active layer that thaws each summer and, in places, causes permafrost to thaw for the first time in thousands of years. Because much of the ground ice that thaws was holding sediment together, the surface subsides unevenly as the ice melts, producing thermokarst — hummocky ground pitted with collapsed hollows and thaw ponds — which can in turn undermine the very infrastructure that caused the warming.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what raises ground temperature in a landscape used for resource extraction.","Trace the change through in order: what the activity adds to the ground, what happens to the ice within it, then what landform results.","Name your example landscape early, then follow activity → impact → landform change in developed sentences."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"'Glacial erosion is the most important process in producing the landscape of a valley-glaciated area.' To what extent do you agree? Refer to a named case study in your answer.","a":"Weigh erosion (corries, arêtes, troughs) against weathering/mass movement (supplying debris) and deposition (moraines, drumlins) in the same case study, reaching a judgement on how far erosion dominates.","sol":"In the Lake District, glacial erosion has undeniably produced the landscape's most distinctive high-level features. Rotational sliding, plucking and abrasion carved corries such as the hollow now holding Red Tarn on Helvellyn, and where two corries formed back to back they left the narrow arête of Striding Edge. Lower down, valley glaciers eroded former river valleys into deep U-shaped troughs such as Great Langdale and Borrowdale, straightening their course, truncating interlocking spurs and leaving hanging tributary valleys and ribbon lakes such as Wast Water. This is powerful evidence that erosion dominates the upper and mid-valley landscape. However, erosion did not act alone: freeze-thaw weathering and rockfall from valley-side crags continuously supplied the debris that erosion then transported, and without this debris supply abrasion could not have scoured bedrock as effectively. Further down-valley, it is deposition rather than erosion that dominates the landscape: terminal and lateral moraines mark former ice limits around the lake basins, and drumlin swarms and till sheets on the lowland fringes of the Lake District were built entirely by deposition, not erosion. To a large extent, then, glacial erosion is the most important process where relief is steepest and ice was thickest, producing the landscape's sharpest and most recognisable landforms; but it depended on weathering to supply material, and lower in the same landscape system, deposition — not erosion — has left the more extensive landforms. Erosion is the dominant process in this case study, but not to the exclusion of the others.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide early which parts of the landscape you will treat as most eroded, and which as shaped by other processes.","Build each paragraph around one process, its named landform evidence from your case study, and how it connects to the other processes around it.","Close with a conclusion that states directly how far you agree, rather than simply summarising each paragraph again."],"codes":["2.a","2.b"]},{"q":"Assess the extent to which human activity has changed the glacial landscape system in a named glaciated landscape used by people.","a":"Trace the named activity (e.g. dam construction) through its impact on sediment/flow, the resulting landform change, and the consequence for the valley floor, then judge how widespread versus localised the change is.","sol":"At Kárahnjúkar in the eastern highlands of Iceland, a dam was built across the Jökulsá á Dal, a river fed by meltwater from Brúarjökull, an outlet glacier of the Vatnajökull ice cap, to generate hydroelectric power for an aluminium smelter. This human activity has changed the glacial system significantly at a local scale. The dam holds back the river's glacial sediment load — the fine 'rock flour' and coarser debris the meltwater would otherwise carry downstream — inside the reservoir, Hálslón. With its sediment supply cut off, the river below the dam is left able to erode its own channel more aggressively, increasing scour immediately downstream, while upstream the trapped sediment steadily raises the reservoir bed and buries the valley floor beneath it. The regulated release of water for power generation also smooths out the extreme seasonal meltwater floods that would naturally characterise this glacial river, changing the shape and vegetation of the valley floor over time. The scheme also diverts much of the regulated water through tunnels into the neighbouring Jökulsdálur catchment, releasing it into the Lagarfljót rather than returning it to the Jökulsá á Dal, an inter-basin transfer that leaves the river below the dam even further starved of both water and sediment than the trapped load alone would suggest. To a considerable extent, then, this single piece of infrastructure has reorganised the sediment and water budget of the whole glacial system downstream of it, converting part of a natural glacial trough into an artificial lake and altering channel processes for tens of kilometres. However, the change is concentrated around the dam and reservoir; the glacier itself, and the erosional landforms of the upper catchment, continue to be shaped mainly by natural glacial processes largely unaffected by the dam. Human activity has therefore changed this glaciated landscape substantially, but locally rather than throughout the whole system.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Pick one named case study you can follow from the human activity itself through to a change in the landscape.","Move through the chain in order — activity, impact on sediment or flow, landform change, then landscape consequence — before you reach a judgement.","Finish by weighing how widespread the change is against how localised it is, rather than only describing the impacts."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"'The formation of glacial and periglacial landforms is controlled more by climate than by geology.' How far do you agree?","a":"Weigh climate (controls whether ice/permafrost forms at all, and where the ELA/permafrost limit sits) against geology (controls landform detail and rate via lithology and structure), reaching a judgement.","sol":"Climate is the primary control on whether glacial or periglacial processes operate at all: precipitation totals and temperature decide whether snow can accumulate into a glacier, where the equilibrium line altitude sits, and whether the ground beyond the ice is cold enough to sustain permafrost and periglacial processes such as frost heave. Warming climate at the end of a glacial period switches processes from purely glacial to glacio-fluvial, producing kames, eskers and outwash plains, while a cooling or already-cold climate before or after glaciation produces periglacial patterned ground and pingos instead. Geology, however, still governs the form landforms take once climate has permitted a process to occur: resistant, well-jointed rock such as granite holds up sharp arêtes and pyramidal peaks and is exploited by plucking along its joints, while weaker or unjointed rock erodes into gentler, more rounded shapes; permeable geology also affects how easily groundwater can freeze to form a pingo. On balance, climate is the dominant control because it determines which system — glacial, glacio-fluvial or periglacial — operates in the first place, but geology is a powerful secondary control that decides the precise landforms and their rate of formation within whichever system climate has set up. I agree with the statement to a large extent, but not entirely.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Treat this as two separate controls to weigh against each other, not one list of factors.","For each, show what it controls that the other cannot — the type of system versus the detail of the landform.","End with a direct judgement stating which control you rate as more important, and why the other still matters."],"codes":["1.b","2.a","3.a","3.b"]},{"q":"A stake on a valley glacier's surface moves 60 cm over 4 days. A second marker at 30 m depth in the same glacier moves 20 cm over the same 4 days. Calculate the annual velocity at each depth and use the difference to explain what this suggests about how the glacier is moving.","a":"Surface ≈ 54.75 m per year; at 30 m depth ≈ 18.25 m per year; the surface moves roughly three times faster, consistent with internal deformation increasing towards the surface.","sol":"Surface rate: 60 cm ÷ 4 days = 15 cm per day; × 365 days = 5,475 cm ≈ 54.75 m per year. Rate at 30 m depth: 20 cm ÷ 4 days = 5 cm per day; × 365 days = 1,825 cm ≈ 18.25 m per year. The surface is moving roughly three times faster than the ice at 30 m depth. This pattern — velocity increasing from depth towards the surface — is consistent with internal deformation, where each layer of ice shears a little over the layer beneath it, so the cumulative effect is greatest at the top. A marker 30 m below the surface sits above the bed rather than at it, so its own movement is fully explained by internal deformation of the ice beneath it; this depth profile is evidence of internal deformation only, and would only point to basal sliding as well if the marker were near the bed.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Convert each measurement to a daily rate before scaling it up to a year.","Once you have both annual rates, compare their sizes rather than just stating them separately.","Use the size of the difference between the two rates to say something about how movement is distributed with depth in the ice."],"codes":["1.c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is glacier mass balance?","a":"The balance between accumulation and ablation over a year."},{"q":"Name one erosional glacial landform.","a":"Any one of: corrie, arête, pyramidal peak, glacial trough, roche moutonnée (accept striations)."},{"q":"What is a pingo?","a":"An ice-cored mound in a periglacial landscape, formed by freezing groundwater."},{"q":"Give one human activity that changes a periglacial landscape.","a":"Resource extraction, e.g. oil or gas extraction and its supporting infrastructure."}]},"cards":[{"front":"What is glacier mass balance?","back":"The difference between accumulation (gain) and ablation (loss) of ice/snow over a year, usually measured in metres water equivalent.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Equilibrium line altitude (ELA)","back":"The elevation on a glacier where net mass balance = 0; separates the accumulation zone above from the ablation zone below.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"How does aspect affect glacier formation?","back":"North/east-facing, sheltered slopes (Northern Hemisphere) receive less insolation, so snow survives longer and accumulates, favouring glacier growth.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Warm-based vs cold-based glacier","back":"Warm-based: base at pressure melting point, meltwater present, moves by sliding and deformation, erodes more. Cold-based: frozen to bed, moves by internal deformation only.","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Timescales of change in glaciated landscapes","back":"Change happens at very different speeds: rockfall can alter a slope in seconds, seasonal meltwater changes deposition rates within a year, while erosion of a corrie basin takes millennia.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"How does a corrie form?","back":"Nivation enlarges a hollow; freeze-thaw and plucking steepen the back wall; abrasion deepens the floor; rotational sliding over-deepens the basin, later holding a tarn.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Roche moutonnée","back":"A bedrock hump abraded smooth on the up-glacier (stoss) side and plucked into a jagged face on the down-glacier (lee) side.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Drumlin","back":"A streamlined depositional hillock of till, blunt/steep end facing up-glacier, tapering tail pointing down-glacier, formed subglacially.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Esker","back":"A long, sinuous ridge of sorted sand and gravel deposited by meltwater flowing in a tunnel under or within the ice.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Outwash plain (sandur)","back":"A flat area of sorted, stratified sediment deposited by braided meltwater streams beyond the ice margin.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Pingo","back":"An ice-cored, dome-shaped mound in a periglacial landscape, formed as groundwater freezes and expands beneath the surface.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Patterned ground","back":"Circles, polygons or stripes of sorted stones and fine sediment formed by frost heave repeatedly sorting material in periglacial conditions.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Thermokarst","back":"Irregular, hummocky ground with collapsed hollows and ponds formed when permafrost thaws and the ground surface subsides.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Why dam a glaciated valley?","back":"Steep-sided troughs and reliable glacial meltwater discharge make good sites for hydroelectric power reservoirs, e.g. Kárahnjúkar, Iceland.","code":"4.b"},{"front":"Effect of a dam on a glacial river","back":"The dam traps sediment in the reservoir, starving the river below of sediment, which can increase channel scour and change the valley floor.","code":"4.b"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: 'Glacial erosion is the most important process in producing the landscape of a valley-glaciated area.' To what extent do you agree? Refer to a named case study in your answer. (16 marks)","Glacial erosion has produced the sharpest, most recognisable landforms in the Lake District's valley-glaciated landscape. Rotational sliding, plucking and abrasion carved deep corries such as the hollow beneath Helvellyn that now holds Red Tarn, and where two corries formed back to back on the same ridge they left the narrow arête of Striding Edge between them. Lower in the same valleys, ice eroded pre-existing river valleys into deep U-shaped troughs such as Great Langdale, straightening their course, truncating the interlocking spurs of the original river valley and leaving tributary valleys hanging high above the trough floor. Because these landforms — corries, arêtes and troughs — are the features by which the Lake District's glaciated landscape is most immediately recognised, and because they required the sustained, concentrated erosive power of moving ice rather than the more gradual, diffuse action of weathering or deposition, glacial erosion should be judged the single most important process in producing this landscape, even though it did not act entirely alone."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: 'Glacial erosion is the most important process in producing the landscape of a valley-glaciated area.' To what extent do you agree? Refer to a named case study in your answer. (16 marks)","Glacial erosion made lots of the landforms in the Lake District. Ice carved out corries like Red Tarn and made arêtes like Striding Edge. It also made U-shaped valleys such as Great Langdale, which are deeper and straighter than normal river valleys. Erosion is important because it made these landforms. There are also moraines from when the ice dropped rocks and mud. So glacial erosion is the main process that made the Lake District's landscape look like it does."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Lake District (valley glaciers), UK","items":["Location: the Lake District, Cumbria, north-west England; entirely within the UK.","Corrie: Red Tarn, on the eastern side of Helvellyn, occupies a rotational-sliding basin now holding a small lake (tarn).","Arête: Striding Edge, a narrow, steep-sided ridge on Helvellyn formed between two adjacent corries.","Glacial trough: Great Langdale, a classic U-shaped valley with truncated spurs and hanging valleys, such as the Stickle Ghyll and Dungeon Ghyll tributaries that enter above the level of the main trough floor.","Ribbon lake: Wast Water, England's deepest lake at approximately 79 m, occupies an over-deepened section of a glacial trough.","Deposition: terminal and lateral moraines around Lake District valley mouths, and drumlin swarms on the lowland fringe near Kendal, mark the retreat of the last ice, which had largely disappeared from the area by approximately 11,700 years ago (the end of the last glacial period)."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Canadian Shield (ice sheet), Canada","items":["Location: a vast area of exposed Precambrian bedrock covering approximately 8 million km² across central and eastern Canada; beyond the UK.","Ice sheet: shaped by the Laurentide Ice Sheet, which covered most of Canada during the last glacial period and had largely disappeared by approximately 8,000 years ago.","Erosion: extensive roche moutonnées and striations on exposed bedrock record the direction of past ice flow across the Shield.","Deposition: large drumlin fields, for example in south-central Ontario, formed as till was moulded beneath the moving ice sheet.","Landscape legacy: the Shield's thin, patchy soils and countless lake-filled basins scoured into the bedrock are a direct result of ice-sheet erosion rather than valley-glacier erosion."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — Alaska's North Slope (periglacial landscape used by people), USA","items":["Location: the North Slope of Alaska, USA, between the Brooks Range and the Arctic Ocean; underlain by continuous permafrost.","Activity: oil extraction at the Prudhoe Bay oil field, discovered in 1968, with production beginning in 1977.","Infrastructure: the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System runs approximately 1,300 km (800 miles) from Prudhoe Bay to the port of Valdez, built on elevated supports with heat pipes in many sections specifically to stop pipeline heat thawing the permafrost beneath it.","Impact: heat from buildings, roads and vehicles, and the loss of insulating tundra vegetation, deepen the active layer and can thaw permafrost that had been frozen for thousands of years.","Consequence: thawing produces thermokarst — irregular, hummocky ground pitted with collapsed hollows and thaw ponds — which can damage the infrastructure that caused the warming."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — Kárahnjúkar (glaciated landscape used by people), Iceland","items":["Location: the eastern highlands of Iceland, on the Jökulsá á Dal, a river fed by meltwater from Brúarjökull, an outlet glacier of the Vatnajökull ice cap; beyond the UK.","Activity: the Kárahnjúkar hydropower scheme, completed in 2007, dams the river to generate electricity for the Fjarðaál aluminium smelter at Reyðarfjörður.","Structure: the main dam is approximately 190 m high, among the largest dams in Europe, and creates the Hálslón reservoir.","Impact: the dam traps the river's glacial sediment load in the reservoir, reducing the sediment reaching the channel downstream and altering the river's natural seasonal flood pattern.","Consequence: reduced sediment supply can increase channel scour immediately below the dam, while the flooded reservoir has submerged part of the original glacial valley floor."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — To what extent is glacial erosion the most important process in producing the landscape of a valley-glaciated area? (16 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'most important process' means you must compare erosion against the other processes in the system — weathering, mass movement and deposition — not describe erosion alone; 'to what extent' requires a final judgement on how far you agree, not just a list of landforms.","Paragraph 1 (erosion's strongest evidence): explain how plucking, abrasion and rotational sliding produced the case study's corries, arêtes and troughs, naming specific features and locations as evidence.","Paragraph 2 (what erosion depends on): explain how freeze-thaw weathering and mass movement supply the rock debris that abrasion needs, so erosion could not have shaped the landscape without this input.","Paragraph 3 (the process erosion is competing against): explain how deposition — moraines, drumlins, till sheets — has shaped the lower or wider parts of the same case-study landscape just as extensively as erosion shaped the upper parts.","Evaluation thread: in each paragraph, weigh how much of the landscape that process explains against how much the others explain, rather than presenting the three processes as a simple list.","Conclusion must say: state directly how far you agree with the statement, naming which part of the landscape erosion dominates and which part another process dominates, so the judgement is about extent, not a yes/no answer."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.937Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Content, cards, worked examples and all four required fact files are accurate and spec-faithful (valley-glacier Lake District from the UK plus ice-sheet Canadian Shield, Alaska North Slope and Kárahnjúkar beyond it, so both the valley-glacier/ice-sheet pair and the two-beyond-the-UK rule are met), every real-world figure I checked holds up (Wast Water c.79 m, TAPS c.1,300 km / 800 miles from Prudhoe Bay to Valdez, Prudhoe Bay discovered 1968 and on stream 1977, Kárahnjúkar dam c.190 m and commissioned 2007, Canadian Shield c.8 million km²), all 14 answer keys re-solve correctly and the two model paragraphs are convincingly Level 4 and Level 2 and say so; fix the Mickleden example, the basal-sliding inference in question 13 and the mass-balance sign convention and this is publishable, and note in passing that questions 3's third hint ('referring to accumulation and ablation') hands over the two terms the mark scheme wants and that the Kárahnjúkar model answer omits the inter-basin transfer of the Jökulsá á Dal into the Lagarfljót, which is why downstream scour there is more contested than the spec's generic example implies."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:53:11.231Z"}},"1.1.3":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"1.1.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because Topic 1.1.3 is OCR's Dryland Landscapes option within Physical systems (01): examiners require a systems-based account of how energy and material move through dryland landscapes, how climate, geology, latitude, relief and sediment supply shape distinctive erosional and depositional landforms, how relict pluvial and periglacial features record past climate change, and how named case studies of water supply and economic activity show people altering these systems today. The 16-mark extended response and 6-mark explain questions on Paper 01 reward exactly this: named evidence, developed process chains, and a weighed judgement, not a list of landform names.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Dryland landscapes as systems","t":"Treat a dryland landscape as an open system: inputs of solar energy, precipitation and wind energy; stores such as groundwater, sediment and surface water; processes of weathering, erosion, transport and deposition that move energy and material between stores; and outputs such as evaporation, river discharge leaving the system, and sediment exported beyond the landscape's boundary. Dryness itself is measured by the aridity index, AI = precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration (P/PET), the ratio UNEP uses to classify hyper-arid, arid, semi-arid and dry sub-humid land. A top-level answer never just lists inputs, processes and outputs: it traces one flow all the way through the system, and uses the aridity index as quantified evidence of how dry the named landscape actually is, rather than asserting it is 'very dry'."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Physical factors that shape dryland systems","t":"Five physical factors control how a dryland system behaves: climate (the amount and seasonal pattern of precipitation, not just the annual total); geology (lithology controls resistance to weathering and permeability, structure controls where faults, joints and bedding planes concentrate weathering and channel water); latitude and altitude (latitude fixes a location beneath the descending, stable air of the Hadley cell or in a continental interior, altitude adds diurnal temperature range and orographic effects); relief and aspect (slope angle and direction control microclimate, insolation and where sediment and moisture collect); and sediment availability (without a supply of loose material, wind and water have nothing to erode, transport or deposit). A top-level answer names which factor is dominant for the specific landform or landscape in the question, rather than describing all five with equal weight."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Types of dryland landscape","t":"The specification distinguishes several dryland types by their thermal regime as well as their aridity: polar drylands (very low temperatures, precipitation often locked up as snow and ice so it cannot generate runoff, weathering dominated by frost and salt action, e.g. the McMurdo Dry Valleys of Antarctica); low-latitude deserts (hot year-round, with a low aridity index driven by the descending, stable air of the Hadley cell around the tropics, e.g. the Sonoran Desert of the south-western USA); mid-latitude deserts (dry for a different reason — a continental interior far from moisture-bearing winds and/or the rain shadow of a high mountain range, rather than a permanent high-pressure belt — so they combine hot summers with a large annual temperature range and cold, often sub-zero, winters, e.g. the Gobi Desert of Mongolia and China); and semi-arid environments (a transitional aridity index of 0.20-0.50, enough moisture to support sparse grassland or scrub vegetation that itself buffers erosion). A top-level answer uses this typology to explain why the same process, such as weathering, produces different dominant landforms in a polar dryland compared with a hot desert, and never assumes that all mid- and low-latitude deserts share the same thermal regime."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Processes that build dryland landforms","t":"Geomorphic processes in drylands are driven by the flows of energy (intense insolation, strong, often unidirectional wind) and material (limited, intermittent water, abundant loose sediment) described under 1.a: weathering (insolation weathering, salt crystallisation, occasional frost action) breaks rock down in situ; mass movement moves the debris downslope; fluvial erosion and transport occur in short, powerful bursts during rare rainfall; and aeolian (wind) erosion, transport (suspension, saltation, creep) and deposition operate continuously. Erosional landforms - wadis, canyons, pedestal rocks, ventifacts and desert pavements - record where erosion dominates; depositional landforms - barchans, linear dunes, star dunes, alluvial fans and bajadas - record where material accumulates. A top-level answer names the process responsible for each landform's diagnostic shape, for example that a barchan's crescent horns point downwind because sand at the dune's edges is starved of supply and moves faster than the dune's crest."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Landforms as an inter-related landscape system","t":"A real desert landscape is not a checklist of separate landforms but a linked system: a mid-latitude desert such as the Gobi and a low-latitude desert such as the Sonoran both connect mountain-front relief, alluvial fans and bajadas, ephemeral wadis crossing the basin floor, and playas at the lowest point where fine sediment and salts finally settle out. Change in this system operates on very different timescales at once: a single flash flood can build or breach an alluvial fan in seconds, a seasonal or ephemeral stream reshapes a canyon over one wet season, and pediment development by parallel slope retreat takes millennia. A top-level answer uses one named case study to show how landforms upstream (fans, wadis) supply the material that builds landforms downstream (playas), and states which of these timescales the question is actually asking about."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Fluvial landforms from earlier pluvial periods","t":"Many landforms in today's hot deserts were not formed by today's climate. During pluvial periods (wetter phases linked to past glacial-interglacial cycles, when the descending dry air belts shifted position), far more fluvial and chemical weathering activity occurred than the present hyper-arid or arid climate could produce. This built inselbergs through two-stage weathering: first, deep chemical weathering under the wetter pluvial climate rotted the rock in situ far below the surface; second, subsequent stripping by erosion removed this weathered material, exposing the more resistant, less-altered rock beneath as an isolated residual hill. Pediments (gently sloping bedrock surfaces cut by sheetwash and parallel slope retreat) formed alongside them. Since the climate dried, present-day processes - insolation weathering, rare flash floods, wind - only modify these relict forms rather than creating new ones from scratch. A top-level answer states explicitly that the landform's origin and its present-day modification belong to two different climatic periods."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Periglacial landforms from earlier colder periods","t":"Other dryland landforms are relicts of colder, not wetter, past conditions. During and immediately after past glacial periods, some drylands (including parts of today's mid-latitude deserts) experienced periglacial conditions, and so did some mid-latitude locations that are not classed as dryland today, such as Breckland in East Anglia — used here as the specification's required UK example not for any present-day aridity (it is in fact the UK's driest area, but its climate is humid, not semi-arid) but for the relict periglacial features that survive there: freeze-thaw action produced frost-shattered debris, nivation hollows formed where snow patches lingered and intensified local weathering and erosion, and solifluction moved saturated regolith slowly downslope over permafrost to leave solifluction deposits (lobes and sheets of debris). As the climate warmed, present-day aeolian and occasional fluvial processes rework these deposits without producing new frost features. A top-level answer names the specific relict feature and states what present-day process is now modifying it, rather than describing periglacial processes in the abstract."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Water supply issues changing dryland systems","t":"Where people manage dryland landscapes for water supply, the required case study must show a full chain: the issue itself (for example water shortage from persistent drought and rising demand, driving large dam construction) leads to an impact on the system's flows (for example sediment that would once have travelled downstream is instead trapped behind a dam, and river discharge is regulated rather than seasonal); this changes the dryland landforms fed by that flow (for example decreased growth, or even shrinkage, of downstream channels and wadis, starved of both water and sediment); which in turn changes the wider landscape (for example depositional landforms such as deltas and alluvial fans stop growing or actively shrink, and pediment development slows as less water reaches the slope). A top-level answer follows this cause-and-effect chain through to the landscape-scale consequence, using named figures."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Economic activity changing dryland systems","t":"Where people use a dryland landscape economically, the same chain applies to a different driver: the activity itself (for example tourism, drawn by a landscape's scenery or recreational potential, such as off-road dune buggy use) leads to an impact on the system's material and energy flows (for example vegetation and cryptobiotic (biological) soil crusts are physically damaged, removing the surface binding that normally slows wind erosion); this changes dryland landforms (for example dunes erode and migrate faster once their stabilising cover is stripped); which changes the wider landscape (for example greater loess, or wind-blown fine sediment, accumulates in areas downwind of the damaged source). A top-level answer names the specific mechanism of damage, not just 'tourism causes erosion'."}],"examples":[{"title":"Worked aridity index — is a site arid or semi-arid?","setup":"A weather station in a low-latitude desert records mean annual precipitation of approximately 300 mm and mean annual potential evapotranspiration of approximately 1,900 mm. Use the UNEP aridity index to classify the site.","steps":["Apply the UNEP formula AI = P ÷ PET: 300 mm ÷ 1,900 mm ≈ 0.16, a ratio with no units because both readings are depths of water.","Set UNEP's bands out in order: hyper-arid below 0.05; arid 0.05-0.20; semi-arid 0.20-0.50; dry sub-humid 0.50-0.65.","Place the station's value on that scale: 0.16 is above the hyper-arid threshold of 0.05, but has not reached the 0.20 mark where semi-arid begins.","Classification: the site is arid. Rainfall supplies only about a sixth of the water the atmosphere there could evaporate, which is why its streams are ephemeral and its vegetation cover sparse and drought-adapted."],"cues":["Which two readings go into the index, and how?","What must the value be compared against?","Where does 0.16 sit on that scale?","So how is the site classified?"]},{"title":"Worked unit conversion — a dune's rate of advance","setup":"A barchan dune field is reported to be migrating downwind at approximately 18 m per year by saltation and surface creep. Convert that rate into centimetres per day, and say what the result means for a road or an oasis lying in the dunes' path.","steps":["Daily rate in metres: 18 m per year ÷ 365 days = 0.0493 m per day.","Daily rate in centimetres: 0.0493 m × 100 = 4.93 cm per day.","Put the figure in context: 4.93 cm a day is far too little to see between one visit and the next, yet the same 18 m a year carries the dune roughly 180 m in a decade.","Answer: the dune advances at approximately 4.9 cm per day — imperceptible day to day, but enough to bury a road or swallow the palm groves of an oasis within a few decades, which is why such routes are protected by fences, tree belts or regular clearance."],"cues":["How many days do you divide the year into?","How do you turn metres into centimetres?","What does that rate add up to over a decade?","So what does it mean for a road or oasis?"]},{"title":"Worked sediment budget — an alluvial fan after a flash flood","setup":"A single flash flood deposits approximately 5,000 tonnes of sediment onto an alluvial fan at the mouth of a wadi. Over the following dry season, wind deflation and minor reworking strip approximately 3,200 tonnes of finer material from the fan surface. Work out the net sediment budget and say what it means for the fan.","steps":["Take the dry-season losses off the flood gains: 5,000 tonnes deposited − 3,200 tonnes deflated = +1,800 tonnes.","The balance is positive, so the fan holds 1,800 tonnes more sediment at the end of the cycle than at the start, even though the wind works on it continuously between floods.","Notice what leaves and what stays: deflation can lift only silt and fine sand, so the gravel and cobbles dumped by the flood remain in place — the fan coarsens at the surface as well as growing.","Answer: a net sediment budget of +1,800 tonnes — the fan is still building outwards from the wadi mouth, gaining in sudden flood pulses and being trimmed, not reversed, by wind between them."],"cues":["How do the two figures combine into a budget?","What does the sign of that figure mean for the fan?","Which grain sizes can the wind take, and which stay?","So what is happening to the fan overall?"]},{"title":"Building an explain answer — desert pavement formation","setup":"An explain answer must trace how a stony desert pavement (reg) forms at the desert surface, from the first breakdown of material through to the armoured surface that results.","steps":["Sharp daily heating and cooling, and salts crystallising in pores as the rare rainfall evaporates, shatter the surface layer into a loose mixture of stones, sand and silt.","Wind deflation then lifts out the sand and silt-sized grains, which are light enough to be entrained by the strong, largely unobstructed desert winds.","The pebbles and cobbles are too heavy for the wind to raise, so they stay put and become steadily more concentrated at the surface as a residual lag.","As deflation continues, those lag stones settle and interlock into a continuous stony layer — the desert pavement, or reg — which then shields the finer sediment beneath it, so further deflation is slowed and the surface is stabilised."],"cues":["What breaks the surface material down first?","Which grain sizes can the wind carry off?","What is left when the fines have gone?","What does that lag layer become, and then do?"]},{"title":"Using named evidence — human impact on the Colorado River system","setup":"Hoover Dam (completed 1936) and Glen Canyon Dam (completed 1963) both impound the Colorado River, which before damming carried an estimated 125 million tonnes of sediment a year to its delta on the Sonoran Desert coast. Using this evidence, explain the dams' impact on the river's sediment and on the Colorado River Delta.","steps":["Each dam turns a fast, sediment-laden river into still water: the current dies on entering Lake Mead behind Hoover Dam and Lake Powell behind Glen Canyon Dam, so almost all of the 125 million tonnes a year settles in the reservoirs instead of travelling on.","The water released below the dams is therefore clear and sediment-starved, so it picks up material from the bed and banks — scouring the channel and eroding the sandbars of the Grand Canyon rather than rebuilding them.","At the far end of the system, the Colorado River Delta lost both its sediment supply and its flow: the river now reaches it only in exceptional releases, such as the eight-week 'Minute 319' pulse flow of 2014.","Consequence for the landscape: starved of the sediment that built it, the delta's wetlands have shrunk by an estimated 90% or more since large-scale damming began, so a depositional landform that once grew seaward is now eroding, drying and salinising."],"cues":["What happens to a river's load in still water?","What will clear, sediment-free water do downstream?","What reaches the delta now, and how often?","So what has become of the delta's wetlands?"]},{"title":"Reading a relict landform — periglacial evidence at Breckland","setup":"The sandy heaths of Breckland in East Anglia preserve polygons and stripes of sorted stones, visible from the air as crop marks, together with shallow rimmed hollows, some of them water-filled ponds. Explain what created these features, what is left of them today, and which process acts on them now.","steps":["Features like these require permafrost, which East Anglia had during the last cold stage of the Pleistocene: ground frozen year-round beneath a shallow active layer that thawed each summer.","The sorting: repeated freezing and thawing of that active layer heaved coarse stones upwards and outwards from the fines, arranging them into polygons on flat ground and drawing those polygons out into stripes where the ground sloped.","The hollows: lenses of ground ice grew within the permafrost and arched the overlying sediment up into low pingo mounds, shedding material outwards down their flanks; when the climate warmed the ice core melted out and each mound collapsed, leaving a shallow depression ringed by that displaced rim — a fossil pingo scar, which ponds water where its floor is lined with fines.","Today's climate is far too mild for permafrost, so none of this is still being made: the features are relict, and what works on them now is wind reworking the free-draining Breckland sand, along with ploughing and burrowing that blur the patterns — a landscape that preserves a past process regime rather than an active one."],"cues":["What ground conditions could produce these features?","How does freeze-thaw sort stones into patterns?","What grew, then melted, to leave a hollow?","Is any of this still forming today?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What does a low aridity index value (close to 0) actually mean about a place?","a":"That precipitation is very small compared with potential evapotranspiration, so the climate is extremely dry."},{"q":"Why do erosional and depositional dryland landforms usually occur in the same landscape system rather than separately?","a":"Because material eroded upslope (e.g. from a mountain front) has to be transported and then deposited somewhere else in the same system (e.g. on a fan or in a playa) — they are linked by the same flow of sediment."},{"q":"How can you tell whether a landform is a product of today's climate or a relict of a past one?","a":"Compare the process needed to form it (e.g. deep chemical weathering, permafrost) against today's climate: if today's climate could not produce that process, the landform is a relict, now only being modified, not created."},{"q":"What is the difference between the water supply case study and the economic activity case study in this topic?","a":"The water supply case study traces the impact of managing water (e.g. damming a river for supply) through the system; the economic activity case study traces the impact of an activity such as tourism, even though both use the same point-impact-landform-consequence chain."}]},"room":{"facts":["Aridity index (UNEP): AI = precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration (P/PET). Hyper-arid < 0.05; arid 0.05-0.20; semi-arid 0.20-0.50; dry sub-humid 0.50-0.65.","Erosional dryland landforms: wadis, canyons, pedestal rocks, ventifacts, desert pavements.","Depositional dryland landforms: barchans, linear dunes, star dunes, alluvial fans, bajadas.","Pluvial-period relict landforms: inselbergs, pediments. Periglacial relict landforms: frost-shattered debris, nivation hollows, solifluction deposits.","The five physical factors influencing dryland systems: climate, geology, latitude and altitude, relief and aspect, sediment availability.","The human-impact chain to use for 4.a/4.b: issue/activity → impact on flows of material/energy → effect on landforms → consequence for the landscape."],"questions":[{"q":"Define the aridity index and state the UNEP threshold values either side of the semi-arid category.","a":"Aridity index = precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration (P/PET); UNEP's semi-arid band runs from 0.20 up to 0.50.","sol":"The aridity index is the ratio UNEP uses to classify dryland climates: AI = precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration (P/PET). Semi-arid land is defined as land with an aridity index between 0.20 and 0.50 — drier than dry sub-humid land (0.50-0.65) but wetter than arid land (0.05-0.20).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how the specification defines dryness using a ratio of two climate variables measured over a year, not a single rainfall figure.","This ratio divides one variable by another — recall which climate measure UNEP puts on top and which it puts on the bottom.","Give the formula as one short sentence, then give the two numbers that bound the semi-arid category as a second sentence."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify three flows of energy or material through a dryland landscape system.","a":"Any three of, e.g. solar radiation input, precipitation input, evaporation output, aeolian sediment transport, fluvial sediment transport, groundwater flow.","sol":"A dryland landscape system can be described through its flows: for example, solar radiation is a continuous input of energy driving evaporation and weathering, aeolian sediment transport is a flow of material moving sand between the erosional and depositional zones of the system, and evaporation is an output of energy and moisture leaving the system.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the systems vocabulary from the specification's conceptual overview: a system has inputs, stores, processes and outputs — a flow is what moves between them.","Look separately at what moves energy through the system and what moves material through it, then pick three examples in total from either category.","Name three flows in a single short sentence each; no explanation of how they work is required at this tariff."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify three physical factors, other than climate, that influence the character of a dryland landscape system.","a":"Any three of: geology (lithology and structure), latitude and altitude, relief and aspect, availability of sediment.","sol":"Besides climate, the specification names four further physical factors that influence dryland systems: geology (the lithology and structure of the underlying rock), latitude and altitude, relief and aspect (their effect on microclimate), and the availability of sediment.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look back at the list of physical influences the specification names alongside climate — there are four others.","These are properties of the land itself and its position, not the atmosphere above it.","Name three factors only; a short label for each (e.g. one word or short phrase) is enough."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Explain how geology has influenced the formation of landforms in one named low-latitude desert you have studied.","a":"Named low-latitude desert (e.g. the Sonoran Desert); its basin-and-range structure of resistant fault-block mountains and weaker basin fill explains why erosion concentrates on the ranges (producing pediments and sediment) while deposition concentrates in the basins (bajadas, alluvial fans, playas).","sol":"In the Sonoran Desert, the wider basin-and-range geological structure of the south-western USA controls where erosion and deposition occur. Resistant igneous and metamorphic rock forms the uplifted, fault-bounded mountain ranges; here, insolation and occasional flash-flood erosion strip weathered debris from the steep slopes, exposing bare rock and cutting pediments at the range front. This debris is then carried by ephemeral streams into the structurally lower basins between ranges, where it is deposited as coalescing alluvial fans and bajadas; the finest material is carried furthest, settling as clay and evaporite on the flat basin-floor playas. So the same underlying fault-block geology that raised the ranges also fixes the pattern of erosion (on the resistant highs) and deposition (in the weaker structural lows) across the whole desert.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the underlying rock structure of the desert you have studied, and how it separates high ground from low ground.","Link the type of rock and its structural position to whether erosion or deposition dominates there, then trace material from one to the other.","Open by naming the desert and its structural pattern, then explain the erosion side and the deposition side as two linked stages."],"codes":["1.b","2.a"]},{"q":"Explain the formation of a barchan dune.","a":"Forms where sand supply is limited and wind blows from a consistently unidirectional direction; saltation and creep pile sand into a crescent shape with horns pointing downwind because the thinner edges of the dune move faster than the sand-rich crest.","sol":"A barchan dune forms where wind blows consistently from one direction and the supply of loose sand is limited. Saltation carries sand grains up the gentle windward slope; once they reach the crest, they avalanche down the steeper leeward (slip) face, which builds and maintains the dune's asymmetric cross-section. Because sand supply is limited, the thinner edges of the dune contain less sand than the central body, so wind moves the edges forward faster than the sand-rich centre. This differential rate of advance curves the dune into its distinctive crescent shape, with two horns pointing downwind while the main bulk of the dune lags behind.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what conditions of wind direction and sand supply this dune type needs, compared with dune types that need abundant sand or shifting wind.","Think about how the amount of sand available might differ between the edges of the dune and its centre, and what that could do to the dune's shape over time.","Start by stating the wind and sediment-supply conditions, then explain how they produce the dune's crescent shape and its horns."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"A dryland site records mean annual precipitation of 180 mm and mean annual potential evapotranspiration of 1,200 mm. Calculate the site's aridity index and state which UNEP dryland category it falls into.","a":"AI = 180 ÷ 1,200 = 0.15; this falls in UNEP's arid category (0.05-0.20).","sol":"Aridity index = precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration = 180 ÷ 1,200 = 0.15. Since 0.15 lies between UNEP's thresholds of 0.05 and 0.20, the site is classified as arid.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall which two figures you need to divide, and in which order, to get the aridity index.","Carry out the division fully rather than estimating, so you get a precise decimal value to compare against the UNEP bands.","Show the division as a single calculation line, then state the named UNEP category the result falls into."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"A barchan dune field is reported to be advancing at 12 m per year. Calculate its average rate of advance in centimetres per day, name the aeolian process that carries most sand up the dune's windward slope, and explain why that process dominates over suspension there.","a":"12 m/yr = 1,200 cm/yr ÷ 365 ≈ 3.29 cm per day; the main process moving sand up the windward slope is saltation; saltation dominates over suspension because most windward-slope grains are too large/heavy for the wind to keep permanently airborne, so they move in short, repeated hops rather than sustained flight.","sol":"Convert metres to centimetres: 12 m × 100 = 1,200 cm per year. Convert years to days: 1,200 ÷ 365 ≈ 3.29 cm per day. The process chiefly responsible for moving sand grains up a dune's gentle windward slope is saltation, in which grains are lifted briefly by the wind and bounce forward in a series of short hops, dislodging further grains on impact. Saltation dominates over suspension on the windward slope because most of the sand there is too coarse and heavy for the wind to lift into sustained flight; suspension only carries the much finer silt and dust that forms a small proportion of dune sand, so saltation, not suspension, does most of the work of moving material up the slope.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Work out which conversions you need to go from metres per year to centimetres per day, and do them in a sensible order.","Once you have the daily rate, think about which named aeolian transport process (rather than 'wind erosion' generally) carries sand up a dune's gentle windward slope, as opposed to the process that moves it down the steep slip face.","Show your unit conversion as two clear steps, then name the process and briefly explain what it is about grain size and weight that keeps it airborne only briefly rather than in sustained suspension."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Using a case study of a mid-latitude desert you have studied, explain how physical factors have influenced the formation of one named landform.","a":"Named mid-latitude desert (e.g. the Gobi Desert); a named landform such as ventifacts, explained through the desert's continental/rain-shadow aridity limiting vegetation cover, an abundant supply of loose sand and grit, and strong, persistent unidirectional winds abrading exposed rock surfaces.","sol":"In the Gobi Desert, ventifacts (wind-faceted stones and boulders) form where several physical factors combine. The desert's position deep in the continental interior, in the rain shadow of the Tibetan Plateau and the Himalaya, gives it a very low aridity index and, because it is a mid-latitude rather than a low-latitude desert, a large annual temperature range with cold winters; the resulting sparse vegetation cover leaves rock surfaces and loose sediment exposed rather than protected by plant cover. Strong, persistent winds, sweeping across the open, largely flat gobi (stony desert plain) with little relief to slow them, pick up an abundant supply of loose sand and grit and drive it at low level against any exposed rock. Over time this abrasion cuts smooth, flat facets into the rock; where the wind direction is consistent, facets meet at sharp edges (keels), and where a stone is later rotated or undercut, a second or third facet can form at a different angle. The result is a ventifact whose faceted shape records both the direction of the prevailing wind and the aridity that kept the rock bare long enough for abrasion to act on it.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which named mid-latitude desert you have studied, and what its rain-shadow or continental position does to its vegetation cover compared with a low-latitude desert.","Think about what two things, beyond aridity itself, need to be in place before wind alone can carve solid rock into a distinctive shape over time.","Name the desert and the landform first, then explain the aridity/vegetation factor and the wind/sediment-supply factor in turn."],"codes":["1.b","2.b"]},{"q":"Explain how weathering and wind together produce a desert pavement.","a":"Salt weathering/insolation weathering breaks down the surface into fine and coarse fragments; wind deflation removes the fine, light particles; a residual lag of coarse stones concentrates and interlocks at the surface, armouring it.","sol":"Desert pavement (reg) forms through two linked processes. First, repeated salt crystallisation and insolation weathering break the exposed surface rock down into a mixture of fine and coarse fragments. Second, wind deflation then selectively removes the lighter, finer particles (sand and silt), because the wind's competence is too low to move the heavier stones and pebbles. Over time this leaves a residual lag of coarser material concentrated at the surface; the remaining stones interlock to form a stony pavement that then armours the finer sediment beneath it against further deflation, slowing the process once it has formed.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about this as two separate processes working in sequence, not one single process.","Consider why wind can remove some particle sizes from the weathered debris but not others.","Explain the weathering stage first, then the wind stage, then state what is left behind and why it protects the surface."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"To what extent do pluvial and periglacial processes from the past, rather than today's climate, explain the landforms found in present-day dryland landscapes?","a":"A balanced answer names relict pluvial landforms (inselbergs, pediments) and relict periglacial landforms (frost-shattered debris, nivation hollows, solifluction deposits), explains that both required a climate today's aridity cannot reproduce, but weighs this against present-day processes that actively modify (rather than create) these forms, reaching a supported judgement on how far the past still dominates.","sol":"Both pluvial and periglacial processes have left landforms that today's arid climate could not itself create. Pluvial (wetter) periods in the past permitted the deep chemical weathering and sheetwash needed to form inselbergs and pediments, since today's low rainfall cannot generate the runoff volumes required. Similarly, past periglacial conditions produced frost-shattered debris, nivation hollows and solifluction deposits through freeze-thaw and permafrost processes that today's warmer, drier climate cannot reproduce. In both cases, present-day processes — insolation weathering, rare flash floods, wind deflation — only rework or slowly erase these relict forms rather than generating new ones, so a large share of the erosional and depositional detail visible in many drylands is a genuinely inherited signature of past climates rather than a product of the present one. However, the extent varies by landscape: landscapes with active dune fields or wadis (such as the Sonoran and the Gobi) show much more present-day aeolian and flash-flood activity actively building new landforms, so the past dominates the large-scale relief (inselbergs, pediments, periglacial scars) while the present climate still dominates the smaller, faster-changing surface features (dunes, desert pavements). Overall, pluvial and periglacial legacies are significant and, in places, dominant controls on today's dryland landscapes, but they explain the landscape's skeleton more than its actively changing surface detail.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about two different past climatic conditions this topic covers — one wetter than today, one colder than today — and what each could form that today's climate cannot.","Weigh named relict landforms from both periods against landforms that are still actively forming today, rather than treating the whole landscape as either entirely relict or entirely modern.","Structure your answer to cover the pluvial evidence, then the periglacial evidence, then a judgement that states how far each still dominates the landscape today."],"codes":["3.a","3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how a water supply issue has altered flows of sediment in one dryland landscape you have studied.","a":"Named case study (e.g. the Colorado River, Hoover and Glen Canyon Dams); the water shortage/supply issue led to dam construction, which traps sediment behind the dams (in reservoirs such as Lake Mead and Lake Powell) instead of it travelling downstream to feed landforms such as the river delta.","sol":"In the Colorado River basin, growing demand for a reliable water supply across the south-western USA led to the construction of Hoover Dam (1936) and, later, Glen Canyon Dam (1963). Before damming, the river carried an estimated 125 million tonnes of sediment a year downstream. Once the dams were built, this sediment began settling in the still water of the reservoirs they created (Lake Mead and Lake Powell) rather than continuing downstream, because the dams remove the river's velocity and therefore its competence to carry suspended and bedload material. This has starved the river below the dams, and ultimately the Colorado River Delta on the Sonoran Desert coast, of the sediment supply it depended on, changing the balance of erosion and deposition all the way along the regulated channel.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about why people wanted to build a dam on this river in the first place, before considering what the dam then does to the sediment.","Think about what physically changes about the river's water once it slows down and spreads out into the reservoir, rather than just saying the dam 'blocks' the river.","State the water supply issue first, then explain the mechanism by which the dam changes the sediment flow."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Explain how economic activity has changed the geomorphology of a dryland landscape you have studied.","a":"Named case study (e.g. the Imperial Sand Dunes/Algodones Dunes, California); tourism in the form of off-highway dune buggy use damages vegetation and cryptobiotic crust, exposing loose sand to greater wind erosion and increasing dune mobility.","sol":"The Imperial Sand Dunes (Algodones Dunes) in California attract large numbers of visitors for off-highway vehicle recreation, including dune buggies, because of the scale and accessibility of the dune system. Repeated vehicle passes crush the sparse vegetation and the cryptobiotic (biological) soil crust that would otherwise bind loose surface sand together. With this stabilising cover removed, a much greater area of bare sand is exposed directly to wind, increasing the rate of aeolian erosion and the speed at which dunes migrate compared with undisturbed parts of the same dune field. This has also damaged the habitat of species adapted to the dunes' more stable areas, such as Peirson's milk-vetch, showing that the geomorphological change (faster erosion and migration) and the ecological change are directly connected through the same loss of surface cover.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what the visitors are physically doing to the ground surface, not just that they are 'causing damage'.","Consider what covers and protects the desert surface naturally, and what might happen to that surface once the cover is damaged or removed.","Name the activity and the landscape first, then trace the surface damage through to its wider effects on the dune, in that order."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"Identify the named economic activity used as your case study of human impact on a dryland landscape, and identify one way this activity damages the desert surface.","a":"E.g. off-highway (dune buggy) tourism at the Imperial Sand Dunes (Algodones Dunes), California; damages the surface by crushing vegetation and/or the cryptobiotic (biological) soil crust.","sol":"The Imperial Sand Dunes (Algodones Dunes) in California are used for off-highway vehicle tourism, including dune buggies. Repeated vehicle passes crush the sparse vegetation and the cryptobiotic (biological) soil crust that would otherwise bind the loose surface sand together, damaging the desert surface directly.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the case study used elsewhere in this topic for an economic activity changing a dryland landscape.","Think about what a vehicle's tyres physically do to the ground they repeatedly drive over.","Name the activity and the place first, then identify the one type of surface damage in a single sentence."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"Identify three characteristics that distinguish a polar dryland from a low-latitude desert.","a":"Any three of: polar drylands have much lower average temperatures, often remaining below 0°C for long periods; their limited precipitation is usually stored as snow or ice rather than falling as rain; weathering is dominated by frost and salt action rather than insolation weathering; low-latitude deserts are hot year-round with any rainfall often convective.","sol":"A polar dryland such as the McMurdo Dry Valleys differs from a low-latitude desert such as the Sonoran in three linked ways. First, its average temperatures are much lower, often remaining below 0°C for long periods rather than staying hot year-round. Second, because of this cold, its limited precipitation is stored as snow and ice rather than generating liquid runoff, unlike the low-latitude desert's occasional convective rainfall. Third, weathering there is dominated by frost action and salt crystallisation rather than the intense insolation weathering typical of a hot, low-latitude desert.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the thermal regime named in the specification's typology of drylands — this is what separates a polar dryland from the other types.","Think about two different consequences that such a large temperature difference could have for this landscape, beyond simply being colder.","Give three short, clearly contrasting statements; no case-study evidence is required at this tariff."],"codes":["1.c"]},{"q":"Compare how landscape change occurs over contrasting timescales in the mid-latitude and low-latitude desert case studies you have studied.","a":"Both deserts show near-instant change (flash floods reshaping fans/wadis in seconds) and millennial change (pediment development by slope retreat), but the Gobi's sustained sub-zero winters add freeze-thaw weathering feeding debris at every timescale — winter frost-shattering, spring snowmelt pulses, frost-assisted slope retreat — a mechanism the Sonoran's mild, mostly frost-free winters cannot produce at the same scale.","sol":"The Sonoran (low-latitude) and Gobi (mid-latitude) deserts both change across the same three broad timescales, but a real contrast appears once the mechanism behind each timescale is examined. At the fastest end, a single flash flood can reshape an alluvial fan or scour a wadi in seconds in both deserts; in the Sonoran this is driven by the summer monsoon's intense convective storms, while in the Gobi it is driven by rarer summer convective storms and by pulses of spring snowmelt from the surrounding mountains — a seasonal trigger the Sonoran does not have. On a seasonal timescale, ephemeral streams rework both deserts' wadi networks after rainfall, but the Gobi's sustained sub-zero winters add a process the Sonoran experiences only rarely: freeze-thaw weathering breaks up exposed rock through the winter months, so loosened debris is already available for transport when spring snowmelt and summer storms arrive. Over the longest, millennial timescale, both deserts develop pediments by parallel slope retreat, but in the Gobi this retreat is assisted by repeated winter frost weathering supplying debris to the slope, whereas the Sonoran, where winter frost is only occasional and light — hard freezes are rare enough to be the limiting control on saguaro distribution rather than a routine annual process — depends mainly on insolation weathering and rare flash floods. The key difference, then, is not the timescales themselves, which both deserts share, but that the mid-latitude Gobi's sustained sub-zero winters give it an extra, temperature-driven weathering process operating at every one of those timescales, while the low-latitude Sonoran relies on heat and rare rainfall, with only occasional frost, throughout.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Think about three very different lengths of time this topic asks you to consider, from a single event up to geological time.","For each timescale, identify a process or landform common to both deserts, then note what differs between the two case studies at that same timescale.","Organise your answer timescale by timescale (fastest to slowest), comparing both deserts directly within each, before drawing an overall comparison."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"To what extent is human activity now a more significant control on dryland landscape systems than the physical factors that originally created them?","a":"A top-level answer defines both controls, uses named human evidence (e.g. Colorado River damming reducing sediment flow to the delta; dune buggy damage at the Imperial Sand Dunes increasing erosion) against named physical evidence (e.g. geology and aridity shaping the Sonoran/Gobi landscape over millennia), evaluates the different timescales and reversibility of each, and reaches a supported, qualified judgement rather than a one-sided claim.","sol":"Physical factors — climate, geology, latitude, relief and sediment supply — created the fundamental template of dryland landscapes such as the Sonoran and Gobi Deserts over many millennia: the structural geology of mountain ranges and intervening basins fixed where erosion and deposition occur, and a persistently low aridity index (commonly below 0.20) sustained the weathering and aeolian regimes that built alluvial fans, bajadas and dune fields. Human activity, by contrast, has altered these systems far more rapidly. On the Colorado River, damming for water supply (Hoover Dam, 1936; Glen Canyon Dam, 1963) has trapped an estimated 125 million tonnes of sediment a year that once reached the Colorado River Delta, shrinking its wetlands by an estimated 90% or more within decades rather than millennia — a change of a magnitude that would otherwise have taken a major shift in regional climate. Similarly, off-highway dune buggy tourism at the Imperial Sand Dunes has measurably accelerated dune erosion and migration within a human lifetime by stripping stabilising vegetation and crust. In terms of the rate and visibility of change, human activity is now the dominant control in these specific, heavily used locations. However, this does not make it the more significant control everywhere or at every scale: physical factors still set the underlying limits within which human impacts operate (a dam cannot make an arid basin humid, and even damaged dunes still migrate because of the wind regime the physical geography created), and most of the wider desert landscape beyond these directly managed sites remains governed primarily by climate and geology, with human impact concentrated at a small number of intensively used sites. On balance, human activity has become the more significant control on the rate of change at specific, heavily managed or visited locations within dryland landscapes, but physical factors remain the dominant control on the landscape's overall extent, form and long-term evolution.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what 'significant control' will mean in your answer — the speed of change, the scale of change, or both — before you start weighing the two sides against each other.","Use one named piece of human evidence and one named piece of physical evidence for each side of the argument, and make sure each paragraph ends by relating back to the exact question, not just describing the case study.","Structure your answer as physical evidence, then human evidence, then an explicit weighing of the two against each other, closing with a qualified judgement on the extent to which the claim holds."],"codes":["1.b","2.a","4.a","4.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the formula for the aridity index?","a":"AI = precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration (P/PET)."},{"q":"Name one erosional and one depositional dryland landform.","a":"E.g. a wadi (erosional) and a barchan dune (depositional)."},{"q":"What type of past climatic period produced inselbergs and pediments?","a":"A pluvial (wetter) period."},{"q":"Give one named example of a dryland landscape changed by human economic activity.","a":"E.g. the Imperial Sand Dunes, California, damaged by off-highway dune buggy tourism."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Aridity index (UNEP)","back":"AI = precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration (P/PET). Hyper-arid <0.05; arid 0.05-0.20; semi-arid 0.20-0.50; dry sub-humid 0.50-0.65.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Inputs, stores, processes, outputs","back":"A dryland system has inputs (e.g. solar radiation, precipitation), stores (e.g. sediment, groundwater), processes moving material/energy between stores, and outputs (e.g. evaporation, exported sediment).","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Five physical factors shaping dryland systems","back":"Climate, geology (lithology and structure), latitude and altitude, relief and aspect, sediment availability.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Polar dryland vs low-latitude desert","back":"Polar drylands (e.g. McMurdo Dry Valleys) stay very cold, so precipitation is locked as snow/ice; low-latitude deserts (e.g. Sonoran) are hot year-round with convective rain and stronger insolation weathering.","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Erosional dryland landforms","back":"Wadis, canyons, pedestal rocks, ventifacts, desert pavements.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Depositional dryland landforms","back":"Barchans, linear dunes, star dunes, alluvial fans, bajadas.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Desert pavement (reg) formation","back":"Salt/insolation weathering breaks the surface down; wind deflation removes fine particles; a residual lag of coarse stones armours the surface.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Barchan dune shape","back":"Forms with limited sand and unidirectional wind; thin, sand-starved edges advance faster than the sand-rich crest, curving the dune into a crescent with horns pointing downwind.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Desert landscape timescales","back":"Flash floods reshape fans/wadis in seconds; seasonal streams rework canyons; pediment development by slope retreat takes millennia.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Pluvial relict landforms","back":"Inselbergs and pediments, formed by deep weathering and sheetwash during past wetter (pluvial) periods; today's aeolian and flash-flood processes only modify them.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Periglacial relict landforms","back":"Frost-shattered debris, nivation hollows, solifluction deposits, formed under past colder (periglacial) conditions, e.g. relict features at Breckland, East Anglia.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Colorado River dams (water supply case study)","back":"Hoover Dam (1936) and Glen Canyon Dam (1963) trap most of the river's estimated 125 million tonnes/year of sediment, starving the Colorado River Delta of flow and sediment.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Human-impact chain (4.a/4.b)","back":"Issue or activity → impact on flows of material/energy → effect on landforms → consequence for the wider landscape.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Imperial Sand Dunes (economic activity case study)","back":"Off-highway dune buggy tourism at the Algodones Dunes, California, damages vegetation and cryptobiotic crust, increasing wind erosion and dune migration.","code":"4.b"},{"front":"Cryptobiotic crust","back":"A thin biological soil crust of mosses, lichens and cyanobacteria that binds desert surface sand; once crushed by vehicles, the exposed sand erodes and moves far more easily in wind.","code":"4.b"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: To what extent is human activity now a more significant control on dryland landscape systems than the physical factors that originally created them?","On the Colorado River, damming for water supply has trapped an estimated 125 million tonnes of sediment a year that once reached the Colorado River Delta on the Sonoran Desert coast.","Hoover Dam (completed 1936) and Glen Canyon Dam (completed 1963) removed the river's velocity behind their reservoirs, Lake Mead and Lake Powell, so suspended sediment settles out instead of travelling downstream to feed depositional landforms.","As a direct result, the delta's wetlands are estimated to have shrunk by more than 90% since large-scale damming began, and the river now reaches the sea only in exceptional years, such as the 2014 'Minute 319' pulse-flow release.","This shows human water-supply management can now override the physical sediment supply that originally built the delta, producing landscape-scale change within decades rather than the millennia physical processes alone would need — making human activity, at this specific location, the more significant control on the rate and direction of landscape change."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: To what extent is human activity now a more significant control on dryland landscape systems than the physical factors that originally created them?","Humans have changed dryland landscapes a lot, for example by building dams on rivers like the Colorado River.","Dams stop sediment from moving down the river, which is bad for the landscape further downstream.","This also happens with tourism, because activities like dune buggies damage the desert surface and cause erosion.","So human activity has a big effect on dryland landscapes and is very significant today."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Sonoran Desert (low-latitude desert)","items":["Location: south-western Arizona and south-eastern California, USA, and the states of Sonora and Baja California, Mexico.","Latitude: approximately 25°N to 33°N — a low-latitude, subtropical desert beneath the descending air of the Hadley cell.","Area: approximately 260,000 km².","Rainfall regime: bi-seasonal, with winter frontal rain and a summer monsoon, giving mean annual precipitation of approximately 75-300 mm depending on location — relatively high for a hot desert.","Indicator species: the saguaro cactus (Carnegiea gigantea), protected within Saguaro National Park and Organ Pipe Cactus National Monument.","Characteristic landforms: basin-and-range relief, bajadas, alluvial fans, pediments and playas."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Gobi Desert (mid-latitude desert)","items":["Location: spans southern Mongolia and northern China (including Inner Mongolia), continental East Asia.","Latitude: approximately 40°N to 47°N — a mid-latitude desert, dry because of its continental interior position and the rain-shadow effect of the Himalaya and the Tibetan Plateau to its south-west, not a Hadley-cell high-pressure belt.","Area: approximately 1,300,000 km², one of the largest deserts in the world.","Climate: strongly continental with a large annual temperature range — approximate summer maximums around 40°C and winter minimums approximately -30°C to -40°C.","Mean annual precipitation: approximately 50-200 mm depending on location, falling mostly as light rain or snow.","Characteristic landforms: extensive desert pavement (the Mongolian word 'gobi' itself means a stony, sparsely vegetated desert plain), wind-faceted ventifacts, ephemeral drainage, and dune fields such as the Khongoryn Els.","Thermal regime: unlike a low-latitude desert such as the Sonoran, which stays hot year-round, the Gobi's continental interior position gives it very cold winters (approximate minimums of -30°C to -40°C) alongside hot summers (approximate maximums around 40°C) — a large annual temperature range that drives seasonal freeze-thaw weathering absent from low-latitude deserts."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Colorado River (water supply issue)","items":["The Colorado River is approximately 2,330 km long, draining approximately 637,000 km² across seven US states and part of Mexico.","Water supply issue: growing agricultural, urban and industrial demand across the arid south-western USA led to large-scale damming for a reliable water supply.","Hoover Dam, completed 1936 (Arizona-Nevada border), created Lake Mead, the USA's largest reservoir by capacity at full pool (approximately 35 km³).","Glen Canyon Dam, completed 1963, created Lake Powell further upstream.","Impact on flows: the river historically carried an estimated 125 million tonnes of sediment a year; most now settles in Lake Mead and Lake Powell instead of reaching the lower river.","Consequence: the Colorado River Delta, on the Sonoran Desert coast at the Gulf of California, now receives river flow only in exceptional years (e.g. the 2014 'Minute 319' pulse-flow release), and its wetlands are estimated to have shrunk by more than 90% since major damming began."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Imperial Sand Dunes / Algodones Dunes (economic activity)","items":["Location: south-eastern California, USA, close to the Mexican border, within the Sonoran/Colorado Desert.","Extent: one of the largest dune systems in the USA, approximately 65 km long.","Managed by the US Bureau of Land Management (BLM) as a National Recreation Area.","Economic activity: off-highway vehicle (OHV) tourism, including dune buggies and quad bikes, drawing an estimated one million or more visits a year, concentrated around holiday weekends such as Thanksgiving.","Impact: vehicle use crushes vegetation and cryptobiotic (biological) soil crust, exposing loose sand to greater wind erosion and increasing dune migration rates.","Consequence: habitat loss for species adapted to the dunes, including the federally threatened Peirson's milk-vetch (Astragalus magdalenae var. peirsonii), leading the BLM to close some areas to OHV use."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Supplementary UK example — Breckland, East Anglia","items":["Location: the Norfolk-Suffolk border, East Anglia, England; covers approximately 1,000 km².","The name 'Breckland' comes from 'brecks' — land temporarily cultivated then left fallow as heath.","Soils are light, sandy and free-draining, formed on glacial outwash sand further covered by wind-blown (aeolian) sand deposited under cold, dry conditions at the end of the last glacial period.","Relict periglacial evidence: patterned ground and infilled scars from former ice-wedge polygons and pingos survive beneath the heathland (e.g. the Norfolk pingo sites on Thompson Common/the Stanford Training Area).","The UK's driest area, used here for its relict periglacial features rather than for present-day aridity: mean annual precipitation is approximately 600 mm, giving an aridity index close to 1.0 — humid, not semi-arid, on the UNEP scale — though the light, sandy soils have historically been prone to wind erosion ('Breckland blows') where ploughing disturbed the vegetation cover.","Today largely covered by Thetford Forest, one of England's largest lowland pine forests (planted from the 1920s), and used for military training (STANTA) and heathland conservation."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — To what extent is human activity now a more significant control on dryland landscape systems than the physical factors that originally created them? (16 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'to what extent' demands a weighed judgement, not a one-sided answer — define 'physical factors' (climate, geology, latitude/altitude, relief, sediment supply) and 'human activity' (water supply management, economic activity) before comparing them.","Paragraph 1 (physical control): use the Sonoran/Gobi case studies — mountain-and-basin geology and a persistently low aridity index built the landscape's fundamental template (fans, bajadas, playas) over millennia; this sets the limits within which everything else operates.","Paragraph 2 (human control — water supply): use the Colorado River/Hoover and Glen Canyon Dams — an estimated 125 million tonnes/year of sediment trapped, delta wetlands shrunk by over 90%, change achieved within decades.","Paragraph 3 (human control — economic activity): use the Imperial Sand Dunes — dune buggy tourism damages vegetation/cryptobiotic crust, accelerating erosion and dune migration within a human lifetime.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: compare the rate of change (physical = millennia, human = decades) and the scale of change (human impact concentrated at specific managed/visited sites; physical factors still govern the wider desert and set the limits human activity works within).","Conclusion must: state explicitly how far the claim holds (human activity dominates the rate and visibility of change at specific, heavily used sites, but physical factors remain dominant over the landscape's overall extent and long-term evolution) — a qualified position, not 'yes' or 'no'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.938Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every fault the first verdict raised is genuinely fixed and not merely reworded: 1.c now separates low-latitude (Hadley descent, Sonoran) from mid-latitude (continental interior/rain shadow, large annual range, cold winters, Gobi) and 1.b matches it; the Mojave and the Racetrack Playa sailing-stones question are gone entirely; Breckland is now explicitly hedged in both lesson.idea 3.b and its fact file as the UK's driest area with an aridity index near 1.0, 'humid, not semi-arid'; the three sols no longer close with mark-scheme commentary; questions[1] and [6] hints no longer supply the answer; and questions[12] is retargeted at 4.b instead of duplicating the Colorado question. Substituting the Gobi makes 2.b's required mid-/low-latitude contrast real, and questions[14] now turns on a mechanism (winter freeze-thaw feeding debris at every timescale) rather than conceding the two deserts differ only 'in detail'. I re-solved every data question independently and all agree: 180/1,200 = 0.15 arid; 12 m/yr = 1,200/365 = 3.29 cm/day with saltation; and the worked examples 300/1,900 = 0.16 arid, 18 m/yr = 4.93 cm/day, 5,000 - 3,200 = 1,800 t net. UNEP bands are right in all six places they appear; Hoover 1936, Glen Canyon 1963, Minute 319 in 2014, Peirson's milk-vetch, Lake Mead ~35 km3, Colorado 2,330 km/637,000 km2, Gobi ~1,300,000 km2 and the Sonoran's ~260,000 km2 and bi-seasonal rainfall all check out, and the larger claims (125 Mt/yr of sediment, >90% delta wetland loss, ~1 million OHV visits) are hedged. All nine key ideas have a faithful section closing on what a top-level answer does, all five required case studies have a fact file (four beyond the UK, one UK), the two model paragraphs are truthfully Level 4 and Level 2, and the 16-mark plan decodes the stem, orders the paragraphs, carries the evaluation thread and says what the conclusion must assert. Polish for the same pass as the tariff fix: the last Gobi fact-file item (dinosaur eggs, Bactrian camel) bundles two facts into one item and neither is examinable here — a card or item on mid- vs low-latitude desert thermal regimes would be worth more, since cards currently contrast only polar with low-latitude; lesson.idea 3.a's 'left by deep chemical weathering and subsequent stripping... then further shaped by two-stage weathering' is circular, as the stripping is the second stage; questions[14].a runs to four lines where rule 4 asks indicative content in one or two; questions[7].hints[1] and questions[13].hints[1] are the most leading left, naming the axes of the answer (rain-shadow/continental position and vegetation cover; precipitation state and weathering process) though not its content; and three low-tariff questions ask for only two items at 3 marks."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:07:01.186Z"}},"1.2":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"1.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because Topic 1.2 forms Section B of the Physical systems paper (01) — Section A covers Landscape Systems, and Section B is Earth's Life Support Systems: examiners require a precise, systems-based account of how water and carbon move between the land, oceans, atmosphere and cryosphere, backed by two compulsory named case studies — a tropical rainforest and the Arctic tundra — and by the simple mass balance, rate-of-flow and unit-conversion skills tested from the Resource Booklet. Marks are lost when a student describes the cycles vaguely instead of naming specific stores, flows and processes, and when a case study is asserted rather than used with real places, dates and figures to answer the question set. AO2 (application) carries the largest share of marks across the qualification, so this room drills using the case studies and the water/carbon budget ideas to answer ‘to what extent’ and ‘evaluate’ style questions, not just reciting content.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Why water and carbon matter, and how they move","t":"Water sustains every organism: humans need it to drink, grow food and generate power; flora depends on it for photosynthesis and turgor; fauna depends on it directly and through the habitats it creates. Carbon is the building block of all life on Earth — it forms the organic molecules in every living cell — and is available for use both in the natural world (through photosynthesis and food chains) and by humans (through fossil fuels and farming). Both elements cycle continuously between the land, oceans and atmosphere: the global water cycle is best understood as a closed system (the total volume of water on Earth stays broadly constant, even as it changes state and location) while individual drainage basins and forests behave as open systems, gaining and losing water and carbon across their boundaries. A top-level answer states explicitly which scale — global or local — it is describing as open or closed, rather than treating the two cycles as vaguely ‘linked’ without naming the store, flow or boundary involved."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Stores, inputs and outputs of the two systems","t":"The specification names seven major stores shared or split between the two cycles: the atmosphere, oceans, water bodies, ice (the cryosphere), soil, vegetation and groundwater — and these vary hugely in size, from the ocean (the largest water store) to the much smaller store held in living vegetation. The water cycle's main inputs and outputs are precipitation bringing water into a store, and evapotranspiration removing it; snowmelt (ablation) is different again — it is an output from a snow or ice store, but at the same time an input to a catchment's liquid water store, since the meltwater it releases feeds rivers, soil moisture and groundwater. The carbon cycle's inputs and outputs include precipitation, photosynthesis and chemical weathering drawing carbon down, and decomposition, respiration and combustion releasing it back. A top-level answer names the specific store being discussed (not just ‘the ground’) and states whether a named process is acting as an input or an output for that store, since examiners reward this precision over a general description of ‘the cycle’."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Processes and pathways within the water and carbon cycles","t":"Water moves through a landscape via evaporation, transpiration, condensation (forming clouds), precipitation, interception (by the vegetation canopy), ablation, and runoff — including overland flow and saturated overland flow — before reaching a channel; catchment hydrology further breaks this down into infiltration, percolation, throughflow, groundwater flow and, in cold environments, cryospheric processes such as accumulation, ablation (meltwater generation) and sublimation, together with permafrost limiting infiltration to a shallow active layer. Carbon moves through photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition and combustion (both natural, such as wildfire, and human, such as burning fossil fuels), with natural sequestration locking carbon away in oceans, vegetation, sediments and through the weathering of rock. A top-level answer traces a specific pathway step by step — for example from interception through throughflow to a river channel — rather than naming the processes as an unconnected list, showing the sequence an examiner is looking for."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Case study: the tropical rainforest (Amazon basin)","t":"The specification requires a named case study of a tropical rainforest covering the water and carbon cycles specific to that biome, from an individual tree up to the rainforest as a whole. High, constant temperature and heavy rainfall drive rapid evapotranspiration, photosynthesis and decomposition, while porous, easily weathered rock and dense vegetation cover create distinct water and carbon stores in the canopy, litter layer and soil. For one drainage basin, deforestation and farming have changed these flows and stores — removing interception and transpiration, increasing overland flow and exposing soil carbon to faster decomposition — while strategies such as afforestation and improved agricultural techniques (for example agroforestry) can restore some of these positive effects. A top-level answer uses this case study's own places, figures and dates to answer the specific question set, rather than describing rainforests in general terms."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Case study: the Arctic tundra","t":"The specification also requires a named case study of the Arctic tundra covering its own distinct water and carbon cycles, rates of flow and stores. Low temperatures slow decomposition, so organic matter builds up in the soil rather than being broken down, while permafrost — ground frozen for at least two consecutive years — restricts infiltration and groundwater flow, confining the active layer of seasonal thaw to a shallow surface zone. Seasonal change is extreme: a short summer thaw releases stored water and carbon that refreeze again in winter. The developing oil and gas industry, such as at Alaska's Prudhoe Bay, disturbs both cycles through extraction and infrastructure, and is moderated by management strategies including elevated pipelines and protected land. A top-level answer explains how the case study's cold, seasonal conditions specifically shape its cycles, rather than transferring rainforest explanations to a different biome."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Dynamic equilibrium and human disturbance of the cycles","t":"Dynamic equilibrium describes a system in long-term balance, where inputs and outputs stay roughly equal so stores remain broadly stable despite constant short-term change. Human activity disturbs this balance: land use change (urban growth, farming, forestry) alters how much water infiltrates or runs off, and how much carbon is stored in vegetation and soil; water extraction (surface and groundwater, including from aquifers and artesian basins) directly reduces stores; and fossil fuel combustion, offset only partly by carbon sequestration, adds carbon to the atmosphere faster than natural processes remove it. These disturbances can trigger feedback loops — positive feedback amplifies the original change (for example permafrost thaw releasing methane that drives further warming and thaw), while negative feedback dampens it. A top-level answer identifies whether a named human factor pushes a system away from or back towards equilibrium, and whether any feedback loop that follows is positive or negative."},{"code":"3.b","h":"How the cycles change over time","t":"Change operates across very different timescales. Short-term changes — diurnal and seasonal shifts in climate, temperature, sunlight and foliage — alter rates of photosynthesis, evapotranspiration and river discharge within a single day or year, and matter because they can reverse quickly. Long-term changes, unfolding over millions of years, alter the stores and flows themselves, for example through the slow burial of carbon in sediment or the growth and retreat of ice sheets. Research and monitoring techniques (such as satellite data, ice cores and river gauging) are essential to identify and record these changes at both timescales, because the significance of a change cannot be judged without a long enough record to compare it against. A top-level answer states the timescale a change operates over before assessing its significance, rather than treating all change as equally rapid or equally permanent."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Linking the two cycles","t":"The water and carbon cycles are not separate systems: they link and depend on each other through the oceans (which absorb and release both CO2 and water vapour), the atmosphere (which transports both), the cryosphere (which locks up both frozen water and frozen carbon in permafrost) and vegetation (which draws down carbon while transpiring water). Human activities change the availability of both fossil and terrestrial carbon and water stores by using them as resources — extracting groundwater and burning fossil fuels alike remove a store faster than it is naturally replenished. Long-term climate change, driven mainly by disturbance to the carbon cycle, in turn reshapes the water cycle through altered precipitation patterns and glacial melt. A top-level answer traces a specific linking mechanism (a named store or flow shared by both cycles) rather than asserting that the cycles are ‘connected’ without saying how."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Managing water and carbon at the global scale","t":"Because the two cycles operate at a planetary scale, management strategies are needed at that scale too. Carbon cycle strategies include afforestation and wetland restoration (both restoring natural sequestration), improving agricultural practices, and reducing emissions through mechanisms such as carbon trading and international agreements. Water cycle strategies include improving forestry techniques, allocating water fairly between domestic, industrial and agricultural users, and drainage basin planning that manages run-off, surface stores and groundwater together rather than separately. A top-level answer evaluates a named strategy against a specific criterion — its effectiveness, its cost, or how equitably it is applied — and reaches a judgement on how far global management can realistically balance protection of these cycles against continuing human demand, rather than simply listing strategies."}],"examples":[{"title":"Simple mass balance — a perennial snow patch in the Arctic tundra","setup":"A perennial snow patch in the Arctic tundra — one that does not disappear completely each summer, so its size can be tracked from one year to the next like a small glacier — gains 280 mm water equivalent of snow over the winter and loses 340 mm water equivalent to melt over the following summer. Find the patch's net mass balance for the year, and say what that balance implies for the permafrost below it.","steps":["Mass balance is simply what comes in minus what goes out: 280 mm gained by snowfall, minus 340 mm lost to melt, gives −60 mm water equivalent.","The minus sign means the patch ended the year smaller than it started, having shed 60 mm water equivalent more than it took on.","A thinner patch leaves a thinner blanket of snow on the ground, and snow is a poor conductor of heat, so less of the summer's warmth is kept out of the soil beneath.","With that insulation reduced, the active layer thaws to a greater depth each summer, so the −60 mm balance points to permafrost being lost from the top down, releasing carbon that had been frozen in place for thousands of years."],"cues":["Which figure is subtracted from which here?","What does the minus sign say about the patch's size?","What does losing snow change for the ground underneath?","What happens to the depth of summer thaw, and why does that matter?"]},{"title":"Calculating river discharge — an Amazon tributary","setup":"A tributary of the Amazon has a channel 10 m wide with a mean depth of 2 m, and the mean flow velocity is measured at 0.6 m/s. Calculate the tributary's discharge in cumecs, then convert that figure to litres per second.","steps":["Cross-sectional area = width × mean depth = 10 m × 2 m = 20 m².","Discharge = cross-sectional area × mean velocity = 20 m² × 0.6 m/s = 12 m³/s, and 1 m³/s is 1 cumec.","1 m³ holds 1,000 litres, so 12 m³/s = 12 × 1,000 = 12,000 litres every second.","The tributary's discharge is therefore 12 cumecs, or 12,000 litres per second passing the measuring point."],"cues":["Which two channel measurements combine to give the cross-section?","What is the area multiplied by next?","How many litres are there in one cubic metre?","What is the discharge in each of the two units asked for?"]},{"title":"Converting a carbon store figure — rainforest soil","setup":"A survey of rainforest soil finds that one hectare of it stores 120 tonnes of carbon. Convert that figure into kilograms of carbon stored per square metre.","steps":["1 hectare measures 100 m × 100 m, so it covers 10,000 m² of ground.","Spreading the store over that area: 120 tonnes ÷ 10,000 m² = 0.012 tonnes of carbon per m².","1 tonne = 1,000 kg, so 0.012 tonnes × 1,000 = 12 kg.","The rainforest soil therefore holds 12 kg of carbon per square metre, a density that can now be set directly against the soil carbon figure for another biome such as the tundra."],"cues":["How many square metres are there in one hectare?","What is 120 tonnes spread over that area, in tonnes per m²?","How many kilograms are there in one tonne?","What is the final answer, and in which units?"]},{"title":"Applying the water budget equation — a small drainage basin","setup":"Over one year a small drainage basin receives 1,800 mm of precipitation (P). River discharge at the basin's outlet totals 700 mm (Q) and evapotranspiration losses are estimated at 1,050 mm (ET). Use the water budget equation P = Q + ET ± ΔS to find the change in storage, ΔS, for that year.","steps":["Total the two outputs first: Q + ET = 700 + 1,050 = 1,750 mm of water leaving the basin.","Rearranging the equation to isolate the unknown gives ΔS = P − (Q + ET) = 1,800 − 1,750 = +50 mm.","A positive ΔS means inputs exceeded outputs: 50 mm of the year's rain neither ran off nor evaporated, but stayed in the basin.","So ΔS = +50 mm for the year: the basin's soil moisture and groundwater stores grew by 50 mm rather than holding exactly steady."],"cues":["What do the basin's two outputs come to together?","How is the equation rearranged to leave ΔS on its own?","What does the sign of ΔS tell you about the year?","What is ΔS, and what has happened to the basin's stores?"]},{"title":"Weighing physical against human causes — Arctic tundra oil and gas","setup":"Carbon in the Arctic tundra has been held in permafrost for thousands of years and is released only slowly, as the active layer thaws each summer and refreezes each winter. Alaska's Prudhoe Bay oil field has been in production since 1977, its gravel pads, roads and warm oil pipeline laid across the same tundra, and the fuel it yields is burned elsewhere. Assess whether the faster thaw now recorded around the field is better explained by physical or by human factors.","steps":["The physical control is the seasonal cycle itself: summer warmth thaws the surface soil, autumn cold refreezes it, and the frozen carbon below is given up only gradually — a rhythm that would run on with nobody in Alaska at all.","The human controls work at two scales: locally, the gravel pads, roads and warm pipeline built since 1977 conduct heat into the ground and strip the insulating vegetation and moss; globally, combustion of the oil produced here and elsewhere raises Arctic air temperatures.","These are not competing explanations — the human effects are laid on top of the seasonal cycle, pushing the active layer deeper each summer than seasonal warmth alone would take it, which in turn exposes more frozen carbon (a positive feedback).","Judgement: human factors better explain the accelerated thaw. The seasonal cycle has operated without accelerating for millennia, so the change in rate since 1977 must come from the field's own heat and disturbance and from combustion-driven warming — though the physical cycle still sets the baseline on which those human effects act."],"cues":["Which part of this thaw would happen with no oil field there?","What has the field added on top of that cycle since 1977?","Do the two causes compete, or stack on each other?","Which set of factors better explains the faster thaw?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between an open and a closed system in the context of the water and carbon cycles?","a":"A closed system has no matter crossing its boundary, though energy can (e.g. the global water cycle, since the total amount of water on Earth stays broadly constant); an open system has both matter and energy crossing its boundary (e.g. a single drainage basin, which gains water as precipitation and loses it as river discharge and evapotranspiration)."},{"q":"Name one input and one output of the carbon cycle.","a":"Any one input (e.g. photosynthesis or weathering) and one output (e.g. respiration or combustion) — for example photosynthesis (input, converting atmospheric CO2 into stored carbon in plants) and respiration (output, releasing CO2 back to the atmosphere)."},{"q":"Give one physical factor that affects flows and stores in the water cycle.","a":"Any one of temperature, rock permeability and porosity, or relief (e.g. steep relief speeds up overland flow and reduces the time available for infiltration)."},{"q":"What is dynamic equilibrium in the water and carbon cycles?","a":"A state of long-term balance in a system, where inputs and outputs are roughly equal so that stores stay broadly the same size over time, even though short-term fluctuations occur."}]},"room":{"facts":["Water budget equation: precipitation (P) = river discharge (Q) + evapotranspiration (ET) ± change in storage (ΔS).","Simple mass balance: net balance = accumulation − ablation; a positive balance means a store (such as snow, ice or soil moisture) is gaining mass, a negative balance means it is losing mass.","River discharge (cumecs, m³/s) = cross-sectional area (width × mean depth) × mean velocity; 1 m³ = 1,000 litres.","Approximately 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, but approximately 68% of the planet's freshwater is locked in ice and glaciers.","The Amazon basin covers approximately 6.7 million km² across nine South American countries, and its river discharges an average of roughly 209,000 cumecs into the Atlantic Ocean — the largest discharge of any river on Earth.","Arctic permafrost is estimated to store around 1,500 billion tonnes of carbon — roughly twice the amount currently held in the atmosphere."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify two forms of chemical weathering that act as an input to the carbon cycle, and briefly explain how one of them draws carbon into a store.","a":"Two of: carbonation, hydrolysis (chemical weathering of rock, drawing carbon into solution or into new minerals) — e.g. carbonation: rainwater carrying dissolved CO2 reacts with rock such as limestone to form soluble compounds, carrying the carbon away in solution.","sol":"Chemical weathering acts as an input to the carbon cycle by breaking down rock and drawing carbon into new stores. Two accepted processes are carbonation, in which rainwater containing dissolved CO2 reacts with rock such as limestone to form soluble compounds, and hydrolysis, in which water reacts chemically with silicate minerals in rock to break them down, releasing ions that carry some carbon away in solution. One mark is given for each of the two correctly named processes, and the third mark is for a brief explanation of how one of them moves carbon into a store, as in either example above.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall which specific term in the specification's list of carbon cycle inputs covers rock breakdown by chemical reaction.","Think of this as weathering that happens through a chemical reaction with rock, not weathering caused by frost, plants or temperature change alone.","Name two separate processes, then add one short sentence on how just one of them moves carbon into a store — that extra sentence is what earns the third mark."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between an open system and a closed system, using an example of each from the water or carbon cycle.","a":"Closed = only energy crosses the boundary (e.g. the global water cycle, total water broadly constant); open = both matter and energy cross the boundary (e.g. a drainage basin, gaining and losing water).","sol":"A closed system is one in which energy can cross the system boundary but matter cannot; the global water cycle is usually treated this way, since the total volume of water on Earth stays broadly constant even as it changes location and state. An open system is one in which both matter and energy cross the boundary; a single drainage basin is an open system because it gains water and sediment as inputs (precipitation) and loses them as outputs (river discharge, evapotranspiration) that leave the system entirely. Full marks require a correct definition and a correctly matched, named example for each system type: two marks for the closed-system definition and example, two for the open-system definition and example, and two for explaining why each example fits its definition rather than simply asserting it.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what is allowed to cross a system's boundary in each case — is it only energy, or matter as well?","Choose one global-scale example and one smaller-scale example from the water or carbon cycle to illustrate the contrast.","Give one definition and one named example for each type of system, in two clearly separated parts of your answer."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Explain how evapotranspiration links the land surface to the atmosphere in the water cycle.","a":"Evapotranspiration combines evaporation from water/soil surfaces and transpiration from plants, moving water vapour from the land into the atmosphere as an input to future precipitation.","sol":"Evapotranspiration is the combined loss of water vapour from the land surface into the atmosphere through evaporation (from open water, soil and other wet surfaces) and transpiration (water vapour released through the stomata of plants during photosynthesis). Because this water vapour rises into the atmosphere, it becomes available to condense and fall again as precipitation elsewhere, meaning evapotranspiration is the main pathway by which water returns from the land store to the atmospheric store, sustaining the water cycle rather than allowing water to remain permanently on the land. One mark is given for naming the two component processes, one for identifying the two stores connected (land to atmosphere), and one for linking this to future precipitation.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the two separate sources of water vapour that combine to make up this one term.","Consider which store the water vapour is moving out of, and which store it is moving into.","State what evapotranspiration moves, and name the two stores it connects, in a short explanatory sentence."],"codes":["1.c"]},{"q":"Identify two short-term changes to the water or carbon cycle named in the specification.","a":"Any two of: diurnal change, seasonal change of climate, temperature, sunlight or foliage.","sol":"Short-term changes named in the specification include diurnal changes (occurring within a single day) and seasonal changes in climate, temperature, sunlight and foliage (occurring across a year). One mark is given for each of two such changes, correctly named; at this tariff no explanation of their effects is required.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Recall that this part of the specification distinguishes 'short-term' change from 'long-term' change — think about how short a timescale still counts as short-term here, from its briefest unit to its longest.","Think broadly about the category of condition that can shift within hours as well as change again over the course of a year, rather than a single specific example of it.","Name two separate short-term changes; no explanation of their effects is needed to earn full marks at this tariff."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how temperature and vegetation affect the rate of decomposition in the tropical rainforest.","a":"High, constant temperature speeds up the metabolism of decomposer organisms; abundant vegetation supplies a constant, large litter input — together these produce very rapid decomposition and nutrient cycling.","sol":"In the tropical rainforest, consistently high temperatures throughout the year speed up the metabolic and enzyme activity of decomposer organisms such as bacteria and fungi, so dead organic matter is broken down far faster than in a cooler biome. Dense vegetation cover also supplies a constant, large volume of leaf litter and other organic matter to the forest floor, giving decomposers an abundant and continuous food supply rather than a seasonal pulse. Together, high temperature and abundant litter input mean nutrients and carbon are recycled through the decomposition pathway extremely quickly, which is why the rainforest's own litter layer stays thin despite the huge volume of organic matter produced by the canopy above it.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what decomposer organisms need in order to work quickly, and what the rainforest's climate and canopy each supply.","Deal with the effect of temperature and the effect of vegetation as two separate linked points, rather than blending them together.","Build each point as cause leading to effect, and note what this fast process means for how much litter actually stays on the forest floor."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Analyse how deforestation for farming has changed flows and stores in the water cycle in one named drainage basin in the tropical rainforest.","a":"In a named Amazon basin/state (e.g. Rondônia), removing trees cuts interception and transpiration, so more rainfall reaches the ground and runs off overland rather than infiltrating, increasing peak discharge and flood risk.","sol":"In drainage basins of the Brazilian Amazon, such as those in Rondônia where forest has been cleared along ‘fishbone’ road patterns for cattle ranching and farming, removing the forest canopy sharply reduces interception, so far more rainfall reaches the ground directly rather than being caught, evaporated or channelled slowly down tree trunks. With fewer roots to hold and aerate the soil and less shade to slow evaporation, infiltration rates fall and a greater proportion of rainfall becomes overland flow. This speeds up the basin's response to storm rainfall, raising peak river discharge and flood risk, while the store of water held in vegetation and topsoil falls because there is less canopy to intercept and transpire it. The basin's water cycle therefore shifts from one dominated by slow, vegetated pathways to one dominated by rapid surface runoff.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which stage of the water cycle depends directly on the tree canopy being present.","Trace what happens to rainfall once that stage is removed, following it through to how quickly water reaches a river channel.","Name the drainage basin or region your answer is based on, then build a chain from canopy loss through to the effect on river discharge."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"A perennial snow patch on Arctic tundra — one that does not fully melt away each summer, so its mass can be compared from one year to the next — had winter accumulation equivalent to 320 mm of water and summer ablation (melt) equivalent to 210 mm of water this year. (a) Calculate the net mass balance for the year. (b) State whether the patch is gaining or losing mass, and outline what this could mean for the permafrost carbon store beneath it.","a":"Net balance = +110 mm w.e. (accumulation exceeds ablation), a positive/gaining balance; continued snow cover for longer could insulate the permafrost and limit further active-layer thaw and carbon release.","sol":"Net mass balance = accumulation − ablation = 320 mm − 210 mm = +110 mm water equivalent for the year. Because accumulation exceeds ablation, the snow patch has a positive balance and is gaining mass overall this year, growing rather than shrinking. A positive balance means the ground beneath it is protected by snow cover for longer, which could keep the active layer shallower, limiting how much previously frozen soil carbon thaws and becomes available for decomposition and release as CO2 or methane; a run of years with a negative balance instead would deepen the active layer and increase carbon release from thawing permafrost.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what ‘mass balance’ compares in a store that both gains and loses water through the year.","Work out which of the two figures given represents what was added to the store and which represents what was lost, then combine them.","State a single net figure with the correct sign and unit, then explain briefly what a positive or negative result implies for the permafrost beneath."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"A tributary draining part of the Amazon rainforest has a channel width of 15 m, a mean depth of 3 m and a mean velocity of 0.5 m/s. Calculate its discharge in cumecs, then convert this figure to litres per second.","a":"Discharge = 15 × 3 × 0.5 = 22.5 cumecs (m³/s); 22.5 m³/s = 22,500 litres per second (1 m³ = 1,000 litres).","sol":"Discharge = cross-sectional area × mean velocity. Cross-sectional area = width × mean depth = 15 m × 3 m = 45 m². Discharge = 45 m² × 0.5 m/s = 22.5 cumecs (m³ per second). To convert to litres per second, multiply by 1,000 (since 1 m³ = 1,000 litres): 22.5 × 1,000 = 22,500 litres per second.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall what discharge measures about a river channel, and which two channel measurements combine to give its cross-sectional area.","Work out the cross-sectional area first, then bring in the velocity figure.","Give your discharge in cumecs first, then convert that single figure into litres per second, showing the conversion factor you used."],"codes":["1.c","2.a"]},{"q":"Assess how effective afforestation and improved agricultural techniques are as strategies to protect the water and carbon cycles in the tropical rainforest, and reach a judgement on their overall effectiveness.","a":"Afforestation restores interception, transpiration and carbon sequestration; improved agriculture (e.g. agroforestry) reduces soil exposure and maintains some canopy — both help, but recovery is slow and effectiveness relies on enforcement and funding.","sol":"Afforestation restores tree cover, rebuilding interception and transpiration so that overland flow and flood risk fall back towards pre-clearance levels, while new tree growth draws carbon back out of the atmosphere and rebuilds the vegetation carbon store lost to deforestation. Improved agricultural techniques, such as agroforestry that keeps some tree cover among crops, reduce the amount of bare soil exposed to rainfall and sun, limiting the rapid decomposition and carbon release that follows full clearance while still allowing farming to continue. Both strategies are genuinely effective at slowing further loss and beginning recovery of the cycles. However, their effectiveness is limited by time — newly planted trees take decades to intercept and store carbon at the rate mature rainforest did — and by how consistently they are enforced and funded, since farmers may revert to full clearance if agroforestry yields less than conventional farming. Overall, both strategies move the water and carbon cycles back towards their pre-disturbance state, but neither restores them quickly, so their effectiveness depends heavily on sustained investment and enforcement over a long period.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which part of the rainforest's water and carbon cycle each strategy directly restores.","Weigh what each strategy achieves against how long it takes, or how reliably it is kept up, to reach a balanced view.","End with an overall judgement on how effective the two strategies are together, not a simple description of what each one does."],"codes":["2.a","4.b"]},{"q":"Explain how the developing oil and gas industry has disturbed the carbon cycle in the Arctic tundra, and outline one strategy used to moderate this impact.","a":"Extraction and infrastructure (e.g. Prudhoe Bay, Alaska) release stored carbon and can warm and disturb the ground, deepening the active layer and releasing further carbon; moderation strategies include elevating pipelines to limit ground disturbance.","sol":"Extracting and burning oil and gas releases carbon that had been stored underground for millions of years directly into the atmosphere, while the heat and physical disturbance from wells, roads and pipelines — such as at Alaska's Prudhoe Bay field, in production since 1977 — can warm the ground locally and deepen the active layer, exposing previously frozen soil carbon to decomposition and further release as CO2 or methane. One strategy used to moderate this impact is elevating pipelines, such as the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System, above the ground on supports, which reduces heat transfer into the permafrost below and allows wildlife such as caribou to continue moving beneath the pipeline route.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about two separate ways the industry disturbs carbon: what is released by extraction itself, and what its infrastructure does to the ground around it.","Name a specific piece of infrastructure or a named oil and gas development to anchor your explanation.","Cover the disturbance first, then give one named management response as a separate final point."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"Analyse the role of positive and negative feedback loops in disturbing or maintaining dynamic equilibrium in the water and carbon cycles.","a":"Positive feedback amplifies an initial change away from equilibrium (e.g. permafrost thaw releasing methane, driving further warming and thaw); negative feedback dampens change, pushing a system back towards balance (e.g. extra plant growth from higher CO2 absorbing more carbon).","sol":"Dynamic equilibrium exists where a system's inputs and outputs stay roughly balanced over time, so its stores remain broadly stable. Positive feedback loops disturb this balance by amplifying an initial change in the same direction: for example, as permafrost in the Arctic tundra thaws, it releases stored methane and CO2, which adds to atmospheric warming, which in turn causes further permafrost thaw — each stage reinforcing the last and pushing the system further from its original equilibrium. Negative feedback loops instead work to restore balance by dampening a change: for example, higher atmospheric CO2 concentrations can stimulate increased plant growth (the ‘CO2 fertilisation effect’), and this extra vegetation absorbs more carbon through photosynthesis, partly offsetting the original rise in atmospheric CO2 and pulling the system back towards its previous balance. Because these two loop types push in opposite directions, the overall trajectory of a system such as the Arctic carbon cycle depends on which loops currently dominate — and evidence that permafrost carbon release is now outpacing plant carbon uptake suggests positive feedback is currently the stronger of the two forces disturbing this system's equilibrium.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what separates a feedback loop that pushes a system further from balance from one that pulls it back towards balance.","Choose one clear named example of each loop type from the water or carbon cycle, tracing each through more than one stage.","Finish by weighing which of the two loop types is currently having the stronger effect on the system you have discussed."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"‘Human activity is now the main control on the carbon cycle in the tropical rainforest.’ To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Deforestation (e.g. in Rondônia, Brazil) has become a powerful, rapid control on carbon flows and stores, but the underlying physical controls (temperature, rainfall) still govern the natural rates of photosynthesis and decomposition that make the biome significant — so human activity now dominates the rate of change, though it has not replaced the physical basis of the system.","sol":"Human activity has become a powerful control on the tropical rainforest's carbon cycle, most visibly through deforestation. In Rondônia, in the Brazilian Amazon, clearance for cattle ranching and farming along the ‘fishbone’ pattern of roads removes vegetation that would otherwise photosynthesise and store carbon, while exposing soil organic matter to rapid decomposition in the tropical heat; Brazil's national monitoring recorded approximately 11,000 km² of Amazon deforestation in the year to July 2020 alone, a rate of change no natural process in the region approaches. This human-driven loss converts a long-term carbon store into a source of emissions far faster than natural disturbances such as wildfire or windthrow could. However, the underlying physical controls have not been replaced. The same high, constant temperatures and heavy rainfall that make deforestation so damaging also drive the rainforest's naturally rapid rates of photosynthesis, decomposition and nutrient cycling — the reason an undisturbed rainforest stores and cycles so much carbon in the first place. Where clearance stops, these physical conditions allow carbon cycling to resume quickly through natural regrowth, showing that physical factors still set the baseline the human disturbance acts upon. On balance, human activity is now the main control on the rate and direction of recent change in the Amazon's carbon cycle, since deforestation currently outpaces any natural process of carbon release or recovery; but it operates by disturbing physical processes that remain the underlying reason this biome is such a significant carbon store, so physical factors have not been replaced as the foundation of the system, only overridden in the short term by the scale of human disturbance.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what ‘main control’ would mean here — which factor is currently driving the fastest rate of change, not just which factor is present.","Set out the case for human activity with a named, dated example, then weigh it against what the physical conditions of the biome still explain.","End with a qualified judgement that states how far you agree, rather than a simple yes or no."],"codes":["2.a","3.a","4.a"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of global management strategies in protecting the water and carbon cycles.","a":"Strategies such as afforestation, wetland restoration, carbon trading, international agreements and drainage basin planning genuinely slow disturbance and rebuild stores, but effectiveness varies by strategy, is often slow to take effect, and depends on cooperation that is not guaranteed — so overall effectiveness is real but partial and uneven.","sol":"Global strategies to protect the carbon cycle, such as afforestation and wetland restoration, are effective in principle because they directly rebuild natural carbon stores that human activity has depleted, while reducing emissions through mechanisms such as carbon trading gives businesses and countries a financial incentive to cut fossil fuel combustion rather than relying on voluntary action alone. International agreements extend this by coordinating action across countries, which matters because carbon and water move across national borders regardless of where they are managed. Global water cycle strategies, such as drainage basin planning that manages run-off, surface stores and groundwater together, and fairer water allocation between domestic, industrial and agricultural users, similarly aim to prevent any one use from depleting a shared store faster than it is replenished. These strategies have real, measurable successes: reforestation schemes have restored carbon sequestration capacity in specific regions, and international agreements have set binding targets that would not exist otherwise. However, effectiveness is uneven. Afforestation takes decades to sequester carbon at the rate mature forest once did, so it addresses future flows more than it reverses current stores quickly; carbon trading and international agreements depend on consistent enforcement and on every major emitter participating, and a single large economy withdrawing can undermine the whole scheme's impact; and drainage basin planning requires cooperation between users and, in transboundary basins, between countries with competing interests in the same water. Overall, global management strategies are effective at slowing further disturbance and beginning to rebuild stores, and are more effective the more consistently they are enforced and the more countries commit to them together, but none of them restores the cycles quickly or guarantees results, so their effectiveness should be judged as real but partial rather than complete.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what each type of strategy is trying to restore or protect, for the carbon cycle and for the water cycle separately.","Weigh genuine successes of these strategies against what limits how quickly or completely they work.","Reach an overall judgement on how far these strategies succeed, rather than judging each strategy in isolation."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"Compare the physical factors affecting the water and carbon cycles in the tropical rainforest with those in the Arctic tundra.","a":"Both are affected by temperature, rock permeability/porosity and relief, but the rainforest's high, constant temperatures and rainfall drive fast flows and decomposition, while the tundra's low temperatures and permafrost slow flows and decomposition, building up frozen carbon and water stores instead.","sol":"Both biomes are shaped by the same three physical factors named in the specification — temperature, rock permeability and porosity, and relief — but these factors act in almost opposite ways in each case. In the tropical rainforest, consistently high temperatures and heavy rainfall drive rapid evapotranspiration, fast infiltration through porous, weathered tropical soils, and very fast decomposition, so water and carbon cycle through the system quickly and stores such as the litter layer stay thin. In the Arctic tundra, low temperatures instead slow evapotranspiration and decomposition dramatically, while permafrost beneath the active layer acts as an impermeable barrier regardless of the rock's natural porosity, restricting infiltration and groundwater flow to a shallow surface zone; this combination means organic matter and water accumulate rather than cycling quickly, building a large frozen carbon store below ground rather than a fast-cycling store above it. Relief affects both biomes similarly in principle — steeper relief speeds up runoff and limits infiltration in both — but its practical effect is far smaller in the tundra, where permafrost already controls infiltration regardless of slope. Overall, the same physical factors operate in both biomes, but temperature is the dominant control in each case, and it produces contrasting outcomes: rapid cycling and thin stores in the rainforest, against slow cycling and large frozen stores in the tundra.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Start from the three physical factors both case studies share, then think about how differently each one behaves in a hot biome compared with a cold one.","Build your comparison factor by factor, contrasting the rainforest and the tundra directly rather than describing each case study separately.","End with a statement of which factor matters most in each biome, and what contrasting outcome for stores and flows this produces."],"codes":["2.a","2.b"]},{"q":"A climate graph for an Arctic tundra weather station shows mean monthly temperature ranging from about −28°C in January to 6°C in July, and total annual precipitation of 240 mm, with the wettest month (August) recording 35 mm. (a) Read off and state the station's approximate annual temperature range. (b) Using the shape of this climate graph, explain one reason why the water cycle at this site is dominated by snow rather than rainfall as an input.","a":"(a) Annual range ≈ 34°C (6 − (−28) = 34°C). (b) Mean temperature stays below 0°C for most of the year, only briefly rising above freezing around the short summer peak, so precipitation falling across most months arrives and remains as snow rather than as rain.","sol":"Reading a climate graph means taking specific values directly off the temperature line and precipitation bars rather than estimating. (a) The annual temperature range is the difference between the highest and lowest mean monthly temperatures shown: 6°C (July) − (−28°C, January) = 34°C. (b) The graph's shape — a temperature line that stays below freezing for most months and rises above 0°C only briefly around the summer peak — shows that precipitation falling across most of the year arrives as snow, since air temperatures are too low for rain to occur or to remain liquid on the surface; this is why the tundra's water cycle is fed mainly by snow accumulation and a short summer ablation (melt) season rather than by rainfall runoff, unlike a temperate or tropical climate graph where the temperature line stays above freezing all year.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Work out the temperature range first by reading the highest and lowest points directly from the graph, then think about what a temperature line mostly below freezing implies for the state precipitation falls in.","Consider how many months of the year the temperature line sits below 0°C, and what that means for whether precipitation stays solid or turns to liquid water.","Give your range as a single number with its unit, then link the overall shape of the graph — not just one figure from it — to the form the cycle's main input takes here."],"codes":["1.c","2.b"]},{"q":"A student investigating a small stream measured its velocity at five equally spaced points across the channel using a flow meter, recording 0.28, 0.35, 0.40, 0.33 and 0.24 m/s. (a) Calculate the mean velocity from this field data. (b) Suggest one appropriate way to present this set of readings, and explain why it suits data collected at points across a channel.","a":"(a) Mean = (0.28 + 0.35 + 0.40 + 0.33 + 0.24) ÷ 5 = 1.60 ÷ 5 = 0.32 m/s. (b) A bar chart or line graph plotting velocity against distance across the channel, because it shows how velocity varies from bank to bank (typically fastest near the centre/surface) rather than hiding that pattern inside a single averaged figure.","sol":"(a) To find the mean, sum the five readings (0.28 + 0.35 + 0.40 + 0.33 + 0.24 = 1.60 m/s) and divide by the number of readings (5), giving a mean velocity of 0.32 m/s. (b) Because the readings were taken at known points across the channel, a graph that plots velocity on one axis against distance from the bank on the other — a bar chart or line graph — is the most appropriate presentation technique, since it preserves the spatial pattern of the data (typically showing velocity increasing towards the centre and surface of the channel, away from frictional drag at the bed and banks), which a single averaged figure or a table alone would obscure. This kind of field data underpins the discharge calculations used elsewhere in this topic, since mean velocity measured this way is exactly the figure used in discharge = cross-sectional area × mean velocity.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Add the five readings together first, then divide by how many readings you have to find the mean.","Think about what these five numbers actually represent — measurements taken at different positions — and what kind of presentation keeps that positional information visible.","Give one clear numerical answer for the mean, then name one presentation method and give a single reason it fits data collected at points across a space, not just any data."],"codes":["1.c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is stored in the cryosphere?","a":"Frozen water, held as ice, snow and permafrost."},{"q":"Name one input to the water cycle.","a":"Any one of precipitation (an input to a water store) or snowmelt/ablation (an output from a snow or ice store that acts as an input to a catchment's liquid water store, e.g. as meltwater feeding a river or groundwater)."},{"q":"Is a small drainage basin an open or a closed system?","a":"Open — water and matter cross its boundary as inputs (precipitation) and outputs (discharge, evapotranspiration)."},{"q":"What does permafrost do to infiltration in the Arctic tundra?","a":"It blocks it, restricting water to a shallow active layer at the surface."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Open system","back":"A system in which both matter and energy cross the boundary, e.g. a single drainage basin gaining water as precipitation and losing it as discharge and evapotranspiration.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Closed system","back":"A system in which energy but not matter crosses the boundary, e.g. the global water cycle, where the total volume of water on Earth stays broadly constant.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Major water cycle stores","back":"Atmosphere, oceans, water bodies (rivers/lakes), ice (cryosphere), soil, vegetation and groundwater.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Ablation","back":"The loss of ice or snow through melting or sublimation — an output from a snow or ice store, but an input to a catchment's liquid water store as meltwater feeding rivers and groundwater.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Catchment hydrology terms","back":"Infiltration (water entering soil), percolation (water moving down through soil/rock), throughflow (lateral movement through soil) and groundwater flow (movement through saturated rock).","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Photosynthesis","back":"6CO2 + 6H2O + light energy → C6H12O6 + 6O2; removes carbon from the atmosphere and stores it in plant tissue.","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Amazon basin case study","back":"Covers approximately 6.7 million km² across nine countries; stores an estimated 150–200 billion tonnes of carbon; around half its rainfall is recycled by the forest's own evapotranspiration.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Deforestation impact (rainforest)","back":"Removing trees cuts interception and transpiration, increasing overland flow and flood risk, while exposing soil carbon to faster decomposition and release.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Active layer","back":"The surface layer of tundra soil that thaws each summer and refreezes each winter, sitting above the permanently frozen permafrost.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Prudhoe Bay","back":"Alaska's largest oil field, in production since 1977 and linked to the coast by the roughly 1,300 km Trans-Alaska Pipeline System — a named example of the oil and gas industry's impact on the Arctic tundra.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Dynamic equilibrium","back":"A long-term balance in a system where inputs and outputs are roughly equal, so stores stay broadly stable despite short-term fluctuations.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Positive feedback loop","back":"A change that triggers further change in the same direction, amplifying the original disturbance, e.g. permafrost thaw releasing methane that increases warming, causing more thaw.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Short-term vs long-term change","back":"Short-term changes include diurnal and seasonal shifts in temperature, sunlight and foliage; long-term changes occur over millions of years and alter stores and flows permanently.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Link between the cycles","back":"Vegetation: photosynthesis draws CO2 down while transpiration returns water vapour to the air; oceans: cold water dissolves more CO2, warm water evaporates faster and holds less; cryosphere: thawing permafrost both releases stored carbon and adds meltwater to the water cycle.","code":"4.a","open":true},{"front":"Global carbon management strategies","back":"Afforestation and forest protection, such as UN REDD+ schemes in the Amazon; wetland and peatland restoration; improved agriculture, e.g. reduced tillage; cutting emissions through carbon trading (the EU Emissions Trading System) and international agreements (the 2015 Paris Agreement).","code":"4.b","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: ‘Human activity is now the main control on the carbon cycle in the tropical rainforest.’ To what extent do you agree? (16 marks)","Human activity is now the dominant control on the pace of change in the tropical rainforest's carbon cycle, which is shown most clearly by how far policy alone can move the deforestation rate. Brazil's Action Plan for the Prevention and Control of Deforestation in the Legal Amazon (PPCDAm), launched in 2004, was followed by a sustained fall in the annual deforestation rate through the following decade — a swing in the carbon cycle's main source of disturbance driven by enforcement and land-use policy rather than by any change in climate or vegetation. This shows that human decisions, not physical conditions, currently set the rate at which carbon leaves this store. However, the underlying physical controls have not been displaced: the same high, constant temperatures and heavy year-round rainfall that make cleared land vulnerable to rapid carbon loss also drive the naturally fast photosynthesis and decomposition that let an undisturbed rainforest regenerate its carbon store once clearance pressure eases, as the post-2004 slowdown itself demonstrates. Human activity — expressed through policy as much as through clearance — is therefore the main control on the current rate and direction of change in the Amazon's carbon cycle, but it operates only by speeding up or slowing down physical processes that remain the reason this biome cycles and stores carbon so quickly in the first place."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: ‘Human activity is now the main control on the carbon cycle in the tropical rainforest.’ To what extent do you agree? (16 marks)","Humans affect the carbon cycle in the rainforest because they cut down trees. This is bad because trees store carbon, so if you cut them down the carbon cycle is disturbed. In Brazil a lot of the rainforest has been cut down for farming. This releases carbon into the air instead of it staying stored in the trees. Deforestation also affects the soil because there are no trees to protect it. So human activity does affect the carbon cycle in the rainforest a lot, mainly through cutting down trees for farming."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the tropical rainforest (Amazon basin)","items":["Location: the Amazon basin covers approximately 6.7 million km² across nine South American countries, roughly 60% of it within Brazil.","Climate: hot and wet all year, with mean temperatures of approximately 26–28°C and annual rainfall typically above 2,000 mm.","River: the Amazon River has the largest discharge of any river on Earth, averaging approximately 209,000 cumecs (m³/s) into the Atlantic Ocean.","Moisture recycling: an estimated 50% or more of rainfall over the Amazon is generated by the forest's own evapotranspiration, sometimes called ‘flying rivers’.","Carbon store: the Amazon's vegetation and soil are estimated to store approximately 150–200 billion tonnes of carbon.","Deforestation: Brazil's national space research agency (INPE) recorded approximately 11,000 km² of Amazon deforestation in the year to July 2020.","Named example: Rondônia state, Brazil, shows a ‘fishbone’ pattern of deforestation for cattle ranching and farming spreading out from access roads.","Management: Brazil's Action Plan for the Prevention and Control of Deforestation in the Legal Amazon (PPCDAm), launched in 2004, helped cut the annual deforestation rate through the following decade; REDD+ is an international scheme that pays to reduce emissions from deforestation and forest degradation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — the Arctic tundra","items":["Location: Arctic tundra covers northern regions including Alaska (USA), northern Canada, Siberia and northern Scandinavia; the North Slope of Alaska is a widely studied example.","Climate: long, severe winters with mean temperatures often below −30°C and a short summer growing season; annual precipitation is low, typically around 250 mm, classing the tundra as a cold desert.","Permafrost: ground that has remained frozen for at least two consecutive years lies beneath a surface ‘active layer’, typically around 0.3–1 m deep, which thaws each summer and refreezes each winter.","Carbon store: Arctic permafrost is estimated to store around 1,500 billion tonnes of carbon — roughly twice the amount currently held in the atmosphere.","Vegetation: low-growing mosses, lichens, sedges and dwarf shrubs give the tundra low net primary productivity, which, combined with cold, slow decomposition, builds up large stores of organic carbon in the soil.","Oil and gas: Prudhoe Bay, on Alaska's North Slope, is the largest oil field in North America, in production since 1977.","Infrastructure: the Trans-Alaska Pipeline System (TAPS) runs approximately 1,300 km (800 miles) from Prudhoe Bay to the port of Valdez.","Management: sections of the pipeline are raised on supports above the ground to reduce heat transfer into the permafrost and to allow caribou migration to continue beneath it."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — How far are the water and carbon cycles linked? (16 marks)","items":["Decode the question: ‘water and carbon cycles’ means both systems must appear in every paragraph, not just one; ‘how far’ means the answer must reach a qualified judgement about the strength of the link, not simply list connections; ‘linked’ points to a shared store or flow, not just a general similarity.","Paragraph 1 (establish the strongest link): explain how the cryosphere links the two cycles by locking up both frozen water and frozen carbon together in permafrost, and what happens to both stores at once when permafrost thaws.","Paragraph 2 (develop a second, different link): explain how the oceans link the two cycles by absorbing and releasing both CO2 and water vapour, and how a change driven by one cycle (such as ocean warming) affects the other.","Paragraph 3 (test the limits of the link): explain a case where the two cycles can change largely independently of each other — for example, water extraction from an aquifer having little direct effect on carbon stores — to show the link is not absolute.","Evaluation thread: throughout, weigh how directly each mechanism connects the two cycles (a shared store, acting on both at once) against cases that mainly affect one cycle with only an indirect knock-on effect on the other.","Conclusion must: state a qualified judgement — the two cycles are linked closely and directly through specific shared stores such as the cryosphere and the oceans, but not every change in one cycle has a significant effect on the other, so ‘linked’ is a matter of degree depending on the mechanism involved."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.938Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"No answer key is wrong — I re-solved every calculation independently (mass balance 320 − 210 = +110 mm w.e.; discharge 15 × 3 × 0.5 = 22.5 cumecs = 22,500 l/s; and the four lesson examples, including P = Q + ET ± ΔS giving ΔS = +50 mm) and all agree with their keys — and the real-world figures hold up (INPE's ~11,000 km² for the year to July 2020 against the recorded 10,851 km²; Amazon ~6.7 million km² and ~209,000 cumecs; Prudhoe Bay in production since 1977; TAPS ~1,300 km; permafrost ~1,500 Gt C, which is nearer 1.7× the atmosphere's ~880 Gt C than the \"roughly twice\" claimed, but that is the standard textbook phrasing); the family rules are met in full — two model paragraphs correctly labelled Level 4 and Level 2 and genuinely at those levels, a fact file for each of the two required case studies, an essay plan in the board's command words, and all nine lesson sections closing with what a top-level answer does — so this is close to publishable, but fix the give-away hint at question 3 and the condensation/ablation input-output muddle before it goes to students, and be aware that the two snow-balance scenarios (accumulation exceeding ablation in tundra, and the reverse in the lesson example) are arithmetically sound but physically implausible as stated for a site with no year-to-year snow carryover, and that the Level 4 model paragraph is near-verbatim the solution to question 11."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:47:56.667Z"}},"2.1":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"2.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room prepares you for Component 2 (Human Interactions), where ‘Changing Spaces; Making Places’ is examined through short-answer, 6- to 10-mark and sixteen-mark extended-response questions, and the same content can resurface in the synoptic Geographical Debates paper. Examiners reward answers that use named case-study evidence and skills such as reading deprivation data to build a weighed argument about places, not a description of places for its own sake.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"What shapes a place: characteristics and changing flows","t":"A place's identity is built from its demographic, socio-economic, cultural, political, built and natural characteristics, and from the past and present connections that embed it at regional, national, international and global scales. Crucially, these characteristics are not fixed: shifting flows of people (such as commuters or migrants), resources (such as natural resources or new technology), money and investment (such as EU funding or TNC investment) and ideas (such as the knowledge economy) continually reshape a place's demographic, socio-economic and cultural profile over time. A top-level answer names two contrasting places at a local scale and traces a specific flow through to a specific change in their profile, rather than listing static characteristics as if a place were unchanging."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Defining place, and how identity shapes perception","t":"Geographers distinguish space, a physical location, from place, space that has been given meaning through human experience. How people perceive a place depends on their identity — age, gender, sexuality, religion and role all shape what someone notices, values or fears about it — and how strongly they feel emotionally attached to it shapes their behaviour, from whether they stay, invest in or defend it. Globalisation and time-space compression, the sense that distant places feel closer as travel and communication get faster, also reshape how connected or distant a place feels to us. A top-level answer explains how a named aspect of identity changes what a specific group notices or values about a place, rather than asserting that perception simply varies."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Representing place: formal and informal sources","t":"Places are represented through contrasting agencies. Informal representations — TV, film, music, art, photography, literature, graffiti and blogs — are created by individuals or organisations and are selective, foregrounding some features of a place and omitting others to construct a particular version of it. Formal and statistical representations, such as census or geospatial data, are collected systematically and presented as measurable data, which makes them feel more objective, though the choice of what to measure and how to present it still shapes the picture given. A top-level answer names who produced a representation and what it foregrounds or omits, showing that even formal data constructs a version of a place rather than simply recording a neutral truth."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Measuring and mapping social inequality","t":"Social inequality is the uneven distribution of resources, wealth and opportunities within and between places, and it is commonly measured through indices covering housing, healthcare, education, employment and access to services. Spatial patterns of inequality vary both within a single place (for example between streets or wards) and between different places (for example between a city and a village), reflecting different historical, economic and social processes rather than random chance. A top-level answer names a specific index and uses it to evidence a specific spatial pattern, such as a contrast between two named areas, rather than describing inequality in general terms."},{"code":"3.b","h":"How economic change creates winners and losers","t":"Global connections and globalisation drive structural economic change in places, such as de-industrialisation and the growth of the service industry, which reshapes patterns of social opportunity and inequality — often creating opportunities for workers with transferable skills while exacerbating inequality for those tied to a declining industry. Cyclical economic change, the shorter-term pattern of booms and recessions, has its own varied impacts on top of this longer-term structural shift. Government spending or cuts to services such as education, healthcare, infrastructure and community services can reduce, reinforce or create these patterns of inequality. A top-level answer traces a mechanism — globalisation to structural change to a specific impact on opportunity — rather than stating that economic change simply affects inequality."},{"code":"3.c","h":"Case studies of inequality: how it plays out for people","t":"Social inequality is not an abstract statistic: it shows up as evidence in a place — housing quality, environmental quality, crime rates and the digital divide — and it is experienced differently depending on factors such as income, gender, age, health, personal mobility, ethnicity and education. Two contrasting places, studied side by side, let you show how the same broad process of inequality produces very different daily realities for the people living in each. A top-level answer uses named evidence from two contrasting places together, in the same sentence or paragraph, rather than describing one place and then the other in isolation."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Players driving economic change: government, MNCs and institutions","t":"Economic change in a place is driven by a range of players operating at different scales, including local and national government, multinational companies (MNCs) and international institutions, each with different power, motivations and reach. A named case study of one country or region impacted by structural economic change should cover its characteristics before the change, the change or changes that took place and the role the players had in driving them, and the resulting socio-economic, demographic, cultural and environmental impacts on people and place. A top-level answer weighs the relative importance of different named players against each other, rather than simply listing which players were involved."},{"code":"5.a","h":"Making places: government, architects/planners and communities","t":"Placemaking happens through a variety of players working at different scales. Governments and organisations present places to the wider world to attract inward investment and regeneration; architects and planners try to create meaningful, authentic places through design choices such as encouraging mixed community use or a 24-hour city; and local community groups, such as residents associations, heritage associations and social media users, shape the place they live in from the bottom up. A top-level answer contrasts a top-down and a bottom-up example of placemaking and considers whose vision of the place is actually realised."},{"code":"5.b","h":"Rebranding: reimaging and regeneration","t":"Rebranding is the placemaking process through which places reimage and regenerate themselves to construct a different place meaning, often to reverse decline or attract investment. Strategies — sport, art, heritage, retail, architecture and food — can be used singularly or in combination, delivered by a range of players including government or EU funding, corporate bodies, not-for-profit organisations and community groups. Rebranding is frequently contested, since the costs and benefits of reimaging a place are not shared equally between residents, investors and visitors. A top-level answer explains contestation as arising from this uneven distribution of costs and benefits, not as places simply resisting change."},{"code":"5.c","h":"Case study: evaluating a successful rebrand","t":"A case study of one place that has undergone rebranding should be able to explain why the place needed to rebrand, the strategy or strategies used, the role and influence of the range of players involved, how the rebranding altered people's perception of the place, and the relative success of the rebranding. Judging 'success' means weighing measurable outcomes, such as investment or visitor numbers, against who actually benefited and who may have been excluded or displaced. A top-level answer reaches an explicit, evidenced judgement about how successful the rebrand was, rather than simply describing what was done."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading an Index of Multiple Deprivation decile","setup":"The English Indices of Deprivation rank every small neighbourhood in the country on income, employment, education, health, crime, housing and living environment, then cut that ranking into ten equal deciles. Parts of Toxteth, in inner-city Liverpool, fall in decile 1 — the most deprived 10% of small areas nationally — while Lympstone, an affluent Devon village, falls in decile 10, the least deprived 10%. Explain what this comparison shows about spatial inequality between the two places.","steps":["Decile 1 is a rank against every other neighbourhood in England, not a mark out of ten: it places those Toxteth neighbourhoods in the worst-off tenth of the country once the domains are combined.","Lympstone's decile 10 comes from exactly that same national ranking, so the two figures are directly comparable — one measure read at its two ends, rather than two unrelated local statistics.","Both places sit inside the same country, economy and welfare system, so the distance between them cannot be explained by national conditions; it is produced at the local scale by the contrasting fortunes of an inner-city dock area and a rural commuter village.","The comparison therefore shows that deprivation in England is spatially patterned: two places a few hundred miles apart occupy opposite ends of one national ranking, decile 1 against decile 10, and that gap in decile is itself the evidence of inequality between places."],"cues":["What does the number in 'decile 1' actually measure?","Can the two deciles be compared directly, and why?","Both are in one country — so where does the gap come from?","What does the pair of deciles show about inequality?"]},{"title":"Calculating population change — Detroit","setup":"Detroit's population peaked at approximately 1.85 million in 1950, when the city was the heart of the US motor industry. By the 2020 census it had fallen to approximately 640,000. Calculate the percentage change in Detroit's population between 1950 and 2020.","steps":["Absolute change = 640,000 − 1,850,000 = −1,210,000 people, the minus sign recording a loss rather than a gain.","Percentage change is always measured against the starting value, so the divisor is the 1950 peak of 1,850,000 — not the 2020 figure.","−1,210,000 ÷ 1,850,000 × 100 = −65.4%, which to the nearest whole number is −65%.","Detroit's population fell by approximately 65% between 1950 and 2020 — close to two-thirds of its people — quantifying the structural economic change that followed the decline of the motor industry rather than merely describing it."],"cues":["What is the raw change in people between the two censuses?","Which figure is the divisor here, and why that one?","What is that loss as a percentage of the 1950 peak?","State the percentage change — and what does it evidence?"]},{"title":"Comparing two contrasting places using a single indicator","setup":"Toxteth, an inner-city area of Liverpool shaped by the decline of dock-related industry, has an unemployment rate of approximately 8%. Lympstone, a small, affluent village on the Exe Estuary in Devon, has a rate of approximately 2%. The England average is about 4%. Compare the two places using this indicator.","steps":["Set each place against the national benchmark first: Toxteth's 8% is double the England average of 4%, while Lympstone's 2% is half of it.","Now compare the places with each other: 8% against 2% is a fourfold gap, so joblessness is four times as common in Toxteth as in Lympstone.","Because the two sit either side of the same national figure, neither is simply an oddity — the England average is itself the product of places at both extremes.","Compared directly, then, unemployment in Toxteth (about 8%) runs at roughly four times Lympstone's (about 2%) and twice the national rate, evidence that economic opportunity is distributed unevenly between places rather than spread evenly across the country."],"cues":["How does each rate stand against the England average?","How many times larger is one rate than the other?","What follows from the average sitting between the two?","State the comparison — what does the gap evidence?"]},{"title":"Decoding a 'to what extent' essay question","setup":"An exam question asks: 'To what extent has rebranding made a place successful?' Answer it for Liverpool, which was European Capital of Culture in 2008, opened the Liverpool ONE retail quarter in the same year, and whose culture-led year is estimated to have brought around £750 million into the local economy — while parts of Toxteth remain in the most deprived decile nationally. Plan an answer that reaches a top-level judgement.","steps":["'To what extent' asks how far, not whether, so the plan needs one line of argument carried from the opening paragraph to the conclusion, weighing evidence both ways.","Define the two terms the question turns on before arguing: rebranding as constructing a new place meaning through reimaging and regeneration, and success against stated criteria — economic gain, physical regeneration, and change in residents' conditions.","Build the supporting side from the case: the 2008 Capital of Culture year brought around £750 million into the local economy, and Liverpool ONE replaced derelict land with a retail quarter, changing how outsiders picture the city.","Build the limiting side from the same city: those gains cluster on the waterfront and in the centre, while Toxteth's place in the most deprived decile shows the rebrand has not reached every neighbourhood.","Judgement: rebranding has made Liverpool successful to a significant but partial extent — since 2008 it has transformed the city's external image and its central economy, but the persistence of deprivation in Toxteth shows it has redistributed opportunity within the city far less than it has changed perceptions of it."],"cues":["What does this command word demand of the answer?","Which two words must be defined before arguing?","Which Liverpool evidence supports the claim?","Which evidence in the same city cuts against it?","How far does the claim hold, and why only that far?"]},{"title":"Spotting bias in an informal representation of place","setup":"A tourist board photograph of Liverpool's Albert Dock foregrounds restored Victorian warehouses, waterfront cafés and clean public space. Nothing of the higher-deprivation areas a short distance inland, such as parts of Toxteth, appears in the frame. Explain what this photograph represents about Liverpool and how a geographer would test that representation.","steps":["Begin with the producer: a tourist board exists to draw visitors and inward investment, so the photograph is a promotional document with an interest in the answer, not a neutral record.","Then read the frame: restored warehouses, cafés and clean public space have been chosen and foregrounded, while the deprived areas inland, Toxteth among them, are cropped out entirely — selection, not falsification, is how the image works.","Test it against a formal representation: census and Indices of Deprivation data cover every neighbourhood on the same measures, including the ones the camera excluded, so they can confirm or contradict the picture the image gives.","The photograph therefore represents Liverpool as a regenerated heritage and leisure city — an informal, selective, interest-driven representation — and a geographer tests it by naming who produced it, what it foregrounds and omits, and setting it against formal statistical data for the same place, which is what AO3 analysis of a source asks for."],"cues":["Who made this image, and what do they want from it?","What is inside the frame, and what has been left out?","What kind of source covers what the camera missed?","What version of Liverpool is this, and how is it checked?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between 'space' and 'place'?","a":"Space is a physical, largely neutral location; place is space that has been given meaning and identity through human experience, memory and attachment."},{"q":"Give one indicator used to measure social inequality.","a":"Any one of housing, healthcare, education, employment or access to services."},{"q":"Name one strategy used to rebrand a place.","a":"Any one of sport, art, heritage, retail, architecture or food, used singularly or together."},{"q":"What does 'time-space compression' mean?","a":"The sense that the world feels smaller and more connected because faster transport and communication technology reduce the time it takes to travel or communicate between places."}]},"room":{"facts":["Social inequality is measured through indices including housing, healthcare, education, employment and access to services.","The main players that drive economic change and placemaking include local and national government, MNCs, international institutions, not-for-profit organisations and community groups.","Rebranding strategies include sport, art, heritage, retail, architecture and food, used singularly or in combination.","Structural economic change (such as deindustrialisation and the rise of the service sector) is a long-term shift; cyclical economic change (booms and recessions) is shorter-term.","A place's identity is shaped by its demographic, socio-economic, cultural, political, built and natural characteristics, plus its past and present connections across scales."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify three characteristics, other than economic ones, that can shape a place's identity.","a":"Any three of: demographic, cultural, political, built or natural characteristics.","sol":"A place's identity is shaped by more than its economy. Three accepted characteristics are its demographic profile (such as age structure or ethnic mix), its built characteristics (such as its architecture or street layout) and its cultural characteristics (such as local traditions); natural characteristics (such as its landscape or coastline) are also accepted, as is its political character (such as which party controls the local council). Any three named characteristics, each correctly categorised, gain full marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look back at the list of characteristics the specification uses to describe what shapes a place's identity, besides the economy.","Sort the list into named categories rather than describing a single example in detail.","Give three short category names, one phrase each; no further explanation is needed at this tariff."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify how formal representations of a place, such as census data, differ from informal representations, such as a blog.","a":"Formal representations are statistical and systematically collected; informal representations are subjective, selective and created through personal or creative media.","sol":"Formal representations of place, such as census or geospatial data, are collected systematically using standardised methods and are presented as statistics or maps. Informal representations, such as a blog, are created by an individual or organisation, are selective in what they show and reflect a personal or creative viewpoint rather than a standardised measurement. The key difference is therefore that one is a systematic, quantitative record and the other is a subjective, qualitative construction of place.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the two broad categories of representation this key idea contrasts.","Consider how the way each type of source is produced might affect how objective or selective it ends up being.","Give one clear point of contrast in a sentence; no examples beyond those in the question are required."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"Identify three indices used to measure social inequality.","a":"Any three of: housing, healthcare, education, employment or access to services.","sol":"Social inequality can be measured through indices covering housing (such as overcrowding or tenure), healthcare (such as life expectancy or access to GP services), education (such as qualification levels), employment (such as unemployment rate) and access to services (such as distance to amenities). Any three named indices gain full marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the specification's list of indices used to measure social inequality.","Name broad categories of measurement, not specific numerical figures.","Give three single-word or short-phrase indices; no further detail is required."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Identify three strategies used to rebrand a place.","a":"Any three of: sport, art, heritage, retail, architecture or food.","sol":"Places can rebrand using a range of strategies, including sport (such as hosting a major event), art (such as public art installations or festivals), heritage (such as restoring historic buildings), retail (such as a new shopping quarter), architecture (such as flagship new buildings) and food (such as a food festival or quarter). Any three named strategies gain full marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Recall the specification's list of ways a place can be rebranded.","Name the categories of strategy rather than a single named example.","Give three short named strategies; no explanation of how they work is required."],"codes":["5.b"]},{"q":"Explain how shifting flows of people and investment can change a place's socio-economic profile over time.","a":"In-migration or commuting changes population and skills; investment (e.g. TNCs, EU funding) creates jobs and raises income or property value; flows moving away can cause the reverse.","sol":"Places change over time as flows move through them rather than staying fixed. A flow of migrants or commuters into a place brings new skills, labour and demand for housing and services, which can raise average income and shift the socio-economic profile upward if better-paid workers move in, or increase pressure on services if the in-flow outpaces them. Flows of investment, such as a TNC opening a new facility or a place receiving EU regeneration funding, create jobs and can raise local wages and property values, further changing who lives there and what the place can afford. Where these flows instead move away — for example when investment or people leave for opportunities elsewhere — the reverse happens: employment and income can fall and the socio-economic profile can decline.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the categories of flow this key idea lists — people, resources, money/investment and ideas.","For one flow, trace what happens step by step from the flow arriving (or leaving) through to a change in income, employment or housing.","Write this as one developed explanation: choose one flow, then follow its effect through a short chain of two or three linked steps. A top-level answer names a specific type of flow and traces its effect through to a change in the socio-economic profile, rather than describing flows in general terms."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Explain how emotional attachment to a place can influence people's behaviour and activities.","a":"Strong attachment can encourage people to stay, invest in or protect a place; weak attachment can lead to disengagement or a willingness to leave.","sol":"People's level of emotional attachment to a place affects what they do within it. Where someone feels a strong attachment — built through long residence, family ties or shared memories — they are more likely to stay in the place even when better opportunities exist elsewhere, to invest time or money in it (such as volunteering or setting up a local business) and to resist changes that threaten what they value about it, such as opposing a development. Where attachment is weak, for example among a resident who has recently moved for work and has few local ties, they are more likely to disengage from local activities, feel comfortable leaving if opportunities arise, or show less concern about changes to the area.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what this key idea says emotional attachment can influence.","Consider both a strong-attachment behaviour and a weak-attachment behaviour, and what causes each.","Structure the answer as two linked points: one behaviour explained by strong attachment, one by weak attachment. A top-level answer links a specific level of attachment to a specific resulting behaviour, rather than asserting that attachment simply exists."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"The 2019 Index of Multiple Deprivation places the most deprived neighbourhood in Toxteth, Liverpool, in decile 1 (the most deprived 10% of areas in England). The equivalent area covering Lympstone, Devon, falls in one of the least deprived deciles nationally. Analyse what this data shows about the spatial pattern of social inequality between the two places.","a":"Decile 1 means the most deprived 10% nationally; a high decile for Lympstone means it is among the least deprived; the wide gap between the deciles is quantifiable evidence of large spatial inequality between two English places.","sol":"Decile 1 on the Index of Multiple Deprivation means that neighbourhood ranks among the most deprived 10% of all small areas in England, so Toxteth's most deprived neighbourhood is among the most disadvantaged places nationally on this measure. Lympstone's much lower deprivation ranking (a high decile) means it sits among the least deprived areas in the country. The gap between the two deciles is therefore not a small difference but spans nearly the full national range, which is strong quantitative evidence that social inequality is spatially very unevenly distributed — Toxteth (in the North West) and Lympstone (in the South West), roughly 330 km apart in different regions, show starkly different levels of relative deprivation. This supports the specification's point that spatial patterns of inequality vary both within and between places, since the same national index places these two English places at opposite ends of the scale.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall how the Index of Multiple Deprivation groups small areas into deciles.","Work out what decile 1 means, then compare that to what a high decile means, before commenting on the size of the gap between them.","Write this as data-first analysis: state what each decile shows, then explain what the size of the gap between them tells you about spatial inequality."],"codes":["3.a","3.c"]},{"q":"Explain why government spending or cuts can reduce or reinforce social inequality in places.","a":"Spending on education, healthcare, infrastructure or community services can improve opportunity and reduce inequality; cuts to the same services can widen inequality, especially where places rely most on state provision.","sol":"Government decisions about spending directly affect the services that shape social inequality. Where government increases spending on education, healthcare, infrastructure or community services in a disadvantaged place, it can improve opportunities — for example, better-funded schools can raise qualification levels, which in turn improves employment prospects, reducing inequality relative to more advantaged places. Where government cuts the same services, the effect can be reversed: reduced funding for healthcare or community services falls hardest on places that rely most on state provision because residents cannot easily pay for private alternatives, so cuts reinforce or even create new inequality between that place and wealthier places with more private options available.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the specific services this key idea names as being affected by government spending or cuts.","Consider the mechanism separately for spending, which narrows a gap, and for cuts, which can widen it.","Structure the answer as two linked points: what spending does, and what cuts do, each explained through to an effect on inequality."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Using your case study of a country or region affected by structural economic change, evaluate the relative importance of the different players involved in driving that change.","a":"Named case study (e.g. Detroit); identify at least two players (e.g. car manufacturers/MNCs, city/state government); weigh their relative power; reach a judgement on which mattered most.","sol":"Economic change in a place is driven by a range of players operating at different scales, including local and national government, multinational companies (MNCs) and international institutions, and their relative importance can be judged by whether each caused the change or mainly responded to it. In Detroit, Michigan, the 'Big Three' car manufacturers (Ford, General Motors and Chrysler) were arguably the most powerful player: their decisions to automate production and relocate manufacturing away from the city from the 1950s onwards directly caused the job losses that triggered Detroit's decline, and as private companies operating globally they were not bound to protect the city's economy, so this was a cause rather than a response. City and state government played an important but more reactive role: facing decades of a shrinking tax base as residents and jobs left, the State of Michigan appointed Kevyn Orr as the city's emergency manager in March 2013 to take control of its finances, and four months later, in July 2013, Orr filed the largest municipal bankruptcy petition in US history under Chapter 9 on the city's behalf — government intervention here was managing the consequences of the manufacturers' decisions rather than driving the original change, even though appointing an emergency manager who could override elected local government was itself a decisive exercise of power once government did act. Community and not-for-profit players, such as urban agriculture groups repurposing vacant land, have had a real but much smaller-scale influence, focused on adapting to change rather than causing or reversing it, since they lack the capital or legal authority the other two players hold. Weighing these three against each other: the manufacturers set the direction of change through decisions made decades before government was forced to respond; government then exercised the greatest formal power once it finally acted, restructuring the city's finances in ways no other player could; and community players have had the least influence over the change itself, however important their role in adapting to its aftermath. On balance, the manufacturers were the most important players in driving the original structural change, because their global relocation decisions set the direction that government and community players subsequently had to respond to, even though the state's emergency-manager intervention shows government could still exercise decisive power once change was under way.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about the different categories of player this key idea lists, and which ones feature in your named case study region.","For each player, consider whether their role caused the change or mainly responded to it, then weigh which mattered more.","Write this as a full extended response: a brief definition, developed paragraphs on two or three players each with named evidence, and a conclusion that takes a clear position on which mattered most."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Explain why some groups contest efforts to rebrand a place.","a":"Rebranding can raise living costs and gentrify an area, change its character, or benefit investors and visitors more than existing lower-income residents.","sol":"Rebranding is contested because it does not benefit everyone in a place equally. Strategies such as new retail or upgraded architecture often raise property prices and the cost of living in the area, a process known as gentrification, which can price out existing lower-income residents even while attracting wealthier newcomers and visitors. Rebranding can also change the character of a place in ways that clash with the identity or heritage valued by long-term residents, for example replacing informal, community-used spaces with commercialised ones. Because the benefits of rebranding — investment, tourism revenue, an improved image — tend to flow most directly to businesses, investors and incoming residents rather than to existing lower-income communities, some groups organise to contest the changes, for example through community campaigns opposing a specific development.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about who gains and who might lose out when a place rebrands.","Consider that residents might object for more than one kind of reason, not just one.","Structure the answer as two linked reasons, each explained through to why a group would actively oppose the change."],"codes":["5.b"]},{"q":"'Structural economic change creates more losers than winners.' To what extent do you agree?","a":"Losers include those tied to a declining industry, facing job losses and falling income (e.g. Detroit); winners include higher-skilled workers and places that attract new investment; a qualified conclusion should weigh both.","sol":"Structural economic change, such as deindustrialisation and the growth of the service sector, undeniably creates losers. In Detroit, the decline of the motor industry from the 1950s onwards removed tens of thousands of well-paid manufacturing jobs; workers without the skills or means to move into the growing but lower-paid service sector experienced falling income, and the city's population fell from approximately 1.85 million in 1950 to approximately 640,000 by 2020 as people left in search of work elsewhere, leaving behind rising unemployment, vacant housing and a shrinking tax base that further cut the public services residents depended on. This pattern of losing manufacturing employment and gaining precarious or lower-paid service work is repeated wherever structural change hits an economy built around a single declining industry, which is why the specification links structural change directly to exacerbating social inequality. However, structural change does not create only losers. Workers with transferable or higher-level skills can move into the growing service and knowledge economy and see rising incomes, and some places use the land and buildings freed by industrial decline for new investment — in Detroit, downtown regeneration led by private investors has created new jobs in technology and services, even while many outer neighbourhoods have not shared in this recovery. Government intervention can also convert some of what would otherwise be pure loss into opportunity, for example through retraining schemes or targeted regeneration spending, although this depends on political will and available funding. On balance, structural economic change does create more losers than winners, particularly among lower-skilled workers tied to the declining industry and in the short-to-medium term before any recovery takes hold, but the gains made by higher-skilled workers and by parts of a place that successfully repurpose old industrial land show that the change is not purely negative for everyone, so the extent of agreement should be qualified rather than total.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which groups of people this key idea says structural economic change creates opportunity for, and which it disadvantages.","Plan one or two paragraphs of case-study evidence for 'losers' and at least one paragraph of evidence for 'winners', before you write a conclusion.","Write this as a full extended response: definitions, developed paragraphs each with named evidence, and a conclusion that states how far you agree, not simply 'yes' or 'no'."],"codes":["3.b","3.c","4.a"]},{"q":"'The most important influence on the success of a rebranding project is the players involved, not the strategy chosen.' To what extent do you agree?","a":"Players (funding and co-ordination) determine whether a strategy can be resourced and delivered at scale, but the strategy chosen must also suit the place's existing assets; a qualified conclusion should weigh both.","sol":"Liverpool's rebranding as European Capital of Culture in 2008 supports the view that players are the most important influence on success. Liverpool City Council and the purpose-built Liverpool Culture Company provided the co-ordination needed to plan a year-long cultural programme, private developer Grosvenor delivered the Liverpool ONE retail and leisure quarter, and European Regional Development Fund money part-financed improvements to the historic waterfront around Albert Dock and the Pier Head. Because this combination of public co-ordination, private capital and EU funding could resource the rebrand at a scale a single organisation could not, an independent evaluation estimated the year generated approximately £750 million for the local economy and around 9.7 million additional visits — evidence that the players involved were able to convert the chosen strategy into a large, measurable success. However, the strategy itself was not incidental to this outcome. Liverpool's heritage-and-culture strategy suited a city with a genuine maritime and musical heritage to draw on; the same players applying a strategy that did not fit the place's existing assets would likely have struggled regardless of how well co-ordinated or funded they were. The rebrand was also contested by some residents who felt the benefits reached the regenerated waterfront and city centre more than poorer inner-city areas such as Toxteth, showing that even a well-resourced strategy delivered by capable players does not guarantee success is shared evenly. On balance, players are usually the more important influence, because they determine whether any strategy can be resourced, co-ordinated and delivered at the scale needed to succeed — but only where the strategy chosen genuinely fits the place, so the two are best understood as interdependent rather than one being decisive alone.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about the different kind of contribution players make to a rebrand compared with what the chosen strategy itself contributes.","Plan a paragraph showing players enabling success, a paragraph showing the strategy's own fit with the place still matters, and evidence of contestation to complicate the picture.","Write this as a full extended response with a definition of both terms, developed paragraphs with named evidence, and a conclusion that states how far you agree."],"codes":["5.a","5.b","5.c"]},{"q":"Explain how local community groups can shape the place they live in.","a":"Residents' associations, heritage associations and social media let communities protect valued features, organise campaigns or oppose unwanted development.","sol":"Local community groups shape places from the bottom up, working alongside or in place of official players such as government or developers. Residents' associations can organise to maintain or improve shared local spaces, such as campaigning for a park to be better maintained, or can formally object to planning applications they feel do not suit the area, giving ordinary residents a direct influence over what gets built. Heritage associations work to identify and protect buildings or features that give a place its distinctive identity, for example campaigning for a building to be listed so it cannot be demolished, which preserves the physical character that contributes to a place's meaning. Social media allows community groups to organise more quickly and reach a wider audience than before, for example mobilising support for or against a local issue, or promoting positive images of the area to counter negative external perceptions.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the specific types of local community group this key idea names.","For one type of group, consider what action they can take and what effect that action has on the place.","Structure the answer around one or two named types of group, each explained through to a specific effect on the place. A top-level answer explains a mechanism by which a named type of group changes what happens in a place, rather than simply asserting that communities have some influence."],"codes":["5.a"]},{"q":"'A place is shaped more by how it is represented than by what people actually experience living there.' How far do you agree?","a":"Representations shape outsiders' and investors' view of a place; lived experience and emotional attachment shape residents' own sense of place more strongly; the answer should distinguish whose sense of place is meant.","sol":"Representations of a place can powerfully shape how it is understood, particularly by people who do not live there. An informal representation, such as a film or a tourist board photograph that foregrounds a restored, attractive waterfront while omitting nearby areas of high deprivation, constructs a selective image that can influence whether outside investors, visitors or potential residents choose to engage with a place at all — this is precisely why rebranding strategies rely so heavily on managing representation rather than only changing physical conditions. Formal representations, such as census or deprivation data, can carry similar power in shaping how policymakers or investors judge a place, even though the data is more systematically collected than an informal image. However, for the people who actually live in a place, lived experience — shaped by daily flows of people, resources and money, and by personal emotional attachment built through routine, memory and social connection — arguably matters more in forming their own sense of place than any external representation. A resident's sense of place is grounded in tangible, repeated experience, such as knowing their neighbours or using local services, which an outsider's representation of the area, however widely seen, cannot override. On balance, external representation is the more powerful influence on how a place is perceived by those who do not live there and by the players who make investment decisions, but for residents themselves lived experience remains the stronger influence on their own sense of place, so the extent to which representation dominates depends on whose sense of place is being considered.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about the two things this question sets against each other — the ideas about representation and the ideas about identity and emotional attachment.","Consider whether one single answer can cover everyone, or whether your answer needs to be more precise about who is doing the perceiving.","Write this as a full extended response: definitions, developed paragraphs with named evidence weighed against each other, and a conclusion that takes a clear position."],"codes":["1.a","2.a","2.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Identify one demographic characteristic that can shape a place's identity.","a":"Any one of age structure, ethnicity or population size (or a similar demographic feature)."},{"q":"What is one way formal representations of place differ from informal ones?","a":"Formal representations, like census or geospatial data, are statistical and systematically collected, whereas informal representations, like film or blogs, are subjective and selective."},{"q":"Give one strategy that places use to rebrand.","a":"Any one of sport, art, heritage, retail, architecture or food."},{"q":"Name one player who can be involved in driving structural economic change.","a":"Any one of local government, national government, MNCs or international institutions."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Space vs place","back":"Space is a physical location; place is space given meaning and identity through human experience, memory and attachment.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Time-space compression","back":"The sense that distant places feel closer because faster transport and communication technology have reduced the time it takes to reach or contact them.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Formal vs informal representation","back":"Formal representations (e.g. census, geospatial data) are statistical and systematic; informal representations (e.g. film, art, blogs) are subjective and selectively construct a version of place.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Indices of social inequality","back":"Housing, healthcare, education, employment and access to services — the main indices used to measure and compare social inequality between places.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Structural vs cyclical economic change","back":"Structural change is a long-term shift, such as deindustrialisation and the rise of services; cyclical change is short-term, following booms and recessions.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Government's role in inequality","back":"Government spending or cuts to education, healthcare, infrastructure and community services can reduce, reinforce or create social inequality in places.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Evidence of social inequality","back":"Housing: overcrowded flats and terraces in inner-city Toxteth against detached homes in Lympstone; environmental quality and crime rates, typically worse in the most deprived deciles; the digital divide, where low-income households lack home broadband and children cannot work online.","code":"3.c","open":true},{"front":"Toxteth and Lympstone","back":"Two contrasting places: Toxteth, inner-city Liverpool, has high deprivation and dense housing; Lympstone, coastal Devon, is affluent, low-density and less deprived.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Players in economic change","back":"Local and national government, through planning, funding and cuts to services; MNCs, whose decisions to relocate or close plants moved jobs out of cities such as Detroit; international institutions such as the EU, whose structural funding supported regeneration on Merseyside.","code":"4.a","open":true},{"front":"Detroit's structural change","back":"Detroit's population fell from approximately 1.85 million in 1950 to approximately 640,000 by 2020 as its motor industry declined and jobs were lost.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Placemaking","back":"The process by which governments, planners, architects and communities create, design and present meaningful places, often to attract investment or regeneration.","code":"5.a"},{"front":"Rebranding strategies","back":"Sport, e.g. Manchester's 2002 Commonwealth Games; heritage and art, e.g. Liverpool's 2008 European Capital of Culture; retail and architecture, e.g. the Liverpool ONE quarter — used singly or together to reimage a place.","code":"5.b","open":true},{"front":"Contesting rebranding","back":"Some groups contest rebranding when it raises costs, changes an area's character or excludes existing residents, such as through gentrification.","code":"5.b"},{"front":"Liverpool 2008 Capital of Culture","back":"Liverpool's rebranding as European Capital of Culture in 2008 used heritage, art and retail (e.g. Liverpool ONE) and generated approximately £750 million for the local economy.","code":"5.c"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: ‘The most important influence on the success of a rebranding project is the players involved, not the strategy chosen.’ To what extent do you agree? (16 marks)","One of the strongest determinants of a rebranding project's success is the range of players who co-ordinate and fund it, because a strategy on its own cannot be delivered without organisations able to plan, finance and promote it. Liverpool's designation as European Capital of Culture in 2008 shows this: Liverpool City Council and the purpose-built Liverpool Culture Company co-ordinated the cultural programme, while private developer Grosvenor delivered the Liverpool ONE retail and leisure quarter and European Regional Development Fund money part-financed public realm improvements around the Pier Head and Albert Dock. Because these players combined public co-ordination, private capital and EU funding, the chosen strategy of using culture and heritage to rebrand the city could be delivered at a scale a strategy alone could not have achieved — an independent evaluation estimated the year generated approximately £750 million for the local economy and around 9.7 million additional visits. This suggests that without this specific combination of players, the same cultural strategy applied elsewhere would likely have achieved far less, so the players involved are at least as important as the strategy chosen."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: ‘The most important influence on the success of a rebranding project is the players involved, not the strategy chosen.’ To what extent do you agree? (16 marks)","Rebranding depends on players because they help make it happen. In Liverpool, the council and businesses worked together to rebrand the city in 2008 when it was Capital of Culture. They built new shops and fixed up the docks so people would want to visit. This brought in lots of visitors and money for Liverpool. So players are important for rebranding to be successful."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — contrasting place profiles: Toxteth, Liverpool and Lympstone, Devon","items":["Toxteth (Liverpool 8): an inner-city area close to Liverpool's historic docks, with a long-established, ethnically diverse population, including one of the UK's oldest Black communities.","Toxteth was the site of significant unrest in 1981 (the Toxteth riots), which triggered government-backed regeneration initiatives in the area.","Toxteth has undergone targeted regeneration, including the Welsh Streets housing renovation project and investment linked to Liverpool John Moores University and the health campuses of the city-centre Knowledge Quarter.","Lympstone: a small village on the Exe Estuary in East Devon, within commuting distance of Exeter, with a population of approximately 2,000.","Lympstone is home to the Commando Training Centre Royal Marines (CTCRM), a major employer and landmark in the village.","Lympstone's housing is predominantly owner-occupied and the village has a higher proportion of older residents than the national average, reflecting its popularity as a retirement and commuter location."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — social inequality in contrasting places: Toxteth and Lympstone","items":["On the Index of Multiple Deprivation, parts of Toxteth typically fall into decile 1, the most deprived 10% of small areas in England.","Lympstone typically falls into one of the least deprived deciles nationally on the same index.","Housing in parts of Toxteth includes a higher proportion of rented, higher-density terraced housing, compared with Lympstone's predominantly owner-occupied, lower-density housing.","Car ownership and access to services differ sharply between the two places: Lympstone's residents typically have higher car ownership, reflecting its more rural, higher-income population, while parts of Toxteth have lower car ownership and rely more on local public transport.","The two places illustrate how social inequality in housing, income and access to services can exist between two English places only a few hundred kilometres apart."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — structural economic change: Detroit, Michigan, USA","items":["Detroit grew into the centre of the US motor industry in the twentieth century, home to major manufacturers including Ford, General Motors and Chrysler, earning it the nickname 'Motor City'.","Detroit's population peaked at approximately 1.85 million in 1950.","By the 2020 census, Detroit's population had fallen to approximately 640,000, as automation and the relocation of manufacturing away from the city led to sustained job losses from the 1950s onwards.","In March 2013, the State of Michigan appointed Kevyn Orr as Detroit's emergency manager to take control of the city's finances; four months later, in July 2013, Orr filed for Chapter 9 bankruptcy on the city's behalf, the largest municipal bankruptcy in US history at the time.","Since the mid-2010s, downtown Detroit has seen renewed private investment in technology and services, while many outer residential neighbourhoods still show high vacancy and have been targeted for demolition of derelict housing."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — rebranding: Liverpool as European Capital of Culture, 2008","items":["Liverpool held the title of European Capital of Culture in 2008, using heritage, art, architecture and retail to rebrand the city's image.","Liverpool City Council and the purpose-built Liverpool Culture Company co-ordinated the year's cultural programme.","The Liverpool ONE retail and leisure development, delivered by private developer Grosvenor, opened in 2008 as part of the wider regeneration.","European Regional Development Fund (ERDF) money part-financed improvements to the historic waterfront, including around Albert Dock and the Pier Head.","An independent evaluation (the Impacts 08 study) estimated the Capital of Culture year generated approximately £750 million for the Liverpool economy and around 9.7 million additional visits.","The rebrand was contested by some residents and community groups who felt its benefits reached the regenerated waterfront and city centre more than poorer inner-city areas such as Toxteth."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Placemaking is driven more by top-down players than by local communities.' How far do you agree? (16 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'top-down players' means government, corporate bodies and international institutions; 'local communities' means residents associations, heritage associations and informal social media organising; 'how far' means the answer must reach a qualified judgement about the balance between the two, not simply describe both.","Paragraph 1 (establish the case for top-down players): explain how government and corporate funding, such as council co-ordination and private investment in a rebranding project, can resource placemaking at a scale no community group can match, using named evidence.","Paragraph 2 (develop the case for local communities): explain how residents associations, heritage associations and social media allow communities to shape a place from the bottom up, such as protecting a valued building or organising around a local issue, using named evidence.","Paragraph 3 (test the balance): explain a case where community action has changed or contested a top-down placemaking decision, to show local communities are not simply passive recipients of decisions made above them.","Evaluation thread: throughout, weigh the scale and speed of change each type of player can achieve against how directly it reflects what residents themselves want for the place.","Conclusion must: state a qualified judgement — top-down players usually drive the largest, most visible placemaking projects because they command the greatest resources, but local communities meaningfully shape and sometimes contest the outcome, so placemaking is best understood as an interaction between the two rather than one side alone driving it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.939Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All four faults from the first verdict are genuinely fixed: the five leaky hints are now scaffolding that points at where to look without naming the marking point (Q1 now asks the student to consider how each source is produced rather than supplying 'standardised collection'; Q6 asks only what deciles are, not what decile 1 means; Q9 and Q11 no longer list the reasons or the indicative content; Q13 now says 'be more precise about who is doing the perceiving' rather than handing over 'whose sense of place'); the Detroit chronology in both the fact file and questions[8].sol is now correct (State of Michigan appointed Kevyn Orr in March 2013, Orr filed the Chapter 9 petition in July 2013); questions[6].sol now correctly places Toxteth in the North West and Lympstone in the South West, roughly 330 km apart, consistent with the second inequality fact file; lesson.examples[2] now carries real hedged figures (Toxteth ~8%, Lympstone ~2%, England ~4%) so it models the skill it names; and every tariff is now the Human interactions (02) set of low-tariff, 6 and 16 (3/6/16, no 12-mark items). I re-solved all fourteen questions and found no wrong or ambiguous answer key. Facts check out: Detroit 1.85m (1950) to ~640k (2020 census) is a 65.4% fall as calculated; Impacts 08's ~GBP 750m and ~9.7m additional visits, Liverpool ONE/Grosvenor (2008), the Liverpool Culture Company and ERDF waterfront funding are all accurate; IMD decile mechanics (decile 1 = most deprived 10%, a rank not a score) are right; place details (CTCRM at Lympstone, Toxteth 1981 unrest, the Welsh Streets) are correct and figures are hedged with 'approximately'. All ten key ideas have a faithful lesson section closing on what a top-level answer does, all four required case studies have fact files, the two model paragraphs are truly at Level 4 and Level 2 and correctly labelled for OCR's four-level 16-mark scheme, and the essay plan is in a board command word ('How far') at the right tariff. Minor polish only: questions[6].sol's clause 'starkly different levels of relative deprivation regardless of that distance' reads oddly, since the distance is not what the comparison turns on; lesson.why says the paper sets 'six-mark' questions where the specification says 6 to 10; the exit ticket's first item uses the stem 'State', which is not in the command-word list extracted for this course; and 'nearby Liverpool John Moores University and health campuses' in the first fact file stretches 'nearby' slightly (both sit in the city-centre Knowledge Quarter)."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:07:20.519Z"}},"2.2.1":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"2.2.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room matters because trade is one of two options within Global Systems (2.2.1) on the Human interactions paper, and Section 3a confirms this paper mixes short answer questions, 6 to 10 mark questions and 16-mark extended response questions, so this topic is examined at every tariff up to a full essay. It also supplies the case-study material — an AC, an EDC and an LIDC — that can be drawn into synoptic Geographical debates answers, so learning the case studies precisely pays off beyond this topic's own questions.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Merchandise, services and capital: the three flows of trade","t":"International trade takes three forms: merchandise (trade in tangible physical goods, such as machinery or clothing), services (trade in intangible activities, such as finance, insurance, tourism or IT), and capital (flows of money for investment, such as foreign direct investment, portfolio investment and remittances). Global trade patterns include both inter-regional trade, between different world regions such as the EU and East Asia, and intra-regional trade, within a single region such as ASEAN or the EU. A top-level answer names the type of flow precisely and locates it correctly as inter-regional or intra-regional, rather than describing 'trade' as one undifferentiated flow."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Trade and development: promoting growth, and creating inequality","t":"Patterns of trade correlate with socio-economic development: countries with a higher value of exports tend to have a higher Human Development Index (HDI), though the relationship is not perfectly linear. Trade can promote stability, growth and development through flows of people (skilled migration), money (investment), ideas (knowledge transfer) and technology (new production methods). The same flows can also cause inequalities, conflicts and injustices, for example when the gains from trade are captured unevenly, or when reliance on a single export commodity leaves an economy exposed to price shocks. A top-level answer uses these same four flows to argue both sides, rather than treating development and inequality as separate topics."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Why access to global markets has become more complex","t":"Access to markets in the 21st century is shaped by several inter-related factors: improvements in technology, transport and communications have deepened the connectivity of global supply chains; multinational corporations (MNCs) have grown more influential in EDCs, including through outsourcing; regional trading blocs, such as the EU, shape which countries trade most easily with each other; 'south-south' trade between developing countries has grown; services have grown as a share of the global economy; and labour mobility has increased alongside a new international division of labour. A top-level answer explains how two or more of these factors interact, rather than listing them separately."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Interdependence: the EDC case study","t":"An EDC such as China shows how a rapidly industrialising economy becomes interdependent with its trading partners: the direction and components of its trade, how these have changed over time (for example, moving from low-value manufacturing towards higher-value electronics), and its economic, political, social and environmental interdependence with the partners it trades with. Trade brings impacts including economic development, but also risks to political stability, for example through trade disputes, and to social equality, for example uneven regional development within the country. A top-level answer uses specific, dated evidence from the named EDC to trace change over time, rather than describing its trade as static."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Unequal power: the AC and LIDC case studies","t":"Trade reflects unequal power relations between countries. A core economy, an AC such as the USA, uses its economic, political and social influence to shape trade to its advantage, through its trade patterns, partners, negotiations and agreements; this brings opportunities such as sustained economic growth, alongside challenges such as a persistent trade deficit. A peripheral economy, an LIDC such as Ethiopia, has far more limited influence and can mainly only respond to changes made elsewhere; it faces limited access to global markets, though trade also creates opportunities such as diversifying its economic activity, alongside challenges such as political instability. A top-level answer compares the two case studies directly, rather than describing each in isolation."}],"examples":[{"title":"Classifying a flow as merchandise, services or capital","setup":"Three international transactions need classifying as merchandise, services or capital trade: (1) Boeing selling an aircraft built in Seattle to an airline in Japan; (2) a UK insurance company selling a policy to a firm based in Germany; (3) a US pension fund buying shares in an Indian technology company. Classify each one, and say which of them a country's trade balance would record.","steps":["Transaction 1 is merchandise trade: the aircraft is a tangible physical good, built in one country and delivered across a border to a buyer in another.","Transaction 2 is services trade: insurance cover is intangible, so nothing physical is shipped — what crosses the border is an activity sold by a UK firm to a German one.","Transaction 3 is neither: the pension fund is not buying a good or an activity but an ownership stake, so the money moving to India is a capital flow.","So the trio sorts into one merchandise flow, one services flow and one capital flow — and only the first two count as trade in the trade balance, since transaction 3 is investment and is recorded as a capital movement instead."],"cues":["Is an aircraft a good, an activity, or an investment?","Does an insurance policy send anything physical across the border?","Is buying foreign shares trade at all, or something else?","Which of the three would a trade balance actually record?"]},{"title":"Calculating an overall trade balance from its goods and services components","setup":"In a recent year the USA's goods trade deficit was approximately $1,000 billion — imports of goods exceeded exports of goods by that much — while in the same year its services trade surplus was approximately $250 billion, services exports exceeding services imports. Find the USA's overall trade balance for goods and services combined.","steps":["The two components pull opposite ways: the $250 billion services surplus is value coming in, the $1,000 billion goods deficit value going out, so the surplus can only cancel part of the deficit.","Netting them off: $1,000 billion deficit − $250 billion surplus = $750 billion.","The goods deficit is four times the size of the services surplus, so the combined figure cannot cross into positive territory — the sign stays negative.","The USA's overall trade balance for goods and services combined is therefore a deficit of approximately $750 billion."],"cues":["Do the two components push the balance the same way?","What is left once the surplus is netted off the deficit?","Which component is larger, and what does that settle?","State the combined balance: surplus or deficit, and how much?"]},{"title":"Linking export value to HDI to show the trade-development relationship","setup":"China's merchandise export value has grown rapidly since the 1990s, and over the same period its HDI has climbed out of the 'medium' band into the 'high' band, where it now stands at roughly 0.79. Ethiopia's export value is far lower and dominated by a single commodity, coffee, and its HDI sits in the 'low' band. Assess what this pairing shows about the relationship between trade and development.","steps":["Take China first: export value and HDI have climbed together since the 1990s, so the country with the larger, more diverse export trade is also the one that has moved up an HDI band.","Ethiopia sits at the other end of both scales at once — low export value, narrow export base, low HDI band — so the two cases point the same way rather than contradicting each other.","Two cases pointing the same way give a correlation, not a cause: HDI also rises with government spending on schooling and healthcare, and a country can export heavily while the earnings reach few of its people.","Assessment: the pairing shows trade and development are strongly associated, and that what a country exports matters as much as how much — Ethiopia's reliance on coffee leaves its earnings at the mercy of one world price — but two countries cannot show that trade alone raises HDI, so trade is best judged a powerful contributing factor rather than the cause."],"cues":["What has China's HDI done as its export value grew?","Does Ethiopia sit the same way round on both measures?","What else, besides trade, lifts a country's HDI?","What does the pairing show — and what can it not show?"]},{"title":"Tracing outsourcing through a global supply chain","setup":"A US technology MNC designs a smartphone at its headquarters in California, an AC, but manufactures none of it there. Explain how the stages of making this phone are outsourced to other countries and coordinated across them.","steps":["Component manufacturing goes to where wages are low and supplier clusters are dense: to firms in China and elsewhere in East and Southeast Asia, EDC regions where labour costs a fraction of Californian rates.","Final assembly is then placed with a second EDC, such as Vietnam or India, so that a strike, tariff or shutdown in one country cannot stop the whole chain.","The highest-value stages — design, software and branding — never leave California, so profit stays in the AC while labour-intensive stages sit in EDCs: the new international division of labour in one product.","Holding it together are container shipping, supplier tracking software and instant communications, which let stages spread across three or more countries be sequenced within weeks — without them, a chain this dispersed would be too slow and costly to run."],"cues":["Where do the labour-intensive stages go, and why there?","Why not put every factory in a single country?","Which stage never leaves California, and who profits?","What makes stages in different countries work as one chain?"]},{"title":"Weighing an AC's opportunities and challenges from trade","setup":"The USA's openness to trade keeps consumer prices low and gives its exporters access to large foreign markets under agreements such as USMCA, in force since 2020. That same openness means it has bought in more goods than it has sold abroad for decades: in the early 2020s it exported roughly $2 trillion of goods a year while importing roughly $3 trillion. Weigh up the opportunities and the challenges this creates for the USA.","steps":["The opportunity side: cheap imports hold down prices in American shops, while USMCA gives US exporters near tariff-free access to Canada and Mexico — market access on that scale is what sustains growth in US export industries.","The challenge side: the two goods flows do not balance — roughly $3 trillion in against roughly $2 trillion out leaves a goods deficit of about $1 trillion a year, a gap that has persisted for decades rather than a one-off.","That deficit figure alone does not show the USA losing at trade: the same openness earns it a surplus on services and draws in capital investment, neither of which appears in a goods balance.","Weighing them: openness buys the USA cheap imports, guaranteed market access under USMCA and the standing of a core economy that negotiates the rules of trade, at the price of a persistent goods deficit of about $1 trillion a year. Because that deficit is offset by service exports and financed by capital flowing in, the opportunities outweigh the challenges — the deficit is a cost of the USA's central position, not proof that the system works against it."],"cues":["What does openness give US shoppers and US exporters?","What is the gap between the two goods flows worth?","Does a goods deficit on its own prove the USA loses out?","On balance, which side wins for the USA — and why?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between merchandise and services in international trade?","a":"Merchandise is trade in tangible physical goods; services is trade in intangible activities such as finance or tourism."},{"q":"Name the trade bloc that connects the USA, Mexico and Canada.","a":"USMCA, the United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement."},{"q":"Which country is used in this room as the case study of an LIDC?","a":"Ethiopia."},{"q":"What does 'south-south' trade mean?","a":"Trade between developing countries, rather than between developing and developed countries."}]},"room":{"facts":["Merchandise = trade in tangible physical goods; services = trade in intangible activities; capital = flows of money for investment, such as FDI, portfolio investment and remittances.","Trade balance = value of exports minus value of imports; a trade deficit is when imports exceed exports, a trade surplus is when exports exceed imports.","China joined the World Trade Organization (WTO) in 2001, opening its access to global export markets.","The USA, Mexico and Canada trade under USMCA (in force from 2020), which replaced NAFTA.","Ethiopia is landlocked and relies on the port of Djibouti for approximately 95% of its trade.","China's export-led manufacturing embodies approximately a quarter of its carbon emissions, and its growing raw material imports drive resource extraction in exporting countries."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify two components of international trade, other than merchandise.","a":"Services and capital.","sol":"Alongside merchandise (trade in tangible physical goods), international trade also has a services component (trade in intangible activities such as finance, insurance or tourism) and a capital component (flows of money for investment, such as foreign direct investment, portfolio investment and remittances).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the three terms the specification uses to break international trade into its component parts.","Both are named in the same key idea as merchandise; think about what else, besides physical goods, can cross a border in trade.","Two short named terms are enough at this tariff; no definition is required unless the question asks for one."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"In a recent year, the USA's approximate goods trade deficit was $1,000 billion and its approximate services trade surplus was $250 billion. Calculate the USA's approximate overall trade deficit for that year.","a":"Approximately $750 billion.","sol":"Overall trade balance = goods balance + services balance. The goods deficit of $1,000 billion is partly offset by the services surplus of $250 billion: $1,000 billion minus $250 billion gives an approximate overall trade deficit of $750 billion.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Remember that a country's overall trade position combines its goods trade and its services trade, not just one of them.","A surplus in one component works in the opposite direction to a deficit in the other when you combine them.","Subtract the smaller figure from the larger one to reach a single overall value."],"codes":["1.a","3.a"]},{"q":"Identify one national indicator, other than value of exports, that geographers use to compare patterns of trade with socio-economic development.","a":"Human Development Index (HDI).","sol":"The Human Development Index (HDI), which combines life expectancy, education and income into a single score, is used alongside value of exports to compare a country's trade with its socio-economic development.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think of the composite index that geographers use to measure development more broadly than income alone.","It combines health, education and income data into one figure for each country.","Give the full name of the index, spelling out what each letter of its abbreviation stands for."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Identify one regional trading bloc of European member states whose growing influence shapes market access for its members.","a":"The EU (European Union).","sol":"The EU (European Union) is a regional trading bloc of European member states whose growing influence shapes access to markets for its member countries.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the group of neighbouring countries that trade under shared rules, named directly in this key idea's content.","It is a bloc, not a single country — think of the group of neighbouring states whose shared rules on trade come up elsewhere in this topic.","Give its name in full or as its usual abbreviation; either is acceptable."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain two ways in which changes in technology, transport and communications have increased the connectivity of global supply chains.","a":"Any two of: faster/cheaper shipping and logistics; digital communication and tracking co-ordinating production across countries; e-commerce connecting buyers and sellers directly.","sol":"Improvements in shipping and logistics, such as containerisation, have cut the cost and time of moving components and finished goods between countries, letting a supply chain be split across several sites without a large cost penalty. Improvements in digital communications, such as real-time tracking and video conferencing, let firms co-ordinate design, ordering and delivery across time zones almost instantly, so a supply chain can be managed as one system even when its stages are in different countries.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what has changed since the late twentieth century in how goods physically move and how firms communicate across borders.","Choose one change in physical transport and one change in communication or information technology, and explain what each lets firms do differently.","Develop each point into two or three linked sentences showing cause and effect, rather than naming the change alone."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how international trade can promote development within a country, using named flows.","a":"Flows of money (investment), ideas/technology (knowledge transfer) or people (skilled migration) raise productivity, incomes or infrastructure.","sol":"Trade can promote development through the same flows that make up trade itself. Flows of money, such as foreign direct investment, fund new factories, infrastructure and jobs. Flows of technology and ideas, such as production methods transferred by a multinational corporation to a local supplier, raise productivity in the receiving country. Flows of people, such as skilled migrants moving for trade-related work, can bring expertise home again through remittances and returning migration. Together, these flows can raise incomes, employment and infrastructure quality in the country receiving them.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Return to the four types of flow this key idea uses to link trade with development.","Pick two of the four and explain the mechanism by which each one raises incomes, jobs or infrastructure.","State the flow, then explain the chain of cause and effect it sets off, rather than simply asserting that trade 'helps'."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"The table shows the approximate share of China's merchandise exports by destination in a recent year: ASEAN approximately 16%; European Union approximately 15%; United States approximately 15%; rest of world (including Hong Kong, Japan and South Korea) approximately 54%. Calculate the combined share of China's exports going to these three named partners, and use your answer to describe the pattern of intra-regional and inter-regional trade shown.","a":"Combined share is approximately 46%; over half of China's exports (approximately 54%) go beyond these three partners, showing exports spread across many inter-regional and intra-regional destinations rather than concentrated on a few.","sol":"16% + 15% + 15% gives an approximate combined share of 46% of China's exports going to ASEAN, the EU and the USA. This shows a mixed pattern: trade with ASEAN is intra-regional (within East and Southeast Asia), while trade with the EU and USA is inter-regional (crossing into different world regions). Because the combined share of these three named partners is under half, the remaining approximate 54% (including Hong Kong, Japan and South Korea, and many smaller markets) shows that China's export market is highly diversified rather than dependent on a small number of trading partners.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Read the table as showing both a calculation to complete and a pattern to describe in terms of intra- and inter-regional trade.","Add the three given percentages together first, then compare your total with the 'rest of world' figure to judge how concentrated or spread out the trade is.","Give your working as a single addition, then look at each of the three named partners in turn and decide whether trade with it falls within China's own world region or crosses into a different one, before describing the overall spread."],"codes":["1.a","2.b"]},{"q":"Using the case study of an EDC you have studied, explain how its international trade patterns have changed over time.","a":"e.g. China: shift from low-value manufacturing (textiles, toys) towards higher-value electronics and advanced manufacturing; growth of a large trade surplus; WTO membership from 2001 widening market access.","sol":"China's trade has changed markedly since it joined the World Trade Organization in 2001, which gave it far wider and more secure access to global export markets. In the 1990s and 2000s, its exports were dominated by low-value, labour-intensive manufactured goods such as textiles, toys and basic electronics assembly. Since the 2010s, China has moved up the value chain towards higher-value exports, including advanced electronics, machinery and, more recently, electric vehicles and solar panels, while also becoming a much larger importer of raw materials such as crude oil and iron ore to supply its own manufacturing. This shift has been accompanied by a large and sustained trade surplus, which has itself become a source of tension with trading partners such as the USA.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Think about your EDC case study across two clearly different time periods, not just its trade pattern today.","Identify what kind of goods it exported earlier on, and contrast this with what it exports now, plus what has happened to its overall trade balance.","Organise your answer as 'then... now...', naming a turning point or approximate date where your case study allows it."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"Using the case study of an AC you have studied, explain how a core economy uses its influence to drive change in the global trade system to its own advantage.","a":"e.g. USA: negotiates trade agreements on favourable terms (USMCA), uses the scale of its market and currency to set terms, uses tariffs to protect or reshape sectors.","sol":"The USA, as a core economy, shapes the global trade system through the scale of its market and its ability to set the terms of negotiation. It has renegotiated regional trade agreements, such as replacing NAFTA with USMCA in 2020, to secure terms it judged more favourable to US industries including manufacturing and agriculture. Its currency, the US dollar, is used to price and settle a large share of world trade, giving the USA additional influence over global financial and trade conditions that peripheral economies do not have. The USA has also used tariffs, such as those imposed on Chinese goods from 2018, to try to protect domestic industries and pressure trading partners into new terms, showing its ability to act to shape the system rather than simply respond to it.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what marks a core economy out from a peripheral one in how much say it has over the rules of trade, not just how much it trades.","Choose two mechanisms your AC case study has used, such as negotiating agreements on its own terms or using tariffs, and explain what each lets it achieve.","Name a specific agreement, policy or date from your case study for each mechanism, rather than describing its influence in general terms."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Using the case study of an LIDC you have studied, explain why it has limited access to global markets.","a":"e.g. Ethiopia: landlocked, reliant on Djibouti's port; narrow export base (coffee) vulnerable to price swings; political instability disrupting trade.","sol":"Ethiopia's limited access to global markets stems from several linked factors. It is landlocked, having lost its coastline when Eritrea became independent in 1993, so it depends on neighbouring Djibouti's port for the great majority of its trade, adding transport cost, time and vulnerability to disruption beyond its own control. Its exports remain heavily concentrated on a small number of primary commodities, above all coffee, so it has limited capital or infrastructure to process goods into higher-value manufactured exports that would earn more in global markets. Periods of internal political instability, such as the conflict in Tigray from 2020, have further disrupted production, transport and investor confidence, making trading partners more cautious about relying on Ethiopia as a supplier.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Consider your LIDC case study's geography, its export base and its recent political history, not just one of these on its own.","Explain how being landlocked, relying on a narrow range of exports, and periods of instability each separately reduce its access to markets.","Link each factor you choose to a specific consequence for market access, rather than listing the factors alone."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"To what extent has the growth of 'south-south' trade changed the global trade system?","a":"Growth of trade between developing countries (e.g. China-Africa investment) reduces some dependence on ACs, but ACs/MNCs still dominate high-value trade, finance and trade rules, so change is real but partial.","sol":"The growth of 'south-south' trade, between developing countries, has genuinely changed parts of the global trade system: China has become a major trading partner and investor across sub-Saharan Africa, including in Ethiopia, reducing some LIDCs' historic dependence on trade with advanced core economies. This has given some EDCs and LIDCs alternative markets and sources of investment outside the traditional core. However, the change should not be overstated: advanced economies and their multinational corporations still dominate the highest-value stages of global supply chains, control most global financial flows, and continue to shape many of the rules of world trade through international institutions. South-south trade has therefore diversified, rather than replaced, the trading relationships that developing economies depend on, so the global trade system has changed in degree more than in its underlying structure of core and periphery.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Weigh this change against how much of the underlying structure of global trade, who holds the most influence, has actually shifted.","Set evidence of growing trade between developing countries against evidence that advanced economies and their institutions still hold most trade power.","End with a single judgement about the degree of change, rather than simply describing south-south trade growth on its own."],"codes":["2.a","3.a"]},{"q":"'International trade creates more opportunities than challenges for LIDCs.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Balanced answer using Ethiopia: opportunities (diversification via industrial parks, FDI) against challenges (limited market access, landlocked, political instability, narrow export base); qualified conclusion that challenges currently outweigh the opportunities.","sol":"International trade has created some genuine opportunities for Ethiopia, an LIDC: state-led investment in export-oriented industrial parks, such as Hawassa Industrial Park, has attracted foreign manufacturers into garments and textiles, adding manufactured goods to an export base long dominated by coffee, and bringing foreign exchange, jobs and some technology transfer. Trade has also created serious challenges. Ethiopia is landlocked and depends on the port of Djibouti for the great majority of its trade, adding cost and vulnerability outside its control. Its exports remain concentrated on a small number of primary commodities exposed to volatile world prices, and periods of internal conflict, such as in Tigray from 2020, have disrupted production and deterred investment, further narrowing its access to global markets. Weighing the two: the opportunities created so far are real but narrow, resting on one industrial park and a small manufacturing sector, while the challenges are structural and longer-standing, rooted in Ethiopia's geography, its export base and its politics. On balance, trade has therefore done more, so far, to expose Ethiopia's disadvantages as a peripheral economy than to overcome them, although the diversification strategy shows a credible route by which this balance could shift over time.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what 'opportunities' and 'challenges' mean for an LIDC specifically, before you decide how to weigh them against each other.","Build one paragraph of named evidence for opportunities and one for challenges, from the same case study, before you compare their scale.","Reach a single, qualified judgement in your conclusion about which side currently outweighs the other, rather than ending on a list of both."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"'Trade does more to create interdependence between countries than to create inequality.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Balanced answer: interdependence shown via the EDC's trade links and shared reliance; inequality shown via unequal power between the AC and LIDC case studies; conclusion that both exist together.","sol":"Trade clearly creates interdependence: an EDC such as China is now economically, politically and socially bound to its trading partners, needing continued export demand from the EU and USA to sustain growth, while those partners depend on China for manufactured goods and increasingly for components in their own supply chains, so a disruption on either side has consequences for the other. At the same time, trade also creates clear inequality rooted in unequal power. A core economy such as the USA can negotiate agreements largely on its own terms and use tariffs to reshape trade relationships in its favour, while a peripheral economy such as Ethiopia can mainly only respond to changes made elsewhere, facing limited market access and a narrow export base it cannot easily alter alone. Interdependence and inequality are therefore not opposites but exist together: countries can be mutually dependent on trade while still holding very unequal power within that relationship, so trade creates interdependence between trading countries, but the terms of that interdependence remain unequal between core and peripheral economies.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about whether interdependence and inequality are opposites, or whether trade could produce both at once between the same two types of country.","Use your EDC case study to show mutual dependence, and your AC and LIDC case studies to show unequal power, before comparing the two ideas.","Conclude by stating how the two ideas relate to each other, rather than treating 'interdependence' and 'inequality' as two separate essays."],"codes":["1.b","2.b","3.a"]},{"q":"Compare the trade advantages of an AC with the trade challenges of an LIDC, using named case study evidence.","a":"USA: large market, ability to set agreement terms (USMCA), tariff use, sustained growth despite a deficit. Ethiopia: landlocked/Djibouti dependence, narrow export base, political instability limiting access. The AC shapes the system, the LIDC responds to it.","sol":"The USA's trade advantages as an AC rest on its ability to shape the system: it can negotiate agreements largely on its own terms, such as replacing NAFTA with USMCA in 2020, and use the scale of its market and currency to influence global trade conditions, sustaining economic growth even while running a persistent trade deficit. Ethiopia's trade challenges as an LIDC rest on the opposite position: it is landlocked and depends on Djibouti's port for the great majority of its trade, has a narrow export base concentrated on coffee that leaves it exposed to world price swings, and has faced periods of political instability, such as the Tigray conflict from 2020, that further limit its access to global markets. The comparison shows the difference between a core and a peripheral economy directly: the USA actively drives change in the global trade system to its advantage, while Ethiopia can largely only respond to changes made elsewhere, which is precisely the unequal power relationship this key idea describes.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Set your AC and LIDC case studies directly against each other on the same points, rather than describing each one in full separately.","For each case study, choose one piece of evidence about how much control it has over the terms of its own trade.","End by naming the underlying difference in power your comparison shows, using the specification's own wording for core and peripheral economies."],"codes":["3.a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Name the three components of international trade.","a":"Merchandise, services and capital."},{"q":"Which regional trading bloc groups together most of Europe's economies under shared trade rules?","a":"The EU."},{"q":"Name this room's case study LIDC.","a":"Ethiopia."},{"q":"Name this room's case study AC.","a":"The USA."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Merchandise trade","back":"Trade in tangible physical goods, such as machinery, clothing or foodstuffs, that cross a border.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Services trade","back":"Trade in intangible activities, such as finance, insurance, tourism or IT, that cross a border.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Capital flows","back":"Flows of money for investment across borders, such as foreign direct investment, portfolio investment and remittances.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Trade balance","back":"Value of exports minus value of imports; positive is a surplus, negative is a deficit.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Intra-regional vs inter-regional trade","back":"Intra-regional trade happens within one world region (e.g. within ASEAN); inter-regional trade crosses between different world regions.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"HDI (Human Development Index)","back":"A composite index of life expectancy, education and income, compared against value of exports to link trade with development.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"How trade can promote development","back":"Export earnings fund schooling, healthcare and infrastructure, as in China since it joined the WTO in 2001; inward investment builds transport links, such as the Chinese-financed Addis Ababa to Djibouti railway; MNC factories transfer technology and skills, raising productivity and wages.","code":"1.b","open":true},{"front":"How trade can cause inequality","back":"Dependence on one commodity, such as Ethiopian coffee, leaves export earnings exposed to a single world price; producers in LIDCs capture only a small share of the final retail price, with most value added in ACs; landlocked states pay more to reach markets, as Ethiopia does routing roughly 95% of trade through Djibouti.","code":"1.b","open":true},{"front":"Outsourcing","back":"An MNC moving part of its production to another country, often an EDC, usually to reduce labour or production costs.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"South-south trade","back":"Trade between developing countries, rather than between developing and developed countries.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"New international division of labour","back":"The global shift of labour-intensive production stages to lower-wage countries, while higher-value stages stay in advanced economies.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"USMCA","back":"The United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement, in force from 2020, a regional trading bloc that replaced NAFTA.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"China's trade change over time","back":"China has shifted from low-value manufacturing towards higher-value electronics, since joining the WTO in 2001.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Ethiopia and market access","back":"Ethiopia is landlocked and depends on Djibouti's port for approximately 95% of its trade, limiting its access to global markets.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"USA and trade deficit","back":"The USA runs a persistent goods trade deficit, of approximately $1,000 billion a year in the early 2020s, despite its trade advantages.","code":"3.a"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: 'International trade creates more opportunities than challenges for LIDCs.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? (16 marks)","One of the clearest opportunities international trade creates for an LIDC is the diversification of economic activity away from a narrow base of primary exports. Ethiopia has traditionally depended on coffee for the largest single share of its export earnings, which exposes the economy to volatile world commodity prices largely set beyond its control. Since the 2010s, the Ethiopian government has used state-led investment to build export-oriented industrial parks such as Hawassa Industrial Park, which attracted foreign manufacturers, mainly in garments and textiles, drawn by low labour costs and duty-free access to markets such as the EU. This has begun to add manufactured goods alongside coffee and oilseeds in Ethiopia's export mix, bringing foreign exchange, jobs and some technology transfer that a purely agricultural export base could not provide. However, this diversification remains narrow and fragile: garment exports are still a small share of total exports, wages are low, and the sector is highly exposed to shifts in global demand and to Ethiopia's own political instability, so trade has created a genuine but as yet limited opportunity rather than a transformation of Ethiopia's position in the global trade system."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: 'International trade creates more opportunities than challenges for LIDCs.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? (16 marks)","Trade gives LIDCs opportunities because they can sell more things to other countries. Ethiopia mainly exports coffee but the government has built industrial parks such as Hawassa so companies can make clothes there instead. This means Ethiopia is not just selling coffee any more and it can make more money from trade. Foreign companies have come to Ethiopia because it is cheap to make things there. This has created some jobs for people. So trade has given Ethiopia an opportunity to diversify its economy."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — AC trade patterns: the USA","items":["The USA is approximately the world's largest single economy by nominal GDP, and one of the largest single-country traders of goods and services.","The USA's largest trading partners by approximate value in the 2020s are Mexico, Canada and China.","The USA typically runs a substantial trade deficit: an approximate goods trade deficit of around $1,000 billion a year in the early 2020s, partly offset by an approximate services trade surplus of around $250 billion.","Key US exports include services (finance, technology, intellectual property), civil aircraft, machinery and agricultural products such as soybeans and corn; since approximately 2020 the USA has also been a net exporter of petroleum products, following its shale energy boom.","Key US imports include consumer electronics, vehicles, machinery and pharmaceuticals, much of it from Mexico, Canada and East and Southeast Asia.","The USA trades under USMCA (the United States-Mexico-Canada Agreement), in force since 2020, which replaced NAFTA; US trade policy has also used tariffs, notably on Chinese goods from 2018 onward, to try to reshape trade terms.","Trade has changed since the 1990s from a manufacturing-heavy export base towards a much larger role for services and, more recently, energy exports, alongside a policy shift towards reshoring some manufacturing supply chains."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — EDC trade patterns: China","items":["China is approximately the world's largest exporter of merchandise goods by value, and the world's second-largest economy.","China's largest trading partners include ASEAN (an approximate 16% share of exports), and the European Union and the United States (each an approximate 15% share of exports), alongside Hong Kong, Japan and South Korea.","China typically runs a large trade surplus, of the order of approximately $800 billion a year in the early 2020s.","Key Chinese exports have shifted from low-value manufactured goods such as textiles and toys in the 1990s-2000s towards higher-value electronics, machinery, electric vehicles and solar panels.","Key Chinese imports include crude oil, iron ore, semiconductors and soybeans, reflecting its need for raw materials and advanced components to supply its own manufacturing.","China joined the World Trade Organization (WTO) in 2001, widening its access to global export markets, and joined the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership (RCEP) trade bloc, in force from 2022.","China's Belt and Road Initiative, launched in 2013, has expanded its trade and investment links with developing countries across Asia, Africa and Latin America, contributing to the growth of 'south-south' trade.","China's export-led manufacturing carries a large embodied environmental cost, with approximately a quarter of its carbon emissions estimated to be linked to producing goods for export, while its growing imports of raw materials such as iron ore and soybeans drive resource extraction and land-use change in exporting countries."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case study fact file — LIDC trade patterns: Ethiopia","items":["Ethiopia is a low-income developing country (LIDC) and one of Africa's fastest-growing economies, though it remains landlocked, having lost its coastline when Eritrea became independent in 1993.","Ethiopia depends on the port of Djibouti for approximately 95% of its trade, adding transport cost and time beyond its own control.","Ethiopia's exports are dominated by primary commodities, above all coffee, its largest single export earner, alongside oilseeds, khat, cut flowers and gold.","Ethiopia typically runs a large trade deficit: approximate export earnings of a few billion US dollars a year against approximate imports of machinery, fuel, vehicles and manufactured goods worth several times that value.","Ethiopia's main trading and investment partners include China, a major infrastructure investor and trading partner, along with the European Union and the United States; Ethiopia is also a member of the Common Market for Eastern and Southern Africa (COMESA) and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA).","Since the 2010s, Ethiopia has sought to diversify its economy through state-led investment in export-oriented industrial parks, such as Hawassa Industrial Park, attracting foreign manufacturers into garments and textiles alongside its traditional coffee exports.","Political instability, including the conflict in the Tigray region from 2020 to 2022, has disrupted production, transport and investor confidence, illustrating the challenges an LIDC faces in sustaining its trade relationships."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'International trade creates more opportunities than challenges for LIDCs.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? (16 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'opportunities' means positive outcomes such as diversification of economic activity and new export earnings; 'challenges' means negative outcomes such as limited market access, a narrow export base and political instability; 'to what extent' requires a single qualified judgement about how far opportunities outweigh challenges, not a list of each treated separately.","Paragraph 1 (build the case for opportunities): explain how state-led investment in export-oriented industrial parks, such as Hawassa Industrial Park, has diversified Ethiopia's exports beyond coffee and attracted foreign manufacturers, using named evidence.","Paragraph 2 (build the case for challenges): explain how being landlocked and dependent on Djibouti's port, a narrow export base concentrated on coffee, and periods of political instability such as the Tigray conflict limit Ethiopia's access to global markets, using named evidence.","Paragraph 3 (weigh the two): compare the scale of the opportunities created so far against the scale of the challenges, considering how far each is within Ethiopia's own control.","Evaluation thread: throughout, weigh how far each opportunity or challenge is something Ethiopia can influence itself against how far it is set by forces outside its control, such as world commodity prices, geography or conflict, since this determines how far trade can be said to benefit a peripheral economy.","Conclusion must: state a qualified judgement — that trade has created real but still narrow opportunities for Ethiopia, such as diversification through industrial parks, while the challenges of limited market access, dependence on a neighbouring country's port and political instability remain more deep-seated and structural, so trade has so far done more to expose Ethiopia's position as a peripheral economy than to overcome it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.939Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Facts, figures and case studies are sound and properly hedged — USA/China/Ethiopia are correctly used as the AC/EDC/LIDC, the two calculations (1,000 - 250 = 750; 16 + 15 + 15 = 46) both re-solve correctly, tariffs stay within the Human interactions paper's low-tariff/6-10/16 range with no 'State' stems, and 'Level 4'/'Level 2' are truthful labels for OCR's four-level 16-mark scheme; fix the three leaky hints and add an environmental line to the China fact file and this is publishable, with 'Assess the extent to which' (q10) worth swapping for the board's own 'To what extent' as polish."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:06:00.460Z"}},"2.2.2":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"2.2.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room underpins Human interactions (Paper 02), where the specification sets short answer questions, medium length questions of 6 to 10 marks and 16-mark extended responses on Global migration. Beyond Paper 02, the case-study evidence here can also be drawn on synoptically to support answers on related physical and human themes elsewhere in the specification, without this option itself being re-examined as a debate topic on Geographical debates (Paper 03). Every tariff tests these five ideas, and the three required case studies — an AC, an EDC and an LIDC — supply the named evidence that separates a top-level analytical or evaluative answer from a generic, example-free one.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Patterns of global migration flows","t":"International migration is not evenly spread: current flows vary in number, composition (who is moving — economic migrants, refugees, students, family members) and direction. Inter-regional migration crosses continents (for example Central America to North America, or West Africa to Western Europe), while intra-regional migration stays within one world region (for example within South-East Asia). A top-level answer names the type of flow precisely — inter-regional or intra-regional — and supports it with a real corridor and a rough scale, rather than describing migration as a single, uniform global process."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Migration and socio-economic development","t":"International migration and a country's level of socio-economic development are linked in both directions: national indices such as the Human Development Index (HDI) and the value of migrant remittances received show how migration correlates with development, while migration itself can promote stability, growth and development — through flows of people (skills), money (remittances), ideas and technology — or cause inequalities, conflicts and injustices, such as an origin country losing its trained workforce. A top-level answer uses a named index or figure and states which direction of effect it is showing, rather than asserting migration is simply 'good' or 'bad' for development."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Why migration has become more complex","t":"Twenty-first-century change has made global migration harder to characterise with one pattern: economic globalisation has created new source areas and host destinations beyond the traditional route; migrant flows now include a high concentration of young workers and female migrants; South-South corridors (for example into the Gulf states) are now equal in magnitude to South-North corridors; conflict and persecution have increased refugee numbers; national immigration and emigration policies keep changing; and distinct corridors of bi-lateral flows between two named countries have developed. A top-level answer selects two or three of these named drivers relevant to the question, rather than listing all six without development."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Migration corridors and interdependence — the EDC case study","t":"A corridor is a persistent, high-volume flow of migrants between two or more countries; over time these corridors create economic, political, social and environmental interdependence between the countries they connect. The required EDC case study must show current patterns of immigration and emigration, how these have changed over time, the interdependence this creates with connected countries, and the impact on the EDC's own economic development, political stability and social equality. A top-level answer traces a named corridor through to a specific impact on the EDC, rather than describing the EDC's migration in isolation from the countries it is linked to."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Unequal power in the global migration system — the AC and LIDC case studies","t":"Global migration creates opportunities and challenges that reflect unequal power between countries: the required AC case study shows how an Advanced Country's economic, political and social factors let it influence and drive change in the global migration system — through migration policy and interdependence with linked countries, opportunities such as labour supply, and challenges such as border issues — while the required LIDC case study shows how a Low Income Developing Country has limited influence over, and a restricted response to, the same system, facing opportunities such as migrant remittances and challenges such as the loss of skilled workers. A top-level answer compares the two case studies directly against the idea of unequal power, rather than describing each country's migration separately."}],"examples":[{"title":"Calculating a percentage change in a migration corridor — Brazil and Venezuela","setup":"Venezuelan asylum applications recorded in Brazil numbered approximately 3,000 in 2016. By 2023 the R4V platform estimated that approximately 500,000 Venezuelan refugees and migrants were living in Brazil. Calculate the percentage change between the two figures, and judge how fairly that percentage measures the growth of the corridor.","steps":["Percentage change = (500,000 − 3,000) ÷ 3,000 × 100 = 497,000 ÷ 3,000 × 100 ≈ 16,567%.","Before using that number, check the two figures count the same thing: the 2016 figure is new asylum applications lodged in a single year, while the 2023 figure is a stock — everyone from Venezuela living in Brazil on that date, whether or not they ever claimed asylum.","Comparing a one-year flow with a cumulative stock inflates the percentage: the 2023 total has been built up over seven years of arrivals, and it counts people the 2016 asylum figure was never designed to capture.","So the change works out at approximately +16,567%, but it is not a fair measure of the corridor's growth, because the two figures measure different things; what it does show beyond doubt is a corridor transformed within seven years — the pressure that led Brazil to run Operação Acolhida, interiorising arrivals away from the border state of Roraima."],"cues":["Which of the two figures is the baseline in the formula?","Do the 2016 and 2023 figures count the same thing?","What does that mismatch do to the percentage?","Is the percentage fair — and what does it still prove?"]},{"title":"Reading remittances as a development indicator — Laos","setup":"Laos receives remittances from Lao migrant workers in Thailand worth several hundred million US dollars a year, put by the World Bank at roughly 1.5–2% of GDP in recent years. Explain what this remittance flow shows about the relationship between migration and development.","steps":["Follow the money: wages earned in Thailand's higher-wage economy are sent back to Lao households and spent there on food, schooling, healthcare and small businesses — income earned abroad but consumed at home.","Note what did not have to happen for that income to arrive: Laos built no new industry and passed no new policy; the gain comes entirely from its own citizens working across a border.","Weigh the size and the security of the flow: 1.5–2% of GDP is about 1 US dollar arriving from abroad for every 50 to 70 dollars the Lao economy produces for itself, so the money is substantial in the hands of the households that receive it but far too small to transform the national economy — and it lasts only as long as Thai employers want Lao labour.","The flow therefore shows that migration and development are linked by movements of money as much as by movements of people: emigration can lift household incomes and reduce poverty in an LIDC, but a development gain that depends on another country's labour market is a fragile one, and it does not build the industries that would raise HDI on its own."],"cues":["What happens to Thai wages once they reach Lao households?","What did Laos itself have to do to earn this income?","Is 1.5–2% of GDP large, and how secure is it?","What does the flow show about migration and development?"]},{"title":"Naming an opportunity and a challenge — the USA as an AC","setup":"The USA's agriculture, construction and technology sectors depend on migrant workers, from seasonal farm labour to H-1B skilled-visa holders, whose visas are issued for 3 years at a time. The USA also polices approximately 3,200 km of land border with Mexico, where unauthorised crossings and asylum claims recur year after year. Using the USA as the AC case study, identify one opportunity and one challenge migration creates for it, and say what the pair shows about an AC's place in the global migration system.","steps":["The opportunity is labour supply: migrants fill vacancies at both ends of the skills range — seasonal pickers in the fields, H-1B holders on 3-year visas in technology firms — so migration underwrites output in sectors US employers cannot staff domestically.","The challenge is the border: approximately 3,200 km of land frontier has to be policed against unauthorised crossings, and every asylum claim made there has to be processed, at a cost in money and political capital that recurs each year.","The two are not separate problems with separate causes: the wage gap that pulls workers into American farms and building sites is the same gap that brings people to the border, so the opportunity and the challenge come from one source.","Opportunity and challenge together show an AC that drives the global migration system rather than merely sitting in it: through visa categories such as H-1B the USA selects the migrants it wants, while the border is the cost of controlling everyone it does not — influence over the system and exposure to it at once."],"cues":["Which of these facts is a gain for the US economy?","Which of them is a cost the USA has to carry?","Do the two have separate causes, or one shared cause?","What does the pair show about an AC's role?"]},{"title":"Naming an opportunity and a challenge — Laos as an LIDC","setup":"Lao migrant workers in Thailand send remittances home to Laos, while Laos loses working-age and skilled citizens from its health, education and labour markets to Thai employers. Thailand, host to roughly 3 million migrant workers from its poorer neighbours, sets the permit quotas and border rules that govern the corridor. Using Laos as the LIDC case study, identify one opportunity and one challenge migration creates for it, and say what its position in this corridor shows.","steps":["The opportunity is the money coming back: remittances from Thailand arrive as household income in Lao villages that have little paid work of their own to offer, raising living standards and reducing poverty.","The challenge is who does the leaving: the same corridor takes working-age and skilled people, so Lao clinics, schools and employers lose the staff they trained — cash gained at the cost of human capital.","Now look at where the controls sit: Thailand sets the quotas and permit rules for roughly 3 million migrant workers, so whether a Lao household receives anything at all is decided in Bangkok, not Vientiane.","Laos therefore gains remittances and loses skilled workers, and can do little to alter either: it has limited influence over the global migration system and can only respond to decisions taken in a neighbouring country — the opposite of an AC, which sets the terms others must live with."],"cues":["Which part of this corridor works in Laos's favour?","Which part of it costs Laos, and what exactly is lost?","Who decides the rules of this corridor?","What does Laos's position show about an LIDC?"]},{"title":"Tracing interdependence along a corridor — Brazil's EDC case study","setup":"Brazil's EDC case study includes: Venezuelan and Haitian migrants adding to Brazil's low-wage labour supply while Brazilian dekasegi workers in Japan send remittances home; Brazil being home to the largest Japanese-descended population outside Japan; and Brazil's Operação Acolhida programme working with the UNHCR and alongside neighbouring Colombia, the region's other main host of Venezuelans. Identify the types of interdependence these examples show.","steps":["The first pairing is economic, and it runs both ways: Venezuelan and Haitian arrivals add to Brazil's low-wage labour supply while Brazilian dekasegi workers in Japan send remittances back, making Brazil a host and a source country at the same time.","The diaspora point is social: over a century of Japanese immigration left Brazil with the largest Japanese-descended population outside Japan, and it was that shared ancestry, rather than a labour shortage alone, that later opened the return route to Japan.","Operação Acolhida is political: managing the Venezuelan corridor obliges Brazil to act with the UNHCR and alongside Colombia, so no single state on the corridor sets policy for it by itself.","The material therefore shows three of the four types the case study requires — economic, social and political interdependence, each of them two-way. The fourth, environmental, is not evidenced here and would need material of its own, such as the strain arrivals place on water and sanitation in Roraima."],"cues":["What type of link is labour in and remittances out?","What type of link does shared ancestry create?","What type of link is a programme run with the UNHCR?","Which of the four types is missing from this material?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Is a migrant moving from West Africa to Western Europe an example of inter-regional or intra-regional migration, and why?","a":"Inter-regional — the flow crosses from one world region (West Africa) into another (Europe)."},{"q":"Name the two national indices used to link migration to socio-economic development.","a":"The Human Development Index (HDI) and the value of migrant remittances."},{"q":"Give one reason South-South migration corridors have grown to equal South-North corridors in size.","a":"For example, new host destinations such as the Gulf states drawing workers from other developing countries (also acceptable: tightening immigration policy in advanced countries making South-North moves harder)."},{"q":"Which of the three required case studies — AC, EDC or LIDC — is used to show 'limited influence over and restricted response to' the global migration system?","a":"The LIDC case study."}]},"room":{"facts":["Inter-regional migration crosses between world regions (e.g. Central America to North America); intra-regional migration stays within one world region (e.g. within South-East Asia).","The two named national indices linking migration to socio-economic development are the Human Development Index (HDI) and the value of migrant remittances received.","Six named 21st-century drivers of migration complexity: new source/host areas from economic globalisation; a high concentration of young and female migrants; South-South corridors now equalling South-North corridors; conflict and persecution raising refugee numbers; changing immigration/emigration policies; and distinct bi-lateral corridors.","The EDC case study (Brazil) must show current and changing patterns of immigration/emigration, economic, political, social and environmental interdependence with connected countries, and the impact on Brazil's own development, stability and equality.","The AC case study (the USA) shows how an Advanced Country drives the global migration system through economic, political and social factors, with labour supply as an opportunity and border issues as a challenge.","The LIDC case study (Laos) shows limited influence over and restricted response to the global migration system, with migrant remittances as an opportunity and loss of skilled workers as a challenge."],"questions":[{"q":"Explain the difference between inter-regional and intra-regional international migration, using one example of each.","a":"Inter-regional migration crosses between world regions (e.g. West Africa to Western Europe); intra-regional migration stays within one world region (e.g. within South-East Asia).","sol":"Inter-regional migration is a flow of migrants that crosses from one world region into a different one — for example, migration from West Africa to Western Europe crosses from the African region into the European region. Intra-regional migration, by contrast, is a flow that stays within a single world region — for example, migration within South-East Asia, such as from Myanmar to Thailand, does not leave the South-East Asian region. Both types appear within current global migration flows, and a precise answer distinguishes them by the regional boundary crossed, not simply by the distance travelled.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what marks the boundary of a 'world region', as distinct from a continent or the whole world.","For each of your two examples, work out which side of a regional boundary the migrant crosses.","Structure your answer as: define the difference → give one example of each type."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify two national indices used to link international migration to socio-economic development.","a":"The Human Development Index (HDI) and the value of migrant remittances received.","sol":"Two national indices link migration to socio-economic development. The first is the Human Development Index (HDI), a composite measure of a country's health, education and income, which can be compared against its migration profile. The second is the value of migrant remittances a country receives, an economic indicator that shows how much money migrants abroad are sending back to their country of origin. Both indices allow a comparison across countries at different points on the development spectrum.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the different kinds of national statistic that can show how a country's development links to migration.","Recall the two indices used for this idea specifically, rather than any other development measure you may know.","Structure your answer as: name index one → name index two."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Identify two factors that have increased the complexity of global migration in the 21st century.","a":"For example: economic globalisation creating new source and host areas, and South-South corridors now equalling South-North corridors in size (also acceptable: young/female worker concentration, conflict and persecution, changing immigration/emigration policy, distinct bi-lateral corridors).","sol":"Two named factors are: first, economic globalisation, which has created new source areas and host destinations for migration beyond the traditional pattern; and second, the growth of South-South migration corridors, which are now equal in magnitude to South-North corridors, meaning as many migrants now move between developing countries as move from developing to advanced countries.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think back to the list of twenty-first-century changes covered for this idea.","Choose two that you can name precisely, rather than describing migration change in general terms.","Structure your answer as: name factor one → name factor two."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Identify one opportunity and one challenge that the USA illustrates for an AC driving the global migration system.","a":"Opportunity: labour supply (e.g. migrant workers in agriculture, construction or technology). Challenge: border issues (e.g. unauthorised crossings or asylum pressure at the USA–Mexico border).","sol":"The USA case study illustrates one opportunity as labour supply: migrant workers fill roles across agriculture, construction and the technology sector, including through visa routes such as the H-1B programme. It illustrates one challenge as border issues: the USA–Mexico border sees repeated pressure from unauthorised crossings and asylum claims, generating ongoing political and administrative strain. Together these show how an AC both benefits from and must manage its position at the centre of the global migration system.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what migrant workers give the USA economy, and what problem repeated large-scale arrivals create for it.","Match your opportunity and challenge to the two examples used for the AC case study.","Structure your answer as: name the opportunity → name the challenge."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Explain two reasons why South-South migration corridors have grown to equal South-North corridors in magnitude during the 21st century.","a":"For example: new job markets in destinations such as the Gulf states draw workers from other developing countries; and tightening immigration policy in advanced countries has pushed more migrants towards developing-country destinations instead.","sol":"One reason is the emergence of new host destinations within the developing world: rapidly growing economies such as the Gulf states have created large demand for foreign labour in construction, domestic work and services, drawing workers from other developing countries such as Bangladesh, India and the Philippines rather than from advanced countries. A second reason is that many advanced countries have tightened their immigration and asylum policies over the 21st century, making South-North migration harder to achieve, so a larger share of migrants who would once have aimed for the Global North instead move to another developing country where entry is easier or labour demand is stronger. Together, these push and pull effects explain why South-South flows have grown to match South-North flows in scale.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what has changed on the demand side of migration, and separately on the policy side.","Develop each reason into a short cause-and-effect chain, rather than just naming it.","Structure your answer as: reason one, developed → reason two, developed."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how flows of remittances can promote stability, growth and development in a migrant's country of origin.","a":"Remittances raise household income directly, increasing spending, savings and investment (e.g. in education or small business), which can lift the wider economy and reduce poverty in the origin country.","sol":"Remittances are money sent home by migrants working abroad, and they flow directly into the household incomes of families in the country of origin, often bypassing weak formal financial or government channels. This extra income lets families increase their spending on essentials, invest in their children's education, or start small businesses, which raises demand and can stimulate local economic growth. At a national scale, a country with a high overall value of remittances can see this income smooth over economic shocks, because remittance flows tend to be more stable than trade or investment income during a downturn, contributing to greater economic stability as well as household-level development.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about where remittance money goes first, and then what a household is able to do with that extra income.","Trace the effect from an individual household's spending up to the wider national economy.","Structure your answer as: describe the household effect → scale it up to national stability or growth."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"In 2016, Venezuelan asylum applications recorded in Brazil totalled approximately 3,000; by 2023 the R4V (Regional Interagency Coordination Platform) estimated that approximately 500,000 Venezuelan refugees and migrants were living in Brazil. Calculate the percentage increase between these two figures, and explain one reason for this rise.","a":"Percentage increase = (500,000 − 3,000) ÷ 3,000 × 100 ≈ 16,567%, though part of this reflects a change of metric — from a single year's asylum applications to a cumulative resident estimate. Reason: Venezuela's deepening economic and political crisis pushed far larger numbers of Venezuelans to cross into Brazil's border state of Roraima seeking safety and work than had applied for asylum in 2016.","sol":"The percentage increase is calculated as (500,000 − 3,000) ÷ 3,000 × 100 ≈ 16,567%, showing that the estimated Venezuelan population in Brazil grew by well over a hundredfold across this period. Part of this dramatic rise reflects a change in what is being measured — from the number of new asylum applications lodged in a single year (2016) to a cumulative estimate, published by the R4V coordination platform, of the total number of Venezuelan refugees and migrants resident in Brazil by 2023 — rather than a single consistent count taken twice. Even allowing for this, the underlying rise is real and substantial: Venezuela's deepening economic and political crisis, including hyperinflation, shortages and political instability, pushed increasing numbers of Venezuelans to cross the land border into Brazil's northern state of Roraima from 2017 onwards in search of safety, food security and employment. Brazil's response, including the Operação Acolhida programme, shows how a single push factor in one country can generate a rapidly growing migration corridor into a neighbouring EDC.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Work out the change in the number of migrants before turning it into a percentage of the starting figure.","Think about what was happening inside Venezuela over this period that would push people to leave for a neighbouring country.","Structure your answer as: state the percentage calculation → give the push factor driving it."],"codes":["2.b"]},{"q":"Analyse the opportunities and challenges the USA experiences as an AC that drives change in the global migration system.","a":"Opportunities: labour supply across sectors from agriculture to technology, and the economic, political and social influence that comes with hosting the world's largest foreign-born population. Challenges: managing the USA–Mexico border and shifting immigration policy amid domestic political pressure.","sol":"As an AC, the USA drives change in the global migration system through the sheer scale of the opportunities and challenges it generates. On the opportunity side, migrant labour supply underpins whole sectors of the US economy: seasonal and undocumented workers support agriculture, skilled visa holders (for example on H-1B visas) support the technology sector, and the country's status as host to the world's largest foreign-born population gives it significant political and economic influence over the countries linked to it by migration, from Mexico to India and the Philippines. On the challenge side, the roughly 3,200 km USA–Mexico border generates recurring pressure from unauthorised crossings and asylum claims, particularly from Central America and, more recently, Venezuela, which strains border infrastructure and has repeatedly shifted US immigration policy, from the diversity visa lottery to stricter asylum and deportation rules. These opportunities and challenges are two sides of the same position: the USA's economic pull creates the labour supply it benefits from, but that same pull is what generates the border pressure it must manage, showing how a powerful AC shapes, and is shaped by, the global migration system it sits at the centre of.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Think of the opportunity and the challenge as two effects of the same underlying pull the USA has on migrants.","Develop each side with a specific sector, visa route or border detail rather than a general statement.","Structure your answer as: opportunities, developed → challenges, developed → a link showing how one produces the other."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Analyse why Laos has limited influence over, and a restricted response to, the global migration system.","a":"As an LIDC, Laos depends on Thailand's labour market and policy decisions for its main migration corridor, has little bargaining power over bilateral terms, and relies on the remittances this generates while struggling to retain its own skilled workers.","sol":"Laos's limited influence stems from its position as an LIDC dependent on a single dominant migration corridor: large numbers of Lao workers migrate to neighbouring Thailand for better-paid work, but the terms of this movement — border crossing rules, work-permit quotas and enforcement — are set largely by Thai policy rather than negotiated on equal terms by Laos. When Thailand tightens its border or labour-permit system, as happened during the COVID-19 pandemic, Lao migrants and the households depending on their remittances are directly affected with little Laos can do in response, showing its restricted response to shocks in the system. This dependence brings an opportunity in the form of remittances, estimated at approximately 1.5–2% of GDP in recent years (World Bank) and supporting household consumption, but it also brings the challenge of losing working-age and skilled citizens to Thailand, weakening Laos's own domestic labour market in sectors such as health and education. Because Laos cannot offer wages or opportunities that keep pace with Thailand's, and because it has little diplomatic or economic leverage to reshape the terms of the corridor, it remains a taker rather than a shaper of the global migration system it is part of.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which country sets the terms of the main corridor Laos depends on, and why.","Link the opportunity Laos gains from this corridor to the challenge it creates for Laos's own workforce.","Structure your answer as: explain the dependence on the dominant corridor → give the opportunity → give the challenge → conclude on why influence is limited."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"To what extent have Brazil's migration corridors created interdependence that benefits, rather than costs, Brazil as an EDC?","a":"Benefits: labour supply and remittance links (e.g. dekasegi workers in Japan) support parts of the economy and diaspora ties; costs: rapid, unplanned in-migration (e.g. from Venezuela) has strained services and public finances in border states such as Roraima, so the balance is mixed rather than clearly favourable.","sol":"Brazil's migration corridors cut both ways. On the benefit side, Brazil's historic emigration corridor to Japan — the dekasegi movement of Brazilians of Japanese descent — sends remittances home and maintains strong social and economic ties with a major advanced economy, while earlier waves of Italian, Japanese and Portuguese immigration built the diaspora and trade links Brazil still draws on today. On the cost side, the rapid growth of the Venezuelan corridor — from around 3,000 asylum applications in 2016 to an R4V estimate of roughly 500,000 Venezuelan refugees and migrants resident in Brazil by 2023 — placed severe pressure on public services, housing and employment in the border state of Roraima, forcing the federal government to fund an interiorisation programme to redistribute migrants across the country. Politically, this corridor has also tested Brazil's relationship with Venezuela's government and required coordination with the UNHCR and neighbouring Colombia. On balance, the interdependence Brazil has built through migration brings clear long-run benefits through established, two-way corridors such as the Japan link, but the more recent, largely one-way Venezuelan corridor has imposed real short-term costs on specific regions and on public spending, so its overall effect on Brazil depends heavily on which corridor and time period is examined, rather than being uniformly positive.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Think about at least two different Brazilian migration corridors, not just the most recent one, before judging the balance.","Weigh a longer-established, two-way corridor against a more recent, largely one-way corridor before reaching your judgement.","Structure your answer as: benefit, evidenced → cost, evidenced → weighed conclusion on the overall balance."],"codes":["2.b","1.b"]},{"q":"'Global migration systems mainly benefit Advanced Countries (ACs), not Low Income Developing Countries (LIDCs).' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Uses the USA (AC) and Laos (LIDC): the AC gains labour supply and geopolitical influence while managing manageable challenges; the LIDC gains remittances but loses skilled workers and control over the corridor's terms — so the claim holds broadly but the LIDC does see some, more limited, benefit.","sol":"There is strong evidence to support the statement. The USA, as an AC, gains labour supply across multiple sectors, retains overall control of its immigration policy (setting visa quotas, border enforcement and asylum rules), and its economic pull gives it significant influence over the countries linked to it by migration. Laos, as an LIDC, by contrast, depends on a single dominant corridor to Thailand whose terms are set largely by Thai policy, has little power to negotiate better conditions, and loses working-age and skilled citizens it cannot easily replace, weakening its own health and education sectors. This asymmetry supports the claim that the system is shaped in the AC's favour. However, the statement should not be accepted without qualification: Laos does receive a genuine benefit in the form of remittances, estimated at approximately 1.5–2% of GDP in recent years (World Bank), which raise household incomes and reduce poverty in ways the LIDC could not otherwise achieve, and the USA also carries real costs from its position, including recurring pressure and political conflict over its border with Mexico. So while the AC clearly holds more power to shape the system's terms and captures the larger share of the benefit, the LIDC is not left with nothing: it is a smaller, more dependent beneficiary rather than an equal partner. On balance, the statement is largely accurate — the global migration system does distribute power and benefit unevenly towards ACs — but 'mainly' should not be read as 'entirely', since remittance-based benefit to the LIDC is real, even if it comes at the cost of skilled-worker loss and no control over the corridor's terms.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which side controls the terms of the corridor between the two case studies, and which side depends on the other.","Set out the AC's benefits and costs, then the LIDC's benefits and costs, before comparing the scale of each.","Structure your answer as: evidence for the statement, from both case studies → evidence qualifying it → a conclusion stating how far you agree."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Evaluate the extent to which international migration reduces inequalities between countries.","a":"Migration reduces some inequality through remittances and skills/technology transfer to origin countries, but it can also deepen inequality through brain drain from LIDCs and unequal control over migration policy by ACs — so the overall effect is mixed and depends on which country and flow is examined.","sol":"International migration can reduce inequalities between countries in several ways: remittances raise household and, at scale, national income in origin countries such as Laos, where they are estimated at approximately 1.5–2% of GDP in recent years (World Bank); the flow of ideas and technology can accompany returning or diaspora migrants, as with Brazil's dekasegi workers returning skills gained in Japan; and access to labour markets abroad gives citizens of LIDCs opportunities unavailable at home. However, migration can also widen inequalities. Brain drain — the loss of skilled and working-age citizens, as seen in Laos's dependence on the Thai labour market — can weaken an LIDC's own health, education and productive capacity, entrenching the very underdevelopment that drove people to migrate. Furthermore, ACs such as the USA retain far greater control over the terms of migration than LIDCs do, setting visa quotas, border policy and asylum rules largely in their own interest, so the structure of the system itself reflects and can reinforce existing global inequalities rather than levelling them. On balance, migration narrows some specific gaps, particularly income, through remittances, but because the power to set the rules of the system remains concentrated in ACs and the loss of skilled workers can undermine an LIDC's long-term development, migration alone is unlikely to substantially reduce the deeper structural inequalities between countries, and its effect is better described as redistributing some income while leaving the underlying imbalance of power largely in place.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think of at least one way migration narrows the gap between countries, and at least one way it can widen it.","Bring in evidence from more than one of the required case studies before weighing the two sides against each other.","Structure your answer as: evidence migration reduces inequality → evidence it can deepen inequality → a conclusion stating how far, overall, it does either."],"codes":["1.b","3.a"]},{"q":"How far does a country's ability to shape the global migration system depend on its level of economic development?","a":"Largely: the AC (USA) sets policy and draws labour with little external constraint, while the LIDC (Laos) has almost no influence over corridor terms; the EDC (Brazil) sits between the two, able to manage some corridors (e.g. via its own policy responses) but not fully control others (e.g. the scale of Venezuelan in-migration) — so development level is the main, but not sole, factor.","sol":"Economic development level does largely explain a country's ability to shape the global migration system. The USA, an AC, sets the terms of its own migration policy — visa quotas, border enforcement, asylum rules — largely on its own terms, and its economic pull gives it influence over migration decisions in the countries linked to it, from Mexico to the Philippines. Laos, an LIDC, sits at the opposite end: it has almost no power to set the terms of its dominant corridor to Thailand, must accept Thai labour-market and border policy as given, and can only respond to shocks such as COVID-19 border closures rather than prevent them. Brazil, an EDC, occupies a middle position that supports the pattern without being a perfect fit: as a relatively larger, more institutionally developed economy, it can design its own policy response to migration pressure, such as the Operação Acolhida interiorisation programme for Venezuelan migrants, showing more agency than an LIDC like Laos; but it still could not control the scale or timing of that inflow, which was driven entirely by conditions inside Venezuela, showing it lacks the AC's level of control over its corridors. This suggests development level sets the broad ceiling on a country's influence — an LIDC cannot behave like an AC — but it does not fully determine day-to-day ability to respond, since Brazil's institutional capacity let it manage a migration shock more effectively than its middle-income status alone might predict. Overall, economic development is the main driver of a country's power within the global migration system, but the EDC case study shows that governance capacity, not income level alone, also shapes how much a country can do with the development it has.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Rank the three required case studies from most to least able to set the terms of their own migration, before asking why.","Use the EDC case study to test whether development level alone explains the pattern, or whether something else also matters.","Structure your answer as: AC evidence → LIDC evidence → EDC evidence that qualifies the pattern → a conclusion on how far development level explains it."],"codes":["2.a","2.b","3.a"]},{"q":"Explain how a change in Japan's immigration policy affected Brazil's pattern of migration over time.","a":"In 1990, Japan revised its Immigration Control Act to create a descent-based long-term resident visa, letting Brazilians of Japanese descent (dekasegi) work there; Brazil did not create this visa, but it turned a historic one-way immigration link into Brazil into a sustained two-way labour and remittance corridor.","sol":"In 1990, Japan revised its Immigration Control Act to create a new long-term resident visa available to foreign nationals of Japanese descent. Brazil — home to the largest Japanese diaspora outside Japan following large-scale Japanese immigration in the early twentieth century — saw large numbers of Brazilians of Japanese descent (dekasegi) take up this route to work in Japan's manufacturing sector. Brazil did not create or grant this visa: the policy change was Japan's, opening its own labour market to the descendants of its earlier emigrants. The effect on Brazil's own migration pattern was nonetheless significant: a corridor that had run one way, as Japanese migrants moved to Brazil a century earlier, was reshaped by this foreign policy change into a sustained two-way flow, with dekasegi workers sending remittances back to Brazil and some returning with savings and new skills. This shows how a policy change made by a destination country, not by the origin country itself, can reshape the direction and scale of a country's own emigration pattern over time.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about a specific visa or policy change, rather than a general economic reason, that reshaped one of Brazil's corridors.","Trace how that policy change turned an old immigration link into a new emigration flow.","Structure your answer as: name the policy change → explain how it altered the corridor → state the resulting pattern."],"codes":["2.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the difference between inter-regional and intra-regional migration?","a":"Inter-regional crosses between world regions; intra-regional stays within one world region."},{"q":"Name the two national indices used to link migration to socio-economic development.","a":"The Human Development Index (HDI) and the value of migrant remittances."},{"q":"Which case study — AC, EDC or LIDC — shows a country driving change in the global migration system?","a":"The AC case study (the USA)."},{"q":"Give one challenge Laos faces as an LIDC in the global migration system.","a":"Loss of skilled/working-age workers to Thailand (brain drain)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Inter-regional vs intra-regional migration","back":"Inter-regional crosses between world regions (e.g. West Africa to Europe); intra-regional stays within one region (e.g. within South-East Asia).","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Two indices linking migration to development","back":"The Human Development Index (HDI) and the value of migrant remittances received.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"How remittances promote development","back":"They raise household income at once, paying for food, schooling and healthcare, as in Lao villages living on Thai wages; they fund small businesses and better housing; nationally they are a steady foreign-currency inflow — roughly 1.5-2% of GDP in Laos — that holds up when export earnings fall.","code":"1.b","open":true},{"front":"Six drivers of 21st-century migration complexity","back":"New source/host areas from globalisation; young/female worker concentration; South-South corridors equalling South-North; conflict/persecution; changing policy; bi-lateral corridors.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"South-South vs South-North corridors","back":"South-South flows (e.g. to the Gulf states) are now equal in magnitude to South-North flows, as new developing-country destinations have emerged.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Brazil's EDC case study — required content","back":"Current and changing immigration/emigration patterns, interdependence (economic, political, social, environmental) with linked countries, and impact on Brazil's own development, stability and equality.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Brazil–Venezuela migration corridor","back":"Venezuelan asylum applications in Brazil totalled approximately 3,000 in 2016; by 2023 the R4V platform estimated approximately 500,000 Venezuelan refugees and migrants were living in Brazil, driven by Venezuela's crisis.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Brazil–Japan dekasegi corridor","back":"A 1990s Japanese visa for descendants let Brazilians of Japanese descent work in Japan, turning a historic immigration link into a two-way remittance corridor.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"USA (AC) case study — opportunity and challenge","back":"Opportunity: labour supply across agriculture, construction and technology. Challenge: managing pressure at the USA–Mexico border (unauthorised crossings, asylum claims).","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Laos (LIDC) case study — opportunity and challenge","back":"Opportunity: remittances from workers in Thailand, estimated at approximately 1.5–2% of GDP in recent years (World Bank). Challenge: loss of skilled/working-age workers to Thailand.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Why Laos has limited influence","back":"Its dominant corridor depends on Thai labour-market and border policy, set largely by Thailand, leaving Laos to respond to, not shape, the system.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Why the USA drives the global migration system","back":"Sheer scale of labour demand in agriculture, construction and technology; policy choices other countries must react to, such as H-1B skilled visas and enforcement along the 3,200 km Mexican border; wage levels high enough to shape corridors reaching far beyond the Americas.","code":"3.a","open":true},{"front":"Unequal power in global migration","back":"ACs like the USA set the terms of migration policy and gain most; LIDCs like Laos have little control and gain a smaller, more dependent benefit.","code":"3.a"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4: evaluating the extent to which migration reduces inequalities between countries","items":["International migration does narrow some specific inequalities between countries, but it does not resolve the deeper imbalance of power that underlies them. Remittances are the clearest evidence of narrowing: in Laos, an LIDC, remittances sent home by migrant workers in Thailand are estimated at approximately 1.5–2% of GDP in recent years (World Bank), directly raising household income and funding spending that the domestic economy alone could not generate. However, this benefit is bought at a cost that widens a different inequality: Laos loses working-age and skilled citizens to the same corridor, weakening its own health and education sectors, while having almost no power to set the terms of the corridor it depends on — those terms are set largely by Thai labour-market and border policy. By contrast, the USA, an AC, sets its own migration policy largely on its own terms and draws labour from across the system with comparatively little external constraint. This asymmetry in who controls the rules of the system, not just who gains income from it, is why migration should be judged as redistributing some income between countries while leaving the underlying structural imbalance of power largely intact."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2: evaluating the extent to which migration reduces inequalities between countries","items":["Migration helps to reduce inequality between countries because when people migrate they send money back home, which is called remittances. This money helps poorer countries like Laos because families can use it to buy things they need and improve their lives. It also helps the country's economy because more money is coming in. On the other hand, some countries lose their skilled workers when they migrate, like Laos losing workers to Thailand, which is bad for their economy. Overall, migration does help to reduce inequality between countries because it gives poorer countries more money through remittances, so it is a good thing for reducing inequality between countries."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — the USA (AC)","items":["Population: approximately 340 million (2024); hosts the largest foreign-born population of any country, estimated at approximately 50 million people.","Largest bilateral migration corridor in the world: Mexico–USA, with an estimated 10–11 million Mexican-born migrants living in the USA.","The USA–Mexico border runs for approximately 3,200 km and is a recurring site of unauthorised crossings and asylum claims, including from Central America's 'Northern Triangle' (Guatemala, Honduras, El Salvador) and, more recently, Venezuela.","Outward remittances: the USA is estimated to be the world's largest source of outward remittances, sending about $80 billion a year (World Bank/KNOMAD, 2022).","Immigration routes include the employer-sponsored H-1B skilled-worker visa, the diversity visa lottery, family-sponsored green cards, and refugee/asylum resettlement, alongside large numbers of unauthorised migrants working in agriculture and construction.","Policy has shifted repeatedly this century, including tightened border enforcement, changes to asylum processing rules, and the DACA programme for migrants who arrived as children.","Impact: migrant labour underpins sectors including agriculture, construction, meatpacking and technology, while border and asylum pressures have repeatedly become major domestic political issues."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — Brazil (EDC)","items":["Population: approximately 213 million (2024).","Historic immigration: 19th- and 20th-century immigration from Italy, Portugal, Germany and Japan makes Brazil home to the largest Japanese diaspora outside Japan, estimated at approximately 1.5–2 million people of Japanese descent.","Emigration: since the 1990s, a Japanese visa route for descendants has enabled an estimated hundreds of thousands of dekasegi Brazilians of Japanese descent to work in Japan, sending remittances home.","Recent immigration: Venezuelan asylum applications in Brazil totalled approximately 3,000 in 2016; by 2023 the R4V platform estimated approximately 500,000 Venezuelan refugees and migrants were living in Brazil, driven by Venezuela's economic and political crisis, alongside earlier Haitian migration after the 2010 earthquake.","Policy response: Brazil's Operação Acolhida programme registers Venezuelan migrants at the border in Roraima state and relocates ('interiorises') many to other Brazilian states to ease pressure on local services.","Interdependence: Brazil is linked economically, politically, socially and environmentally to Venezuela (recent immigration), Japan (historic and ongoing emigration) and Portugal (colonial and linguistic ties, a long-standing emigration destination for Brazilians).","Environmental link: the rapid, unplanned inflow of Venezuelan migrants into Roraima has put pressure on land, water and waste services around the border city of Boa Vista, while environmental stress within Venezuela (shortages and failing infrastructure) is itself one of the push factors behind the corridor.","Impact: rapid Venezuelan in-migration has strained services and housing in Roraima, while established corridors such as the Japan link continue to bring remittances and cultural ties."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — Laos (LIDC)","items":["Population: approximately 7.7 million (2024); one of South-East Asia's least developed and only landlocked countries.","Dominant corridor: large-scale labour migration to neighbouring Thailand, with an estimated 300,000 or more Lao migrant workers registered there, and likely more working informally.","Remittances sent home by Lao migrants in Thailand are estimated at approximately 1.5–2% of Laos's GDP in recent years (World Bank), a significant source of household income.","Migration is governed mainly by bilateral labour agreements (MOUs) between Laos and Thailand, whose terms — quotas, permit rules, border procedures — are set largely by Thailand.","Loss of skilled and working-age citizens ('brain drain') to Thailand's higher wages has left gaps in Laos's own health, education and agricultural labour markets.","Shocks in Thai policy, such as border and work-permit restrictions during the COVID-19 pandemic, directly disrupted Lao migrant workers and the households depending on their remittances, with little Laos could do in response.","Opportunities: remittance income supports household consumption and can reduce poverty; challenges: loss of skilled workers and near-total dependence on Thailand's policy decisions."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — how far ability to shape the global migration system depends on development level (16 marks)","items":["Question (as set): 'How far does a country's ability to shape the global migration system depend on its level of economic development?' (16 marks, How far)","Decode: the command word 'How far' needs a weighed judgement with a qualified conclusion, not a description of each country in turn; the question names one variable to test (economic development level) against one outcome (ability to shape the system), so every paragraph must return to that link.","Opening paragraph: define 'ability to shape the system' (setting policy terms, controlling corridor conditions, influencing linked countries) and signal that the answer will test this against all three required case studies before qualifying the pattern.","Paragraph 2 (point, evidence, analysis): the USA (AC) sets its own migration policy and draws labour from across the system with little external constraint — use named routes (H-1B visas, border enforcement) as evidence.","Paragraph 3 (point, evidence, analysis): Laos (LIDC) has almost no power over its dominant corridor to Thailand, taking Thai labour-market and border decisions as given — use the COVID-19 border-closure evidence.","Paragraph 4 (the qualifying case): Brazil (EDC) sits in between — able to design its own policy response to the Venezuelan inflow (Operação Acolhida) but not to control the inflow's scale or timing, showing development level sets a ceiling rather than a complete explanation.","Evaluation thread: weigh development level as the main driver of ability to shape the system against Brazil's evidence that governance capacity, not income level alone, also matters.","Conclusion must: state how far you agree (largely, but not entirely), name the case-study evidence that qualifies a simple 'yes', and give the one factor besides development level (institutional/governance capacity) the answer has shown also matters."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.940Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All four faults the second verdict named are genuinely fixed: lesson.examples[0] now works 3,000 (2016 Venezuelan asylum applications) to approximately 500,000 (R4V 2023) and I re-solved it to 16,566.67% ≈ 16,567%, matching room.questions[6].a, the 'Brazil-Venezuela migration corridor' card and the Brazil fact file, with the change of metric flagged in all three places; the USA fact file now reads 'about $80 billion a year (World Bank/KNOMAD, 2022)', which is the published outward-remittance figure; Brazil now has a real environmental strand (Roraima/Boa Vista land, water and waste pressure plus environmental push factors inside Venezuela) in its fact file, its interdependence bullet reads 'economically, politically, socially and environmentally', and lesson.examples[4] no longer misstates the number of strands the specification names; and the three stems are cleaned up (lesson.check[1] no longer cites the specification, room.questions[2] no longer reveals a six-item list, room.questions[3] now asks about the USA rather than about its case study). I re-solved every other question independently and found no wrong or ambiguous answer key: Q0-Q3 name the right indices, factors and USA opportunity/challenge; Q4-Q5 and Q13 are sound (the 1990 revision of Japan's Immigration Control and Refugee Recognition Act did create the descent-based long-term resident status, correctly attributed to Japan, not Brazil); Q7-Q12 carry defensible, hedged evidence (USA approximately 340m, approximately 50m foreign-born, 10-11m Mexican-born, approximately 3,200 km border; Brazil approximately 213m, 1.5-2m Japanese diaspora; Laos approximately 7.7m, remittances 'approximately 1.5-2% of GDP (World Bank)' consistently in all seven places and consistent with the 'several hundred million US dollars' figure in lesson.examples[1]). Tariffs are Human interactions only (3/6/10/16), there are no 'State' stems, no stem carries a syllabus reference (the four surviving mentions of 'specification' are all in lesson.why and lesson.examples teaching prose), no hint states a marking point or names the required answer, the fact files are the USA (AC), Brazil (EDC) and Laos (LIDC) as idea 3.a and 2.b require, the Level 4 and Level 2 model paragraphs are honestly labelled against OCR's four-level 16-mark scheme, the lesson has one faithful section per key idea each closing on what a top-level answer does, and the essay plan decodes a genuine 'How far' question with an evaluation thread and a conclusion instruction. Publishable as it stands. Minor polish only: room.questions[2].sol still ends 'Other named factors are also creditable', which is examiner language inside what is meant to be a model answer; lesson.check[1], room.exit[1] and the card 'Two indices linking migration to development' all say 'the two' indices where the specification only says 'such as'; lesson.check[3] and room.exit[2] ask which of 'the three required case studies' fits, which tests course structure rather than geography; lesson.examples[4] names the environmental strand but still demonstrates only three, so it could usefully borrow the Roraima evidence the fact file now carries; and the 'Brazil-Japan dekasegi corridor' card says 'a 1990s Japanese visa' where room.questions[13] correctly dates it to 1990."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:07:09.313Z"}},"2.2.3":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"2.2.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room covers Option C: Human Rights within Global Governance for Component 2 (Human Interactions), examined through short-answer, six-to-ten-mark and sixteen-mark extended-response questions, and the same case-study evidence can resurface in the synoptic Geographical Debates paper. Examiners reward named case-study evidence — a country's gender inequality, an area of conflict, and an LIDC — used to build a weighed argument, not a description of human rights issues in isolation.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Defining human rights, norms, intervention and geopolitics","t":"Human rights are the basic rights and freedoms every person is entitled to, regardless of nationality, sex, ethnicity or religion — for example freedom from torture, the right to a fair trial and freedom of expression. A norm is a widely shared expectation of how states or people should behave, which varies between cultures and over time, so what counts as an acceptable practice in one place may be viewed as a violation elsewhere. Intervention is action taken, often by an outside actor, to change a situation inside a state — this can range from economic sanctions and diplomacy to military action. Geopolitics is the study of how power, resources and ideology shape relationships between states, and it explains why some human rights issues attract intervention while others are largely ignored. A top-level answer keeps these four terms distinct and uses the correct one for the concept being tested, rather than treating 'human rights' and 'norms' as interchangeable."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Global patterns in forced labour, maternal mortality and capital punishment","t":"Human rights violations show clear spatial patterns. Forced labour is concentrated in the Asia-Pacific region and in supply chains linked to agriculture, construction and domestic work, and is driven by poverty, weak labour law enforcement, migration and debt bondage. Maternal mortality is highest in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia, reflecting poor access to skilled birth attendants, poverty, low female education and weak health infrastructure, and is far lower in high-income countries with well-funded healthcare systems. Capital punishment is retained by a minority of states worldwide, with the great majority of confirmed executions concentrated in a small number of countries, and its use is influenced by political system, religion and public attitudes to crime. A top-level answer explains a named factor's mechanism for one of these three issues — for example how weak law enforcement allows debt bondage to persist — rather than simply stating that the pattern varies."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Explaining variation in gender inequality: economic, political and social factors","t":"The geography of gender inequality is complex because economic factors (such as unequal pay and lower female labour force participation), political factors (such as under-representation of women in government and weak enforcement of equal-rights laws) and social factors (such as cultural norms around marriage age, education and reproductive health) interact differently in every country. Educational opportunity, access to reproductive health services and employment opportunity are three specific challenges the specification names, and each can be a cause and a consequence of the others — for example, limited educational opportunity for girls reduces later employment opportunity. This complexity is why gender inequality is also contested: what one society defines as inequality, another may defend as tradition or choice. A named country case study is required for this idea — this room uses India — to show the gender inequality issues apparent in that country, the consequences of gender inequality on society, and evidence of changing norms and strategies used to address them. A top-level answer weighs which of the economic, political or social factors is doing the most explanatory work for the named case, and uses that case study's own detail rather than listing all three factors generically without linking them."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Human rights violations as a cause and a consequence of conflict","t":"Human rights violations can start conflict: unequal access to education and discrimination against an ethnic, religious or political group can build the grievances that escalate into civil war or insurgency. Violations are also a consequence of conflict — combatants target civilians, displace populations and disrupt healthcare and education — and this is often addressed through geopolitical intervention, action by outside states or organisations to reduce the violations or bring the conflict to an end. Interventions work through flows: flows of people (peacekeepers, refugees), money (aid, sanctions), ideas (human rights norms, ceasefire negotiations) and technology (surveillance, satellite monitoring of atrocities) all shape how effective an intervention is. A top-level answer traces a specific mechanism — for example how a named flow of money or people changed a named conflict's trajectory — rather than asserting that intervention simply 'helps'."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Global governance of human rights: institutions, treaties and organisations working together","t":"Human rights are promoted and protected through institutions (such as the United Nations and the International Criminal Court), treaties (such as the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and, in Europe, the European Convention on Human Rights), laws (such as national legislation implementing these treaties) and norms (shared international expectations of acceptable state behaviour). Global governance of human rights in an area of conflict typically involves cooperation between organisations working at different scales: the United Nations acting globally, a national government acting within its own borders, and an NGO working directly with affected communities — and these organisations do not always agree on priorities or methods. The consequences for local communities can include protection, humanitarian relief and access to justice, but also dependency on outside actors and continued insecurity where governance is only partially effective. A top-level answer names the specific institution, treaty or organisation operating at each scale for a named case, and judges how well their contributions combined, rather than listing organisations separately."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Consequences of global governance for citizens and places, and the case of an LIDC","t":"Global governance of human rights has short-term effects, such as immediate relief delivered by NGOs (food, shelter, medical care), and longer-term effects, such as changes in national laws, constitutions or institutions that are intended to outlast the intervention itself. In a named LIDC, these consequences can be evaluated against the human rights issue or issues that triggered governance action, the global governance strategy or strategies used, the opportunities this created for stability, growth and development, and the challenges of inequality and injustice that remain or emerge. Progress is rarely uniform: gains such as expanded access to education or new legal protections can coexist with continued poverty, corruption or a reversal once external support withdraws. A top-level answer reaches a qualified judgement — how far governance contributed to development relative to other factors — rather than treating intervention as an automatic success or failure."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a Gender Inequality Index (GII) table","setup":"The UNDP's Gender Inequality Index (GII) scores a country from 0 (women and men equal) to 1 (maximum inequality), combining reproductive health, empowerment and labour market data. In the Human Development Report 2023/24 (2022 data) Norway scores 0.012 and India scores 0.437. Work out the difference between the two scores and say what it shows about gender inequality in the two countries.","steps":["0.437 − 0.012 = 0.425, so India's figure sits 0.425 index points above Norway's on an index that runs only from 0 to 1.","A gap of 0.43 covers well over two fifths of the whole index, so the two countries sit near opposite ends of it rather than a short distance apart.","Norway's 0.012 means women are close to parity with men on all three dimensions, while India's 0.437 reflects a far higher maternal mortality ratio, a much smaller share of women in paid work and fewer women in parliament.","So the difference shows that gender inequality in India is not marginally but substantially greater than in Norway: 0.425 of the index's full range separates them, and the gap is built from reproductive health, empowerment and labour market gaps together rather than from any single measure."],"cues":["How do you measure the size of the gap between the two scores?","How much of the index's full range does that gap take up?","Which GII dimensions lie behind each country's score?","What does the gap show about inequality in the two countries?"]},{"title":"Calculating the scale of global forced labour","setup":"The ILO's Global Estimates of Modern Slavery put the number of people in forced labour worldwide at about 27.6 million (2021 estimate), when world population stood at about 7.9 billion. Express the forced-labour figure as a percentage of world population, and say what that percentage amounts to in people.","steps":["Put both figures in the same units before dividing: the ILO's 27.6 million (2021) is 0.0276 billion, set against 7.9 billion people.","0.0276 ÷ 7.9 × 100 = 0.349, which rounds to approximately 0.35%.","So approximately 0.35% of the world's population — about one person in every 286 — was in forced labour on the ILO's 27.6 million estimate: a percentage that looks small, yet a count larger than the population of most individual countries."],"cues":["What has to match before the two figures can be divided?","Which two operations turn the pair of figures into a percentage?","What does that percentage amount to in people?"]},{"title":"Decoding a 'to what extent' essay question","setup":"An exam question asks: 'To what extent has intervention in human rights contributed to development?' You have studied UN and NGO intervention in Sierra Leone after its civil war, and intervention in Afghanistan after 2001. Plan the answer: define the key terms, choose which case supports and which case tests the claim, and reach a judgement.","steps":["'To what extent' presents a claim to be tested, so the plan needs a paragraph that supports it and a paragraph that qualifies it, with the balance settled in the conclusion rather than asserted in the opening line.","Define the terms narrowly: 'intervention' here means outside action inside a state — peacekeeping, hybrid courts, aid and sanctions — and 'development' means more than growth, taking in schooling, health, rights and security.","Supporting paragraph — Sierra Leone: UNAMSIL peacekeeping and the Special Court for Sierra Leone ended the fighting and tried those most responsible, after which disarmament, schooling and investment could resume.","Qualifying paragraph — Afghanistan: girls' enrolment and a new constitution followed the 2001 intervention, yet both were reversed when the Taliban returned in August 2021, so the gains rested on outside presence rather than on domestic institutions.","Judgement: intervention has contributed to development to a limited extent — substantially where it left durable domestic institutions behind, as in Sierra Leone, but only reversibly where it did not, as in Afghanistan."],"cues":["What does the command phrase 'to what extent' demand?","Which two terms must be pinned down before any evidence?","Which of the two cases supports the claim most clearly?","Which case tests the claim from the other side?","What qualified judgement do the two cases together support?"]},{"title":"Tracing intervention in Sierra Leone: cause to consequence","setup":"Sierra Leone's civil war (1991–2002) involved widespread violations of human rights, including the forced recruitment of child soldiers and the mutilation of civilians. Using Sierra Leone as your case study of the global governance of human rights, trace the sequence from those violations to the response, naming the organisation acting at each scale from global to local and what each contributed.","steps":["The violations were a consequence of the conflict: rebel forces, principally the Revolutionary United Front, abducted children as fighters and mutilated civilians in order to hold territory and terrorise the population.","At the global scale the United Nations deployed UNAMSIL from 1999, authorised at up to 17,500 personnel and one of the largest peacekeeping missions of its time, to disarm combatants and protect civilians.","At the hybrid global–national scale the Special Court for Sierra Leone, created in 2002 by agreement between the UN and the Sierra Leonean government, tried those bearing greatest responsibility; it convicted the former Liberian president Charles Taylor in 2012 and sentenced him to 50 years.","At the national and local scale the Sierra Leonean government ran disarmament and a Truth and Reconciliation Commission, while NGOs such as Caritas Makeni reunited and rehabilitated former child soldiers within their communities.","So the chain runs from violations by the RUF, through UN peacekeeping and disarmament, to hybrid justice in the Special Court and then to national reconciliation and NGO-led reintegration: a consequence of conflict addressed by different organisations at every scale from global to local."],"cues":["Who carried out the violations, and to what end?","Which body acted first at the global scale, and how?","Which court was created, and whom did it convict?","Which actors worked at the national and local scale?","How does the sequence read from violation through to response?"]},{"title":"Evaluating opportunities and challenges in Afghanistan, 2001–2021","setup":"After the 2001 intervention in Afghanistan, girls' school enrolment rose from close to zero under Taliban rule to about 3.5 million by the late 2010s, and the 2004 constitution guaranteed women's political participation, reserving roughly a quarter of parliamentary seats for women. In August 2021 the Taliban retook control as international forces withdrew. Judge how far the global governance of human rights contributed to lasting development in Afghanistan.","steps":["Measured against the starting point, the gains were large: girls' enrolment went from near zero to about 3.5 million, and the reserved seats gave women a share of parliament comparable with many long-established democracies.","Both gains rested on outside support, however: the enrolment figures depended on internationally funded schools and teachers, and the seat quota on a constitution written and underwritten under international supervision.","The test of durability came in August 2021: within a year of the Taliban's return, secondary schooling for girls was suspended and women were shut out of parliament and most public employment, so the 2004 guarantees ceased to operate.","Judgement: global governance contributed substantially to development in the short and medium term — a generation of girls schooled and two decades of women in public life — but little to lasting development, because gains held in place by external presence rather than by Afghan institutions proved reversible the moment that presence ended."],"cues":["Against what starting point should the gains be measured?","What was holding the enrolment and the seat quota in place?","What became of those guarantees after August 2021?","Overall, how far did governance deliver lasting development?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is a 'norm' in the context of human rights?","a":"A widely shared expectation of how states or people should behave, which varies between cultures and over time."},{"q":"Name one economic factor that can cause variation in gender inequality.","a":"Any one of: unequal pay, lower female labour force participation, or unequal access to land and credit."},{"q":"How can a human rights violation be both a cause and a consequence of conflict?","a":"It can be a cause — for example discrimination sparking grievance and violence — and a consequence, as combatants violate rights such as by displacing or targeting civilians once conflict has started."},{"q":"Name one type of flow involved in geopolitical intervention.","a":"Any one of: flows of people, money, ideas or technology."}]},"room":{"facts":["The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) was adopted by the UN General Assembly in 1948 and sets out 30 articles of rights every person is entitled to.","The European Convention on Human Rights (1950), enforced by the European Court of Human Rights, is a regional treaty protecting human rights across Council of Europe member states.","The ILO estimates approximately 27.6 million people were in forced labour worldwide, in its 2021 Global Estimates of Modern Slavery.","The UK's Modern Slavery Act 2015 consolidated offences of forced labour, slavery and human trafficking into a single law and created the role of Independent Anti-Slavery Commissioner.","Approximately 55 countries retain the death penalty in law or practice, with the majority of confirmed executions concentrated in a small number of states (Amnesty International).","The global maternal mortality ratio is approximately 223 deaths per 100,000 live births (WHO/UNICEF/UNFPA estimate, 2020), and is highest in sub-Saharan Africa.","In Sierra Leone, the UN peacekeeping mission UNAMSIL and the hybrid Special Court for Sierra Leone worked alongside the national government to address human rights violations from the 1991–2002 civil war.","Following the 2001 intervention in Afghanistan, girls' school enrolment rose to approximately 3.5 million by the late 2010s, before the Taliban retook control in August 2021."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify what is meant by a 'norm' in the context of human rights.","a":"A widely shared expectation of how states or people should behave, which can vary between cultures and over time.","sol":"A norm is a widely shared expectation of how states or people ought to behave. In the context of human rights, norms are the internationally recognised standards — such as freedom from torture or the right to a fair trial — that most states have agreed should be upheld, even though what counts as acceptable practice can still vary between cultures and change over time.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the four key terms for this idea, and pick out the one this question is actually asking you to define.","A definition question at this tariff needs one precise sentence, not an example.","Give a single sentence that starts by naming the term and finishes with its definition."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify two human rights issues that show clear patterns of global variation.","a":"Any two of: forced labour, maternal mortality rates, capital punishment.","sol":"Forced labour and capital punishment are two human rights issues that show clear patterns of global variation. Forced labour is concentrated in the Asia-Pacific region and in exploitative supply chains such as agriculture, construction and domestic work, while capital punishment is retained by only a minority of states worldwide, with confirmed executions concentrated in a small number of countries.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the key idea on global patterns of human rights violations, and which issues it covers.","List them as short named issues rather than describing their patterns or causes.","Give two issue names only; no further detail is needed at this tariff."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Identify two scales at which organisations cooperate in the global governance of human rights.","a":"Any two of: global (e.g. the United Nations), national (e.g. a national government) and local (e.g. an NGO working directly with communities).","sol":"Global governance of human rights involves organisations working together at scales from global to local. Two accepted scales are the global scale, for example the United Nations, and the local scale, for example an NGO working directly with affected communities; the national scale, such as a national government, is also accepted.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how far the organisations involved reach — from the whole world down to a single community.","Name the scale and give one type of organisation that typically works at it.","Give two scale-and-organisation pairs only; no explanation of what they do is required."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Identify one short-term and one long-term effect of the global governance of human rights.","a":"Short-term: immediate relief from NGOs (e.g. food, shelter, medical care). Long-term: changes in laws (e.g. new legislation or constitutional protections).","sol":"A short-term effect of global governance of human rights is immediate relief, such as an NGO providing food, shelter or medical care to affected people. A long-term effect is a change in laws, such as new legislation or constitutional protections intended to outlast the immediate crisis.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the two timescales distinguished for the consequences of global governance.","Give one concrete type of action for each timescale, not a general description.","Structure your answer as two short sentences: one for short-term, one for long-term."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Explain how economic, political and social factors can cause variation in the patterns of gender inequality.","a":"Economic factors (e.g. unequal pay, lower female labour force participation), political factors (e.g. under-representation in government, weak enforcement of equal-rights laws) and social factors (e.g. cultural norms around marriage, education and reproductive health) combine differently in each country to produce different levels of gender inequality.","sol":"Gender inequality varies because economic, political and social factors interact differently between countries. Economically, women in many countries have lower labour force participation and are paid less for the same work, which limits their financial independence. Politically, women remain under-represented in national government in most countries, and where equal-rights laws exist, enforcement can be weak, leaving norms unchanged in practice. Socially, cultural norms around the age of marriage, educational opportunity and access to reproductive health services shape what choices are realistically open to women and girls. Because these three sets of factors reinforce each other — for example, limited education reduces later employment opportunity — the pattern of gender inequality in any one country reflects the particular combination and strength of all three, not a single cause.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the three categories of factor used to explain variation in gender inequality.","For each category, think of one specific example, then explain how it produces inequality rather than just naming it.","Structure your answer in three short paragraphs, one for each factor, each ending with its effect on gender inequality."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Using the example of India, explain the consequences of gender inequality for society and evidence of changing norms or strategies used to address it.","a":"Consequences: e.g. a smaller effective workforce and lower household incomes because female labour force participation is low; continued underrepresentation of women in politics and business. Changing norms/strategies: e.g. the Beti Bachao Beti Padhao scheme (2015) promoting girls' education and addressing the declining child sex ratio, and stronger sexual-violence laws such as the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013.","sol":"In India, gender inequality has clear consequences for society: with female labour force participation at only around 25%, a large share of the potential workforce is not economically active, which reduces household incomes and national output, and women remain underrepresented in politics and business leadership. Gender inequality also has social consequences, such as a skewed sex ratio at birth that reflects a long-standing cultural preference for sons in parts of the country. However, norms and strategies are changing: the Beti Bachao Beti Padhao ('Save the daughter, educate the daughter') scheme, launched in 2015, is a government strategy addressing the declining child sex ratio and promoting girls' education, and stronger sexual-violence laws, including the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013, followed public pressure for change after the widely reported December 2012 Delhi gang-rape case. Together, these show that while gender inequality continues to have real economic and social consequences in India, government strategies are actively working to shift the underlying norms that produce it.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the India case study you have studied for this idea, and which parts of it cover consequences for society rather than causes of inequality.","Separate your answer into consequences first, then a change in norms or a named strategy, rather than blending the two together.","Write two short sections: one on consequences for society, one on a strategy or changing norm, each anchored in the India case study."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how the violation of human rights can be both a cause and a consequence of conflict.","a":"As a cause: discrimination or unequal access to education can build grievance that escalates into conflict. As a consequence: conflict leads to violations such as displacement and targeting of civilians, often addressed through geopolitical intervention.","sol":"Human rights violations can cause conflict: when a group is denied equal access to education or faces systematic discrimination, the resulting grievance can escalate into insurgency or civil war. Violations are equally a consequence of conflict — once fighting begins, combatants may target civilians, displace populations and disrupt access to healthcare and education, deepening the human rights crisis. This consequence is often addressed through geopolitical intervention, where outside states or organisations act, using flows of people, money, ideas or technology, to reduce the violations or end the conflict. The relationship is therefore cyclical: unaddressed violations can restart conflict even after intervention appears to have succeeded.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the two different points in a conflict's timeline where human rights violations can appear.","For each point, use a specific type of violation, not a general statement that rights are violated.","Write two short paragraphs: one showing violation leading to conflict, one showing conflict leading to violation."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Study the table showing the Gender Inequality Index (GII) for three countries (source: UNDP Human Development Report 2023/24, 2022 data).\nCountry | GII value (0 = equal, 1 = unequal)\nNorway | approximately 0.012\nIndia | approximately 0.437\nNiger | approximately 0.609\nCalculate the difference in GII value between Norway and India, and use the data to explain what this shows about gender inequality in the two countries.","a":"Difference ≈ 0.425 (0.437 − 0.012). India's much higher GII value shows far greater gender inequality than Norway across reproductive health, empowerment and labour market participation.","sol":"The difference in GII value between Norway and India is approximately 0.437 − 0.012 = 0.425. Because the GII runs from 0 (full equality) to 1 (full inequality), this gap of over 0.4 shows that India has substantially greater gender inequality than Norway across the index's components — reproductive health, political and educational empowerment, and labour market participation. Norway's very low value reflects strong legal protection, high female political representation and near-equal labour force participation, while India's higher value reflects greater gaps in areas such as reproductive health access and female employment. Niger's even higher value of approximately 0.609 shows that gender inequality is not simply a 'developed versus developing' divide: India and Niger are not at similar levels of development — Niger has among the lowest Human Development Index scores in the world — so the scale of inequality varies widely even among lower-income countries, rather than following a single global pattern.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Subtract the smaller GII value from the larger one to find the gap between the two countries.","Remember which end of the 0–1 scale represents greater inequality before you interpret the gap.","Give your calculated figure first, then write one or two sentences interpreting what it shows."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain, using a named example, how organisations at different scales can work together in the global governance of human rights in an area of conflict.","a":"e.g. in Sierra Leone, the UN peacekeeping mission (UNAMSIL) worked at the global/international scale, the national government implemented disarmament and reconciliation, and NGOs delivered support to affected communities, together addressing the human rights violations of the civil war.","sol":"In Sierra Leone, following the 1991–2002 civil war, organisations at different scales worked together to address human rights violations such as forced recruitment of child soldiers. At the global scale, the United Nations deployed the UNAMSIL peacekeeping mission and supported the Special Court for Sierra Leone, a hybrid UN–national court that prosecuted those most responsible for the violations. At the national scale, the Sierra Leonean government carried out disarmament, demobilisation and reintegration of former combatants and established a Truth and Reconciliation Commission. At the local scale, NGOs supported the rehabilitation of former child soldiers and affected communities directly. This combination of scales meant that prosecution, disarmament and community recovery could happen simultaneously rather than relying on any single organisation.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the three scales of organisation named for this case study, from global to local.","Give one named organisation for each scale and one specific action it took, rather than a general description.","Structure your answer as three short sections, one scale at a time, in the same order given in the question."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain the short-term and long-term consequences of the global governance of human rights for citizens and places.","a":"Short-term: immediate relief such as NGOs providing food, shelter or medical care. Long-term: structural changes such as new laws, constitutions or institutions intended to outlast the crisis.","sol":"Short-term consequences of global governance of human rights include immediate relief, such as an NGO providing emergency food, shelter or medical care to citizens affected by a human rights crisis. Long-term consequences include structural changes such as new or amended laws, constitutional protections or institutions — for example a new constitution guaranteeing rights that did not exist before. These two timescales matter because short-term relief addresses urgent need but does not by itself change the underlying causes of the violation, while long-term legal change can outlast the immediate intervention but takes longer to have an effect and can be reversed if not embedded in stable institutions.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the two timescales distinguished for these consequences.","Give a concrete type of action for each timescale, and briefly say why the timescale matters, not just what happens.","Write two short paragraphs, one per timescale, in the order named in the question."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Explain why maternal mortality rates vary between countries.","a":"Variation reflects access to skilled birth attendants and healthcare infrastructure, poverty, female education levels, and government spending on maternal health services.","sol":"Maternal mortality rates vary because of differences in access to skilled birth attendants and functioning healthcare infrastructure — countries with well-funded health systems can respond quickly to complications during childbirth, while those with weak infrastructure often cannot. Poverty limits the ability of individuals and governments to pay for antenatal care and emergency obstetric services. Lower levels of female education are also linked to higher maternal mortality, as education is associated with greater awareness of health risks and earlier use of health services. As a result, maternal mortality is concentrated in lower-income regions such as sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia, where these factors combine, and is much lower in high-income countries with well-resourced healthcare systems.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the factors linked to global variation in this specific human rights issue.","Pick two or three of these factors and explain the mechanism linking each to a mother's risk of dying, rather than just naming them.","End your answer by linking the factors back to the regions where maternal mortality is highest."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Using a named LIDC case study, evaluate the extent to which global governance of human rights has contributed to development.","a":"A top-level answer defines terms, uses a named LIDC (e.g. Afghanistan), sets out the human rights issue(s), the governance strategy/strategies used, weighs opportunities for stability, growth and development against challenges of inequality and injustice, and reaches a qualified judgement on how far governance, versus other factors, drove development.","sol":"Global governance of human rights can be said to have contributed to development where it addresses a specific human rights issue and creates lasting opportunities, but this contribution is rarely complete or permanent, as the case of Afghanistan (2001–2021) shows.\n\nFollowing the 2001 intervention, which removed the Taliban regime that had systematically denied girls education, a range of governance strategies were used: the UN Assistance Mission in Afghanistan (UNAMA) supported state-building, international forces provided security, the new Afghan government adopted a 2004 constitution guaranteeing women's political participation, and NGOs delivered education and health programmes. These strategies created genuine opportunities: girls' school enrolment rose from close to zero to approximately 3.5 million by the late 2010s, women returned to parliament in significant numbers under a constitutional quota reserving roughly a quarter of seats, and basic health and education infrastructure expanded, all markers of development beyond simple economic growth.\n\nHowever, these opportunities coexisted with persistent challenges of inequality and injustice: corruption limited how far aid and governance reforms reached rural areas, insecurity continued throughout the intervention, and the gains were heavily dependent on continued international presence and funding rather than embedded in self-sustaining institutions.\n\nThe clearest evidence of how limited the contribution was is that when international forces withdrew in August 2021, the Taliban rapidly retook control and reversed many of the legal and educational gains, particularly for girls' access to secondary education. This suggests global governance of human rights in Afghanistan contributed significantly to development while externally supported, but did not achieve development that could survive its own withdrawal — so its contribution to lasting development was real but ultimately limited and reversible.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which named LIDC case study lets you discuss both a human rights issue and a governance strategy, not just one or the other.","Plan one paragraph on the opportunities the case study shows and a separate paragraph on the challenges, so you can weigh them against each other.","Structure your answer as an opening that defines the terms, two or three paragraphs each using named evidence, and a conclusion giving a qualified judgement on how far governance contributed to development."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"'Global governance of human rights does more to protect the interests of powerful states than the human rights of the vulnerable.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"A weighed answer using named examples of governance in action (e.g. Sierra Leone, Afghanistan) to show cases where governance genuinely protected vulnerable people, set against evidence that powerful states' interests shaped whether and how intervention happened, reaching a qualified overall judgement.","sol":"This statement has some truth, but the case-study evidence shows global governance of human rights protects vulnerable people in real, if uneven, ways.\n\nEvidence against the statement comes from Sierra Leone, where UN peacekeeping (UNAMSIL) and the hybrid Special Court for Sierra Leone directly protected civilians and prosecuted perpetrators of violations such as forced recruitment of child soldiers, with NGOs supporting the vulnerable, former child soldiers, in rehabilitation. This was governance acting clearly in the interests of the vulnerable, not powerful states.\n\nHowever, evidence supporting the statement comes from the pattern of which conflicts attract sustained intervention at all: the intervention in Afghanistan was shaped significantly by the geopolitical and security interests of intervening states after the 2001 attacks on the United States, not by human rights need alone, and support was withdrawn in 2021 once those interests changed, despite continuing human rights need for Afghan citizens, particularly women and girls.\n\nWeighing these, global governance does protect vulnerable people once it is engaged, through relief, prosecution and legal reform, but whether, how much and for how long it engages is strongly shaped by the interests of powerful states, so the statement is more true of the decision to intervene and to withdraw than of the effect of governance once it is in place.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Think of one case study where governance clearly helped vulnerable people, and one aspect of intervention that shows powerful states' interests at work.","Separate the question of whether governance helps the vulnerable once it happens from the question of what determines whether it happens at all.","Structure your answer with a paragraph for each side of the statement, evidence from a named case in each, and a conclusion that says which side the balance favours."],"codes":["3.b","4.a"]},{"q":"To what extent are global patterns of forced labour explained more by economic factors than by political and social factors?","a":"A judged answer weighing economic drivers (poverty, supply chain pressure) against political factors (weak governance, conflict, corruption) and social factors (discrimination, debt bondage norms), reaching a stated view on which matters most.","sol":"Economic factors do explain a large part of the pattern of forced labour: poverty pushes people into exploitative work, and cost pressure in global supply chains for goods such as agriculture, construction and textiles creates demand for cheap, unprotected labour. However, this is not simply a lower-income-region issue — the ILO's own estimates put over half of the world's forced labour in upper-middle-income and high-income countries, so economic pressure alone does not fully explain the global pattern.\n\nPolitical factors are also central — weak enforcement of labour law, corruption and conflict allow forced labour to continue even where laws against it exist on paper, in richer and poorer states alike — and social factors such as discrimination against migrants or particular ethnic groups, and debt bondage passed through generations, explain why forced labour persists even where poverty alone would not predict it. On balance, economic pressure creates conditions in which forced labour becomes attractive to exploiters, but it is weak political enforcement that allows it to continue regardless of income level, so the pattern is best explained by economic and political factors acting together rather than economic factors alone.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Recall the three categories of factor used elsewhere in this topic to explain variation in a human rights issue, and apply them here to forced labour specifically.","Consider whether each category of factor mainly explains why forced labour arises in the first place, or why it persists once it has, before deciding how to weigh them against each other.","End with a single sentence stating which factor, or combination, you judge to matter most, rather than listing all three with equal weight."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of the global governance of human rights in one named area of conflict.","a":"A named case (e.g. Sierra Leone) evaluated against what governance achieved (peacekeeping, prosecutions, disarmament, community recovery) versus its limits (partial reach, continuing poverty and insecurity), reaching a qualified judgement on effectiveness.","sol":"Global governance of human rights in Sierra Leone, following the 1991–2002 civil war, was effective in several clear respects. The UN peacekeeping mission (UNAMSIL) supported the disarmament of tens of thousands of former combatants, and the hybrid Special Court for Sierra Leone secured convictions of those most responsible for violations, including former Liberian president Charles Taylor, providing accountability that a purely national court may not have achieved. NGOs supported the reintegration of former child soldiers into their communities, addressing the human consequences of the conflict directly.\n\nHowever, effectiveness was limited in other respects: reconciliation and reintegration did not remove the underlying poverty and weak infrastructure that had contributed to the conflict, so many of the social conditions that made recruitment into armed groups attractive to young people persisted after the war ended. Justice through the Special Court also reached only a small number of the most senior perpetrators, leaving many lower-level violations unaddressed.\n\nOverall, governance in Sierra Leone was effective at ending large-scale violence and delivering some accountability and disarmament, but far less effective at addressing the underlying poverty and inequality that had enabled the violations in the first place, so its effectiveness should be judged as strong on security and justice, but limited on development.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Choose the named area-of-conflict case study and think about what governance was aiming to achieve at more than one scale.","Set out what was achieved separately from what remained unresolved, so you can compare the two directly.","Structure your answer as achievements first, limitations second, and a conclusion that judges effectiveness rather than simply listing both."],"codes":["3.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the difference between a human right and a norm?","a":"A human right is a basic entitlement everyone has; a norm is a widely shared expectation of behaviour that can vary between cultures and change over time."},{"q":"Name three human rights issues that show clear patterns of global variation.","a":"Forced labour, maternal mortality rates and capital punishment."},{"q":"Give one organisation involved in the case study of global governance in Sierra Leone.","a":"Any one of: the United Nations (UNAMSIL or the Special Court), the Sierra Leonean government, or an NGO."},{"q":"Give one short-term and one long-term consequence of global governance of human rights.","a":"Short-term: immediate relief from an NGO. Long-term: a change in laws."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Norm","back":"A widely shared expectation of how states or people should behave, which varies between cultures and changes over time.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Geopolitical intervention","back":"Action taken, often by an outside actor, to change a situation inside a state, ranging from sanctions and diplomacy to military action.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Three human rights issues with global patterns","back":"Forced labour, maternal mortality rates and capital punishment.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"ILO forced labour estimate","back":"Approximately 27.6 million people were in forced labour worldwide (ILO, 2021 estimate).","code":"1.b"},{"front":"UK Modern Slavery Act 2015","back":"Consolidated offences of forced labour, slavery and trafficking into one law and created the Independent Anti-Slavery Commissioner.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Three factors explaining gender inequality","back":"Economic (e.g. unequal pay), political (e.g. under-representation in government), and social (e.g. norms around marriage, education, reproductive health).","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Gender Inequality Index (GII)","back":"A UNDP index from 0 (equal) to 1 (unequal), measuring reproductive health, empowerment and labour market gaps between women and men.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Cause vs consequence of conflict","back":"Human rights violations can cause conflict (e.g. discrimination sparking grievance) and can be a consequence of conflict (e.g. civilians targeted, displaced).","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Flows in geopolitical intervention","back":"Flows of people, money, ideas and technology, e.g. peacekeepers, aid, human rights norms, and satellite monitoring.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"UDHR 1948","back":"The Universal Declaration of Human Rights, adopted by the UN General Assembly in 1948, sets out 30 articles of rights for every person.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Sierra Leone case: scales of governance","back":"Global: the UN deployed UNAMSIL peacekeepers and co-created the Special Court for Sierra Leone, which convicted Charles Taylor; national: the government ran disarmament and a Truth and Reconciliation Commission; local: NGOs such as Caritas Makeni rehabilitated and reunited former child soldiers.","code":"3.b","open":true},{"front":"Short-term vs long-term effect of governance","back":"Short-term: immediate relief, e.g. NGO food, shelter or medical care. Long-term: structural change, e.g. new laws or a new constitution.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Afghanistan case: opportunity","back":"Girls' school enrolment rose from close to zero to about 3.5 million by the late 2010s; the 2004 constitution guaranteed women's political participation, reserving roughly a quarter of parliamentary seats; maternal and child mortality fell as clinics and midwife training expanded.","code":"4.a","open":true},{"front":"Afghanistan case: challenge","back":"In August 2021 the Taliban retook control as international forces withdrew; girls' secondary schooling was suspended and women were barred from parliament and most public employment; the gains had depended on external funding and troops, so they proved reversible.","code":"4.a","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: 'To what extent has intervention in human rights contributed to development?' (16 marks)","In Afghanistan, intervention following 2001 removed a Taliban regime that had systematically denied girls education, and the governance strategies that followed — UNAMA's state-building support, the 2004 constitution guaranteeing women's political participation, and NGO-delivered education programmes — drove clear development gains: girls' school enrolment rose from close to zero to approximately 3.5 million by the late 2010s, and women returned to parliament in significant numbers under a constitutional quota reserving roughly a quarter of seats. However, these gains proved dependent on continued international presence rather than embedded in self-sustaining institutions, since when forces withdrew in August 2021 the Taliban rapidly reversed many of them, particularly girls' access to secondary education. This shows intervention contributed significantly to development while it was sustained, but that contribution was conditional rather than permanent, so its overall contribution to lasting development in Afghanistan was real but ultimately limited."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: 'To what extent has intervention in human rights contributed to development?' (16 marks)","Intervention in Afghanistan helped human rights because it stopped the Taliban and let girls go to school, which is a form of development. The UN and NGOs got involved and things got better for women. But then the Taliban came back in 2021 and it got worse again. So intervention did contribute to development but not forever."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: gender inequality in India","items":["India's Gender Inequality Index (GII) value is approximately 0.437, reflecting large gaps in reproductive health, political representation and labour force participation (UNDP HDR 2023/24, 2022 data).","India's female labour force participation rate is approximately 25%, far below the male rate.","India's skewed sex ratio at birth reflects a long-standing cultural preference for sons in parts of the country.","Maternal mortality in India has fallen substantially in recent decades but remains higher than in high-income countries, particularly in rural states.","The Beti Bachao Beti Padhao ('Save the daughter, educate the daughter') scheme, launched in 2015, is a government strategy addressing the declining child sex ratio and promoting girls' education.","The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act 2012 came into force in November 2012, before the widely reported December 2012 Delhi gang-rape case; the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013 was the law that followed public pressure for stronger sexual-violence laws after that case.","Consequences of gender inequality in India include a smaller effective workforce, lower household incomes where women cannot work, and continued underrepresentation of women in politics and business leadership."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: global governance of human rights in Sierra Leone","items":["Sierra Leone's civil war lasted from 1991 to 2002 and involved widespread human rights violations, including forced recruitment of child soldiers and mutilation of civilians by rebel forces.","The UN peacekeeping mission UNAMSIL was deployed in 1999 and, at its peak, was one of the UN's largest peacekeeping operations.","The Special Court for Sierra Leone, a hybrid UN–national court, convicted former Liberian president Charles Taylor in 2012 for his role in the conflict.","The Sierra Leonean government ran a Truth and Reconciliation Commission and a disarmament, demobilisation and reintegration (DDR) programme that disarmed tens of thousands of former combatants.","NGOs, including organisations supporting former child soldiers, worked on community-level rehabilitation and reintegration.","Consequences for local communities included greater security and access to justice, but reintegration did not remove the underlying poverty that had contributed to the conflict."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: global governance of human rights in Afghanistan (an LIDC)","items":["Afghanistan is classified as a low-income developing country (LIDC); international intervention began in 2001, removing the Taliban regime that had denied girls education and restricted women's rights.","The UN Assistance Mission in Afghanistan (UNAMA) supported state-building, alongside international military forces and NGOs delivering education and health programmes.","A new Afghan constitution, adopted in 2004, formally guaranteed women's political participation, and women returned to parliament in significant numbers under a constitutional quota reserving roughly a quarter of seats.","Girls' school enrolment rose from close to zero under Taliban rule to approximately 3.5 million by the late 2010s.","Corruption and continued insecurity limited how far aid and reforms reached rural areas throughout the intervention.","In August 2021, the Taliban retook control as international forces withdrew, reversing many legal and educational gains, particularly for girls' access to secondary education."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'To what extent has intervention in human rights contributed to development?' (16 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'intervention' = geopolitical action by an outside actor (state, UN, NGO) to change a human rights situation; 'development' = improvements in stability, growth and citizens' opportunities, not just economic growth; the question asks how far, so the conclusion must give a qualified answer, not a yes/no one.","Paragraph 1 (define and set out the case for intervention driving development): use the Afghanistan case study — UNAMA state-building, the 2004 constitution, and the rise in girls' school enrolment to approximately 3.5 million — to show intervention created real gains in education and political participation.","Paragraph 2 (a second line — intervention drives development, but conditionally): analyse how these gains depended on continued international funding and security rather than self-sustaining institutions, using the fact that corruption and insecurity limited reach into rural areas even during the intervention.","Paragraph 3 (counter-argument — the limits of intervention's contribution): analyse the reversal after the 2021 withdrawal, when the Taliban rapidly reversed many legal and educational gains, to show how much of the 'development' relied on outside presence rather than lasting change.","Evaluation thread: throughout, weigh how far each gain was embedded in Afghan institutions versus how far it depended on continued external support, rather than simply listing gains and losses separately.","Conclusion must: give a qualified judgement — intervention contributed significantly to development while it was sustained, particularly in education and political participation, but its contribution to lasting development was limited because so much of it reversed once external support was withdrawn."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.941Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"Every fault the first verdict listed is genuinely fixed — POCSO/Delhi 2012 chronology now correct and the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013 correctly attributed to post-Nirbhaya pressure; the GII table is sourced to UNDP HDR 2023/24 (2022 data) with a single consistent edition (Norway 0.012, India 0.437, Niger 0.609) and re-solves correctly to 0.425; the Niger/development sentence is reworded accurately; the forced-labour question is now 10 marks with a hint that frames rather than states the judgement; the spec meta-framing is gone from stems, sols and exit tickets; and new questions[5] makes the India case study (consequences plus Beti Bachao Beti Padhao and the 2013 Act) actually examinable, with lesson.idea 2.a naming India as the required case study. Fact files are India, Sierra Leone and Afghanistan, one per required case study; tariffs are 3/6/10/16 with no 'State' stems; Level 4 and Level 2 labels are truthful for the 16-mark four-level scheme; the ILO 27.6m ÷ 7.9bn = 0.35% example re-solves correct; all other figures (UDHR 1948, ECHR 1950, ~55 retentionist states, MMR ~223/100,000, Charles Taylor 2012) check out and are hedged or attributed. Publish after the one substantive fix — aligning the GII worked example with the re-sourced table — and the two hint rewrites; as polish, add '(2020)' to the maternal mortality fact, a year to the India GII figure in the fact file, and consider breaking questions[13]'s 10-mark model answer into two paragraphs, since at ~215 words it is barely longer than the 6-mark sols; its claim that economic pressure concentrates forced labour 'in lower-income regions' is also worth softening, as the ILO's own 2022 estimates put over half of forced labour in upper-middle and high-income countries."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:48:40.281Z"}},"2.2.4":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"2.2.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room underpins Global governance (Human interactions, Paper 02, Option D: Power and Borders), where the specification sets short answer questions, medium length questions of 6 to 10 marks and 16-mark extended responses, and it can also supply case-study evidence for the synoptic 12- and 33-mark questions on Geographical debates (Paper 03) if this option is carried forward. Every tariff tests these five ideas, and the three required case studies — a country where sovereignty has been challenged, an area of conflict showing global governance across scales, and the impact of global governance in one LIDC — supply the named evidence that separates a top-level analytical or evaluative answer from a generic, example-free one.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Sovereignty, territorial integrity and the vocabulary of global governance","t":"A state is a political-legal unit with defined borders, a permanent population and a sovereign government; a nation is a group sharing identity, culture or ethnicity, which may or may not match a state's borders. Sovereignty is a state's supreme authority to govern its own territory free of external interference, and territorial integrity is its recognised right to keep that territory intact from external threat. These are underpinned by international norms — widely accepted rules of state behaviour, such as non-recognition of forced border change — which interventions can test or break, and by geopolitics, the study of how power, resources and territory shape relations between states. A top-level answer defines these terms precisely against the specification's own wording before applying them, rather than using 'sovereignty' and 'independence' interchangeably."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Contemporary challenges to sovereign state authority","t":"Sovereignty and territorial integrity are eroded by economic, political, social and environmental factors. The specification names four: contested or unclear current political boundaries; transnational corporations (TNCs), whose economic power can outweigh a host government's; supranational institutions such as regional trading blocs, which require states to pool decision-making; and the political dominance of one ethnic group over others within a state's borders. The required case study — a country in which sovereignty has been challenged — must show both the causes of that challenge and its impacts on people and places, using named evidence. A top-level answer selects the factor(s) relevant to the question and develops each into a specific challenge for the named country, rather than listing all four in general terms."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Global governance as a framework for regulating conflict","t":"Challenges to sovereignty and territorial integrity can themselves cause conflict — for example, disputes over access to natural resources such as oil, gas or water. Global governance regulates this through institutions (such as the United Nations and its Security Council), treaties and international law, and norms of accepted state behaviour, which together work to reproduce the global system of sovereign nation-states rather than let force redraw it unchecked. Geopolitical intervention — diplomatic, military, economic or humanitarian — depends on flows of people (peacekeepers, diplomats, refugees), money (aid, sanctions, reconstruction funding), ideas (norms, resolutions) and technology (surveillance, weapons, communications). A top-level answer names the specific institution, treaty or flow doing the regulating, rather than describing 'the international community' acting as one body."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Global governance across scales — the Mali case study","t":"Global governance of conflict rarely comes from one actor: it depends on cooperation between organisations at scales from global to local, often working in partnership. The required case study of strategies for global governance in one area of conflict must show interventions and interactions between organisations at a range of scales, including the United Nations, a national government and an NGO, and must trace the consequences of that governance for local communities — which can be positive (protection, aid, restored services) or negative (continued insecurity, dependency, unmet expectations). A top-level answer names the organisation acting at each scale and links their interaction directly to an outcome for local communities, rather than treating 'the UN' and 'NGOs' as interchangeable, undifferentiated help."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Consequences of global governance for citizens and places — the Somalia case study","t":"The global governance of sovereignty and territorial integrity issues has consequences for citizens and places that unfold on different timescales: short-term effects include humanitarian aid and maintaining peace, while longer-term effects include changes in political regime and shifts in trade relationships. The required case study of the impact of global governance in one LIDC must identify the sovereignty or territorial integrity issue, the governance strategy or strategies used to address it, and weigh the opportunities this creates for stability, growth and development against the challenges of inequality and injustice it leaves unresolved. A top-level answer keeps both sides of that balance in view together, rather than presenting global governance as either wholly successful or wholly failed."}],"examples":[{"title":"Applying the definition of sovereignty — Crimea, 2014","setup":"Sovereignty means a state's supreme authority to govern its own territory without outside interference. In February–March 2014 Russian forces occupied Crimea and a referendum on joining Russia was held under military occupation; on 27 March 2014 the UN General Assembly adopted Resolution 68/262, declaring the referendum invalid and affirming Ukraine's territorial integrity within its internationally recognised borders. Explain what this non-recognition achieves as an act of global governance, and what it does not.","steps":["Test the referendum against the definition: it was organised while a foreign army held the peninsula, so the authority actually being exercised over Crimean territory in March 2014 was Russia's, and a vote run by the occupier cannot express the sovereignty of the occupied state.","Russia holds one of the five permanent seats on the Security Council and vetoed the draft resolution there, so the question moved to the General Assembly, where there is no veto: Resolution 68/262 passed with 100 states in favour, 11 against and 58 abstaining.","The vote applied existing rules rather than inventing new ones: Article 2(1) of the UN Charter makes states sovereign equals, and Article 2(3) and 2(4) require disputes to be settled peacefully and forbid the use of force against the territorial integrity of any state.","So non-recognition works on legitimacy, not on territory: Russia keeps physical control of Crimea, but is denied legal title, other states and institutions continue to deal with Ukraine's recognised borders, and the refusal grounds later sanctions and Russia's exclusion from the G8 — the norm of territorial integrity is reproduced even where it cannot be enforced."],"cues":["Does a vote held under occupation meet the definition given?","Why did the General Assembly rather than the Security Council act?","Which existing rules does the resolution rest on?","What does non-recognition change, and what does it leave untouched?"]},{"title":"Calculating the scale of displacement — Ukraine since 2022","setup":"By 2023 approximately 6 million Ukrainians were registered as refugees abroad, mainly across Europe (UNHCR), and approximately 5 million more were internally displaced inside Ukraine. The pre-war population was approximately 41 million. Work out the total number displaced and express it as a percentage of that pre-war population.","steps":["Refugees abroad and internally displaced people are both displaced, so the two groups add: 6,000,000 + 5,000,000 = 11,000,000 people.","Divide the displaced total by the pre-war population: 11,000,000 ÷ 41,000,000 = 0.268.","Multiply by 100 to turn the proportion into a percentage: 0.268 × 100 = 26.8%, which rounds to roughly 27%.","So about 11 million people, roughly 27% of the pre-war population — close to 1 in 4 Ukrainians — had been displaced from their homes."],"cues":["Which two groups make up the displaced total?","What do you divide the displaced total by?","How do you turn that proportion into a percentage?","What is the answer, in people and as a share of the population?"]},{"title":"Naming an opportunity and a challenge — global governance in Somalia","setup":"Somalia is your case study of the impact of global governance of sovereignty in an LIDC: African Union peacekeeping has supported the federal government against the Al-Shabaab insurgency. Using that case study, give one opportunity for stability, growth or development and one challenge of inequality or injustice, and say what the two together show about what governance has achieved there.","steps":["Opportunity: the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), which succeeded AMISOM in 2022, helped Somali federal forces retake towns from Al-Shabaab, and the security that created allowed the 2022 presidential election to be held in Mogadishu.","Challenge: Al-Shabaab still held large areas of southern and central Somalia through the mid-2020s, levying its own taxes and running its own courts, while clan-based inequality left many regions without reliable access to services.","The support is also temporary: ATMIS was designed to draw down in 4 phases and hand security to Somali forces by the end of 2024, so the gains depend on a national army that is still being built.","Together they show that governance in Somalia has bought security rather than sovereignty: enough stability for a national election to take place, the short-term opportunity, but a state that still cannot govern much of its own territory or share services evenly, the longer-term challenge of inequality and injustice."],"cues":["Which AU mission followed AMISOM, and what did it make possible?","What stood in the way at the same time?","How long is the African Union presence meant to last?","What do the two together show about what governance achieved?"]},{"title":"Tracing intervention across scales — the Mali case study","setup":"Mali is your case study of strategies for global governance in one area of conflict: Islamist and separatist forces advanced in the north after the rebellion and coup of 2012, and outside intervention followed. Trace who acted at all 3 scales — global, national and local/NGO — and judge whether the cooperation between those scales lasted.","steps":["Global scale: France launched Operation Serval in January 2013 to halt the advance south, and in April 2013 the UN Security Council authorised MINUSMA, which took over from the African-led force with a mandate to protect civilians and support the political process.","National scale: Mali's government worked with MINUSMA to restore state authority in northern towns such as Timbuktu and Gao, re-establishing administration, courts and security forces where the state had lost control.","Local and NGO scale: NGOs such as the Norwegian Refugee Council delivered humanitarian aid — shelter, food assistance and support for displaced families — in the communities the fighting had emptied.","The cooperation did not hold: in June 2023 Mali's transitional government, installed by the coups of 2020 and 2021 and by then relying on Russian Wagner forces, demanded MINUSMA's departure, the Security Council ended the mandate, and withdrawal was completed by 31 December 2023.","So the chain runs global (Serval, then MINUSMA), national (the Malian government in Timbuktu and Gao) and local (the Norwegian Refugee Council and other NGOs with the displaced) — but cooperation lasted only as long as the host government consented, so governance across scales is a partnership a sovereign state can end, not control exercised over it."],"cues":["Who intervened first at the global scale, and in what order?","Which scale of government worked with MINUSMA, and in which towns?","Who reached the displaced communities, and with what?","Who could bring the peacekeeping mission to an end?","Did cooperation across the scales last, and why?"]},{"title":"Linking a resource dispute to a governance response — idea 3.a","setup":"In the South China Sea, China's 'nine-dash line' claim overlaps the exclusive economic zones claimed by the Philippines, Vietnam, Malaysia and Brunei, and the contested waters hold oil, gas and some of the world's richest fishing grounds. Explain how institutions, treaties and norms responded to this dispute over access to natural resources, and judge how far they regulated it.","steps":["Start with the treaty: the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea, ratified by both China and the Philippines, gives a coastal state an exclusive economic zone reaching 200 nautical miles from its coast, and it is that rule the nine-dash line cuts across.","Then the institution: the Philippines brought a case under the convention in 2013, and in July 2016 the arbitral tribunal at The Hague ruled that the nine-dash line had no legal basis and that China had breached Philippine rights inside its exclusive economic zone, including at Mischief Reef.","The norm is where it stops: China took no part in the case and rejected the award, no body exists to enforce it, and by then China had already built airstrips on 3 reefs — Mischief, Subi and Fiery Cross; ASEAN and China have negotiated over a Code of Conduct since the 2002 Declaration on the Conduct of Parties without agreeing one.","Judgement: treaties and institutions regulated this resource dispute without settling it — the convention supplied the rule and the tribunal stripped the claim of legitimacy, which rival claimants and other states now cite against China — but because compliance rests on consent, who actually reaches the oil, gas and fish is still decided by presence and patrols rather than by law."],"cues":["Which treaty sets the rule the claim cuts across?","Which body did the Philippines turn to, and under what?","What happens when a ruling meets a state that rejects it?","How far did law regulate this dispute?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between a state and a nation?","a":"A state is a political-legal unit with defined borders and a sovereign government; a nation is a group sharing identity, culture or ethnicity, which may not match state borders."},{"q":"Name the four factors the specification names as challenging sovereignty and territorial integrity.","a":"Current political boundaries, transnational corporations (TNCs), supranational institutions (e.g. regional trading blocs), and the political dominance of ethnic groups."},{"q":"Which three scales of organisation does the Mali case study need to show interacting?","a":"The United Nations, the national government (of Mali) and an NGO."},{"q":"Give one short-term and one long-term consequence of global governance for citizens, as the specification names them.","a":"Short-term: humanitarian aid or maintaining peace. Long-term: a change in political regime or a change in trade relationships."}]},"room":{"facts":["Sovereignty = a state's supreme authority to govern its own territory without external interference; territorial integrity = its right to keep that territory intact from external threat.","Four named challenges to sovereignty/territorial integrity: current political boundaries; transnational corporations (TNCs); supranational institutions (e.g. regional trading blocs); political dominance of ethnic groups.","The sovereignty case study (Ukraine) must show the causes and challenges to the government, and the impacts on people and places.","The global governance case study (Mali) must show intervention/interaction across three scales — the United Nations, a national government and an NGO — and the consequences for local communities.","The LIDC case study (Somalia) must identify the sovereignty/territorial integrity issue, the governance strategy used, opportunities for stability/growth/development, and challenges of inequality/injustice.","Short-term consequences of global governance named by the specification: humanitarian aid, maintaining peace. Long-term consequences: changes in political regime, changes in trade relationships.","The UN General Assembly declared Russia's 2014 Crimea referendum invalid (Resolution 68/262); non-recognition is itself an act of global governance even without restoring territory."],"questions":[{"q":"Define sovereignty and territorial integrity, using the specification's own terms.","a":"Sovereignty: a state's supreme authority to govern its own territory without external interference. Territorial integrity: a state's right to keep that territory intact and free from external threat or change.","sol":"Sovereignty is a state's supreme authority to govern its own territory and population without interference from other states or external actors. Territorial integrity is closely related but distinct: it is a state's recognised right to keep its territory intact, meaning its borders should not be changed or reduced by external force or annexation. Together, these two principles underpin the world political map of sovereign nation-states, and international norms exist specifically to protect them, such as the widespread refusal to recognise borders changed by force.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what makes a state's authority different from a neighbour's opinion about how it should be run.","Separate 'authority to govern' from 'the physical extent of that territory' before you write your two definitions.","Structure your answer as: define sovereignty → define territorial integrity."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify two of the four factors the specification names as challenging a state's sovereignty and territorial integrity.","a":"Any two of: current political boundaries; transnational corporations (TNCs); supranational institutions (e.g. regional trading blocs); political dominance of ethnic groups.","sol":"The specification names four factors that can challenge a state's sovereignty and territorial integrity. Two of these are transnational corporations (TNCs), whose economic power can be large enough to influence or override a host government's decisions, and supranational institutions such as regional trading blocs, which require member states to pool some of their own decision-making power in exchange for the benefits of membership. Any two of the four named factors are creditable.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Focus on the specification's own defined list of factors, not any general challenge to sovereignty you can think of.","Recall the specification's own list rather than migration or conflict factors from elsewhere in the course.","Structure your answer as: name factor one → name factor two."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Identify the three scales of organisation the specification requires the global governance case study to show interacting, and name one organisation at each scale.","a":"Global: the United Nations; national: the national government (of the country in the case study); local/NGO: a named non-governmental organisation.","sol":"The specification requires the case study of strategies for global governance in one area of conflict to show interventions and interactions of organisations across a range of scales, naming three specifically: the United Nations at the global scale, a national government at the national scale, and an NGO at the local or non-state scale. In Mali, this is illustrated by the UN's MINUSMA peacekeeping mission, Mali's own national government, and an NGO such as the Norwegian Refugee Council delivering humanitarian aid.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the scale words the specification itself uses — from the whole world down to the community.","Match a real organisation from your case study to each of the three named scales.","Structure your answer as: global scale, named → national scale, named → local/NGO scale, named."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Identify one short-term and one long-term consequence of global governance for citizens and places, as the specification names them.","a":"Short-term: humanitarian aid, or maintaining peace. Long-term: a change in political regime, or a change in trade relationships.","sol":"The specification names paired short-term and long-term consequences of global governance for citizens and places. For sovereignty issues, a short-term effect is humanitarian aid, while a longer-term effect is a change in political regime. For territorial integrity issues, a short-term effect is maintaining peace, while a longer-term effect is a change in trade relationships. Any one correctly matched short-term and one long-term consequence is creditable.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what happens in the days or months after an intervention, compared with what might change years later.","Pick one example the specification pairs together, rather than mixing a sovereignty effect with an unrelated territorial integrity effect.","Structure your answer as: short-term consequence, named → long-term consequence, named."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Explain how transnational corporations (TNCs) and supranational institutions can each challenge a state's sovereignty.","a":"TNCs can outweigh a host government's economic and political power, shaping national policy in their own interest; supranational institutions require member states to pool decision-making, meaning national governments no longer hold sole authority over certain decisions.","sol":"Transnational corporations (TNCs) can challenge state sovereignty because their revenues and economic influence can exceed the resources of the governments hosting them, giving them leverage to negotiate favourable tax arrangements, regulation or land use, effectively shifting some decision-making power away from the state and towards a private, non-elected actor. Supranational institutions, such as regional trading blocs, challenge sovereignty differently: rather than a private actor gaining leverage, member states voluntarily pool part of their own decision-making authority — for example over trade rules or product standards — into a shared body, meaning that decisions once made entirely by the national government are now made jointly, or by the institution itself. Both processes reduce the practical scope of a government's independent authority, even though the state remains formally sovereign in international law.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Explain the specific way each actor limits government authority, rather than only describing what a TNC or a supranational institution is.","For each factor, explain what specifically the state loses control over as a result.","Structure your answer as: TNC challenge, explained → supranational institution challenge, explained."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how access to natural resources can be a cause of conflict linked to sovereignty and territorial integrity, and how global governance can attempt to regulate it.","a":"Contested control over resources such as oil, gas or water can drive states or groups into conflict over borders or authority; institutions such as the UN Security Council, treaties, or sanctions can be used to try to regulate or de-escalate that conflict.","sol":"Access to natural resources is named directly by the specification as a way that challenges to sovereignty and territorial integrity can cause conflict: where valuable resources such as oil, gas or water sit near or across a contested border, both the state and any rival claimant have a strong incentive to assert authority over that territory, which can escalate into armed conflict. Global governance attempts to regulate this in several ways: the UN Security Council can pass resolutions calling for a ceasefire or authorising peacekeepers; international treaties, such as maritime boundary agreements, can set out an agreed division of a resource in advance; and sanctions can be used to raise the cost of continued conflict for the states or groups involved. None of these mechanisms guarantees the underlying resource dispute is resolved, but they provide a framework through which the conflict can be regulated rather than left to force alone.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think of a resource that sits near or across a contested border, and why both sides would want to control it.","Separate the cause (the resource dispute) from the response (the governance mechanism) before you explain either.","Structure your answer as: explain the resource-driven cause → explain the governance mechanism used to regulate it."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"By 2023, approximately 6 million Ukrainians were registered as refugees abroad (mainly across Europe), and approximately 5 million more were internally displaced within Ukraine. Ukraine's pre-war population (2021) was approximately 41 million. (a) Calculate the total number of Ukrainians displaced by 2023, and express this as a percentage of the pre-war population. (b) Explain one short-term and one long-term consequence of this scale of displacement, as named by the specification.","a":"(a) Total displaced ≈ 6,000,000 + 5,000,000 = 11,000,000; as a percentage of 41,000,000 this is approximately 27%. (b) Short-term: humanitarian aid is needed for displaced people and refugee-hosting states; long-term: a shift in Ukraine's trade relationships and labour market as displacement continues.","sol":"(a) The total number of Ukrainians displaced by 2023 is calculated by adding the refugees registered abroad to those internally displaced: 6,000,000 + 5,000,000 = 11,000,000. Expressed as a percentage of the pre-war population of approximately 41,000,000, this is (11,000,000 ÷ 41,000,000) × 100 ≈ 27%, meaning roughly a quarter Ukrainians had been displaced from their home by this point. (b) In the short term, this scale of displacement requires humanitarian aid, both inside Ukraine for internally displaced people and in neighbouring and European states hosting refugees, straining housing, healthcare and education services. In the longer term, it changes Ukraine's trade relationships and labour supply: a large share of Ukraine's working-age population is now abroad, affecting Ukraine's own economic output, while host states have had to adjust labour markets and, in some cases, trade and aid arrangements to accommodate a sustained refugee population, illustrating the specification's own long-term consequence of changed trade relationships.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Add the two displaced groups together before turning the total into a percentage of the starting population.","For part (b), match one effect to the short timescale and a different effect to the long timescale, rather than repeating the same idea twice.","Structure your answer as: (a) the calculation, shown → (b) short-term consequence → long-term consequence."],"codes":["4.a","2.a"]},{"q":"Explain two challenges Russia's actions since 2014 have created for Ukraine's government.","a":"Loss of effective control over Crimea and, later, parts of eastern Ukraine, reducing the territory the government can govern; and the ongoing military, economic and diplomatic cost of responding to occupation and invasion, diverting resources from other government functions.","sol":"One challenge is the loss of effective territorial control: following the 2014 occupation and annexation of Crimea, and the escalation into full-scale invasion in 2022, Ukraine's government has been unable to exercise authority over Crimea and parts of the eastern Donbas region, undermining its territorial integrity in practice even though these areas remain part of Ukraine in international law and are not recognised as Russian by most states. A second challenge is the sustained cost of responding to this occupation and invasion: Ukraine's government has had to redirect a large share of its budget and administrative capacity towards defence, air-defence systems and managing mass civilian displacement, resources that would otherwise have supported ordinary government functions such as public services and economic development. Together, these challenges show how a sovereignty and territorial integrity challenge can weaken a government's practical authority even where its legal sovereignty is not disputed by most of the international community.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about one challenge to the physical territory the government controls, and one challenge to how it spends its resources.","Keep the legal position (Ukraine remains sovereign in most states' eyes) separate from the practical position (what the government can actually do).","Structure your answer as: territorial challenge, explained → resource/capacity challenge, explained."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Analyse the interventions and interactions of organisations at a range of scales in the Mali case study.","a":"The UN Security Council authorised MINUSMA (global scale) alongside France's Operation Serval; Mali's national government worked with MINUSMA to try to restore authority in northern towns (national scale); NGOs such as the Norwegian Refugee Council delivered humanitarian aid (local/NGO scale) — but these interactions were not always well coordinated, and Mali's 2023 withdrawal request shows the partnership broke down.","sol":"At the global scale, the UN Security Council authorised the MINUSMA peacekeeping mission in Mali from 2013, working alongside France's separately mandated Operation Serval, which had intervened militarily against Islamist and Tuareg separatist forces that had taken control of northern Mali in 2012. At the national scale, Mali's own government depended on this external support to attempt to restore state authority over towns such as Timbuktu and Gao, since its own forces had been unable to prevent the 2012 takeover. At the local and non-state scale, NGOs such as the Norwegian Refugee Council worked alongside, but independently of, MINUSMA and the government, delivering humanitarian aid — shelter, food assistance — directly to communities displaced by the fighting. These interactions show real cooperation across scales, with each actor filling a role the others could not: peacekeeping and security from the UN, political authority from the national government, and humanitarian relief from the NGO. However, the interaction was not seamless: MINUSMA became one of the deadliest UN missions in its history: approximately 300 peacekeepers died, including around 180 killed by hostile acts, by 2023, and after a 2021 military coup Mali's transitional government turned towards Russia's Wagner Group instead, ordering MINUSMA's withdrawal in 2023. This shows that intervention across scales can function well for a period, but the national-scale actor can also withdraw its cooperation, breaking the partnership the global and local scales depended on.","m":10,"d":2,"hints":["Work through the three named scales in order, naming a real organisation at each before analysing how they interacted.","Look for evidence of both cooperation and, later, breakdown between the scales, rather than assuming the partnership held throughout.","Structure your answer as: global-scale actor, explained → national-scale actor, explained → local/NGO-scale actor, explained → how they interacted, including any breakdown."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain two consequences of the African Union's intervention in Somalia for local communities.","a":"Positive: some towns were retaken from Al-Shabaab, allowing services and elections to resume, e.g. the 2022 presidential election in Mogadishu. Negative: insecurity continued in areas still contested or controlled by Al-Shabaab, and communities there saw little benefit from the intervention.","sol":"One consequence for local communities has been positive: the African Union's peacekeeping presence in Somalia — AMISOM until 2022, then ATMIS — supported Somali federal forces in retaking urban centres from Al-Shabaab, which allowed some public services and government administration to resume in those towns, and created the security conditions for Somalia's 2022 presidential election to take place in Mogadishu. A second consequence has been far less positive: in areas of southern and central Somalia that Al-Shabaab continued to control or contest through the mid-2020s, local communities saw comparatively little benefit from the intervention, remaining exposed to insurgent violence, taxation by Al-Shabaab, and limited access to state services. This uneven pattern shows that an intervention's consequences for local communities can depend heavily on which specific place is examined, rather than applying evenly across the whole country.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think of one place that gained from the intervention, and one that has continued to face insecurity despite it.","Link each consequence to a specific outcome for people in that place, not just to the intervention itself.","Structure your answer as: positive consequence, located → negative consequence, located."],"codes":["3.b","4.a"]},{"q":"To what extent global governance strategies in Somalia have created opportunities for stability, growth and development, set against the challenges of inequality and injustice they have left unresolved.","a":"Opportunities: ATMIS/AMISOM enabled retaking of towns, the 2022 election, some economic recovery in Mogadishu; challenges: continued Al-Shabaab control of rural areas, clan-based inequality, aid dependency — overall a partial, geographically uneven success.","sol":"Global governance strategies in Somalia — principally the African Union's AMISOM mission (2007–2022) and its successor ATMIS, alongside UN Security Council-authorised sanctions and an arms embargo — have created genuine opportunities for stability, growth and development in parts of the country. Security gains in Mogadishu and other retaken towns allowed the resumption of trade, construction and public administration, and created the conditions for Somalia's 2022 presidential election to proceed, a marker of political stability that would have been unimaginable during the state collapse of the early 1990s. These are real, if partial, achievements of sustained multilateral peacekeeping. Set against this, however, are challenges of inequality and injustice that the same governance strategies have not resolved. Al-Shabaab continued to control or contest large areas of southern and central Somalia through the mid-2020s, meaning communities there have been largely excluded from the stability and growth visible in Mogadishu, so the benefits of intervention have been geographically very uneven. Clan-based political and economic structures have also persisted, meaning gains in stability have not translated evenly into reduced inequality between clans or regions, and Somalia's government and economy remain heavily dependent on continued international aid and troop contributions rather than having built self-sustaining institutions. On balance, global governance in Somalia has created real but narrow opportunities — concentrated in the capital and other secured towns — while leaving both the territorial challenge of Al-Shabaab's continued control and the structural challenge of clan-based inequality substantially unresolved, so the strategies used should be assessed as a partial rather than a decisive success.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Set out the clearest evidence of stability or growth before turning to evidence of unresolved inequality or injustice.","Think about whether the opportunities you identify apply evenly across the whole country, or mainly in one place.","Structure your answer as: opportunities, evidenced → challenges, evidenced → a weighed conclusion on how far governance has succeeded overall."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"'To what extent has global governance been effective in protecting Ukraine's sovereignty and territorial integrity since 2014?'","a":"Effective in diplomatic/legal terms (non-recognition of Crimea, sanctions, ICJ proceedings, EU candidacy) but largely ineffective in restoring territory, chiefly because the UN Security Council is structurally blocked by Russia's own veto as a P5 member — so effectiveness is real but partial.","sol":"Global governance has had a real, if limited, effect in protecting Ukraine's sovereignty and territorial integrity since 2014. Diplomatically and legally, the UN General Assembly's Resolution 68/262 declared Russia's 2014 Crimea referendum invalid and affirmed Ukraine's borders, and most states continue to withhold recognition of Russian control over Crimea and the occupied areas of eastern Ukraine, denying the annexation legal legitimacy even though it has not reversed it. Coordinated sanctions by the EU, the USA and other states have imposed a sustained economic cost on Russia, and international bodies including the International Court of Justice have heard cases relating to the conflict. Longer-term governance responses have also opened new opportunities for Ukraine, including EU candidate status granted in 2022, a shift in trade and political relationships consistent with the specification's own long-term consequence. However, this effectiveness has clear limits. The central body for enforcing global governance of conflict, the UN Security Council, has been structurally unable to act because Russia itself holds a permanent seat and veto power, meaning no Security Council resolution condemning Russia's actions or authorising intervention can pass. As a result, global governance has not restored Ukrainian sovereignty over Crimea or the occupied eastern territories, and the war that began with a full-scale invasion in 2022 continued for years afterwards despite sustained diplomatic and economic pressure. On balance, global governance has been effective at isolating Russia diplomatically, sustaining economic pressure, and opening longer-term opportunities such as EU accession, but it has not been effective at its most basic aim — restoring Ukraine's territorial integrity — because the UN system's veto structure gives a permanent member the power to block action against itself, a structural limitation the case reveals clearly.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the diplomatic and economic responses from the question of whether Ukraine's actual territory has been restored.","Think about why the UN Security Council in particular has struggled to act in this specific case, given who sits on it.","Structure your answer as: evidence of effectiveness → evidence of ineffectiveness, with the structural reason → a conclusion stating how far, overall, global governance has protected Ukraine."],"codes":["1.a","2.a","3.a"]},{"q":"'How effective is global governance of conflict when intervention occurs across multiple scales?' Use the Mali case study in your answer.","a":"Multi-scale intervention (UN MINUSMA, Malian government, NGOs) achieved short-term stabilisation of some towns and humanitarian access, but longer-term effectiveness was undermined by continuing insurgency, high peacekeeper casualties, and the national-scale partner withdrawing its cooperation in 2023 — so effectiveness fell away over time even though the multi-scale structure was well designed in principle.","sol":"Intervention across multiple scales in Mali was effective in the short to medium term. The UN's MINUSMA mission, authorised in 2013 alongside France's Operation Serval, worked with Mali's national government to help restore state authority in towns such as Timbuktu and Gao, while NGOs including the Norwegian Refugee Council delivered humanitarian aid directly to displaced communities, filling a role neither the UN nor the national government was best placed to provide. This division of roles — security and political authority from the global and national scales, humanitarian relief from the local/NGO scale — is exactly the kind of complementary, multi-scale cooperation global governance frameworks aim for, and it did produce real short-term outcomes: urban centres retaken, aid delivered, some restoration of state services. Over the longer term, however, the same case shows how multi-scale effectiveness can fall away. MINUSMA became one of the deadliest UN peacekeeping missions in its history: approximately 300 peacekeepers died, including around 180 killed by hostile acts, by 2023, while insurgent violence spread into central Mali and across the border into Burkina Faso and Niger rather than being contained. Most significantly, the national-scale partner the intervention depended on changed: following a 2021 military coup, Mali's transitional government turned to Russia's Wagner Group for security support instead and ordered MINUSMA's withdrawal, which was completed by the end of 2023. This shows a structural weakness of multi-scale governance: it depends on continued cooperation from the national government, and if that government's priorities change, the global and local scales can lose their partner and their access. On balance, global governance of conflict across multiple scales can be effective at achieving short-term stabilisation and humanitarian access, but Mali shows this effectiveness is conditional on sustained political cooperation at the national scale, which is not guaranteed to continue, so its longer-term effectiveness is much less certain than its short-term record suggests.","m":16,"d":3,"hints":["Trace what each scale achieved separately before judging how well they worked together.","Pay particular attention to what changed for the intervention once Mali's own national government's position changed.","Structure your answer as: short-term effectiveness, evidenced → longer-term limitation, evidenced → a conclusion on how far multi-scale intervention is effective overall."],"codes":["3.a","3.b","4.a"]},{"q":"Compare the challenge to sovereignty illustrated by the Ukraine case study with the challenge to territorial integrity illustrated by the Somalia case study.","a":"Ukraine: an external state (Russia) directly annexed/occupied territory, a challenge from outside; Somalia: an internal non-state actor (Al-Shabaab) contests government control of its own territory, a challenge from within — so the source of the challenge (external state vs internal non-state actor) is the key contrast, though both reduce the government's effective control and both have prompted an international governance response.","sol":"The Ukraine and Somalia case studies illustrate two different sources of challenge to a state's authority over its own territory. In Ukraine, the challenge came from an external state: Russia's 2014 occupation and annexation of Crimea, followed by full-scale invasion in 2022, directly challenged Ukraine's sovereignty by asserting another recognised state's authority over Ukrainian territory. In Somalia, by contrast, the challenge to territorial integrity has come mainly from an internal, non-state actor: the insurgent group Al-Shabaab, which has controlled or contested large areas of southern and central Somalia, denying the Somali federal government authority over parts of its own recognised territory without any other state formally claiming that land. Both cases reduce the government's effective control over its territory and have prompted a global governance response — non-recognition and sanctions against Russia in Ukraine's case, African Union peacekeeping (AMISOM/ATMIS) in Somalia's case — but the nature of the response differs to match the source of the challenge: Ukraine's case centres on diplomatic and legal non-recognition of an external state's claim, since removing a state actor by force risks wider war, while Somalia's case centres on military and security support to help the government itself regain control from a non-state actor. The key contrast, then, is not the scale of disruption but its source: an external state asserting territorial claims in Ukraine, against an internal non-state actor contesting government authority in Somalia.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Identify who is doing the challenging in each case before comparing how governments and global governance have responded.","Look for one similarity as well as the contrast, rather than treating the two cases as having nothing in common.","Structure your answer as: source of Ukraine's challenge → source of Somalia's challenge → the contrast this creates, with one shared feature noted."],"codes":["2.a","4.a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What does sovereignty mean?","a":"A state's supreme authority to govern its own territory without external interference."},{"q":"Name one organisation the Mali case study shows working at the national scale.","a":"The Malian national government."},{"q":"Which UN resolution declared the 2014 Crimea referendum invalid?","a":"UN General Assembly Resolution 68/262."},{"q":"Name one long-term consequence of global governance for citizens and places named by the specification.","a":"A change in political regime, or a change in trade relationships."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Sovereignty vs territorial integrity","back":"Sovereignty = supreme authority to govern own territory without external interference. Territorial integrity = the right to keep that territory intact from external threat.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"State vs nation","back":"A state is a political-legal unit with defined borders and government; a nation is a group sharing identity/culture, which may not match state borders.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Norms, intervention, geopolitics","back":"Norms = widely accepted rules of state behaviour; intervention = outside action in a state's affairs; geopolitics = how power, resources and territory shape relations between states.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Four named challenges to sovereignty","back":"Current political boundaries; TNCs; supranational institutions (e.g. trading blocs); political dominance of ethnic groups.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Ukraine case study — required content","back":"Causes and challenges to the government, and impacts on people and places, from Russia's 2014 Crimea annexation and 2022 invasion.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"UNGA Resolution 68/262 (2014)","back":"Declared Russia's Crimea referendum invalid and affirmed Ukraine's territorial integrity within its recognised borders.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Natural resources as a conflict trigger","back":"South China Sea: oil, gas and fisheries under waters claimed by China, the Philippines, Vietnam, Malaysia and Brunei; the Nile, where Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam alarms downstream Egypt and Sudan; oilfields on the Sudan–South Sudan border, contested since separation in 2011.","code":"3.a","open":true},{"front":"Global governance tools for regulating conflict","back":"Institutions (e.g. UN Security Council), treaties, international law, and norms; flows of people, money, ideas and technology support geopolitical intervention.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Mali case study — three scales required","back":"UN (MINUSMA, from 2013), national government (Mali), NGO (e.g. Norwegian Refugee Council) — must show their interaction and the consequences for local communities.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"MINUSMA's record in Mali","back":"Deployed 2013; approximately 300 peacekeepers died, including around 180 killed by hostile acts, by 2023; Mali's government ordered its withdrawal, completed by end of 2023.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Short-term vs long-term consequences of global governance","back":"Short-term: humanitarian aid, maintaining peace. Long-term: change in political regime, change in trade relationships.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Somalia case study — required content","back":"The sovereignty/territorial integrity issue (Al-Shabaab insurgency), the governance strategy (AU peacekeeping — AMISOM/ATMIS), opportunities (e.g. 2022 election) and challenges (inequality, injustice).","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Ukraine displacement scale","back":"By 2023: approximately 6 million refugees abroad and approximately 5 million internally displaced — around 27% of the approximately 41 million pre-war population.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"AMISOM to ATMIS transition","back":"The African Union's peacekeeping mission in Somalia (AMISOM, from 2007) transitioned to ATMIS in 2022, supporting Somali forces against Al-Shabaab.","code":"4.a"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4: how effective is global governance of conflict across multiple scales? (Mali)","items":["One measure of the effectiveness of global governance in Mali's conflict is how far cooperation between organisations at different scales actually reached local communities, rather than just changing the picture at a national level. The UN's MINUSMA mission, deployed from 2013 alongside France's Operation Serval, worked with the Malian national government to help restore state control over northern towns such as Timbuktu and Gao that had fallen to Islamist and Tuareg separatist forces in 2012, while NGOs such as the Norwegian Refugee Council delivered humanitarian aid, including shelter and food assistance, to communities displaced by the fighting. This multi-scale intervention was effective in the short term: state authority was restored to several urban centres and humanitarian access improved. However, its longer-term effectiveness for local communities is far more limited: MINUSMA became one of the deadliest UN peacekeeping missions in its history: approximately 300 peacekeepers died, including around 180 killed by hostile acts, by 2023, while insurgent violence continued to spread into central Mali and neighbouring Burkina Faso and Niger rather than being contained. When Mali's transitional military government ordered MINUSMA's withdrawal in 2023, following a 2021 coup and a turn towards Russian Wagner Group support instead, the mission's decade of intervention closed without having secured a durable peace, showing that cooperation across scales can stabilise a conflict in the short term without resolving the underlying challenge to sovereignty that caused it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2: how effective is global governance of conflict across multiple scales? (Mali)","items":["Global governance has helped Mali because the UN sent peacekeepers called MINUSMA to help stop the fighting. France also sent soldiers to help the Malian government fight against the rebels in the north. NGOs also helped by giving aid to people who needed it. This shows that global governance from different scales worked together to help Mali. However, the fighting continued in some places and it was not always safe. Overall, global governance was quite effective in Mali because different organisations worked together to help, even though there were still some problems."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — Ukraine (sovereignty challenged)","items":["Population: approximately 41 million (2021, pre-war estimate).","Russia occupied and annexed Crimea in February–March 2014, following a referendum organised under military occupation and not recognised by the UN General Assembly (Resolution 68/262, 2014).","Conflict also began in 2014 in the eastern Donbas region (Donetsk and Luhansk), where Russian-backed separatists declared unrecognised 'people's republics'.","Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022, extending the territorial conflict far beyond Crimea and Donbas.","By 2023, approximately 6 million Ukrainians were registered as refugees abroad (mainly across Europe), and approximately 5 million more were internally displaced.","Global response: non-recognition of the Crimea annexation by most states, coordinated EU/US/allied economic sanctions on Russia, and Ukraine's EU candidate status granted in 2022.","Russia's permanent seat and veto power on the UN Security Council has prevented that body from passing binding resolutions against Russia's actions in this conflict."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — Mali (global governance in conflict)","items":["Population: approximately 22–23 million (2023).","In 2012, Islamist and Tuareg separatist ('Azawad') forces took control of towns across northern Mali, including Timbuktu and Gao.","France launched Operation Serval in January 2013 to support the Malian government against these forces; it was later replaced by the wider Operation Barkhane.","The UN Security Council authorised the MINUSMA peacekeeping mission in April 2013 to support stabilisation, protection of civilians and restoration of state authority.","MINUSMA became one of the deadliest UN peacekeeping missions in its history: approximately 300 peacekeepers died, including around 180 killed by hostile acts, by 2023.","NGOs such as the Norwegian Refugee Council provided humanitarian aid — including shelter and food assistance — to communities displaced by the conflict.","Following a 2021 military coup, Mali's transitional government turned towards Russia's Wagner Group for security support and ordered MINUSMA's withdrawal in 2023, completed by the end of that year."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — Somalia (LIDC, global governance of sovereignty/territorial integrity)","items":["Population: approximately 18 million (2023); one of the world's lowest-income developing countries (LIDCs).","Somalia's central government effectively collapsed in 1991, leading to decades of civil conflict and the rise of the insurgent Islamist group Al-Shabaab.","The African Union's AMISOM peacekeeping mission deployed from 2007 to support Somalia's Transitional Federal Government (TFG) against Al-Shabaab.","AMISOM transitioned to the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS) in 2022, with a mandate to gradually hand security responsibility to Somali forces.","Somalia held a presidential election in Mogadishu in 2022, held up as a sign of political stability enabled by improved security in the capital.","Despite this, Al-Shabaab continued to control or contest large areas of southern and central Somalia through the mid-2020s.","The UN Security Council has maintained an arms embargo and targeted sanctions relating to Somalia since the early 1990s, alongside its support for AU peacekeeping."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — how effective has global governance been in protecting Ukraine's sovereignty and territorial integrity since 2014? (16 marks)","items":["Question (as set): 'To what extent has global governance been effective in protecting Ukraine's sovereignty and territorial integrity since 2014?' (16 marks, To what extent)","Decode: 'To what extent' needs a weighed judgement with a qualified conclusion, not a one-sided account; the question names one outcome to test (effectiveness) across two related issues (sovereignty and territorial integrity), so every paragraph must return to whether governance actually protected them.","Opening paragraph: define sovereignty and territorial integrity using the specification's own wording, name the norm violated (non-recognition of the Crimea annexation, UNGA Resolution 68/262), and signal that the answer will weigh diplomatic/economic effectiveness against territorial effectiveness.","Paragraph 2 (point, evidence, analysis): diplomatic and legal governance — non-recognition of Crimea by most states, coordinated EU/US sanctions, ICJ proceedings — evidence of real but partial effectiveness that isolates Russia without reversing the annexation.","Paragraph 3 (point, evidence, analysis): longer-term opportunities global governance has opened for Ukraine — EU candidate status (2022) — a shift in trade and political relationships consistent with the specification's own long-term consequence.","Paragraph 4 (the limiting case): the UN Security Council's structural failure — Russia's own permanent seat and veto power block binding resolutions against it — explaining why territorial integrity itself has not been restored despite sustained diplomatic and economic pressure.","Evaluation thread: weigh diplomatic/economic effectiveness (real, sustained) against military/territorial effectiveness (largely absent), and explain the structural reason for that gap.","Conclusion must: state how far governance has been effective (partial — real diplomatic and economic effect, but not territorial restoration), name the structural limit (the veto), and note the longer-term opportunity (EU accession pathway) the effort has still opened up for Ukraine."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.941Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Fact files confirm the topic is sovereignty and intervention (Ukraine for 2.a, Mali for 3.b, Somalia for the 4.a LIDC — one case study per required item, no extras smuggled in); all 14 answer keys re-solve correctly, including the displacement working (6m + 5m = 11m, 11/41 x 100 = 26.8% ~ 27%), tariffs are the Human interactions set (3/6/10/16, no 12- or 33-mark or 'State' stems), the Level 4 / Level 2 labels are truthful for a 16-mark OCR levels grid, and every lesson section closes with what a top-level answer does — fix the MINUSMA hostile-act figure and the question 2 hint and this is publishable; smaller polish: 26.8% is described as 'roughly one in four' in questions[6].sol, AMISOM in 2007 supported the Transitional Federal Government rather than the 'federal government', and questions[9] is tagged 3.b while drawing on the Somalia (4.a) case study."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:52:52.959Z"}},"3.1":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"3.1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because Geographical debates (Component 03) devotes a whole topic, and a 33-mark synoptic essay, to climate change: examiners expect you to explain why climate changes naturally, how humans have altered it since industrialisation, why the issue is debated, and to evaluate whether mitigation, adaptation and international co-operation can deliver an effective response, using named case-study evidence throughout.","idea":[{"h":"The Earth's climate is dynamic","code":"1.a","t":"Long before humans existed, the Earth's climate shifted between 'greenhouse' and 'icehouse' states, driven by plate tectonics, Milankovitch cycles, solar output and natural greenhouse gases, and reconstructed from ice cores, sediments, tree rings and fossils. Examiners reward answers that name a specific natural forcing mechanism and trace its effect on temperature through a clear process chain — for example, how a Milankovitch cycle changes the solar radiation reaching high latitudes and so triggers glacial or interglacial conditions — rather than simply listing forcing factors without explaining how each one works."},{"h":"Humans have driven a new epoch: the Anthropocene","code":"2.a","t":"Multiple independent lines of evidence — rising surface, atmospheric and ocean temperatures, shrinking glaciers and ice sheets, rising sea level, more atmospheric water vapour and less snow and sea ice — show the world has warmed since the late nineteenth century, driven by rising anthropogenic greenhouse gas emissions that enhance the natural greenhouse effect. A top-level answer uses named case-study evidence, such as contrasting an AC and an EDC's contribution to emissions over time, and distinguishes historical (cumulative) responsibility from current annual output rather than treating 'emissions' as a single simple figure."},{"h":"Debates over climate change are shaped by agendas","code":"3.a","t":"The climate change debate has evolved since it first entered public and political discussion, shaped by governments and international organisations such as the EU and UN, and by the media and interest groups, whose coverage can be biased by funding, political interest or the need to attract an audience. A strong answer explains why a particular actor's presentation of climate change might differ from the scientific consensus, rather than simply asserting that 'the media is biased', and links this to how public understanding and political willingness to act are shaped."},{"h":"An effective response depends on knowing the future","code":"4.a","t":"Climate models project future emission scenarios and their resulting impacts on global temperature and sea level, informed by the carbon cycle and by positive feedbacks, which reinforce warming (such as ice-albedo feedback), and negative feedbacks, which partially offset it. Top-level answers explain feedback as a process chain with a clear direction of effect, and use this to justify why the range of future outcomes in climate modelling is uncertain rather than fixed, which matters when evaluating any proposed response."},{"h":"The impacts of climate change are global and dynamic","code":"4.b","t":"Climate change is already affecting ecosystems, human health and the frequency or intensity of extreme weather, and these impacts are projected to intensify; but exposure and vulnerability are uneven, so the same global warming produces very different risks for different people and places. A strong answer names a specific impact and links it explicitly to the vulnerability of a named group or place, rather than describing impacts in the abstract, and recognises that vulnerability depends on wealth, location and adaptive capacity as much as on the hazard itself."},{"h":"Mitigation and adaptation are complementary strategies","code":"4.c","t":"Mitigation strategies — energy efficiency, low-carbon energy, carbon capture and storage, forestry and geoengineering — cut future emissions, while adaptation strategies, organised around the framework of retreat, accommodate and protect, reduce vulnerability to impacts that are already locked in; the two work together rather than as alternatives. Case studies of two contrasting countries at different stages of development are required here, and a top-level answer uses them to compare not just what each strategy involves but the technological, socio-economic and political challenges each country faces in delivering it. Examiners also expect a sense of what a fully adapted world could look like by the end of the century: homes and offices designed or retrofitted for heat and flood resilience, transport and energy systems reorganised around low-carbon technology, whole cities reshaped by green infrastructure or by relocation away from the highest-risk land, and economies restructured around low-carbon industries — a top-level answer uses this end point to judge how far current mitigation and adaptation are actually taking us towards it."},{"h":"International co-operation shapes whether a response works","code":"5.a","t":"The IPCC shapes policy through its assessment of the science, while international directives such as the Kyoto Protocol, carbon trading and carbon credits, and evolving national and sub-national policy, determine whether commitments are actually delivered at every scale from global to local. A top-level answer evaluates success against specific criteria — such as which major emitters were bound by an agreement, and what it achieved in practice — and recognises that international frameworks often work indirectly, by driving stronger national policy, rather than by direct global enforcement."}],"examples":[{"title":"Decoding a 'To what extent' essay question","setup":"An essay asks you to test the claim that 'an effective international response to climate change is impossible', against three criteria: whether an agreement binds the major emitters, whether it can be enforced, and whether it changes what states actually do at home. Your evidence is the Kyoto Protocol (1997), the Paris Agreement (2015) and the UK Climate Change Act 2008. Reach a qualified judgement on the claim.","steps":["Kyoto (1997) against the first two criteria: it did set legally binding targets, including a cut of 7% below 1990 levels for the USA under Annex B, but the USA signed in 1998 and never ratified, Canada withdrew in 2011, and by the second commitment period the parties still bound covered only about 14% of global emissions — binding, but on a shrinking share of the emitters that matter.","Paris against the first criterion: agreed in 2015 by nearly every state, it holds the major emitters inside one framework, with China, the USA and India all filing Nationally Determined Contributions, so breadth of membership is no longer the weakness.","Paris against the second criterion: each NDC is self-set and no penalty follows from missing it; the only levers are the five-yearly global stocktake and the peer and public pressure it generates, so enforcement stays the weak point of both treaties.","The third criterion is where the treaties bite: the UK Climate Change Act 2008, amended in 2019, turned an international commitment into domestic statute with a net zero target for 2050 that ministers can be taken to court over — something neither treaty allows.","Judgement: the claim holds only for enforcement. An effective international response is not impossible, but it works indirectly — Paris (2015) bought near-universal participation where Kyoto (1997) shrank to a small share of emissions, and neither can compel a state; what gives the response force is its conversion into binding national law such as the 2008 Act, so effectiveness rests on domestic politics rather than international sanction."],"cues":["Did Kyoto bind the major emitters, and did that hold?","Does Paris fix the membership problem Kyoto ran into?","Can a state be made to meet its own NDC?","Where does an international commitment become enforceable?","So how far is an effective international response impossible?"]},{"title":"Building a case-study paragraph — the UK Climate Change Act 2008","setup":"The UK Climate Change Act 2008 put a long-term emissions target into law, amended in 2019 to net zero by 2050, and requires five-yearly carbon budgets to be fixed well in advance. Build a paragraph — point, evidence, explanation, link — arguing that national legislation can lock in mitigation more firmly than an international treaty such as Kyoto.","steps":["Point: a statute binds the government of the day and can be enforced by the courts, so domestic law can hold a long-term mitigation target more firmly than a treaty whose only sanction is reputational.","Evidence: the Act as amended in 2019 requires net zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050 relative to 1990, replacing the original requirement of an 80% cut.","Explain the mechanism: the Secretary of State must set each carbon budget in law about 12 years before the period it covers, on the advice of the independent Climate Change Committee, which then reports annually to Parliament on whether the UK is on track — so a government must plan emissions cuts far beyond its own election cycle.","Link: this is what a treaty such as Kyoto could not do — Kyoto fixed a target but left delivery to each state's own politics, whereas the Act fixes the target, the interim budgets and the body that scrutinises them in UK law, so the commitment outlives the government that made it."],"cues":["What argument does the paragraph have to build?","Which target does the amended Act now set, and against what year?","Who sets each carbon budget, and on whose advice?","What can domestic law do that Kyoto could not?"]},{"title":"Calculating the percentage increase in atmospheric CO2, pre-industrial to 1960","setup":"Pre-industrial atmospheric CO2 concentration was approximately 280 ppm; by 1960 it had risen to approximately 317 ppm. Calculate the percentage increase over that period.","steps":["Subtract to find the rise: 317 − 280 = 37 ppm.","Express the rise as a proportion of the starting value, not the later one: 37 ÷ 280 = 0.1321.","Multiply by 100: 0.1321 × 100 = 13.21%, the increase between the pre-industrial level and 1960, which rounds to 13.2%.","So atmospheric CO2 rose by approximately 13.2% — more than a tenth above the pre-industrial concentration had already been added before the steepest growth in emissions began."],"cues":["What do you subtract from what to find the rise?","Which of the two values is the rise a proportion of?","What turns that proportion into a percentage?","By what percentage had CO2 risen, and is that large?"]},{"title":"Explaining a positive feedback loop — Arctic sea ice and albedo","setup":"Sea ice reflects roughly two thirds of the sunlight that falls on it, while the open ocean beneath reflects only about a tenth. Arctic warming is shrinking the extent of summer sea ice. Explain the loop this sets off, and name the type of feedback it is.","steps":["Losing ice swaps a bright surface for a dark one: water exposed where ice has gone absorbs about nine tenths of the incoming solar radiation, instead of reflecting two thirds of it back to space.","That extra absorbed energy warms the surface ocean, which melts the ice edge from below and delays the autumn freeze-up, so the next summer begins with less ice than the last.","Less ice exposes still more dark water, which absorbs still more radiation — the output of the process returns as a fresh input to it, so the loop runs round again.","This is a positive feedback: the original warming is amplified rather than damped, and it is the main reason the Arctic has warmed several times faster than the global mean — Arctic amplification."],"cues":["What replaces the ice, and how much sunlight does it absorb?","What does that extra absorbed energy do to the ocean and the ice?","Why does the process not simply settle down?","Is the first warming damped or amplified — what is such a loop called?"]},{"title":"Interpreting a global temperature anomaly graph","setup":"A graph plots global mean surface temperature as an anomaly against the 1850–1900 average, which is drawn as 0°C. Readings taken from it: 1900 about 0.0°C, 1970 about +0.2°C, the early 2020s about +1.1 to +1.2°C; a second line for the Arctic reaches about +3.3°C in the early 2020s. Describe what the baseline means, the trend in the global line, and the spatial pattern the two lines show together.","steps":["Take the baseline first: because the 1850–1900 average is fixed at 0°C, every point plots a departure from pre-industrial conditions rather than an actual temperature, which is why values before 1900 can sit below the line.","Now compare the two halves of the global line: from 1900 to 1970 the anomaly rose about 0.2°C in 70 years, roughly 0.03°C per decade; from 1970 to the early 2020s it rose from +0.2°C to about +1.15°C, roughly 0.95°C in 50 years, or about 0.19°C per decade.","The rate after 1970 is therefore around six times the rate before it, so the line is not straight: it steepens, which is what an accelerating trend looks like on this graph.","Compare the two lines at the same date: the Arctic reaches about +3.3°C in the early 2020s against a global +1.1 to +1.2°C, so the Arctic anomaly is close to three times the global one.","So: the baseline is the 1850–1900 pre-industrial average plotted as 0°C; the trend is a rise of about 1.1 to 1.2°C that accelerates sharply after 1970; and the pattern is uneven in space, the Arctic warming roughly three times as fast as the global mean — Arctic amplification."],"cues":["What does a reading of 0°C on this graph actually mean?","How fast did the anomaly rise before 1970, and after it?","Is the global line straight, and what does that tell you?","How does the Arctic line compare with the global one?","State the baseline, the trend and the spatial pattern."]}],"check":[{"q":"Name one way scientists reconstruct climate from before written records existed.","a":"For example, ice cores — trapped air bubbles preserve past atmospheric composition."},{"q":"What is the difference between mitigation and adaptation?","a":"Mitigation reduces the causes (emissions) of climate change; adaptation manages its impacts."},{"q":"Why did the USA's non-ratification weaken the Kyoto Protocol?","a":"The USA was then the world's largest emitter, so a large share of global emissions was left outside its binding targets."},{"q":"Give one reason international agreements alone may not guarantee an effective response.","a":"They cannot compel a sovereign country to comply — commitments such as NDCs under the Paris Agreement are self-set, not enforced."},{"q":"Name one feature of what an adapted city or transport network might look like by the end of the 21st century.","a":"For example, buildings and homes retrofitted for heat and flood resilience, low-carbon transport networks, or areas reshaped by green infrastructure or relocation (any one)."}]},"room":{"facts":["Atmospheric CO2 has risen from approximately 280 ppm before industrialisation to approximately 420 ppm in the early 2020s.","Global mean temperature has risen by approximately 1.1-1.2°C (early 2020s) above the 1850-1900 (pre-industrial) baseline.","The three responses in the adaptation framework are retreat, accommodate and protect.","The Kyoto Protocol (1997) bound only developed 'Annex I' countries; the Paris Agreement (2015) applies to almost all countries through Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs).","The three Milankovitch cycles are eccentricity (approximately 100,000 years), obliquity/axial tilt (approximately 41,000 years) and precession (approximately 26,000 years)."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify three methods used to reconstruct the Earth's climate in the geological past.","a":"Any three of: marine or lake sediments, ice cores, tree rings, fossils.","sol":"Three valid methods: (1) ice cores — trapped air bubbles and oxygen isotope ratios in cores such as those from Antarctica preserve a record of past atmospheric composition and temperature; (2) tree rings — the width and density of annual growth rings vary with temperature and rainfall, giving a dated record for the recent geological past; (3) marine or lake sediments — layers of material deposited on the sea or lake floor build up over time and can be dated and analysed for pollen, chemistry or microfossils that indicate past climate conditions.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This asks where evidence of climate from before instrumental records comes from.","Think of different kinds of natural archive that build up their own record over time.","Give three named examples only, each in a short phrase — no explanation needed."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Explain how additional greenhouse gases entering the atmosphere enhance the natural greenhouse effect, referring to at least two named greenhouse gases.","a":"More greenhouse gases (e.g. carbon dioxide and methane) absorb and re-radiate more outgoing longwave radiation, warming the lower atmosphere further than the natural effect alone.","sol":"The natural greenhouse effect already keeps the Earth warmer than it would otherwise be, as gases such as water vapour and carbon dioxide absorb outgoing longwave radiation emitted by the Earth's surface and re-radiate part of it back down. Anthropogenic emissions add to the concentration of these gases: carbon dioxide from fossil fuel combustion and deforestation, and methane from agriculture, landfill and fossil fuel extraction, are both long-lived and effective absorbers of longwave radiation. As their atmospheric concentrations rise, a greater proportion of outgoing longwave radiation is absorbed and re-emitted towards the surface, so less escapes to space. The result is an enhanced greenhouse effect, raising the global mean temperature above what natural forcing alone would produce.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["This is about what happens to outgoing radiation when greenhouse gas concentration rises.","Compare what happens with the natural greenhouse effect to what changes when extra gases are added.","Name at least two greenhouse gases within a short chain of cause and effect, ending with the effect on temperature."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Identify the three responses within the framework of adaptation to climate change.","a":"Retreat, accommodate and protect.","sol":"The adaptation framework identifies three possible responses: retreat, which involves moving people, activities or infrastructure away from areas of risk; accommodate, which involves continuing to use a vulnerable area but modifying activities, buildings or behaviour to reduce harm; and protect, which involves defending an area from the impacts of climate change, for example through hard or soft engineering.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This is about how a community can respond to living somewhere increasingly at risk.","Think about the different overall approaches a place could take towards a growing risk, rather than one specific action.","Name three single words, in any order."],"codes":["4.c"]},{"q":"Identify three current impacts of climate change on ecosystems.","a":"Any three of: species shifting range towards the poles or higher altitude, changes to the timing of seasonal events (phenology), coral bleaching in warming oceans, loss or change of habitat.","sol":"Three valid current ecosystem impacts: (1) species range shift — many species are moving polewards or to higher altitudes as temperatures rise, tracking the climate conditions they need; (2) coral bleaching — warmer, more acidic oceans cause corals to expel the algae they depend on, turning white and often dying, as recorded on reefs such as the Great Barrier Reef; (3) changes to phenology — the timing of seasonal events such as flowering, breeding and migration is shifting earlier as temperatures rise, which can disrupt relationships between species that depend on each other.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how warming affects where species live and how ecosystems function, not human systems directly.","Consider more than one kind of change — not just where species are found, but also when things happen.","Give three short named impacts, each in a brief phrase."],"codes":["4.b"]},{"q":"Explain what is meant by 'carbon trading'.","a":"A market-based system where a cap is set on emissions and permits (carbon credits) can be bought and sold, allowing overall reductions to be achieved at lowest cost.","sol":"Carbon trading is a market-based mechanism for reducing greenhouse gas emissions. A regulator sets a cap on the total emissions allowed across participating organisations or countries, and issues permits, or carbon credits, up to that cap. Organisations that emit less than their allowance can sell their spare credits to those that would otherwise exceed theirs, as happens under the EU Emissions Trading System. This creates a financial incentive to cut emissions where it is cheapest to do so, while still limiting total emissions to the level set by the cap.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the scheme the EU has run since 2005 and what it puts a price on.","Consider what happens to a firm that emits less than it is allowed to, and to one that emits more.","Shape: define the term in one sentence, then add one sentence on how it works in practice."],"codes":["5.a"]},{"q":"Explain how Milankovitch cycles have driven natural climate change over geological time.","a":"Cyclical changes in the Earth's orbit and axis (eccentricity, obliquity/axial tilt, precession) alter the amount and distribution of solar radiation received, triggering glacial-interglacial cycles.","sol":"Milankovitch cycles are long-term, predictable variations in the Earth's orbit and orientation that alter the amount and seasonal distribution of solar radiation reaching different latitudes. Eccentricity describes the changing shape of the Earth's orbit around the Sun, from more circular to more elliptical, over a cycle of approximately 100,000 years. Obliquity (axial tilt) describes the Earth's tilt varying between approximately 22.1 degrees and 24.5 degrees over a cycle of approximately 41,000 years, affecting the strength of the seasons. Precession describes the slow wobble of the Earth's rotational axis over a cycle of approximately 26,000 years, changing which hemisphere faces the Sun at closest approach. Together these cycles alter how much solar radiation high-latitude regions receive in summer, which is a key control on whether ice sheets grow or retreat, helping to explain the timing of quaternary glacial and interglacial periods such as the Holocene.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["This is about changes in the Earth's position and orientation relative to the Sun, not the atmosphere itself.","There are three separate orbital cycles, each operating over a different, very long timescale — deal with each in turn.","Name each cycle, describe what varies, then link the combined effect to glacial-interglacial timing."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Explain why the media and different interest groups may present a biased image of climate change to the public.","a":"Coverage and campaigning are shaped by the interests, funding and audience of the source — for example, fossil-fuel-linked groups may downplay the issue, environmental campaigners may emphasise worst-case impacts, and media outlets may prioritise dramatic or simplified stories over scientific nuance.","sol":"Governments, businesses, environmental campaign groups and the media all have their own interests in how climate change is presented. Organisations linked to fossil fuel industries have sometimes funded research or lobbying that questions the extent or urgency of human-caused warming, protecting their economic interests. Environmental pressure groups may instead emphasise the most severe possible impacts to build public and political pressure for action, even where scientific certainty is lower. The media, driven by the need to attract an audience, can favour dramatic, simplified or conflict-based stories — for example presenting climate change as a two-sided 'debate' — over the more cautious, probabilistic language scientists actually use. Together these agendas mean the public image of climate change is shaped as much by who is communicating it as by the underlying science.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about who benefits, financially or politically, from a particular way of presenting climate change.","Consider more than one type of source, and how their goals differ from each other.","Explain each source's likely bias in turn, then link this to how the public image is shaped overall."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Compare the contribution to anthropogenic greenhouse gas emissions of an AC and an EDC you have studied.","a":"An AC (e.g. the USA) has a high historical/cumulative and per capita contribution from early industrialisation; an EDC (e.g. China) now has the largest current annual total but a lower per capita figure, reflecting rapid recent industrialisation.","sol":"Using the USA as an AC and China as an EDC, the two countries' contributions differ in scale and history. The USA industrialised from the nineteenth century onwards and is responsible for approximately a fifth of all cumulative carbon dioxide emissions since 1850, despite having a much smaller population than China; its per capita emissions remain high, at approximately 14 tonnes of CO2 per person per year. China's industrialisation has been much more recent and rapid, driven by coal-fired power generation and manufacturing for both domestic use and export; it has become the largest annual emitter in total, but because its population is far larger its per capita emissions, at approximately 8 tonnes of CO2 per person per year, remain below the USA's. The comparison shows that historical responsibility, dominated by ACs like the USA, and current annual output, increasingly dominated by EDCs like China, tell different stories about who is contributing most to climate change.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["This asks you to compare, not just describe each country in turn, so think about what changes depending on how you choose to measure a contribution.","Consider that there is more than one fair way to measure a country's contribution to emissions.","Deal with each country in turn, then draw the two together in a final comparative sentence."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Table 1 shows atmospheric carbon dioxide concentration recorded at the Mauna Loa Observatory: 1960, approximately 317 ppm; 1990, approximately 354 ppm; 2020, approximately 414 ppm. Calculate the percentage increase in atmospheric CO2 concentration (a) between 1960 and 1990, and (b) between 1990 and 2020, showing your working for each.","a":"(a) Approximately 11.7% (37 divided by 317, times 100). (b) Approximately 16.9% (60 divided by 354, times 100).","sol":"(a) 1960 to 1990: increase = 354 minus 317 = 37 ppm. Percentage change = (37 divided by 317) times 100 = 11.67%, which rounds to approximately 11.7%. (b) 1990 to 2020: increase = 414 minus 354 = 60 ppm. Percentage change = (60 divided by 354) times 100 = 16.95%, which is approximately 16.95%. The rate of increase was therefore higher in the second thirty-year period than in the first, consistent with accelerating global fossil fuel combustion after 1990.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["This calculation compares three values from the table across two different time periods.","Think about how you would express each change as a proportion of the value at the start of that period, not just as an absolute number.","Set your working out in clear, labelled steps for each period so a reader can follow how you reached each percentage."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between a positive and a negative feedback within the climate system, using one example of each.","a":"A positive feedback amplifies an initial change (e.g. ice-albedo feedback: less ice leads to lower albedo, more warming, and less ice); a negative feedback dampens it (e.g. increased plant growth absorbing more CO2 as temperatures and CO2 rise, reducing the initial increase).","sol":"A positive feedback reinforces an initial change, pushing the climate system further in the same direction. The ice-albedo feedback is a clear example: as warming melts Arctic sea ice, the darker ocean surface that is exposed reflects less solar radiation than the ice did (lower albedo), so more radiation is absorbed, causing further warming and further ice loss. A negative feedback instead counteracts an initial change, pushing the system back towards its original state. For example, as atmospheric CO2 and temperatures rise, some vegetation may grow more vigorously, taking up more CO2 through photosynthesis and so removing some of the extra carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, partially offsetting the initial increase. The key distinction is that positive feedbacks make climate change self-reinforcing, while negative feedbacks act as a partial natural brake on it.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["This question is about feedbacks within the climate system — processes triggered by an initial change that then affect the system further.","Choose two different named processes to illustrate your answer, each drawn from a different part of the climate system.","Explain each named process in turn, and make sure your final sentence draws out how the two differ."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Explain how mitigation and adaptation act as complementary, rather than alternative, strategies for managing the risks of climate change.","a":"Mitigation reduces the future scale of climate change by cutting emissions, while adaptation manages the impacts that are now unavoidable; used together they reduce both the size of future risk and current vulnerability to it.","sol":"Mitigation strategies, such as shifting to low-carbon energy sources or expanding forestry, aim to reduce the emissions that cause climate change and so limit how much further warming and its impacts will occur in future. However, because past emissions have already committed the planet to some further warming, mitigation alone cannot prevent all impacts. Adaptation strategies, such as flood defences or drought-resistant crops, instead manage the impacts of the climate change that is already happening or is now locked in. Because neither strategy alone can fully manage climate risk — mitigation cannot undo warming already built into the system, and adaptation cannot keep pace indefinitely with unmitigated emissions — the two are treated as complementary: mitigation reduces the long-term scale of the problem while adaptation reduces vulnerability to the impacts that remain, so an effective response combines both rather than relying on either alone.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["This question is about two different types of strategy for managing climate risk, and how they relate to each other, not just what each one involves.","Think about what stage of the climate-change process each strategy acts on, before deciding what 'complementary' means here.","Explain each strategy in turn, then finish with a sentence that draws out how they relate to each other."],"codes":["4.c"]},{"q":"To what extent has the Kyoto Protocol (1997) succeeded as part of the international response to climate change, given its links to the carbon cycle and to global food security?","a":"Partial success: Kyoto set the first binding targets and carbon trading mechanisms, and Kyoto-linked forestry projects support the carbon cycle's terrestrial carbon store, but it bound only Annex I countries and covered a shrinking share of global emissions, so continued warming still threatens rainfall reliability and food-producing regions at national and local scale.","sol":"The Kyoto Protocol, adopted in 1997, was the first international agreement to set legally binding emissions-reduction targets for developed 'Annex I' countries and to establish carbon trading, a global-scale mechanism that later agreements built on. Its success reaches beyond the climate change topic into the carbon cycle studied elsewhere in the specification: Kyoto's flexibility mechanisms funded afforestation and reforestation projects in developing countries, directly increasing the terrestrial carbon store and altering the balance of carbon-cycle flows between the atmosphere, vegetation and soil, so its climate benefit works partly through a physical system rather than through emissions cuts alone. However, because the USA never ratified the Protocol and rapidly industrialising countries such as China carried no binding target, Kyoto covered a shrinking share of global emissions and global output continued to rise across its commitment periods. This matters at the national and local scale for global food security, a human topic elsewhere in the specification: unmitigated warming disrupts the reliable rainfall and growing seasons that farming regions such as the Sahel and South and Southeast Asia depend on, threatening yields and, in the most exposed areas, contributing to rural-to-urban and cross-border migration as livelihoods fail. Overall, Kyoto succeeded in establishing the architecture of a binding global response and in strengthening one physical system, the carbon cycle, through its forestry mechanisms, but its narrow, shrinking coverage of global emissions means it did too little to protect the food-producing regions and stable climate that human systems elsewhere in the specification depend on — success that is real but partial, and better judged across systems and scales than by its emissions targets alone.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Weigh what the agreement achieved for the treaty itself against what it achieved for systems that depend on a stable climate.","Think about a physical process elsewhere in your course that an international climate agreement could affect directly, and a human outcome, at a different scale, that a changing climate then affects.","Give the case for success, the case against, then a final judgement that draws the different systems and scales together."],"codes":["5.a","3.a"]},{"q":"To what extent do adaptation strategies reduce the vulnerability of people in contrasting countries you have studied, given the links between climate adaptation, coastal landscapes and global migration?","a":"Adaptation reduces vulnerability further where resources and institutions are strong (e.g. UK coastal and flood defences), but where adaptation is exceeded — as in parts of coastal Bangladesh — vulnerability is not removed but displaced, driving rural-to-urban and cross-border migration; adaptation must therefore be judged alongside coastal landscape management and migration systems, not climate change alone.","sol":"Vulnerability to climate change depends on exposure, sensitivity and the capacity to adapt, and adaptation affects each country differently according to its resources. In the UK, an AC, coastal management decisions are set out in non-statutory Shoreline Management Plans, a coastal-landscapes tool studied elsewhere in the specification whose four policy options are hold the line, advance the line, managed realignment and no active intervention; combined with structures such as the Thames Barrier, this has substantially reduced flood vulnerability for many communities at the national scale, though 'no active intervention' decisions still leave some smaller coastal communities exposed at the local scale. In Bangladesh, an LIDC, adaptation such as the Cyclone Preparedness Programme and raised embankments has cut cyclone death tolls dramatically since the 1970s, applying the specification's own adaptation framework of retreat, accommodate and protect at the community scale. However, where coastal erosion and salinisation outpace what embankments can accommodate, affected farmers and families increasingly move away permanently: this links adaptation directly to global migration, a human topic elsewhere in the specification, as displaced people move first within the delta, then to rapidly growing cities such as Dhaka, and in some cases attempt onward international migration. Vulnerability is therefore not simply reduced or left unreduced; in the lowest-income, most exposed communities it is displaced from one place and scale to another. Overall, adaptation reduces vulnerability substantially in both countries and can be highly effective against specific hazards such as cyclones and coastal flooding, but greater wealth and institutional capacity mean vulnerability is reduced more completely in an AC like the UK, while in an LIDC like Bangladesh incomplete adaptation is increasingly resolved through migration rather than through protection in place — so adaptation and coastal and migration systems elsewhere in the specification must be judged together, not climate change in isolation.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about how a country's wealth and institutions affect what adaptation it can actually deliver, and what happens to people it cannot protect.","Consider a coastal management decision from one case study and a population movement from the other, at more than one scale.","Deal with each country in turn, then compare how far vulnerability is actually reduced rather than simply moved elsewhere."],"codes":["4.b","4.c"]},{"q":"'An effective international response to climate change is impossible.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Partial agreement: international frameworks (IPCC, Kyoto, Paris) have shaped policy and legitimised action but rely on national implementation (e.g. the UK Climate Change Act) and voluntary commitment, so an international response works imperfectly rather than not at all.","sol":"International efforts to tackle climate change include the work of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) in establishing the scientific consensus, binding agreements such as the Kyoto Protocol (1997), and near-universal agreements such as the Paris Agreement (2015), alongside mechanisms such as carbon trading. An effective response would need to reduce global emissions at the pace the science demands and be followed through by the countries that sign up to it.\n\nThere is a case that an effective international response is close to impossible. The Kyoto Protocol bound only developed 'Annex I' countries, was never ratified by the USA, and covered a shrinking share of global emissions as China and other EDCs industrialised; global emissions continued to rise across its commitment periods. The Paris Agreement addressed this by including nearly all countries, but it relies on Nationally Determined Contributions that are self-set and not legally enforceable, so overall global ambition still falls short of limiting warming to well below 2 degrees Celsius. Debates around climate change are also shaped by governments, media and interest groups with differing agendas, which can slow the political consensus needed for strong international action.\n\nHowever, international frameworks have not been without effect. The IPCC's assessment reports have repeatedly shifted the terms of the debate and underpin most national climate law. Carbon trading schemes born from Kyoto, such as the EU Emissions Trading System, have put a price on emissions across dozens of countries. Most importantly, international agreements have driven national and sub-national policy that goes beyond what any single treaty requires: the UK's Climate Change Act 2008, strengthened in 2019 to a legally binding net zero target by 2050, was adopted partly to meet international commitments but now exceeds them, with five-yearly carbon budgets set by the independent Climate Change Committee. In lower-income countries such as Bangladesh, international climate finance has supported adaptation programmes, including the Cyclone Preparedness Programme, that have cut vulnerability substantially even where mitigation capacity is limited.\n\nOn balance, a fully effective international response — one that alone guarantees emissions fall fast enough to avoid dangerous warming — probably is impossible, because international agreements cannot force sovereign states to comply and current commitments under the Paris Agreement are not yet sufficient. But 'impossible' overstates the case: international frameworks have proved able to set direction, build scientific consensus and legitimise stronger national and sub-national policy, such as the UK's Climate Change Act, that then exceeds the international minimum. An international response therefore works, but only when translated into binding national policy — it cannot succeed acting alone.","m":33,"d":3,"hints":["Decide what an effective international response would actually need to achieve before judging whether it is impossible.","Bring in at least one international framework and at least one national case study, and weigh them against each other.","Structure the answer as a case for the statement, a case against it, and a final qualified judgement."],"codes":["5.a","4.c","3.a"]},{"q":"Identify three features of what future adapted homes, cities, transport or economies might look like by the end of the twenty-first century, following widespread climate adaptation.","a":"Any three of: homes and buildings adapted for heat and flood resilience, low-carbon transport networks, cities reshaped by green infrastructure or by relocation from high-risk land, economies restructured around low-carbon industries.","sol":"Following widespread adaptation, homes and offices could be built or retrofitted with features such as improved insulation, shading and flood-resistant design to cope with more extreme heat and heavier rainfall. Transport systems could shift further towards low-carbon and electrified networks, reducing exposure to fuel-supply shocks as well as emissions. Whole cities could be reshaped around green infrastructure, such as parks and permeable surfaces that manage heat and flood risk, with some low-lying areas relocated or redeveloped where protection is no longer viable. Economies would need to restructure around low-carbon industries and adapted agriculture as the basis of employment and trade, rather than around carbon-intensive sectors.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This is about the end point of the adaptation process, not the specific measures used to get there.","Think about how different parts of everyday life — buildings, getting around, and the economy — might each need to change.","Give three short named features, drawn from more than one part of life or the economy."],"codes":["4.c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Name one natural forcing mechanism that drove climate change in the geological past.","a":"For example, Milankovitch cycles (or plate tectonics, or solar output)."},{"q":"Give one piece of evidence that the world has warmed since the late nineteenth century.","a":"For example, rising sea level (or shrinking glaciers, or rising ocean temperature)."},{"q":"What is the difference between mitigation and adaptation?","a":"Mitigation cuts the emissions causing climate change; adaptation manages its impacts."},{"q":"Name one international agreement on climate change and give its date.","a":"For example, the Kyoto Protocol (1997) or the Paris Agreement (2015)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Name four methods used to reconstruct past climate.","back":"Marine and lake sediment cores, ice cores, tree rings (dendrochronology), and fossils.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"What happened to global climate around 35 million years ago?","back":"Antarctica became glaciated as global climate cooled through the Eocene-Oligocene transition, part of a long-term 100-million-year cooling trend.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Name the three Milankovitch cycles.","back":"Eccentricity (approximately 100,000 years), obliquity/axial tilt (approximately 41,000 years), precession (approximately 26,000 years).","code":"1.a"},{"front":"How much has atmospheric CO2 risen since pre-industrial times?","back":"From approximately 280 ppm to approximately 420 ppm in the early 2020s.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"How much has global mean temperature risen since pre-industrial times?","back":"By approximately 1.1-1.2°C above the 1850-1900 baseline. (early 2020s)","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Give two pieces of evidence the world has warmed since the late 19th century.","back":"Rising sea level and shrinking valley glaciers/ice sheets; rising surface, atmospheric and oceanic temperatures and increasing atmospheric water vapour; decreasing snow cover and decreasing sea ice extent.","code":"2.a","open":true},{"front":"Why might media coverage of climate change be biased?","back":"Outlets, governments and interest groups have differing funding, political and commercial interests that shape which evidence and framing they present.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"What does an emission scenario in climate modelling show?","back":"A modelled future path of greenhouse gas emissions and its resulting projected impact on global temperature and sea level.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Give an example of a positive feedback in the climate system.","back":"Ice-albedo feedback: melting ice exposes darker land/ocean, absorbing more solar radiation; water vapour feedback: warming increases atmospheric water vapour, itself a greenhouse gas; permafrost feedback: thawing permafrost releases stored methane and carbon dioxide, adding further warming.","code":"4.a","open":true},{"front":"Name the three responses in the adaptation framework.","back":"Retreat, accommodate, protect.","code":"4.c"},{"front":"Give two mitigation strategies to cut global emissions.","back":"Fuel shift to low-carbon energy, as in the UK's move from coal to offshore wind, coal power ending in 2024; energy efficiency and conservation, such as building insulation and efficiency standards; carbon capture and storage, as at Norway's Sleipner field, storing around a million tonnes of CO2 a year since 1996.","code":"4.c","open":true},{"front":"What did the UK Climate Change Act 2008 originally require, and how was it strengthened?","back":"An 80% cut in emissions by 2050 versus 1990; amended in 2019 to a legally binding net zero target by 2050.","code":"4.c"},{"front":"Why is Bangladesh highly vulnerable to climate change?","back":"Low-lying Ganges-Brahmaputra-Meghna delta, dense population, frequent cyclones and flooding, and limited resources to fund large-scale protection.","code":"4.b"},{"front":"When was the Kyoto Protocol agreed, and who was bound by it?","back":"1997 (in force 2005); legally binding targets applied only to developed 'Annex I' countries.","code":"5.a"},{"front":"When was the Paris Agreement agreed, and what mechanism drives it?","back":"2015 (COP21); each country sets its own Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs), reviewed and strengthened periodically.","code":"5.a"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4: evaluating whether international agreements drive effective national policy","items":["International climate agreements have proved able to drive genuinely effective national policy, even where the agreements themselves are not fully binding. The UK's Climate Change Act 2008 was passed in the context of the Kyoto Protocol and the EU's own targets, committing the UK to an 80% cut in emissions by 2050 against a 1990 baseline; following the Paris Agreement's ambition to pursue 1.5°C, this was strengthened in 2019 to a legally binding net zero target by 2050, with five-yearly carbon budgets set by the independent Climate Change Committee. Because the targets are written into domestic law rather than only into an international treaty, they survive changes of government and are enforceable through UK courts, which is why UK territorial emissions had already fallen by approximately 50%, or almost half, against the 1990 baseline by 2022 while the economy continued to grow. This shows that international frameworks succeed less by imposing global compliance directly and more by legitimising and accelerating binding national policy of this kind, which is a stronger form of implementation than the treaties can achieve alone."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2: evaluating whether international agreements drive effective national policy","items":["International agreements have helped countries deal with climate change. For example, there have been agreements like the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement which countries signed up to. Countries then made their own laws about climate change, like the UK, which has rules about cutting emissions. This means that international agreements are quite useful for climate change because they get countries to do something about it, even though not every country agrees. Overall this shows international agreements have made a difference to how countries respond to climate change."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — the USA (AC, greenhouse gas emissions)","items":["Industrialised from the nineteenth century onwards, one of the first economies to burn fossil fuels at scale.","Responsible for approximately one-fifth of all cumulative CO2 emissions since 1850 — the largest historical (cumulative) contributor.","Per capita CO2 emissions approximately 14 tonnes per person per year in the early 2020s, among the highest of any major economy.","Second-largest current annual emitter after China, at about 5 billion tonnes of CO2 per year.","Signed but never ratified the Kyoto Protocol (1997). Joined the Paris Agreement (2015) but withdrew in 2020, rejoined in 2021, then gave formal notice of withdrawal again in January 2025, taking effect in January 2026."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — China (EDC, greenhouse gas emissions)","items":["Rapid industrialisation since the 1980s-1990s, driven by coal-fired power generation and export manufacturing.","Now the world's largest annual emitter of CO2, at over 10 billion tonnes per year in the early 2020s.","Per capita CO2 emissions approximately 8 tonnes per person per year — lower than the USA's despite the larger total.","Classified under the Kyoto Protocol as a non-Annex I country, so was not bound by its targets.","Set a Nationally Determined Contribution under the Paris Agreement to peak emissions before 2030 and reach carbon neutrality before 2060."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — the UK (an AC): impacts, opportunities, threats, and mitigation and adaptation challenges","items":["UK territorial greenhouse gas emissions were down by approximately 50%, or almost half, against the 1990 baseline by 2022, while GDP continued to grow.","Current impacts: increasingly intense winter storms and river flooding (e.g. Storm Desmond, 2015-16, and Storm Babet, 2023), and record summer heat, with 40.3 degrees Celsius recorded at Coningsby in July 2022.","Current environmental impacts: rising sea level and coastal erosion on low-lying eastern and southern coasts, and species ranges shifting, affecting farming and fisheries.","Opportunities: a leading offshore wind industry, among the largest installed capacities in the world, creating green jobs and export expertise in climate policy and finance.","Threats: rising flood and coastal-erosion risk to homes and infrastructure, and greater heatwave-related mortality among vulnerable groups.","Mitigation challenges: technological — scaling grid capacity and storage to match variable renewable output; socio-economic — the high upfront cost of insulating homes and replacing gas boilers; political — sustaining cross-party support for costly net-zero policy as household bills rise.","Adaptation challenges: technological — ageing flood defences such as the Thames Barrier (built 1982) needing upgrade for use beyond the 2070s; socio-economic — funding the long-term maintenance of coastal defences for smaller, less economically valuable communities; political — local opposition where Shoreline Management Plans recommend no active intervention rather than continued protection.","Climate Change Act 2008, strengthened in 2019 to a legally binding net zero target by 2050, with five-yearly carbon budgets set by the independent Climate Change Committee."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — Bangladesh (an LIDC): impacts, opportunities, threats, and mitigation and adaptation challenges","items":["Bangladesh is classified as a Low Income Developing Country (LIDC), with a population of approximately 170 million, most living on the low-lying Ganges-Brahmaputra-Meghna delta.","Current socio-economic and environmental impacts: increasingly severe cyclones and storm surges (e.g. Cyclone Amphan, 2020, and Cyclone Sidr, 2007), river and coastal flooding, and salinisation of farmland and drinking water as sea level rises.","Around four-fifths of the country lies on flood-prone floodplain, much of it less than 10 metres above sea level, so relatively small sea-level rises threaten large populated areas.","Opportunities: climate finance and technology transfer from international donors, and a globally recognised, low-cost model of community-based disaster preparedness.","Threats: displacement of coastal and delta communities, loss of agricultural land to salinisation, and rural-to-urban migration pressure on cities such as Dhaka.","Mitigation challenges: technological — limited access to low-carbon energy technology and the capital to invest in it; socio-economic — a very low historical and current per capita contribution to emissions gives little domestic incentive to prioritise mitigation over development; political — balancing economic development against emissions reduction in national policy.","Adaptation challenges: technological — engineering embankments and shelters able to withstand increasingly severe cyclones and river erosion; socio-economic — funding large-scale adaptation from a low national income and very high population density; political — coordinating adaptation across many low-lying, densely populated districts with limited institutional capacity.","Cyclone Preparedness Programme runs a network of thousands of community cyclone shelters and an early-warning volunteer system, which has cut cyclone death tolls dramatically since the 1970s."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'Adaptation offers a more realistic response to climate change than mitigation.' To what extent do you agree? (33 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'realistic' means achievable given current technology, cost and political will, not simply 'effective'; the essay must weigh adaptation against mitigation as responses, not just describe each.","Paragraph 1 (define and set up the debate): define mitigation and adaptation and how they relate (complementary, not alternatives); state the essay's line — that adaptation is more immediately realistic but cannot substitute for mitigation in the long run.","Paragraph 2 (case for 'more realistic'): mitigation depends on high-cost, slow-moving technological and political change (e.g. scaling low-carbon energy and carbon capture and storage) and on international co-operation that has repeatedly fallen short, as the Kyoto Protocol's narrow coverage shows (link to 5.a).","Paragraph 3 (case for 'more realistic', continued, with case studies): adaptation can be delivered unilaterally and has demonstrably reduced vulnerability already — the UK's flood defences and Shoreline Management Plans, and Bangladesh's Cyclone Preparedness Programme, both show measurable results without needing global agreement.","Paragraph 4 (case against): adaptation alone cannot keep pace with unmitigated warming — costs and limits rise with the scale of change, and in an LIDC like Bangladesh adaptation is already being outpaced by salinisation and erosion, displacing people rather than protecting them (link to 4.b and migration elsewhere in the specification).","Paragraph 5 (case against, continued): without mitigation, future adaptation needs (rebuilding homes, transport, cities and economies for a much greater degree of warming, as set out in 4.c) become larger and more expensive than adapting to a lower level of warming would be, so treating adaptation as sufficient on its own is short-termism.","Evaluation thread: weigh what is achievable now, one country at a time (adaptation), against what is necessary in the long run, but harder to secure internationally (mitigation) — run this comparison through every paragraph rather than leaving it to the end.","Conclusion must state: adaptation is the more realistic response in the short term because it does not depend on international agreement, as the UK and Bangladesh case studies show; but it is not more realistic overall, because without mitigation the scale of future adaptation needed becomes unmanageable — so the two must be pursued together, with adaptation buying the time mitigation needs to take effect."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.942Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every fault in the first verdict is genuinely fixed: all tariffs are now 3/6/12/33; the hints on questions 7, 8, 9 and 10 nudge without supplying the indicative content, the formula or the working; questions 11 and 12 are now properly synoptic, reaching the carbon cycle and food security, and coastal landscapes and global migration, at more than one scale (all four are real content elsewhere in H481); the UK and Bangladesh fact files each carry impacts, opportunities, threats and technological/socio-economic/political challenges for both mitigation and adaptation; UK emissions read approximately 50% below 1990 by 2022; shoreline management plans are correctly described as non-statutory with hold the line / advance the line / managed realignment / no active intervention; the USA's Paris status now runs to the January 2025 notice effective January 2026; Bangladesh is an LIDC; and the missing 4.c bullet on future homes, cities, transport and economies is now taught in lesson.idea[5], asked in question 14 and checked in lesson.check. The fact files are the USA, China, the UK and Bangladesh as required. I re-solved both calculations — (354-317)/317 = 11.67% and (414-354)/354 = 16.95%, and the worked example's (317-280)/280 = 13.21% — and every answer key, date and figure (Milankovitch periods, obliquity range, 40.3 degrees Celsius at Coningsby 2022, Kyoto 1997/Annex I, Paris 2015/NDCs, USA approximately one-fifth cumulative and approximately 14 t per capita, China over 10 Gt and approximately 8 t per capita, CCA 2008/net zero 2019) checks out; the worked example and essay plan no longer duplicate questions 8 and 13. For polish only: the sol to question 8 says 16.95% 'rounds to approximately 16.9%', which is right from 16.949% but reads like a rounding slip, so quote 16.95% or 16.9% consistently; room.facts[1] and the matching card give warming as approximately 1.1-1.2 degrees Celsius without a date, and should be dated to the early 2020s as the lesson example does, since the decadal figure is now nearer 1.2-1.3; and question 14 (3 marks, tier 1) sits after the 33-mark question, so the room's difficulty order jumps back at the end."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:55:22.800Z"}},"3.2":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"3.2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because Disease Dilemmas is one of the Geographical debates options examined on Component 03, where OCR sets short answer questions of 3 marks, medium length questions of 6 marks, synoptic questions of 12 marks and extended response questions of 33 marks across Sections A, B and C, and every tariff draws on the seven named case studies below. The specification's own enquiry questions — how far can diseases be predicted and mitigated against, and how effectively are communicable and noncommunicable diseases dealt with — are the exact wording examiners set as 33-mark essay titles, so a top-level answer must weigh physical and human causes, use the required named examples as evidence rather than description, and link disease to the wider specification (development, globalisation, governance) across scales.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Classifying and mapping disease","t":"Diseases are classified along several axes the specification names precisely: infectious or non-infectious, communicable (passed between hosts) or noncommunicable, contagious (direct contact) or non-contagious, and by scale of outbreak — epidemic (a local or regional surge), endemic (constantly present at a baseline level) or pandemic (a global outbreak). Global distributions of malaria, HIV, tuberculosis, diabetes and cardio-vascular disease show that communicable disease concentrates in the tropics and sub-tropics while noncommunicable disease concentrates in advanced and emerging economies. The Hägerstrand model explains how a disease diffuses to new areas through phases (primary, diffusion, condensing and saturation), spreading outward from a source unless physical barriers (oceans, mountains, distance) or socio-economic barriers (border controls, poverty, poor infrastructure) slow it. A top-level answer names the correct classification and the correct diffusion phase for the disease in the question, rather than describing spread as one undifferentiated process."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Physical factors and the changing prevalence of disease","t":"Physical geography sets the template for where disease can take hold. Global patterns of temperature, precipitation, relief and water sources determine which vectors can survive: mosquitoes that carry malaria and dengue thrive in warm, humid conditions close to standing water, so disease risk tracks climate rather than population alone. Seasonal variation shifts risk within a single year — drought concentrates people and animals around shrinking water sources and raises cholera risk, while monsoon rains create breeding pools and raise vector-borne disease risk. Climate change is shifting these patterns over time, expanding the range of vectors such as the tsetse fly and disease-carrying ticks into new latitudes and altitudes, and creating conditions for emerging diseases such as West Nile virus to establish in places they were previously absent. Zoonotic diseases such as bird flu and rabies add a further physical dimension: they require close human-animal contact to jump species (spill over) before they can spread person to person. A top-level answer links a specific physical variable (temperature, rainfall, altitude) to a specific vector or pathogen, rather than asserting climate 'affects' disease in general terms."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Natural hazards and disease outbreaks — Haiti's 2010 earthquake and cholera","t":"A natural hazard can trigger a disease outbreak by destroying the infrastructure that normally prevents it. Haiti's magnitude 7.0 earthquake of January 2010 devastated the capital Port-au-Prince and displaced approximately 1.5 million people into overcrowded camps with little sanitation. The geographical area covered by the hazard mattered less than what it did to water and sanitation systems: damaged pipes and open defecation contaminated the Artibonite River, and human factors — high population density in camps, very low pre-existing immunity to cholera in Haiti, and limited immunisation infrastructure — turned this into the country's worst cholera epidemic, introduced (evidence points to UN peacekeepers) into a population with no prior exposure. The disease's impact on the resident population was severe, and the strategy used to minimise it combined national action (rehydration centres, chlorination of water points) with international support (UNICEF, PAHO, an oral cholera vaccination campaign from 2012). A top-level answer separates the hazard itself from the human and environmental factors that turned it into an epidemic, since the earthquake alone did not cause cholera."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Disease and the epidemiological transition — China's air pollution and cancer","t":"As countries develop, rising standards of living — better access to food, clean water and sanitation — reduce exposure to the pathogens behind communicable disease, so a country's epidemiological transition sees infectious disease decline while noncommunicable disease rises as life expectancy lengthens and lifestyles change. This is why LIDCs still carry a disproportionate burden of 'diseases of poverty' such as malaria and diarrhoeal disease, while advanced countries carry a disproportionate burden of 'diseases of affluence' such as cancer and cardio-vascular disease. China's rapid industrialisation illustrates the transition's dark side: heavy reliance on coal power and vehicle growth has produced some of the world's highest urban PM2.5 concentrations, and Chinese public health research links this air pollution to rising incidence of lung and bladder cancers, particularly in industrial cities such as Linfen. China's response has combined national solutions (the 2013 Action Plan on Air Pollution Prevention and Control, closing or relocating coal plants, tighter vehicle emissions standards) with global solutions (Paris Agreement commitments, renewable energy investment). A top-level answer treats the epidemiological transition as a mechanism (which disease types rise or fall, and why) rather than only a description of a country getting richer."},{"code":"3.a","h":"A communicable disease case study — malaria in Ethiopia","t":"Ethiopia's malaria burden shows how environmental and human causes combine. Environmentally, risk concentrates below approximately 2,000m altitude, where warm, humid conditions during the kiremt rainy season (roughly June to September) create pools that breed the Anopheles mosquito vector, so incidence rises sharply after the rains and falls in the cooler, drier months. Human causes include poor-quality housing that lets mosquitoes enter at night, low bed-net coverage in remote highland-fringe communities, and seasonal population movement for agricultural labour into higher-risk lowland areas. The prevalence, incidence and pattern of the disease therefore vary sharply by season and altitude, and its socio-economic impacts include lost agricultural labour during peak farming season and pressure on rural health budgets. Direct strategies used by the Ethiopian government and international agencies (supported by the US President's Malaria Initiative) include distributing insecticide-treated bed nets (ITNs), indoor residual spraying (IRS) and artemisinin-based combination therapies (ACTs); indirect strategies include community health worker training and drainage of standing water. A top-level answer connects the seasonal environmental cause directly to the strategy timed against it, rather than listing causes and strategies separately."},{"code":"3.b","h":"A noncommunicable disease case study — coronary heart disease in the UK","t":"Coronary heart disease (CHD) is the UK's case study for a noncommunicable disease of affluence at an AC scale. Its causes are social, economic and cultural rather than environmental: diets high in saturated fat and salt, smoking, physical inactivity linked to car-dependent and sedentary lifestyles, and an ageing population that has more time to develop the disease. Prevalence is uneven — CHD rates are consistently higher in more deprived areas of the UK, such as parts of Scotland and northern England, reflecting the social gradient in diet, smoking and stress. Its socio-economic impacts include NHS treatment costs (surgery, statins, cardiac rehabilitation) and lost working days. Direct strategies include NHS Health Checks that screen for risk factors, free prescription of statins for at-risk groups, and hospital-based cardiac care; indirect strategies include the ban on smoking in enclosed public places, completed across the UK in 2007 (Scotland from March 2006, England from July 2007), and the sugar tax (Soft Drinks Industry Levy) intended to reduce a dietary risk factor. A top-level answer separates the causes into their social, economic and cultural strands and matches each strategy to the specific risk factor it targets."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Global mobility, outbreak identification and an NGO's response","t":"Global mobility means a local outbreak can become an international one within days, which is why the World Health Organization (WHO) exists to predict diseases, gather and share surveillance data, fund research, run support programmes and coordinate the work of national governments and other agencies. The 2009 H1N1 ('swine flu') outbreak illustrates a disease identified at a global scale: identified in April 2009, with cases later traced back to March 2009 in Mexico, air travel networks carried it onward until the WHO declared a pandemic on 11 June 2009, by which point 74 countries had reported cases, showing how the rate of spread and the pattern of outbreak distribution now follow flight routes rather than simple distance-decay. At national and local level, NGOs fill gaps state health systems cannot reach: Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF) ran Ebola treatment centres during the 2014–16 West African outbreak, including at Kailahun in Sierra Leone, providing isolation wards, safe burial teams and training for local health workers in a country where the formal health system had only a handful of doctors per 100,000 people. A top-level answer distinguishes the WHO's international, predictive role from an NGO's direct, local delivery role rather than treating all disease response as one thing."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Mitigating global pandemics and overcoming physical barriers","t":"Physical barriers cut both ways in disease response. Relief, natural hazards, excess water and the remoteness of communities can protect a place by isolating it from an outbreak, but the same features make it desperately hard to deliver testing, vaccines or treatment once disease does arrive — mountainous or flood-prone regions routinely see the slowest, most expensive response efforts per person reached. Mitigation strategies against a global pandemic such as HIV/AIDS have to work around these barriers at scale: UNAIDS-coordinated screening programmes identify cases, the President's Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) has funded antiretroviral treatment access across dozens of LIDCs since 2003, and education programmes (encouraging condom use and reducing stigma) target behaviour change where treatment access is weakest. A top-level answer weighs the positive and negative effects of a named physical barrier together, rather than treating remoteness as only an obstacle."},{"code":"5.a","h":"Medicines from nature — the rosy periwinkle of Madagascar","t":"Nature has supplied medicines for thousands of years, and the plants involved need specific soil and climate conditions to grow, so their supply is geographically fixed. The rosy periwinkle (Catharanthus roseus) is Madagascar's case study: native to the island's now-shrunk dry deciduous forests, it produces the alkaloids vincristine and vinblastine. Pharmaceutical research (from the 1950s onward, notably by Eli Lilly) turned these into chemotherapy drugs that raised the survival rate for childhood leukaemia from around 10% to approximately 90%, and are also used against Hodgkin's lymphoma. International trade in the plant and its derivatives is now a global pharmaceutical business, but conservation issues follow: Madagascar has lost the great majority of its original forest cover, threatening the wild population's genetic diversity, and Madagascar itself has captured little of the plant's commercial value, a widely cited example of 'biopiracy' where the country of origin does not share fairly in profits made from its biodiversity. A top-level answer links the plant's specific habitat requirement to the conservation threat, rather than treating 'nature provides medicine' as a general claim."},{"code":"5.b","h":"Top-down and bottom-up strategies — GlaxoSmithKline and grassroots eradication","t":"Disease eradication strategies work at very different scales. Top down, a pharmaceutical transnational such as GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) makes scientific breakthroughs — including the RTS,S/Mosquirix malaria vaccine, WHO-recommended for wide use from 2021 — that it protects with patents, manufactures at plants located across several countries and distributes through global supply flows; GSK has pledged to cap prices in the least developed countries at a fraction of prices elsewhere and reinvest a share of related profits into local healthcare infrastructure. Global and national eradication campaigns coordinate this top-down capacity at scale, as the WHO-led effort that eradicated smallpox by 1980 shows. Bottom up, grassroots strategies rely on educating communities rather than new drugs: the Guinea worm eradication campaign, led by the Carter Center with national health ministries, trained village-level volunteers — disproportionately women, who typically collect household water — to filter drinking water and contain infected cases, cutting reported cases from approximately 3.5 million in 1986 to fewer than 15 by the early 2020s without any vaccine or drug. A top-level answer sets a top-down and a bottom-up strategy against each other and judges which did more work, rather than describing them as two unconnected lists."}],"examples":[{"title":"Calculating a malaria incidence rate per 100,000 — a woreda in Ethiopia","setup":"A woreda (district) in Ethiopia has a mid-year population of 620,000 and records 9,300 confirmed malaria cases during one kiremt rainy season. Calculate the incidence rate per 100,000 people, and say what a rate of that size implies for the timing of control measures.","steps":["Use the rate formula: incidence per 100,000 = (cases ÷ population) × 100,000, so the case count is measured against everyone at risk in the district, not against the number treated.","Divide first: 9,300 ÷ 620,000 = 0.015, the share of the woreda's people infected in that one season.","Scale it up: 0.015 × 100,000 = 1,500 cases per 100,000 people.","So the incidence rate is 1,500 per 100,000 in a single kiremt season — a burden concentrated in a few months, which is why indoor residual spraying and bed-net distribution are timed to the weeks just before the rains rather than spread evenly through the year."],"cues":["What must the case count be measured against?","What share of the woreda's people does that division give?","How do you turn that share into a rate per 100,000?","What is the rate, and what does its timing imply?"]},{"title":"Reading a percentage change — Haiti's cholera epidemic, 2011 to 2016","setup":"Haiti recorded approximately 340,000 suspected cholera cases in 2011, the epidemic's first full year, falling to approximately 22,000–28,000 in 2014, then rising to about 36,000 in 2015 and just over 41,000 in 2016. Calculate the percentage change from 2011 to 2016 and interpret the trend the figures show.","steps":["Pick out the two end years the question names: 41,000 cases in 2016 set against 340,000 in 2011, and the change is measured against 2011, the earlier figure.","Percentage change = (new − old) ÷ old × 100 = (41,000 − 340,000) ÷ 340,000 × 100.","= −299,000 ÷ 340,000 × 100 = −87.9%, that is a fall of approximately 88%.","Now check the years in between, because two end points can hide the path: the series bottoms out at 22,000–28,000 in 2014 and then climbs again, to 36,000 in 2015 and just over 41,000 in 2016.","So cholera cases fell by approximately 88% from 2011 to 2016, but not steadily: chlorination, rehydration centres and the oral cholera vaccination campaign cut the epidemic sharply, while the rebound through 2015–16 shows it could resurge wherever clean water and sanitation lapsed, so the response contained the epidemic rather than ending it."],"cues":["Which two figures does the question actually compare?","Which of the two is the change measured against?","What does that come to as a percentage?","Did the fall happen evenly across the years between?","What is the change, and what does the trend really show?"]},{"title":"Applying the Hägerstrand model — H1N1's 2009 spread","setup":"H1N1 was identified in April 2009 and traced back to a source area in Mexico in March 2009; international flight routes carried it out of Mexico and the USA within weeks. The WHO declared a pandemic on 11 June 2009, by which point 74 countries had reported cases, and the virus eventually reached more than 170 countries. Assign these facts to the four stages of the Hägerstrand diffusion model and say what controls the pattern at each stage.","steps":["Primary stage: the single source area in Mexico in March 2009 — one place infected while everywhere else is still clear, which is where Hägerstrand's model starts.","Diffusion stage: the jump out of Mexico and the USA in April 2009 runs along the air network, so what decides which country is reached next is not distance from Mexico but whether it has direct flights — which is why Spain and New Zealand recorded cases before some of Mexico's own neighbours.","Condensing stage: by the WHO's declaration on 11 June 2009, 74 countries had reported cases and outbreaks were thickening inside those already reached, with local transmission rather than fresh imported cases driving the numbers.","Saturation stage: with more than 170 countries affected there were few unreached places left, so change came in case numbers within affected countries rather than in the count of new countries.","So the stages map onto H1N1 as primary (Mexico, March 2009), diffusion (the flight network, April 2009), condensing (74 countries by 11 June 2009) and saturation (beyond 170 countries), and the control at every stage is air-travel connectivity rather than distance from the source — the model's neat distance-decay waves appear within countries, not between them."],"cues":["Which stage is a single source area, and where was it?","What decides which country is reached next — distance, or something else?","What is happening inside infected countries by 11 June 2009?","What changes once most countries have been reached?","Map the four stages onto H1N1 and name what controls the pattern."]},{"title":"Contrasting the epidemiological transition — Ethiopia and the UK","setup":"Ethiopia is an LIDC whose leading disease burden is malaria; the UK is an AC whose leading cause of death includes coronary heart disease. Contrast the two: the type of disease, the causes behind it and the strategy used against it, then say what the contrast is evidence of.","steps":["Malaria in Ethiopia is communicable, a disease of poverty: it needs the Anopheles mosquito breeding in standing water after the kiremt rains, and it spreads where sanitation is limited, housing is open to mosquitoes and bed-net coverage is incomplete in rural areas.","Coronary heart disease in the UK is noncommunicable, a disease of affluence: nothing is transmitted between people, and the drivers are diet high in saturated fat, salt and sugar, physical inactivity, smoking and an ageing population.","Each strategy has to match its cause, so they look nothing alike: Ethiopia attacks the vector and the transmission route with insecticide-treated nets and indoor residual spraying timed to the rains, while the UK attacks behaviour and risk factors through the smoking ban, the soft drinks industry levy and NHS Health Checks.","The contrast is evidence of the epidemiological transition: as living standards, sanitation and life expectancy rise, the leading burden shifts from communicable diseases of poverty to noncommunicable diseases of affluence — Ethiopia and the UK are not simply different places, but two points on the same pathway."],"cues":["What kind of disease is malaria, and what does it need to spread?","How does the UK's leading disease differ in kind and in cause?","What must each country's strategy target to work at all?","What single process does the contrast illustrate?"]},{"title":"Weighing a top-down and a bottom-up strategy — Guinea worm eradication","setup":"In the campaign against Guinea worm disease the Carter Center coordinated national health ministries, funding and surveillance across more than 20 endemic countries, while village volunteers, mostly women, filtered drinking water through cloth and kept infected people away from ponds and wells. Reported cases fell from approximately 3.5 million in 1986 to fewer than 15 by the early 2020s, and no vaccine or drug was ever developed. Weigh the top-down and the bottom-up contributions to that result.","steps":["Start with what the top-down half could not do: with no vaccine and no drug, the only way to break the worm's cycle is to stop an infected person putting a limb into drinking water, and no ministry or donor can supervise every pond, so the intervention itself had to be carried out locally.","The villages could not act alone either: containing single cases means knowing where each one is, which took the surveillance network, the cash rewards for reporting a case and the cross-border coordination that the Carter Center and ministries supplied across more than 20 countries.","The division of labour shows in the shape of the fall: mass filtering and case containment by volunteers took 3.5 million cases in 1986 down to about 25,000 by 2006 and a few thousand by 2010, while the last stretch to fewer than 15 by the early 2020s depended on surveillance tracing individual cases, including infections in dogs.","Judgement: neither strategy earns the credit alone. Top-down coordination supplied the money, the data and the reach across more than 20 countries, bottom-up action by village volunteers supplied the daily behaviour change that actually interrupted transmission, and the pattern of the decline shows why — bottom-up work removed the bulk of the burden, after which only top-down surveillance could find what was left."],"cues":["With no vaccine or drug, what has to be stopped to break the cycle?","What could the villages not manage on their own?","Which half did the early work, and which the final cases?","Which half deserves the credit for the fall in cases?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between an epidemic and a pandemic?","a":"An epidemic is a disease outbreak spreading actively within a local or regional population; a pandemic is an outbreak that has spread across multiple countries or continents, affecting a large number of people globally."},{"q":"Name the four phases of the Hägerstrand diffusion model.","a":"Primary, diffusion, condensing and saturation."},{"q":"Give one environmental and one human cause of Ethiopia's seasonal malaria pattern.","a":"Environmental: warm, humid conditions in the kiremt rainy season create mosquito-breeding pools. Human: low bed-net coverage and poor housing in affected communities."},{"q":"What does 'epidemiological transition' mean?","a":"The shift, as a country develops economically, from communicable disease being the dominant health burden to noncommunicable disease becoming dominant."}]},"room":{"facts":["The four stages of the Hägerstrand diffusion model are: primary, diffusion, condensing and saturation.","Epidemic = an active outbreak within a local/regional population; endemic = constantly present at a baseline level; pandemic = spread across multiple countries or continents.","As countries develop, the epidemiological transition sees communicable 'diseases of poverty' (e.g. malaria in LIDCs) decline in relative importance while noncommunicable 'diseases of affluence' (e.g. cardio-vascular disease in ACs) rise.","A zoonotic disease (e.g. bird flu, rabies) spills over from an animal host to humans before it can spread person to person.","The rosy periwinkle (Catharanthus roseus), native to Madagascar, yields the alkaloids vincristine and vinblastine, used in leukaemia and Hodgkin's lymphoma chemotherapy.","The Guinea worm eradication campaign (Carter Center) cut reported cases from approximately 3.5 million in 1986 to fewer than 15 by the early 2020s, using grassroots education and no vaccine or drug.","Haiti's cholera epidemic followed the January 2010 earthquake; suspected cases fell from approximately 340,000 in 2011 to approximately 22,000–28,000 in 2014, then rose again to about 36,000 in 2015 and just over 41,000 in 2016, before the last confirmed case on 4 February 2019 and Haiti being declared cholera-free in February 2022 (cholera resurged in October 2022).","GlaxoSmithKline's RTS,S/Mosquirix malaria vaccine was recommended by the WHO for wide use in 2021, the first WHO-recommended malaria vaccine."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify the difference between a communicable and a noncommunicable disease.","a":"A communicable disease can be passed between hosts (e.g. by a pathogen, vector or contact); a noncommunicable disease cannot be passed between people.","sol":"A communicable disease is one that can be transmitted from one host to another, whether directly (contact) or indirectly (through a vector such as a mosquito, or a contaminated source such as water). A noncommunicable disease, by contrast, cannot pass between people — it develops within an individual, usually from a combination of genetic, lifestyle, environmental or ageing factors, such as cardio-vascular disease or diabetes.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what has to happen for one person's illness to become another person's illness.","Once you've thought that through, turn it into a formal definition for each term, rather than just describing an example disease.","Structure your answer as: define communicable → define noncommunicable, in contrast."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify the four phases of the Hägerstrand model of disease diffusion, in order.","a":"Primary, diffusion, condensing, saturation.","sol":"The Hägerstrand model describes diffusion in four ordered phases: the primary stage, where the disease is confined to its source area; the diffusion stage, where it spreads rapidly outward, often along transport or contact networks; the condensing stage, where the rate of new cases slows as the disease becomes established more evenly across the affected area; and the saturation stage, where the disease has spread as far as it will, limited by physical or socio-economic barriers.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think of the sequence as moving from a single starting point outward until it stops spreading.","Recall the four named stages, in the order the model sets them.","Structure your answer as: name stage one → stage two → stage three → stage four, in order."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Explain how warm, humid conditions close to water sources influence the vector responsible for malaria.","a":"Warm, humid conditions with standing or slow water let mosquito larvae develop, so the malaria-carrying Anopheles mosquito breeds successfully and disease risk rises where these conditions occur.","sol":"The Anopheles mosquito, the vector for malaria, needs standing or slow-moving water to lay eggs and for its larvae to develop, and needs warmth and humidity for the adult mosquito and the malaria parasite it carries to survive. Where global patterns of temperature, precipitation and water sources combine to provide both — for example in tropical lowlands near rivers or seasonal pools — mosquito populations, and therefore malaria risk, rise; where any one condition is missing, such as in cool highlands or arid areas without standing water, the vector cannot establish and risk falls.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a mosquito needs at each stage of its life cycle, not just as an adult.","Link temperature and water together, since either alone is not enough for the vector to establish.","Structure your answer as: state the two conditions the vector needs → explain why both matter → state the effect on disease risk."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Identify two roles the World Health Organization plays in responding to global disease outbreaks.","a":"Two of: predicting diseases, gathering and sharing surveillance data, funding/coordinating research, running support programmes, coordinating with national governments and agencies.","sol":"The World Health Organization predicts diseases by monitoring emerging risks, gathers and shares surveillance data from member states so outbreaks can be tracked globally, funds and coordinates research into treatments and vaccines, runs support programmes in affected countries, and coordinates the response of national governments and other agencies so efforts are not duplicated or contradictory.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a country cannot do alone once a disease crosses its border.","Choose two distinct roles from the list the specification gives for the WHO, rather than one described twice.","Structure your answer as: name role one → name role two."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Explain how the 2010 Haiti earthquake created the conditions for a major cholera outbreak.","a":"The earthquake destroyed sanitation infrastructure and displaced approximately 1.5 million people into overcrowded camps with contaminated water and no prior population immunity to cholera, which then spread rapidly once introduced.","sol":"Haiti's January 2010 earthquake did not itself cause cholera, but it created the human and environmental conditions for an outbreak. It destroyed water and sanitation infrastructure in and around Port-au-Prince and displaced approximately 1.5 million people into overcrowded camps with poor sanitation and open defecation, which contaminated the Artibonite River. Because Haiti had no recent history of cholera, the population had very low immunity, so once the bacterium was introduced (evidence points to UN peacekeepers), it spread explosively through the contaminated water supply and the high-density camp population, becoming the country's worst cholera epidemic in modern history.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what the earthquake itself damaged from what happened to the population afterwards.","Work through the chain in three general stages: immediate physical damage, the effect on everyday living conditions, then the disease outcome.","Structure your answer as: what the hazard damaged → the human conditions this created → why this allowed the outbreak to spread so fast."],"codes":["1.c"]},{"q":"A woreda in Ethiopia has a mid-year population of 620,000 and records 9,300 confirmed malaria cases during one kiremt rainy season. Calculate the incidence rate per 100,000 population, and explain one reason cases cluster in this season.","a":"Incidence rate = (9,300 ÷ 620,000) × 100,000 = 1,500 per 100,000. Reason: the kiremt rains (roughly June–September) create standing water that lets the Anopheles mosquito vector breed.","sol":"Incidence rate per 100,000 = (cases ÷ population) × 100,000 = (9,300 ÷ 620,000) × 100,000 = 1,500 per 100,000 population. Cases cluster in the kiremt rainy season because the rains create pools and standing water in which the Anopheles mosquito, the vector for malaria, can breed; the following weeks of warm, humid weather then let mosquito numbers, and so transmission, rise sharply before falling again once the dry season returns.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Recall the formula for turning a raw count of cases into a rate per 100,000 population.","Substitute the two figures given into the formula before you explain the seasonal cause.","Structure your answer as: state the formula → show the substitution and answer → explain the seasonal link to rainfall."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Explain how China's air pollution has contributed to rising cancer incidence, and outline one national solution.","a":"Coal power and vehicle growth have raised PM2.5 levels in Chinese cities, linked by public health research to rising lung and bladder cancer incidence; China's 2013 Action Plan on Air Pollution Prevention and Control is a national solution (closing/relocating coal plants, tighter vehicle emissions standards).","sol":"China's rapid industrialisation, built on heavy coal use for power and a fast-growing vehicle fleet, has produced some of the world's highest urban concentrations of fine particulate matter (PM2.5), especially in industrial cities such as Linfen. Long-term exposure to this pollution is linked by Chinese public health research to rising incidence of lung and bladder cancers, since inhaled and ingested particulates carry carcinogens into the lungs and bloodstream. China's national solution, the 2013 Action Plan on Air Pollution Prevention and Control, closed or relocated coal-fired power stations near major cities and introduced tighter vehicle emissions standards, aiming to cut the pollution at its source rather than only treating the cancers it causes.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what two things China burns in very large quantities as it industrialises.","Link the pollutant to the route by which it reaches the body before causing cancer.","Structure your answer as: name the pollution source → explain the route to cancer → name the national policy response."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain two strategies used to mitigate against coronary heart disease in the UK.","a":"For example: the UK-wide ban on smoking in enclosed public places, completed in 2007 (Scotland March 2006, England July 2007), which reduces a direct risk factor, and NHS Health Checks that screen for and treat risk factors such as high cholesterol with statins.","sol":"One direct strategy is NHS Health Checks, offered to adults aged 40–74, which screen for risk factors such as high blood pressure and high cholesterol so at-risk individuals can be prescribed statins or given lifestyle advice before heart disease develops. One indirect strategy is the ban on smoking in enclosed public places, completed across the UK in 2007 — Scotland led with a ban from March 2006, and England completed the UK-wide ban from July 2007 — which reduced both active and passive smoking exposure across the population, addressing one of CHD's major lifestyle risk factors without needing to treat individuals directly. Together these show the UK using both individual medical screening and population-wide policy to reduce CHD risk.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think of one strategy that targets an individual directly and one that changes conditions for everyone at once.","Once you've picked your two strategies, check each is paired with the specific CHD risk factor it targets, rather than both being described only as 'good for the heart' in general.","Structure your answer as: strategy one, with the risk factor it targets → strategy two, with the risk factor it targets."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how GlaxoSmithKline's approach to pricing and manufacturing affects global access to medicines.","a":"GSK manufactures across several countries and has pledged to cap prices in the least developed countries at a fraction of prices elsewhere, and to reinvest a share of related profits into local healthcare infrastructure, which widens access beyond what market pricing alone would allow.","sol":"As a pharmaceutical transnational, GlaxoSmithKline holds patents on its breakthroughs (such as the RTS,S/Mosquirix malaria vaccine, WHO-recommended from 2021) and manufactures at plants located across several countries rather than one, which builds distribution capacity closer to where diseases such as malaria are most prevalent. GSK has committed to capping the price of its products in the least developed countries at a fraction of the price charged in wealthier markets, and to reinvesting a share of the profits earned in LIDCs back into local healthcare infrastructure. This tiered approach widens access for LIDCs that could not otherwise afford the same price as an AC, though it still leaves GSK, not the country of use, setting the terms of access.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about the two separate choices a TNC makes: where it manufactures, and what it charges different markets.","Once you've explained the pricing pledge, think about who ends up benefiting most from a price capped as a fraction of the price charged elsewhere.","Structure your answer as: what GSK does with patents and manufacturing → what GSK does with LIDC pricing → the effect this has on access."],"codes":["5.b"]},{"q":"Explain how conservation issues threaten the future supply of the rosy periwinkle as a source of medicine.","a":"Habitat loss in Madagascar's dry forests threatens the wild rosy periwinkle population and erodes its genetic diversity, which could reduce future medicinal discovery from the species and threatens the ecosystem it depends on.","sol":"The rosy periwinkle is native to Madagascar's dry deciduous forests, but Madagascar has lost the great majority of its original forest cover to agriculture and logging. This habitat loss directly threatens the wild periwinkle population's survival and erodes its genetic diversity, which matters because different wild populations may carry different concentrations of the alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine) that give the plant its medicinal value — losing that diversity narrows future opportunities for pharmaceutical discovery. The conservation issue is compounded by the wider threat to the forest ecosystem the plant depends on, and by the fact that Madagascar has captured little of the commercial value the plant has generated internationally, giving it limited resources to protect the habitat itself.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what habitat loss does to a wild plant population beyond simply reducing its numbers.","Once you've explained the effect on genetic diversity, think one step further about what that diversity might still hold that nobody has found a use for yet.","Structure your answer as: state the habitat threat → explain the effect on genetic diversity → link this to future medicinal use."],"codes":["5.a"]},{"q":"\"Economic development, rather than physical processes such as climate change or human processes such as global migration, is the main reason a country's disease burden shifts from communicable to noncommunicable disease.\" To what extent do you agree, drawing on evidence at more than one scale?","a":"Development-driven improvements in sanitation, food and healthcare do reduce communicable disease and lengthen life expectancy into the age range where noncommunicable disease appears (Ethiopia vs UK contrast), but physical geography (China's pollution-driven cancer) and lifestyle choices also shape the noncommunicable burden independently of development level, so development is a major but not the only driver.","sol":"There is strong evidence that economic development drives the epidemiological transition. As standards of living rise, access to clean water, sanitation and food reduces exposure to the pathogens behind communicable 'diseases of poverty': Ethiopia, an LIDC, still carries a heavy malaria burden linked to limited sanitation and low bed-net coverage in rural areas. As life expectancy lengthens with development, populations live long enough to develop 'diseases of affluence' — the UK's leading noncommunicable disease, coronary heart disease, reflects an ageing population as well as diet and inactivity. This supports the claim that development is the main driver, since it changes both the exposure to communicable disease and the age structure that noncommunicable disease depends on. However, development is not the only driver: China shows that a country can industrialise rapidly and still generate a new noncommunicable disease burden, as air pollution from coal power and vehicles has raised lung and bladder cancer incidence — here it is the process of development itself, through pollution, that has caused a noncommunicable disease, not merely rising living standards reducing communicable disease. Lifestyle and cultural factors, such as UK smoking rates or diet, also operate partly independently of income level. The picture also reaches beyond this topic's own case studies: climate change — part of the water and carbon cycles content examined elsewhere in the specification — is a physical process operating at a global scale that is independently reshaping disease exposure, by expanding the range of disease vectors into new areas regardless of any single country's development; and global migration, examined in Global Migration (Global systems Option B), moves people and lifestyles between countries at the individual and household scale, so a migrant's diet or activity levels can shift a local population's noncommunicable risk well before national income statistics change. On balance, economic development is the single strongest driver of the shift, because it reduces communicable disease exposure and extends life expectancy, but the case of China's air pollution shows development can simultaneously generate noncommunicable disease directly, so the shift is better understood as development changing what a population is exposed to, for better and worse, alongside physical processes such as climate change and human processes such as migration operating at their own scales, rather than a single simple cause operating at the national scale alone.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about development doing two different things to a disease profile: removing old risks and creating new ones.","For the 'for' side, use the case that shows the classic shift as living standards rise; for the complication, use the case where development itself creates a new noncommunicable risk.","Structure your answer as: evidence for development as the driver → the complicating case → a weighed conclusion on how far development alone explains the shift."],"codes":["2.a","3.a","3.b"]},{"q":"Evaluate the effectiveness of top-down and bottom-up strategies in responding to disease outbreaks and disease risk, from local to global scale, and consider how far this mirrors the top-down/bottom-up divide in how the world manages other shared risks, such as water security or food security.","a":"Top-down strategies (WHO coordination, GSK's vaccine and pricing) provide scale, funding and technical capacity; genuinely bottom-up strategies (Guinea worm's village volunteers) reach communities top-down systems cannot; MSF is a third model — an international NGO delivering care at local scale, not the same as grassroots community action. The Guinea worm case shows top-down and bottom-up are usually needed together, so effectiveness depends on how well these models are combined rather than on choosing one.","sol":"Top-down strategies bring scale and technical capacity that bottom-up efforts cannot. GlaxoSmithKline's RTS,S/Mosquirix vaccine and its tiered LIDC pricing show how a pharmaceutical transnational can fund research and manufacture at a scale no NGO or community programme could match, and the WHO's coordination role lets national responses share data and avoid duplicating effort. These strategies are effective at building the tools and infrastructure a response depends on, but they can be slow to reach the local level and depend on a country's existing health system to deliver them. A different model closes part of that gap at local scale without being genuinely bottom-up: Médecins Sans Frontières, an international NGO, ran Ebola treatment centres in Sierra Leone in 2014–16, delivering isolation wards and safe burial teams directly at village and district level in a country where the formal health system was severely under-resourced — but MSF's funding, staff and expertise came from outside the community, so this is international delivery at local scale rather than the grassroots strategy the specification names under idea 5.b. The Guinea worm eradication campaign is the clean bottom-up case: it trained village volunteers, disproportionately women, to filter water and contain infected cases themselves, cutting reported cases from approximately 3.5 million in 1986 to fewer than 15 by the early 2020s without any vaccine ever being developed. This shows genuinely bottom-up, community-led delivery can be transformative where it reaches people top-down systems cannot, but it typically still depends on top-down coordination and funding (the Carter Center's national-level partnerships) to operate at scale. This top-down/bottom-up tension is not confined to disease: the same divide structures how the world manages other risks studied elsewhere in the specification — international governance of water security and food security likewise combines top-down treaties, agencies and funding with bottom-up, community-level adaptation — so disease response is one example of a wider geographical pattern in managing shared risk across local, national and global scales, rather than an isolated case. On balance, neither strategy alone is consistently more effective: the Guinea worm case is the clearest evidence that success comes from combining top-down coordination and funding with genuinely bottom-up, community-level delivery, while MSF shows that international, locally-delivered support is a valuable third model in its own right, so effectiveness should be judged by how well the two are integrated rather than which type of strategy is used, at whatever scale — local, national or global — the risk is being managed.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what scale a top-down strategy can reach that a bottom-up one cannot, and vice versa.","Look for the case that shows what happens when a top-down approach and a bottom-up approach are both used together, and use it as your central evidence.","Structure your answer as: strength of top-down → strength of bottom-up → a judgement on which matters more, or whether they depend on each other."],"codes":["4.a","5.b"]},{"q":"To what extent do physical factors — including the climate change processes examined in the water and carbon cycles topic — rather than human factors such as global migration and the character of place, determine global patterns of disease, at scales from the local to the global?","a":"Physical factors (temperature, rainfall, altitude driving vector ranges, e.g. Ethiopia's seasonal malaria) set the underlying template of where disease can occur, but human factors (sanitation, immunisation, housing, remoteness, conflict) determine whether that potential becomes an actual outbreak and how effectively it is contained, so the two interact rather than physical factors alone determining the pattern.","sol":"Physical factors clearly shape where disease can occur. Global patterns of temperature, rainfall and water sources determine whether a vector such as the Anopheles mosquito can survive, which is why Ethiopia's malaria risk tracks the kiremt rainy season and lowland altitude so closely; climate change is now shifting these patterns further by expanding vector ranges (such as the tsetse fly) into new areas. This supports treating physical factors as a fundamental determinant, since without the right climate the disease's vector cannot establish at all, regardless of human behaviour. However, human factors decide whether that physical potential becomes an actual outbreak, and how badly it affects a population: Haiti's cholera outbreak needed the human factors of damaged sanitation infrastructure, camp overcrowding and no prior population immunity following the 2010 earthquake to turn a bacterium's introduction into an epidemic, and physical remoteness in disease response (idea 4.b) can be overcome or worsened depending on human investment in infrastructure and funding, not physical geography alone. This physical/human balance also connects beyond this topic: climate change, covered in the water and carbon cycles topic, is a physical process reshaping disease risk at a genuinely global scale by shifting vector ranges, while human processes such as global migration and the character of individual places — examined in Global Migration (Global systems Option B) and Changing Spaces; Making Places — determine how quickly a locally physical risk becomes a national or global human problem, showing the physical/human interaction operates across scales, not only within a single country. On balance, physical factors set the underlying possibility for a disease's geographical range, but human factors — sanitation, housing, immunisation coverage, and how remoteness is responded to — determine whether, when and how severely that possibility is realised, so the two operate together across scales rather than physical factors determining the pattern on their own.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about the difference between a disease being physically possible somewhere and a disease actually breaking out there.","For the physical side, choose a case where climate or environment sets the underlying risk; for the human side, choose a case where the outbreak depended on human conditions being disrupted.","Structure your answer as: the case for physical factors → the case for human factors → a judgement on how the two interact."],"codes":["1.b","1.c","4.b"]},{"q":"\"How effectively are communicable and noncommunicable diseases dealt with?\" Evaluate, with reference to named case studies.","a":"Effective where strategy matches cause and is sustained (Ethiopia's seasonally-timed ITN/IRS distribution, the UK's screening plus population-wide smoking/sugar policy, Guinea worm's near-eradication), but effectiveness is limited by funding, access and equity (LIDC health-system capacity, GSK's pricing still setting the terms of access), so the answer must be a qualified judgement rather than a single yes or no.","sol":"Communicable and noncommunicable diseases are dealt with through direct and indirect strategies whose effectiveness depends on whether they are matched to the disease's specific cause and sustained over time. For malaria in Ethiopia, direct strategies (insecticide-treated bed nets, indoor residual spraying, artemisinin-based combination therapies) are timed against the seasonal environmental cause — the kiremt rains — and supported by the President's Malaria Initiative; where coverage is high, this has reduced incidence, showing that matching strategy to cause can be effective. The Guinea worm eradication campaign is stronger evidence still: combining top-down coordination (the Carter Center, national ministries) with bottom-up grassroots education of village volunteers cut cases from approximately 3.5 million in 1986 to fewer than 15 by the early 2020s without any vaccine, showing that a well-targeted, sustained strategy can approach full eradication even for a disease with no medical cure. For coronary heart disease in the UK, a noncommunicable disease, the combination of individual screening (NHS Health Checks) and population-wide policy (the 2007 smoking ban, the sugar tax) targets both individual risk and the social conditions that produce it, and UK cardiovascular mortality has fallen over recent decades, again suggesting effectiveness where multiple strategy types are combined. However, effectiveness is not universal. Ethiopia's remote, low-income rural communities still see gaps in ITN coverage, showing that funding and health-system capacity limit how far a strategy matched in theory can be delivered in practice; and even where a pharmaceutical transnational such as GlaxoSmithKline develops a breakthrough treatment or vaccine, its pricing decisions still set the terms on which LIDCs can access it, meaning global equity in access remains incomplete even when the medical science is effective. Effectiveness is also shaped by processes examined elsewhere in the specification, which is why a top-level judgement has to reason across scales rather than stopping at the national case study. At the global scale, climate change — part of the water and carbon cycles content examined in Earth's Life Support Systems — is expanding the range of disease vectors such as the tsetse fly into new latitudes and altitudes, so a strategy that is well matched to today's seasonal pattern, such as Ethiopia's kiremt-timed bed-net distribution, may need to be re-timed or extended as that pattern shifts, independently of anything the health strategy itself does differently. At the individual and household scale, global migration — examined in Global Migration (Global systems Option B) — moves people, and the lifestyles and disease exposures they carry, between countries and regions faster than national health strategies are designed to track, so a strategy planned around a country's resident population can be outpaced by risk that arrives with movement rather than developing locally. Both processes show that 'how effectively' a disease is dealt with cannot be judged from a single national scale alone: a strategy that is effective against the disease pattern of one year, at one scale, can be undermined by a physical process reshaping where the disease can occur or a human process reshaping who is exposed to it, so effectiveness must be reasoned across local, national and global scales together, not asserted once and left unqualified. Overall, both communicable and noncommunicable disease strategies can be highly effective where they are matched precisely to the disease's cause and sustained with adequate funding and coordination across scales, as Guinea worm's near-eradication and the UK's falling CHD mortality both show, but effectiveness remains uneven across the world because funding, health-system capacity and pricing by pharmaceutical transnationals still determine how far communities in the poorest countries can actually access what has been proven to work.","m":33,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what 'effectively' means here: not whether a strategy exists, but whether it is matched to the cause and reaches the people at risk.","Draw your evidence from at least one communicable and one noncommunicable case, and at least one that shows a limit on effectiveness.","Structure your answer as: define effectiveness → evidence it has worked (communicable, then noncommunicable) → evidence of its limits → a qualified conclusion on how effectively, overall."],"codes":["3.a","3.b","5.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Name one communicable and one noncommunicable disease named in the specification.","a":"For example: malaria (communicable) and cardio-vascular disease (noncommunicable)."},{"q":"What physical condition does the Anopheles mosquito vector need close by to breed?","a":"Standing or slow-moving water."},{"q":"Which NGO ran Ebola treatment centres in Sierra Leone during the 2014–16 outbreak?","a":"Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF)."},{"q":"Roughly how many reported Guinea worm cases were there in 1986, and how many by the early 2020s?","a":"Approximately 3.5 million in 1986, fewer than 15 by the early 2020s."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Define an epidemic.","back":"A disease outbreak spreading actively within a local or regional population, above its normal baseline level.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Define endemic disease.","back":"A disease constantly present in a population at a stable, baseline level, rather than suddenly surging.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Name the four Hägerstrand diffusion stages, in order.","back":"Primary, diffusion, condensing, saturation.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"What does 'zoonotic' mean?","back":"A disease (e.g. bird flu, rabies) that spills over from an animal host to humans before it can spread person to person.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"What season drives Ethiopia's malaria peak, and why?","back":"The kiremt rains (roughly June–September): standing water lets the Anopheles mosquito vector breed.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"What triggered Haiti's 2010 cholera epidemic?","back":"The January 2010 earthquake damaged sanitation infrastructure and displaced approximately 1.5 million people into overcrowded camps with contaminated water.","code":"1.c"},{"front":"What is the epidemiological transition?","back":"As a country develops economically, communicable 'diseases of poverty' decline in relative importance while noncommunicable 'diseases of affluence' rise.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Name China's 2013 national air-pollution policy.","back":"The Action Plan on Air Pollution Prevention and Control — closed/relocated coal plants and tightened vehicle emissions standards.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Name two direct strategies against malaria in Ethiopia.","back":"Insecticide-treated bed nets (ITNs), distributed before the kiremt rains; indoor residual spraying (IRS) of houses with insecticide; rapid diagnostic testing and treatment with artemisinin-based combination therapy by health extension workers.","code":"3.a","open":true},{"front":"Name two UK strategies against coronary heart disease.","back":"The ban on smoking in enclosed public places (Scotland 2006, England 2007); the soft drinks industry levy from 2018; NHS Health Checks, offered to adults aged 40–74, with statins and rapid angioplasty for those at highest risk.","code":"3.b","open":true},{"front":"Which NGO ran Ebola treatment centres in Sierra Leone, 2014–16?","back":"Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF), including at Kailahun.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"When was H1N1 identified, and when did the WHO declare a pandemic?","back":"Identified April 2009, with cases traced back to March 2009 in Mexico; WHO declared a pandemic on 11 June 2009, when 74 countries had reported cases.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Name one funding programme that mitigates HIV/AIDS globally.","back":"PEPFAR, the US President's Emergency Plan for AIDS Relief, funding antiretroviral treatment since 2003; the Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria, set up in 2002; Unitaid, which since 2006 has funded cheaper HIV medicines using a levy on airline tickets.","code":"4.b","open":true},{"front":"What two alkaloids does the rosy periwinkle produce, and what are they used for?","back":"Vincristine and vinblastine, used in chemotherapy for childhood leukaemia and Hodgkin's lymphoma.","code":"5.a"},{"front":"How many Guinea worm cases were there in 1986 vs the early 2020s?","back":"Approximately 3.5 million in 1986, fewer than 15 by the early 2020s — no vaccine or drug was ever developed.","code":"5.b"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (12-mark question)","items":["Question (12 marks): 'Evaluate the effectiveness of top-down and bottom-up strategies in responding to disease outbreaks and disease risk, from local to global scale, and consider how far this mirrors the top-down/bottom-up divide in how the world manages other shared risks, such as water security or food security.' Top-down strategies bring scale and technical capacity no local actor can match: GlaxoSmithKline's RTS,S/Mosquirix vaccine and its tiered pricing for the least developed countries show how a pharmaceutical transnational can fund research and manufacture at a scale beyond any NGO, while the WHO's coordinating role lets national governments share surveillance data rather than duplicate effort. These strategies build the tools a response depends on, but can be slow to reach the local level and rely on national health systems to deliver them. Local delivery then splits into two different models: Médecins Sans Frontières, an international NGO, delivered isolation wards, safe burial teams and health-worker training directly at village and district level during the 2014–16 Sierra Leone Ebola outbreak, but its funding and expertise came from outside the community, so this is international support delivered at local scale rather than a bottom-up strategy in the specification's sense. The Guinea worm campaign's village volunteers, by contrast, are genuinely bottom-up and community-led, coordinated nationally by the Carter Center, and cut reported cases from approximately 3.5 million in 1986 to fewer than 15 by the early 2020s without any vaccine ever being developed — clear evidence that top-down funding and bottom-up delivery are usually needed together rather than as alternatives. This top-down/bottom-up tension is not unique to disease: the same structure governs how the world manages other shared risks studied elsewhere in the specification, such as international treaties and agencies alongside community-level adaptation in water security and food security, showing disease response is one case of a wider geographical pattern in managing shared risk across local, national and global scales. On balance, neither approach is consistently more effective alone: Guinea worm is the clearest evidence that success depends on how well top-down coordination and bottom-up delivery are integrated, while MSF shows that international, locally-delivered support is a valuable model in its own right, so effectiveness should be judged by how well all these models are combined, at whatever scale the risk is managed, rather than by which type of strategy is used."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (12-mark question)","items":["Question (12 marks): 'Evaluate the effectiveness of top-down and bottom-up strategies in responding to disease outbreaks and disease risk, from local to global scale, and consider how far this mirrors the top-down/bottom-up divide in how the world manages other shared risks, such as water security or food security.' There are top-down strategies and bottom-up strategies for dealing with disease. Top-down ones are done by big organisations like GSK or the WHO, who make vaccines and coordinate countries. Bottom-up ones are done by local people, like the volunteers in the Guinea worm campaign who helped stop the disease spreading in their own villages. MSF also helps at a local level by treating patients directly, although MSF is really an international charity rather than a local group. Both types of strategy have helped reduce disease, but bottom-up ones can reach people that top-down ones can't. Other risks like water security and food security are probably managed in a similar way, with big organisations and local people both involved. Overall both top-down and bottom-up strategies are effective in different ways, but the essay does not clearly judge which one matters more."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — Haiti's 2010 earthquake and cholera (idea 1.c)","items":["Earthquake: magnitude 7.0, struck near Port-au-Prince on 12 January 2010.","Approximately 1.5 million people were displaced into overcrowded camps with poor sanitation.","Cholera was confirmed in Haiti in October 2010, its first recorded outbreak in over a century.","Evidence points to the bacterium being introduced via a UN peacekeeping base near the Artibonite River.","Suspected cases fell from approximately 340,000 in 2011 to approximately 22,000–28,000 in 2014.","The trend then reversed: about 36,000 suspected cases in 2015 and just over 41,000 in 2016.","The last confirmed case was recorded on 4 February 2019.","Haiti was declared cholera-free in February 2022, after three years with no confirmed case — but cholera resurged in October 2022.","Approximately 10,000 deaths are attributed to the epidemic overall.","Response combined national action (chlorination, oral rehydration centres) with international support (UNICEF, PAHO, an oral cholera vaccination campaign from 2012)."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — China's air pollution and cancer (idea 2.a)","items":["China's rapid industrialisation has relied heavily on coal-fired power generation and a fast-growing vehicle fleet.","Cities including Beijing and Linfen have recorded among the world's highest urban PM2.5 concentrations.","Public health research links long-term air pollution exposure to rising lung and bladder cancer incidence in affected cities.","China launched the Action Plan on Air Pollution Prevention and Control in 2013, its main national policy response.","The plan closed or relocated coal-fired power stations near major cities and tightened vehicle emissions standards.","China's global-scale response includes commitments made under the Paris Agreement and continued investment in renewable energy capacity."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — malaria in Ethiopia (idea 3.a)","items":["Malaria risk in Ethiopia concentrates below approximately 2,000m altitude.","The kiremt rainy season, roughly June to September, drives the main annual peak in cases.","The Anopheles mosquito is the vector; it breeds in the standing water the kiremt rains create.","Direct strategies include insecticide-treated bed nets (ITNs), indoor residual spraying (IRS) and artemisinin-based combination therapies (ACTs).","The US President's Malaria Initiative (PMI) is a major international agency supporting Ethiopia's response.","Impacts include lost agricultural labour during the peak farming season and pressure on rural health budgets.","Human factors raising risk include poor-quality housing, low rural bed-net coverage and seasonal movement of workers into lowland areas."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — coronary heart disease in the UK (idea 3.b)","items":["Coronary heart disease (CHD) is a leading cause of death in the UK, a noncommunicable disease of affluence.","Causes include diets high in saturated fat and salt, smoking, physical inactivity and an ageing population.","CHD prevalence is consistently higher in more deprived areas, including parts of Scotland and northern England.","NHS Health Checks screen adults aged 40–74 for CHD risk factors such as high cholesterol and blood pressure.","The UK-wide ban on smoking in enclosed public places was completed in 2007: Scotland led with a ban from March 2006, and England completed UK-wide coverage with its own ban from July 2007.","The Soft Drinks Industry Levy ('sugar tax') was introduced in 2018 to reduce a dietary risk factor.","Statins are prescribed free of charge on the NHS to at-risk groups to lower cholesterol."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — MSF and the 2014–16 Ebola outbreak in Sierra Leone (idea 4.a)","items":["The West African Ebola outbreak (2014–16) was the largest in history, concentrated in Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone.","Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF) ran Ebola treatment centres, including at Kailahun in Sierra Leone.","MSF's centres provided isolation wards, safe burial teams and training for local health workers.","Sierra Leone had only a small number of doctors per 100,000 people before the outbreak, limiting the formal health system's own capacity.","The World Health Organization declared the outbreak a Public Health Emergency of International Concern in August 2014.","The outbreak is estimated to have caused approximately 11,000 deaths across the three worst-affected countries.","An Ebola vaccine (rVSV-ZEBOV) was trialled in Guinea during the outbreak and later licensed."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — the rosy periwinkle, Madagascar (idea 5.a)","items":["The rosy periwinkle (Catharanthus roseus) is native to Madagascar's dry deciduous forests.","It produces the alkaloids vincristine and vinblastine.","Pharmaceutical development of these compounds (from the 1950s, notably by Eli Lilly) raised childhood leukaemia survival from around 10% to approximately 90%.","The compounds are also used to treat Hodgkin's lymphoma.","Madagascar has lost the great majority of its original forest cover, threatening the wild plant population.","Habitat loss erodes the species' genetic diversity, narrowing future opportunities for medicinal discovery.","Madagascar has captured little of the commercial value generated internationally by the plant, a widely cited example of biopiracy."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Case-study fact file — GlaxoSmithKline, a pharmaceutical transnational (idea 5.b)","items":["GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) is headquartered in the UK and operates manufacturing and research sites across several countries.","GSK developed the RTS,S/Mosquirix malaria vaccine, recommended by the WHO for wide use from 2021 — the first WHO-recommended malaria vaccine.","GSK holds patents protecting its breakthrough drugs and vaccines.","GSK has pledged to cap prices in the least developed countries at a fraction of prices charged in wealthier markets.","GSK has committed to reinvesting a share of profits earned in least developed countries into local healthcare infrastructure.","GSK's global supply flows distribute vaccines and medicines manufactured in one country to patients in many others."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — \"The greatest obstacle to disease eradication is not biological, but political and economic.\" To what extent do you agree? (33 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'eradication' means reducing a disease to zero cases worldwide; the claim to test is that political and economic obstacles (not the biology of the disease itself) are the main barrier — you must judge how far this is true, not just describe obstacles.","Introduction: define eradication and the political/economic vs biological distinction; state the line of argument — that biology sets the possibility but political and economic factors usually decide the outcome.","Paragraph 1 (evidence the claim is true): Guinea worm eradication shows a disease reduced from approximately 3.5 million cases (1986) to fewer than 15 (early 2020s) without any vaccine or drug — success came from political/economic coordination (Carter Center funding, national ministries) and bottom-up community education, not a biological breakthrough.","Paragraph 2 (further political/economic evidence): GlaxoSmithKline's RTS,S vaccine exists as a biological solution to malaria, but its reach is shaped by pricing and patent decisions — a political-economic choice about access, not a biological limit.","Paragraph 3 (counter-argument: biology still matters): some diseases resist eradication for biological reasons regardless of funding — drug resistance in malaria parasites, and zoonotic reservoirs (e.g. rabies, bird flu) that let a disease persist in animal hosts even if human cases are controlled.","Paragraph 4 (weighing the two sides): compare a case where political/economic factors were the deciding barrier (Guinea worm, once funded and coordinated, succeeded) against a case where biology still limits full eradication (a zoonotic reservoir) even with full funding.","Synoptic link: connect the political/economic obstacle to a physical or human process studied elsewhere in the specification — e.g. climate change (Earth's Life Support Systems) expanding vector ranges regardless of funding, or global migration (Global Migration, Global systems Option B) moving disease risk across borders faster than political and economic systems can respond — to show the obstacle operates across scales, not only within a single disease campaign.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: for each obstacle, ask 'would removing political/economic constraints alone be enough to eradicate this disease?' — if yes, that supports the claim; if no, that qualifies it.","Conclusion: state a qualified judgement — political and economic factors are usually the dominant obstacle where a biological solution or method already exists (as Guinea worm shows), but for diseases with an animal reservoir or evolving drug resistance, biological factors remain a genuine limit even under ideal funding, so the claim is largely but not entirely true."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.942Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every fault the first verdict raised is genuinely fixed - the Haiti series is now correctly dated and shows the 2015-16 reversal rather than a smooth decline, the 2019 last case and 2022 elimination-then-resurgence are right, the H1N1 April/March/11 June dates are consistent, tariffs are now 3/6/12/33 throughout and match lesson.why, every stem uses a board command word, the model paragraphs state their tariff so Level 4 reads truthfully, and the 12-mark hints no longer name the case study; the fact files are one per required case study (Haiti cholera, China air pollution and cancer, malaria in Ethiopia, UK CHD, MSF and Ebola, rosy periwinkle, GSK), all ten key ideas get a faithful section closing on what a top-level answer does, and both data items re-solve correctly - (9,300 / 620,000) x 100,000 = 1,500 per 100,000, and (41,000 - 340,000) / 340,000 x 100 = -88%. Correct the two non-OCR topic names in the 12-mark model answers and this is ready to hand out."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:08:13.953Z"}},"3.3":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"3.3","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Geographical debates (Component 03) asks you to bring oceans into synoptic essays worth up to 33 marks, connecting ocean processes, resources, pollution, climate change and geopolitics to the rest of the specification. Examiners reward named, up-to-date case studies used as evidence rather than described in isolation, and a weighed judgement in the conclusion rather than a list of points. This room builds the physical foundations (relief, circulation, ecosystems) and the human debates (resources, governance, pollution, climate change, geopolitics) you need to argue, with the case studies OCR names.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"The shape of the ocean floor","t":"Oceans cover most of the Earth and their basins have a relief as varied as the continents: the continental shelf is the shallow, gently sloping edge of a landmass, dropping away at the continental slope into the deep abyssal plain, the flat sediment-covered ocean floor. Ocean ridges and rifts mark constructive plate boundaries where new crust forms and pulls apart, while ocean trenches mark destructive boundaries where crust is subducted; guyots are flat-topped seamounts, once volcanic islands eroded by waves then submerged as the crust cooled and subsided. A top-level answer names these features accurately and uses them as the physical stage on which resource, pollution and climate debates in this topic play out."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Why the water itself varies","t":"Salinity and temperature vary horizontally, with warmer, often more saline water near the equator and colder water at high latitudes, and vertically, with a thermocline separating warm surface water from cold, dense deep water. Warm surface currents, such as the Gulf Stream, flow polewards from low latitudes; cold currents, such as the Peru (Humboldt) Current, flow towards the equator. The North Atlantic shows both: the Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift carry warm surface water north-east, while cooling and sinking near Greenland and Labrador drives cold, dense North Atlantic Deep Water south as part of the thermohaline 'conveyor belt'. A top-level answer uses this circulation to explain heat transfer, not just to describe currents on a map."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Light, temperature, nutrients and life","t":"Light, temperature and nutrient supply fall with depth and vary with latitude, so productivity concentrates where light and nutrients coincide, such as continental shelves and zones of upwelling. Inter-tidal ecosystems have short, simple food chains built on algae and plankton, exposed to fluctuating temperature and light twice daily, but support high biodiversity of adapted species; deep-water ecosystems beyond the photic zone rely on detritus falling from above (the 'marine snow') or, near hydrothermal vents, on chemosynthesis, giving longer, more complex food webs but lower overall biodiversity in most locations. A top-level answer compares the two systems directly against named controls (light, temperature, nutrients) rather than describing each in turn."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Managing a renewable biological resource: krill","t":"Antarctic krill are a small crustacean at the base of the Southern Ocean food web, harvested for fishmeal, aquaculture feed and omega-3 oil, and managed by CCAMLR (the Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources), which sets a precautionary catch limit for its main fishing area. Fishing nations such as Norway, China, South Korea and Chile value krill economically; conservation NGOs and scientists emphasise krill's role as food for whales, seals and penguins, so management must set thresholds below which the population's resilience to recover is put at risk. A top-level answer uses this contest of values to explain why the trigger-level catch limit is set well below the surveyed biomass, rather than simply describing the fishery."},{"code":"2.b","h":"Contested energy and mineral resources","t":"Oil and gas are non-renewable ocean energy resources extracted from continental shelves and deeper water, generating high economic value but carrying spill and decommissioning risks; wave and tidal energy are renewable flow resources that exploit predictable ocean movement without depleting a stock, but are constrained to specific coastlines and remain a small share of global energy. Sea-bed minerals, including ferrous nodules and non-ferrous polymetallic sulphides, are a growing non-renewable resource, targeted increasingly in international and Arctic waters as land-based reserves are depleted. A top-level answer sets these resource types against each other — renewable versus non-renewable, and the environmental and economic trade-offs of extracting each — rather than treating them as a list."},{"code":"2.c","h":"Governing a global commons","t":"Oceans beyond national control are a 'global commons': a resource no single state owns, which risks the 'tragedy of the commons' if individual users overexploit it because the cost of depletion is shared while the benefit of extraction is private. UNCLOS (the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, 1982) manages this by defining zones of decreasing state control moving seawards — territorial waters (12 nautical miles), the contiguous zone (24 nautical miles), the exclusive economic zone or EEZ (200 nautical miles, where the state has resource rights but not full sovereignty) — beyond which lie the high seas, alongside tools such as marine protected areas. A top-level answer explains how these zones and frameworks are an attempt to solve the tragedy of the commons, and evaluates how far they succeed."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Where ocean pollution comes from","t":"Ocean pollutants come from diffuse and point sources: the combustion of fossil fuels raises atmospheric and dissolved carbon dioxide, driving acidification, while domestic and industrial sources add plastics, heavy metals and, in some cases, nuclear waste directly or via rivers and the atmosphere. These pollutants differ in how they behave — some, like plastics, persist and disperse via currents, while others, like heavy metals, bioaccumulate up food chains — so their management requires different responses. A top-level answer distinguishes pollutant sources and pathways precisely rather than treating 'ocean pollution' as one undifferentiated problem."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Oil spills as a case of threat and response","t":"Offshore oil production and the tankers that transport it create a risk of catastrophic spills, illustrated by the Deepwater Horizon blowout in the Gulf of Mexico on 20 April 2010, which released oil from BP's Macondo well for 87 days. Impacts fall on the physical environment and marine ecosystems (coating marshes, killing wildlife, contaminating the water column) and on human activities such as fishing (closures of fishing grounds) and tourism (beach closures and lost income); management combined capping the well, chemical dispersants, controlled burning, mechanical skimming and, afterwards, compensation and fines running into tens of billions of dollars. A top-level answer uses the named spill's chronology and impacts as evidence, not as a story told for its own sake."},{"code":"3.c","h":"How currents spread plastic pollution","t":"Ocean surface currents converge in the centre of each subtropical gyre, so buoyant pollution such as plastic entering the ocean from rivers and coastlines anywhere around the gyre's edge is carried inward and accumulates, rather than dispersing evenly across the ocean. The Great Pacific Garbage Patch, in the North Pacific Subtropical Gyre between Hawaii and California, is the best-known example: its causes are this convergent circulation acting on decades of land-based and fishing-gear plastic waste, and its impacts include ingestion and entanglement across marine species, from plankton-sized microplastics to seabirds and turtles. A top-level answer explains the causal chain from current circulation to accumulation, and links this back to the currents taught in 1.b, rather than describing the patch in isolation."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Warmer, more acidic oceans","t":"Oceans absorb a large share of anthropogenic carbon dioxide, which reacts with seawater to lower its pH (ocean acidification); this reduces the availability of carbonate ions that shellfish, corals and some plankton need to build shells and skeletons, threatening the base of marine food chains and, through them, fish stocks and the people who depend on them. Rising ocean temperature separately stresses coral, which expels the symbiotic algae (zooxanthellae) that give it colour and much of its energy when water is too warm for too long — coral bleaching — reducing reef biodiversity and threatening the fishing, tourism and coastal protection that local communities rely on. A top-level answer keeps acidification (a chemical threat to shell- and skeleton-builders) distinct from warming-driven bleaching (a thermal stress response), even though both stem from rising carbon dioxide."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Rising seas and island communities","t":"Sea level is rising due to the thermal expansion of warming ocean water and the transfer of water from land ice (glaciers and ice sheets) into the ocean, and both processes are accelerating as global temperatures rise. Low-lying island communities, such as Tuvalu in the Pacific, face direct threats — saltwater intrusion into freshwater lenses and food-growing land, more damaging storm surges, and eventual submergence of low atolls — with impacts on freshwater supply, agriculture and habitable land; governments and communities adapt in the short term through sea walls and rainwater harvesting and, in the longer term, through land reclamation, migration agreements with other states, and planning for the possibility of losing territory. A top-level answer uses the named island case study to show both the physical cause and the human adaptation response, at more than one timescale."},{"code":"4.c","h":"A warming Arctic and its geopolitics","t":"Global warming is shrinking the extent of Arctic sea ice, and because ice reflects sunlight while open ocean absorbs it, less ice cover causes more warming, which melts more ice — a positive feedback that risks pushing the system past a threshold beyond which the change is difficult to reverse. In the Arctic case study, shrinking ice has geopolitical implications, opening shipping routes such as the Northwest Passage and increasing access to sea-bed oil, gas and mineral reserves, which has sharpened territorial claims among Arctic states; it also threatens indigenous peoples whose hunting and travel depend on stable ice, while the Arctic Council brings the eight Arctic states and indigenous permanent participants together to manage these changes. A top-level answer links the physical feedback to the specific opportunities, threats and governance response, not just to 'melting ice'."},{"code":"5.a","h":"Oceans and globalisation","t":"Oceans carry the great majority of world trade by volume, along principal shipping routes shaped by the location of major producing and consuming regions, chokepoints such as canals and straits, and the physical geography of currents and prevailing winds. The scale of ocean shipping has grown as ships have grown larger (containerisation and larger bulk and tanker vessels lowering the cost per unit shipped), deepening globalisation by making long-distance trade in bulky, low-value goods economically viable, while the direction and type of trade reflects the changing pattern of global production, from raw materials and manufactured goods to energy exports. A top-level answer connects the physical route (why ships go where they do) to the economic driver (what is being traded and why) rather than describing shipping lanes alone."},{"code":"5.b","h":"Oceans as spaces of rivalry","t":"States use oceans to project power as well as to trade, maintaining naval strongholds — home ports and overseas bases — to secure trade routes, deter rivals and project influence; the USA, for example, bases its Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor and its largest naval base at Norfolk, Virginia, while maintaining overseas bases such as Yokosuka in Japan and Bahrain in the Persian Gulf. Where resource-rich or strategically important waters are claimed by more than one state, marine conflict zones emerge, such as the South China Sea, where China's expansive 'nine-dash line' claim and built-up artificial islands are disputed by Vietnam, the Philippines and other neighbours, and were rejected as having no legal basis under UNCLOS by a 2016 international arbitration ruling. A top-level answer uses named strongholds and a named conflict zone as evidence of how oceans are spaces of geopolitical competition, not just economic exchange."},{"code":"5.c","h":"Oceans as hazardous obstacles","t":"Oceans can obstruct as well as connect: 21st-century piracy concentrates in specific chokepoints and weak-governance coastal waters, such as off Somalia and in the Gulf of Guinea, where it threatens shipping and is managed through naval patrols, armed guards and improved onshore governance. Oceans are also crossed as escape routes by migrants seeking safety or economic opportunity, such as journeys from South East Asia towards Australia or from North Africa across the Mediterranean towards Europe, often in overcrowded and unsafe vessels, raising humanitarian, legal and search-and-rescue challenges for the states involved. A top-level answer treats piracy and migration as two distinct hazards with different causes and different management responses, using named regions as evidence for each."}],"examples":[{"title":"Calculating the extent of a state's exclusive economic zone","setup":"Under UNCLOS a coastal state's territorial sea extends 12 nautical miles from its baseline, its contiguous zone runs a further 12 nautical miles beyond that, and its exclusive economic zone (EEZ) reaches 200 nautical miles from the same baseline. How far from the baseline does the contiguous zone reach, how many nautical miles does the EEZ carry on beyond that edge, and what is the status of the water further out still?","steps":["Measured from the baseline, the contiguous zone begins where the territorial sea stops and runs a further 12, giving 12 + 12 = 24 nautical miles.","Both limits are measured from the baseline, not from each other, so the EEZ carries on 200 − 24 = 176 nautical miles past the edge of the contiguous zone.","Past 200 nautical miles no coastal state holds resource rights: that water is high seas, part of the 'global commons' open to all, which is where the tragedy-of-the-commons problem bites hardest.","So the contiguous zone reaches 24 nautical miles from the baseline, the EEZ continues for another 176 nautical miles beyond it, and everything past 200 nautical miles is high seas."],"cues":["Which two 12-mile belts stack up here?","What do you subtract from 200 nautical miles, and why?","Who holds resource rights past the EEZ?","Give the two distances and name the zone beyond them."]},{"title":"Explaining Antarctic krill fishery management as a response to the tragedy of the commons","setup":"In CCAMLR's Southern Ocean Area 48, krill may be fished by the vessels of any CCAMLR member. CCAMLR has set an overall precautionary catch limit for the area, calculated well below the surveyed krill biomass, but has not yet shared that limit out between sub-areas, so it also applies an interim 'trigger level' of approximately 620,000 tonnes a year. Krill are the key food of whales, seals and penguins there. Explain why the trigger level is a response to the tragedy of the commons, and say what it shows about the resource's resilience.","steps":["No member owns the stock, so a fleet that leaves krill in the water simply leaves it for another member's vessels: each fleet gains the whole value of its own catch while the cost of depletion is shared by all — the tragedy-of-the-commons pressure.","An area-wide precautionary limit alone would not hold that pressure back while it is unallocated, because fleets could race for it and take it all from the same few feeding grounds, so CCAMLR caps the area at the trigger level of approximately 620,000 tonnes a year instead.","Members whose interest is conservation rather than catch pressed for a ceiling this far below the precautionary limit, because a local crash would also starve the whales, seals and penguins that feed on the krill, not merely cut the fishery's yield.","So the trigger level answers the commons problem by replacing open access with one agreed, enforceable ceiling, and holding the catch this far below the surveyed biomass shows management pitched deliberately inside the stock's resilience — a threshold that prompts action long before recruitment is threatened."],"cues":["Why does a stock no one owns invite overfishing?","Why cap the whole area rather than wait for sub-area shares?","Which members would want a ceiling lower still, and why?","What two things does the trigger level therefore demonstrate?"]},{"title":"Explaining the causes of the Great Pacific Garbage Patch","setup":"Plastic waste enters the North Pacific Ocean from rivers, fishing fleets and coastlines all around its rim, and once afloat it degrades only very slowly; the accumulation zone it has formed was mapped by The Ocean Cleanup in 2018 at roughly 1.6 million km². Explain how ocean circulation turns that scattered input into the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.","steps":["The Kuroshio, North Pacific, California and North Equatorial currents link up as the North Pacific Subtropical Gyre, turning clockwise around the basin and carrying rim-sourced plastic away from the coasts into open water.","Towards the calm centre of the gyre those currents converge; the water there sinks, but buoyant plastic cannot follow it down, so the debris is left behind and concentrated instead of dispersed.","Because the plastic breaks down so slowly, each year's input is added to what earlier decades left in the same convergence rather than being flushed out or decomposed.","So circulation, convergence and persistence together have built the Great Pacific Garbage Patch: the gyre gathers rim-sourced plastic from the whole basin, the convergence at its calm centre holds it there, and slow degradation lets decade upon decade of input pile up in the same place — much of it microplastic suspended near the surface rather than a solid raft."],"cues":["Which four currents make this gyre, and which way does it turn?","What happens to floating debris where currents converge?","Why does last decade's plastic still count?","How do the three processes combine to build the patch?"]},{"title":"Calculating the percentage change in Antarctic krill catch, 2010–2020","setup":"CCAMLR's reported krill catch in Area 48 was approximately 210 thousand tonnes in 2010 and approximately 450 thousand tonnes in 2020, against an interim trigger level for the area of approximately 620,000 tonnes a year. Calculate the percentage change in catch over the decade and comment on what it shows about the fishery's sustainability.","steps":["Change in catch = 450 − 210 = 240 thousand tonnes more krill landed at the end of the decade than at the start.","Percentage change = (240 ÷ 210) × 100 = 114.3%, taking 2010 as the base year, so the catch rose by approximately 114%.","The 2020 catch of 450 thousand tonnes still sits below the area's trigger level of approximately 620,000 tonnes, so the fishery has grown fast but has not yet reached the ceiling CCAMLR set for it.","So catch rose by approximately 114% between 2010 and 2020; that shows a rapidly expanding fishery rather than a demonstrably unsustainable one, since judging sustainability needs krill biomass and predator-breeding data as well as the landings figure."],"cues":["What do you subtract to find the change, in thousand tonnes?","Which year's catch is the base you divide by?","How does the 2020 catch compare with the trigger level?","Does a rise this large by itself prove overfishing?"]},{"title":"Explaining the ice–albedo feedback in the Arctic","setup":"Arctic sea ice is pale and has a high albedo, reflecting most incoming solar radiation straight back to space, while the open ocean beneath it is dark and has a low albedo, absorbing most of that radiation instead. Explain how global warming turns this contrast into a self-reinforcing feedback loop.","steps":["Warming melts ice at the edges of the summer pack, so each square metre lost swaps a reflective white surface for dark water that takes in the radiation the ice used to send back.","The energy absorbed by that newly exposed water heats the surface ocean, which then melts the neighbouring floes from beneath and from the sides as well as from above.","Heat stored in the water over summer delays the autumn freeze-up, so the ice that does form is thin first-year ice, which melts out more easily the next summer and exposes more dark water again.","Each turn of the loop therefore enlarges the cause that drove it: this is a positive feedback, and with enough thick multi-year ice gone it can carry the Arctic past a threshold beyond which the loss of summer ice is effectively irreversible."],"cues":["What surface replaces the ice, and how does its albedo differ?","Where does the energy that is no longer reflected go?","How does a warmer ocean change next winter's ice?","What kind of feedback is this, and what can it cross?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between a continental shelf and an abyssal plain?","a":"The continental shelf is the shallow, gently sloping edge of a landmass; the abyssal plain is the flat, deep, sediment-covered ocean floor beyond it."},{"q":"Why does CCAMLR set a krill catch limit below the surveyed biomass?","a":"To leave a margin so the krill population stays resilient and can keep supporting whales, seals and penguins, not just the fishery."},{"q":"What causes coral bleaching?","a":"Sustained high water temperature causes coral to expel its symbiotic algae, removing its colour and much of its energy supply."},{"q":"Why does floating plastic concentrate at the centre of an ocean gyre rather than spreading out evenly?","a":"Surface currents around the rim of the gyre converge towards its centre, carrying buoyant plastic inward faster than it disperses."}]},"room":{"facts":["Oceans cover approximately 71% of the Earth's surface and hold approximately 96.5% of all Earth's water.","UNCLOS zones extend from the baseline: territorial waters to 12 nautical miles, the contiguous zone to 24 nautical miles, and the exclusive economic zone (EEZ) to 200 nautical miles; beyond that lie the high seas.","Average ocean salinity is approximately 35 parts per thousand, varying with evaporation, precipitation, river input and sea-ice formation.","The 'tragedy of the commons' describes a shared resource being overexploited because the cost of depletion is shared while the benefit of use is private (Garrett Hardin, 1968).","Global mean sea level is rising at approximately 3.3 mm per year, driven by the thermal expansion of ocean water and the transfer of water from land ice into the ocean.","Arctic September minimum sea ice extent has declined by approximately 13% per decade in recent decades (NSIDC data), driven by the ice–albedo feedback; the annual mean decline is roughly a third of that rate."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify three features of ocean relief, other than the continental shelf, found across an ocean basin.","a":"Any three of: continental slope, abyssal plain, ocean ridge/rift, ocean trench, guyot.","sol":"Three creditable features: the continental slope (the steep drop from the continental shelf to the deep ocean floor), the abyssal plain (the flat, sediment-covered floor of the deep ocean), and an ocean trench (a deep, narrow depression formed at a destructive plate boundary where oceanic crust is subducted).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This is about the shape of the ocean floor away from the coast, not about the water itself.","Think about what a cross-section from the coast to the middle of an ocean would pass through, moving from shallow to deep.","Name three separate relief features, using the specification's own terms for each."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Define the term 'exclusive economic zone (EEZ)' and state its maximum extent from a state's baseline.","a":"A zone where a coastal state has rights over resources (but not full sovereignty), extending up to 200 nautical miles from its baseline.","sol":"An exclusive economic zone is a maritime zone, defined under UNCLOS, within which a coastal state has sovereign rights to explore, exploit, manage and conserve natural resources (living and non-living) in the water column and on/under the sea bed, without holding full territorial sovereignty over it. It extends to a maximum of 200 nautical miles from the state's baseline.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This zone sits further out than territorial waters and the contiguous zone.","Check whether the state's control in that zone is the same as on its own territory, or something narrower.","Give the definition first, then the distance, in that order."],"codes":["2.c"]},{"q":"Identify one warm ocean surface current and one cold ocean surface current, naming an ocean each flows through.","a":"Warm: the Gulf Stream/North Atlantic Drift (Atlantic Ocean). Cold: the Peru/Humboldt Current (Pacific Ocean).","sol":"A warm current: the Gulf Stream (continuing as the North Atlantic Drift), which flows north-eastwards across the North Atlantic Ocean from the Gulf of Mexico towards north-west Europe. A cold current: the Peru (Humboldt) Current, which flows north-westwards along the Pacific coast of South America towards the equator.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["One current should be flowing broadly away from the equator, the other broadly towards it.","Recall one named current for each direction of travel, and check you can place each on a real ocean rather than only describe its temperature.","Name each current and state which ocean it is found in."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Identify two impacts of the Deepwater Horizon oil spill (2010) on human activities in the Gulf of Mexico.","a":"Any two of: fishing grounds closed / fishing industry losses; beach and tourism closures/losses; clean-up and compensation costs to BP.","sol":"Two creditable impacts: commercial and recreational fishing grounds across parts of the Gulf of Mexico were closed for extended periods, cutting fishers' incomes; and coastal tourism suffered as oiled beaches and negative publicity reduced visitor numbers along the Gulf coast during and after the spill.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Focus on people and their livelihoods, not on wildlife or the water itself.","Think about which human activities in the Gulf of Mexico depend most directly on clean water and an undamaged coastline.","Name two distinct human activities and state how each was affected."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain how differences in light and nutrient supply create contrasts in biodiversity between inter-tidal and deep-water ecosystems.","a":"Inter-tidal zones have abundant light and nutrients so support short, productive food chains with many adapted species; deep water has little or no light and relies on falling detritus, supporting fewer, more specialised species.","sol":"Inter-tidal ecosystems receive full sunlight and are close to nutrient-rich coastal water, so photosynthesis by algae supports a productive base of the food chain; but organisms must also tolerate twice-daily exposure and submersion, so biodiversity reflects species adapted to that stress as well as to nutrient supply. Deep-water ecosystems lie below the photic zone, so most food arrives as detritus ('marine snow') sinking from above, or, near vents, via chemosynthesis; the resulting food chains are longer and productivity lower, so — away from hotspots like vents — fewer species are supported than in productive shallow water, giving lower overall biodiversity despite the stable physical conditions.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Consider how far light and nutrients change between the shore and the deep ocean floor, and what depends on each.","Set out how each ecosystem gets its energy — from the sun directly, or from material sinking from above — before comparing biodiversity.","Structure your answer as a direct comparison of the two ecosystems against the same controls, not two separate descriptions."],"codes":["1.c"]},{"q":"Explain how the values and socio-economic status of stakeholders have shaped the management of the Antarctic krill fishery.","a":"Fishing nations value krill economically (fishmeal, aquaculture feed, omega-3), pushing for higher catches; conservation-minded scientists and NGOs value krill's food-web role, pushing CCAMLR toward a precautionary catch limit well below surveyed biomass.","sol":"Fishing nations with the capital to operate distant-water fleets, such as Norway, China, South Korea and Chile, have an economic interest in maximising krill catch for fishmeal, aquaculture feed and omega-3 products, and their socio-economic weight gives them influence within CCAMLR's decision-making. Conservation scientists and NGOs place greater value on krill's role as food for whales, seals and penguins, and have pushed for management that protects the wider food web rather than just fishery yield. The resulting political compromise is a precautionary 'trigger level' catch limit for the main fishing area, set well below the surveyed biomass, which reflects both interests being represented rather than either dominating outright.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about who benefits economically from krill and who values it for a different reason.","Connect each stakeholder group's values to the position they are likely to argue for within CCAMLR.","Explain how the eventual catch limit reflects a balance between those competing positions, not just one side winning."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain the causes of the accumulation of plastic in the North Pacific gyre.","a":"Plastic enters from rivers/coastlines around the Pacific rim, and the gyre's converging currents (Kuroshio, North Pacific, California, North Equatorial) carry buoyant plastic inward, concentrating it rather than dispersing it.","sol":"Land-based and fishing-related plastic waste enters the ocean from coastlines and river mouths around the rim of the North Pacific. The North Pacific Subtropical Gyre — formed by the Kuroshio, North Pacific, California and North Equatorial currents circulating clockwise — carries floating debris inward towards its calm, converging centre rather than dispersing it outward. Because plastic degrades very slowly, this inward transport allows debris entering over many decades to accumulate in one region, forming a persistent patch rather than a temporary one.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think first about where the plastic enters the ocean, then about what the currents around that ocean do to it.","Name the currents that make up this gyre and describe the direction they move surface water.","Build your answer as a chain: source of plastic, then current pattern, then why it concentrates rather than spreads."],"codes":["3.c","1.b"]},{"q":"Explain the feedback between the extent of Arctic sea ice cover and the degree of global warming.","a":"Ice reflects solar radiation (high albedo); open water absorbs it (low albedo). Warming melts ice, exposing more dark water, which absorbs more heat and causes further warming and further melting — a positive feedback.","sol":"Sea ice has a high albedo, reflecting most incoming solar radiation back to space, while open ocean water has a low albedo and absorbs most of the solar radiation reaching it. As rising global temperatures melt Arctic sea ice, more dark ocean surface is exposed, so more solar energy is absorbed by the ocean rather than reflected; this extra absorbed heat warms the water, which melts more ice, exposing yet more open water the following season. This is a positive feedback loop, and because each cycle reinforces the last, it risks pushing the Arctic past a threshold beyond which further ice loss becomes difficult or impossible to reverse.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Start by thinking about how ice and open water differ in what they do to sunlight.","Trace what happens once some ice has already melted and more dark water is exposed.","Present your answer as a repeating cycle, and name the type of feedback it represents."],"codes":["4.c"]},{"q":"Table 1 shows total reported Antarctic krill catch in CCAMLR Area 48. Identify the percentage change in catch between 2010 and 2020, showing your working.\nTable 1 — Reported krill catch, CCAMLR Area 48 (thousand tonnes)\n2010: approximately 210\n2020: approximately 450","a":"An increase of approximately 114%.","sol":"Change in catch = 450 − 210 = 240 thousand tonnes. Percentage change = (240 ÷ 210) × 100 = 114.3%, so catch rose by approximately 114% between 2010 and 2020.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is a percentage-change calculation, so you need the difference between the two years as a fraction of the earlier year.","Subtract the 2010 figure from the 2020 figure first, then divide that difference by the 2010 figure.","Give your answer as a percentage, to one decimal place or rounded, with the correct sign showing an increase."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how changes in the scale of ocean shipping have influenced the pattern of principal shipping routes.","a":"Larger container, bulk and tanker vessels lowered the cost per unit shipped, encouraging trade to concentrate on fewer, larger hub ports and routes able to handle bigger ships, reinforcing established chokepoints and long-haul routes.","sol":"As ships have grown larger — through containerisation and the development of larger bulk carriers and tankers — the cost of shipping each unit of cargo has fallen, making long-distance trade in bulky or low-value goods economically viable and deepening globalisation. Because only a limited number of ports have the depth and infrastructure to handle the largest vessels, trade has concentrated onto a smaller number of major hub ports and the routes connecting them, reinforcing the importance of existing chokepoints (such as straits and canals) on principal shipping routes rather than dispersing shipping more widely.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what has changed about the ships themselves, not just about what is being traded.","Connect the cost of shipping to the size of the vessel, and then to which ports can handle that vessel.","Explain the route pattern as a consequence of ship size and port capacity, not just as a fixed map of trade."],"codes":["5.a"]},{"q":"'Ocean territory disputes are best explained by resource geography rather than by global governance of sovereignty and territorial integrity.' Evaluate this statement, with reference to a named marine conflict zone.","a":"The South China Sea dispute has resource stakes at local (fishing grounds), national (seabed oil/gas, sovereignty) and global (shipping trade) scale, but the dispute's persistence after the 2016 ruling shows it is equally a case of weak global governance of sovereignty — so resource geography alone is not the fuller explanation.","sol":"Resource geography shapes the South China Sea dispute at more than one scale. Locally, Filipino and Vietnamese fishing communities have lost access to grounds inside their own claimed exclusive economic zones as Chinese vessels and coastguard patrols enforce the 'nine-dash line'. Nationally, China, Vietnam and the Philippines each have an interest in seabed oil and gas reserves and in projecting regional power from the reefs they occupy. Globally, an estimated third of world shipping trade passes through the sea, so control of the route carries global economic weight, connecting back to the pattern of ocean shipping and globalisation studied earlier in this topic. But resource geography alone does not explain why the dispute persists: it is equally a case of global governance failing to resolve contested sovereignty and territorial integrity, the same tension the specification examines in other governance debates. UNCLOS's 2016 arbitration ruling rejected China's nine-dash line claim as having no legal basis, showing governance can define rights and rule on them — but China continued building and militarising artificial islands regardless, showing that a legal ruling cannot compel a powerful state unwilling to be bound by it, a failure of governance at the global scale rather than a resource shortage. On balance, resource geography supplies the material stakes that make the zone worth disputing at the local and national scale, but it is the weakness of global governance in enforcing sovereignty rulings at the global scale that explains why the dispute has proved so intractable, so the statement understates how far governance, not resources alone, is the fuller explanation.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Ask who is affected at each scale, and whether the answer is the same at every scale.","Set the resource-geography evidence against evidence about sovereignty and the effectiveness of international governance, rather than treating the two as separate lists.","End with a judgement on which explanation carries more weight overall, drawing on evidence from more than one scale."],"codes":["5.b","2.c"]},{"q":"'The opening of Arctic ocean routeways creates opportunities that outweigh the threats it poses.' To what extent do you agree?","a":"Opportunities (shorter shipping routes, access to oil/gas/mineral reserves) are real but geographically narrow; the ice loss driving them is a local expression of the global carbon cycle and climate-change processes studied elsewhere in the specification, and the threats — to indigenous communities, to interstate governance, and to the climate system itself — operate at a larger scale, so opportunities do not outweigh threats.","sol":"Shrinking Arctic sea ice creates opportunities at the national and local scale: routeways such as the Northwest Passage could shorten shipping distances considerably compared with the Panama or Suez canals, and access improves to Arctic oil, gas and mineral reserves, which the USGS estimates may include a substantial share of the world's undiscovered oil and gas. But the ice loss enabling these opportunities is not a self-contained Arctic process: it is a regional expression of the global carbon cycle and the wider climate-change content studied elsewhere in the specification, since it is rising atmospheric carbon dioxide from fossil fuel combustion that drives the warming, and the ice-albedo feedback this triggers operates within the global climate system rather than in the Arctic alone. The threats operate across the same range of scales. Locally, indigenous peoples' hunting and travel depend on stable, predictable ice. Nationally, Arctic states dispute extended continental-shelf claims, a sovereignty tension that parallels the limits of global governance of sovereignty and territorial integrity seen in other ocean and territorial disputes. Globally, the underlying feedback risks pushing the climate system past a threshold beyond which further ice loss is difficult to reverse, a consequence far larger in scale than any shipping-route saving. On balance, the opportunities are real but limited to a narrow shipping season and a small number of Arctic states, while the threats connect to global-scale carbon-cycle processes and governance gaps that extend well beyond the Arctic itself, so the statement's claim that opportunities outweigh threats is not well supported.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what is gained locally and nationally from what is put at risk, and think about where the underlying cause of the ice loss actually comes from.","For the political and environmental threats, choose named evidence from the Arctic case study rather than describing threats in general terms, and consider how the ice loss connects to processes studied elsewhere in the specification.","Reach a clear judgement on which side is more significant overall, showing your reasoning works across more than one scale."],"codes":["4.c"]},{"q":"'Human activity is now the greatest threat to ocean ecosystems.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Human activity drives most major pressures (acidification, warming, sea-level rise, pollution) from the global carbon cycle down to local ecosystems and national island communities such as Tuvalu, connecting this debate to the carbon cycle, coastal landscapes and global migration content studied elsewhere in the specification; natural processes persist but are smaller-scale or entangled with human emissions, so on balance human activity dominates.","sol":"Human activity now drives most of the major pressures on ocean ecosystems examined in this topic, and does so across scales. At the global scale, the combustion of fossil fuels raises atmospheric carbon dioxide as part of the global carbon cycle studied in the physical systems content, which oceans absorb, lowering pH and reducing the carbonate available for shell- and skeleton-building organisms, and separately raises ocean temperature, triggering coral bleaching; the same carbon-driven warming causes the thermal expansion of ocean water and the melting of land ice that raises global sea level. At the national and local scale, this sea-level rise threatens island states such as Tuvalu with saltwater intrusion and the eventual loss of habitable land — a human consequence of a physical process that connects this ocean debate to the coastal landscapes content and to the study of global migration, since sustained displacement could ultimately force Tuvaluans to migrate under agreements such as the 2023 Falepili Union. Locally, domestic and industrial pollution adds plastics and heavy metals that persist and concentrate in gyres such as the North Pacific, and offshore oil production carries the risk of catastrophic local spills such as Deepwater Horizon, with direct and lasting ecosystem damage. Natural processes — volcanic activity and natural climate variability — still operate, but at the global scale their current influence is smaller than, and in the case of warming and sea-level rise is now entangled with, human greenhouse gas emissions, so natural and human causes are not always separable. On balance, because human activity is the dominant driver behind the acidification, warming, sea-level rise and pollution pressures that connect from the global carbon cycle down to local ecosystems and national communities, and because these pressures compound each other, the statement is well supported, though natural variability means it is not the sole threat everywhere.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Ask where the carbon in the ocean comes from, and which other part of the specification explains that.","Consider each pressure's origin at more than one scale, from what drives it globally to who experiences it locally or nationally, rather than treating 'natural' and 'human' as fixed, separate categories.","Conclude with a clear position on how far human activity dominates across the scales you have used, acknowledging what still counts as a natural contribution."],"codes":["3.a","3.b","3.c","4.a","4.b"]},{"q":"How far does the management of the Antarctic krill fishery demonstrate that biological resources within oceans can be used sustainably?","a":"CCAMLR shows real sustainable-management features from local vessels to global governance (precautionary limit and trigger level below biomass, monitoring, conservation stakeholders), comparable to global governance of other shared commons studied elsewhere in the specification, but rising catches and a warming Southern Ocean affecting krill's sea-ice habitat — linking this debate to the wider climate-change and carbon-cycle content — mean sustainability is not guaranteed.","sol":"The krill fishery shows genuine features of sustainable management at more than one scale. Locally, individual fishing vessels from nations such as Norway, China, South Korea and Chile operate within Area 48 under agreed catch limits rather than open access. Nationally, CCAMLR member states have agreed an overall precautionary catch limit for the area, well below surveyed biomass, and a more conservative interim trigger level of approximately 620,000 tonnes a year while catch remains undivided between sub-areas; regular scientific surveys monitor the population, and stakeholders who value krill's role feeding whales, seals and penguins sit alongside fishing nations in setting these limits. At the global scale, CCAMLR is itself a working example of the kind of international governance of a shared commons studied more widely in the specification, comparable to UNCLOS's management of ocean zones and to global governance of other shared resources and territories. However, sustainability is not guaranteed at any of these scales. Reported catch rose by around 114% between 2010 and 2020, still within the trigger level but showing rising pressure on the resource; more fundamentally, krill depend on sea ice in their first year (larval krill overwinter and feed under the ice), so the warming of the Southern Ocean — driven by the same global carbon cycle and climate-change processes that warm and acidify oceans elsewhere in this topic — threatens the resource's resilience independently of the catch limit itself, connecting this debate to the wider climate-change content of the specification rather than confining the threat to fishing pressure alone. Management also depends on continued political agreement among CCAMLR's members, which is not certain given competing economic interests. On balance, the krill fishery is a genuine example of a precautionary, evidence-based approach to managing a renewable ocean resource across local, national and global scales, but 'sustainable' should be read as 'sustainable so far, under current limits and a stable climate' rather than as a guarantee, so the statement holds to a considerable but not complete extent.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what CCAMLR actually does, at more than one scale, that goes beyond simply allowing a fishery to operate.","Weigh the safeguards already in place against anything — including a cause you would not normally think of as part of a fishery — that still leaves the resource at risk in future.","Give a judgement that states how far, not simply whether, the resource is being used sustainably."],"codes":["2.a","2.c","4.a"]},{"q":"'International governance has failed to prevent the tragedy of the commons in the world's oceans.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? (33 marks)","a":"Some frameworks succeed (CCAMLR/krill, UNCLOS zones) while others show clear failure (South China Sea, North Pacific plastic, Arctic claims), so governance has partially but not wholly failed — extent depends on which ocean commons and scale is examined.","sol":"International governance shows real successes against the tragedy of the commons. UNCLOS (1982) converts an undifferentiated global commons into zones with defined rights — territorial waters, the contiguous zone, the 200-nautical-mile EEZ and the high seas — giving states an incentive to manage resources within their own EEZ rather than race to extract them before a rival does; and within the high seas, CCAMLR's precautionary catch limit for Antarctic krill, set well below surveyed biomass and supported by regular scientific monitoring, is a working example of a shared resource being managed rather than overexploited. Marine protected areas extend this logic further, restricting extraction in specific high-seas or EEZ areas to protect stocks and habitats. Set against this, governance has clearly failed in other ocean commons. Plastic entering the ocean from many states accumulates in gyres such as the North Pacific, forming the Great Pacific Garbage Patch of roughly 1.6 million km², because no framework effectively limits land-based plastic pollution at its many diffuse sources, unlike the single, countable catch that CCAMLR regulates. In the South China Sea, UNCLOS's authority was directly tested and, in practice, disregarded: the 2016 arbitration ruling rejected China's 'nine-dash line' claim as having no legal basis, yet China continued building and militarising artificial islands on disputed reefs, showing that a legal ruling without enforcement power cannot compel a determined state. The Arctic shows a middle case: the Arctic Council coordinates environmental and indigenous issues among its eight member states relatively successfully, but has no mandate over the territorial claims — such as Russia's claim to the Lomonosov Ridge — that shrinking ice is now making commercially relevant, so governance covers some Arctic issues but not the ones with the highest political stakes. Taken together, international governance succeeds where a resource is countable, monitorable and the actors extracting it are a manageable, cooperating number of states, as with krill; it fails where pollution sources are diffuse and hard to attribute, as with plastic, or where a powerful state is willing to disregard a ruling it does not enforce militarily, as in the South China Sea. The statement is therefore too absolute: governance has not failed everywhere, but it has failed specifically in the commons where enforcement is weakest, so the extent to which the statement is true depends heavily on which ocean commons and which scale of governance is being judged.","m":33,"d":3,"hints":["Draw your evidence from more than one governance framework and more than one ocean commons across this topic, not just one.","Organise your paragraphs so each weighs a success against a failure, rather than listing successes then failures separately.","Close with a judgement on what generally determines whether governance succeeds or fails across your examples, rather than a simple yes or no."],"codes":["2.c","2.a","3.c","4.c","5.b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the abyssal plain?","a":"The flat, sediment-covered ocean floor beyond the continental slope."},{"q":"How far does a state's EEZ extend from its baseline?","a":"Up to 200 nautical miles."},{"q":"What is coral bleaching caused by?","a":"Sustained high water temperature causing coral to expel its symbiotic algae."},{"q":"Name the ocean gyre that contains the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.","a":"The North Pacific Subtropical Gyre."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Continental shelf","back":"The shallow, gently sloping edge of a continent, extending from the coast to where the sea floor drops away at the continental slope.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Guyot","back":"A flat-topped seamount — a former volcanic island eroded by waves, then submerged as the ocean crust cooled and subsided.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"North Atlantic circulation","back":"The Gulf Stream/North Atlantic Drift carries warm surface water north-east; cooling and sinking near Greenland drives cold North Atlantic Deep Water south as part of the thermohaline 'conveyor belt'.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Inter-tidal vs deep-water food webs","back":"Inter-tidal: short chains, high light/nutrients, high biodiversity but stressful conditions. Deep-water: longer chains fed by sinking detritus, low light, generally lower biodiversity.","code":"1.c"},{"front":"CCAMLR","back":"The Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources; sets an overall precautionary catch limit for Area 48 and a more conservative interim 'trigger level' of approximately 620,000 tonnes/year, well below that limit.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Wave/tidal energy vs oil/gas","back":"Wave and tidal energy are renewable flow resources limited to specific coastlines; oil, gas and sea-bed minerals are non-renewable stock resources with spill and depletion risk.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Scale of ocean shipping","back":"Containerisation and larger bulk/tanker vessels cut the cost per unit shipped, deepening globalisation, but only ports able to handle the largest ships stay on principal routes, concentrating trade at fewer hub ports and chokepoints.","code":"5.a"},{"front":"UNCLOS zones","back":"Territorial waters (12 nm), contiguous zone (24 nm), exclusive economic zone/EEZ (200 nm), then high seas — distance measured from the state's baseline.","code":"2.c"},{"front":"Deepwater Horizon","back":"BP-operated Macondo well, Gulf of Mexico; blew out 20 April 2010, releasing oil for 87 days; closed fisheries and damaged tourism along the Gulf coast.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Great Pacific Garbage Patch","back":"Plastic accumulation covering approximately 1.6 million km² in the North Pacific Subtropical Gyre, caused by convergent surface currents concentrating rim-sourced plastic.","code":"3.c"},{"front":"Ocean acidification","back":"Dissolved CO2 lowers ocean pH, reducing carbonate available for shell- and skeleton-building organisms and, through the food chain, fish stocks.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Tuvalu","back":"Pacific atoll nation, average elevation approximately 2 m; adapting through land reclamation (Funafuti) and the 2023 Falepili Union climate-mobility treaty with Australia.","code":"4.b"},{"front":"Ice–albedo feedback","back":"Ice reflects sunlight (high albedo); open water absorbs it (low albedo). Melting exposes more dark water, causing further warming and further melting — a positive feedback.","code":"4.c"},{"front":"Ocean hazards: piracy and migration","back":"21st-century piracy concentrates off Somalia and in the Gulf of Guinea, managed by naval patrols and onshore governance; migrants cross as escape routes, e.g. South East Asia to Australia or North Africa to Europe, raising rescue and legal challenges.","code":"5.c"},{"front":"South China Sea dispute","back":"China's 'nine-dash line' claim and artificial islands on Spratly/Paracel reefs are disputed by Vietnam and the Philippines; a 2016 arbitration ruling rejected the claim's legal basis, but China continued building.","code":"5.b"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (top band, 33-mark question)","items":["Question: \"'International governance has failed to prevent the tragedy of the commons in the world's oceans.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?\" (33 marks)","In the South China Sea, governance has visibly failed to prevent contest over a shared marine commons: China's 'nine-dash line' claim and its construction of artificial islands on the Spratly and Paracel reefs were rejected as having no legal basis under UNCLOS by a 2016 international arbitration ruling, yet China continued to militarise the reefs it had built, and neighbouring claimants such as Vietnam and the Philippines have been unable to secure their contested waters despite the ruling in their favour. This contrasts sharply with CCAMLR's management of Antarctic krill, where a precautionary catch limit set well below surveyed biomass has, so far, kept catch within sustainable bounds without any comparable defiance by fishing states. The difference is that CCAMLR regulates a single, countable extraction that member states have agreed to monitor, whereas the South China Sea dispute is about sovereignty as much as resources, and UNCLOS has no mechanism to enforce a ruling against a state unwilling to comply militarily. This shows that international governance can prevent the tragedy of the commons where the resource is monitorable and the parties are willing to be bound by the framework, but fails where sovereignty claims override the legal ruling, so the statement holds only where enforcement, not just law, is absent."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (lower band, 33-mark question)","items":["Question: \"'International governance has failed to prevent the tragedy of the commons in the world's oceans.' To what extent do you agree with this statement?\" (33 marks)","International governance has failed in some places. In the South China Sea, China ignored the 2016 ruling about the nine-dash line and kept building on the islands, which shows governance did not work there. But in the krill fishery, CCAMLR set a catch limit and this has worked better. So governance has failed in some places but not others, so the statement is partly true."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Antarctic krill fishery","items":["Krill are managed by CCAMLR (the Commission for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources), established in 1982 as part of the Antarctic Treaty System.","CCAMLR has set an overall precautionary catch limit for Area 48 (the South Atlantic sector), well below surveyed biomass; pending agreement on how to divide this between sub-areas, it also applies a more conservative interim 'trigger level' of approximately 620,000 tonnes per year, well below that precautionary limit.","Reported catch rose from approximately 210 thousand tonnes in 2010 to approximately 450 thousand tonnes in 2020 — an increase of approximately 114%, still within the trigger level.","Main fishing nations include Norway, China, South Korea and Chile; krill is processed into fishmeal, aquaculture feed and omega-3 oil.","Krill underpin the Southern Ocean food web, feeding baleen whales, seals, penguins and fish, which is why conservation stakeholders pushed for a precautionary rather than maximum-yield catch limit."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Deepwater Horizon oil spill (2010)","items":["The Deepwater Horizon rig, operating BP's Macondo well in the Gulf of Mexico, exploded on 20 April 2010, killing 11 workers.","Oil flowed for approximately 87 days until the well was capped on 15 July 2010, releasing approximately 4.9 million barrels of oil (approximately 780 million litres).","Impacts included oiled Louisiana marshes and beaches, contaminated water and sediment across parts of the Gulf, and harm to marine life including dolphins, turtles and fish.","Commercial and recreational fishing grounds were closed across large areas of the Gulf, and Gulf coast tourism lost income as beaches closed and visitor numbers fell.","Management combined capping the well, chemical dispersants, controlled burning and mechanical skimming; BP's total costs (clean-up, fines and compensation) exceeded approximately $60 billion."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: plastic accumulation in the North Pacific gyre","items":["The Great Pacific Garbage Patch lies within the North Pacific Subtropical Gyre, between Hawaii and California.","A 2018 study by The Ocean Cleanup Foundation estimated its area at approximately 1.6 million km², roughly three times the area of France.","The same study estimated approximately 80,000 tonnes of plastic and approximately 1.8 trillion plastic pieces within the patch.","The gyre is formed by the Kuroshio, North Pacific, California and North Equatorial currents circulating clockwise, converging plastic at its calm centre.","Impacts on marine ecosystems include ingestion of microplastics by fish and seabirds (including albatross on Midway Atoll) and entanglement of turtles and marine mammals in larger debris."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: an island community and sea level rise — Tuvalu","items":["Tuvalu is a Pacific island nation of nine coral atolls and reef islands, with a population of approximately 11,000.","Average elevation is approximately 2 metres above sea level, with the highest point only approximately 4.5 metres.","Threats include saltwater intrusion into freshwater lenses and taro-growing pits, and increased storm-surge damage as sea level rises.","Short-term adaptation includes the Funafuti foreshore land reclamation project, raising new land above projected future sea levels, funded with international support.","Long-term adaptation includes the 2023 Falepili Union treaty with Australia, offering a climate-mobility pathway for Tuvaluans, and a government project to preserve digital records of the nation."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Arctic region","items":["Arctic September minimum sea ice extent has declined by approximately 13% per decade in recent decades (NSIDC data), driven by the ice–albedo feedback; the annual mean decline is roughly a third of that rate.","The Arctic Council, founded in 1996, brings together the eight Arctic states (Canada, Denmark/Greenland, Finland, Iceland, Norway, Russia, Sweden and the USA) and indigenous permanent participants such as the Inuit Circumpolar Council.","Shrinking ice is opening the Northwest Passage through the Canadian Arctic Archipelago to more seasonal shipping.","The US Geological Survey (2008) estimated the Arctic may hold approximately 13% of the world's undiscovered oil and approximately 30% of undiscovered natural gas.","Russia has submitted a claim to the United Nations for an extended continental shelf including the Lomonosov Ridge, disputed by Canada and Denmark."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: naval strongholds and a marine conflict zone","items":["The USA bases its Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor, Hawaii, and holds its largest naval base at Naval Station Norfolk, Virginia.","US overseas naval bases include Yokosuka in Japan (Seventh Fleet) and Naval Support Activity Bahrain (Fifth Fleet, Persian Gulf).","In the South China Sea, China claims most of the sea within a 'nine-dash line' and has built artificial islands with airstrips and military facilities on Spratly and Paracel reefs.","Vietnam, the Philippines, Malaysia, Brunei and Taiwan also claim parts of the South China Sea.","A 2016 Permanent Court of Arbitration ruling (Philippines v. China) found China's nine-dash line claim had no legal basis under UNCLOS; approximately a third of world shipping trade passes through the South China Sea."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan: \"International governance has failed to prevent the tragedy of the commons in the world's oceans.\" To what extent do you agree? (33 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'tragedy of the commons' means the ocean being overexploited because no one state bears the full cost — the essay must judge how far international governance (UNCLOS, CCAMLR, the Arctic Council, arbitration rulings) has stopped this happening, not just describe the governance that exists.","Introduction: define 'tragedy of the commons' and 'international governance', and state the line of argument — that governance succeeds where a resource is monitorable and states cooperate, but fails where sovereignty claims override rulings or pollution sources are too diffuse to attribute.","Paragraph 1 (success): UNCLOS's zones (territorial waters, contiguous zone, 200 nm EEZ, high seas) and CCAMLR's precautionary krill trigger level (approximately 620,000 tonnes/year) — evidence that clear, monitorable frameworks change state behaviour; close with an interim judgement that governance can work.","Paragraph 2 (failure): the North Pacific gyre's plastic accumulation (approximately 1.6 million km²) — no framework limits the many diffuse land-based sources feeding it, unlike krill's single countable catch; close with an interim judgement on why diffuse pollution defeats this kind of governance.","Paragraph 3 (failure): the South China Sea — the 2016 arbitration ruling against China's nine-dash line was disregarded, showing a legal ruling without enforcement power cannot bind an unwilling, powerful state; close with an interim judgement on the limits of law without enforcement.","Paragraph 4 (mixed case, synoptic link): the Arctic Council manages environmental and indigenous issues among its eight states reasonably well, but has no mandate over the territorial claims (e.g. the Lomonosov Ridge) that melting ice is making valuable — link this to the ice–albedo feedback and to resource geography, showing governance succeeds on some issues within one region but not others.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: does the framework regulate a monitorable, attributable extraction or emission, and are the states involved willing to be bound by it? Success (krill, EEZ zones) versus failure (plastic, South China Sea) should be explained by this same test each time, not treated as separate stories.","Conclusion: state directly how far the claim is true — governance has not failed everywhere, but has failed specifically where monitoring or enforcement is weakest — and name which commons (plastic, contested sovereignty waters) show the clearest failure and which (krill, EEZ resource rights) show the clearest partial success."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.943Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"The five faults in the first verdict are genuinely fixed — all four 12-mark sols now cross into named content elsewhere in the specification (carbon cycle, coastal landscapes, global migration, global governance of sovereignty) and work local-national-global; every stem uses a listed command word (Identify, Define, Explain, Evaluate, To what extent, How far), including the reworded calculation in room.questions[8]; the krill series is now the real 210 to 450 thousand tonnes for CCAMLR Area 48 and 114% checks out (240/210 = 114.3%) in room.questions[8].sol, lesson.examples[3], extras[2] and room.questions[13].sol alike; the Arctic figure is qualified as the September minimum with the annual-mean rate given in room.facts[5] and extras[6]; and CCAMLR's 620,000 t is now correctly described as an interim trigger level sitting below the precautionary catch limit in lesson.examples[1], cards[4] and extras[2]. Tariffs are all 3, 6, 12 or 33; the lesson has a faithful section for all fifteen key ideas each closing on what a top-level answer does; there are six fact files for the six required case studies (krill/CCAMLR, Deepwater Horizon, North Pacific gyre, Tuvalu, the Arctic, naval strongholds and the South China Sea) and every date and figure spot-checked is right (4.9 million barrels over 87 days to 15 July 2010, >$60bn BP costs, 1.6 million km² and 80,000 t and 1.8 trillion pieces, Tuvalu ~11,000 people and ~2 m mean elevation with the 2023 Falepili Union, Arctic Council 1996 and USGS 2008 13%/30%, 2016 Philippines v. China); cards now cover 5.a and 5.c, and the model paragraphs are labelled Level 4 (top band) and Level 2 (lower band) against OCR's four-level grid. Polish before publishing: room.questions[13].sol says krill 'depend on sea ice for breeding habitat' when it is larval krill that overwinter and feed under the ice, so 'depend on sea ice in their first year' is safer; room.questions[10].sol's 'an estimated third of world trade' should read 'world shipping trade', as extras[7] correctly has it; no question carries code 2.b, so ocean energy and sea-bed minerals are taught and carded but never practised; the 33-mark sol and extras[8] stay largely inside topic 3.3, so a line of explicit linkage to physical or human content elsewhere would lift them to the same standard as the 12-mark answers; and the Level 4/Level 2 labels would be more useful with the band's mark range beside them."},"refined":"2026-09-11T15:07:50.844Z"}},"3.4":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"3.4","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Future of Food sits in Geographical debates (Component 03), examined with the component's own mix of tariffs set out in the specification: short answer questions that carry 3 marks each, 6-mark medium-length questions, 12-mark synoptic questions and 33-mark extended-response essays that are set on all Debates topics and reward a sustained, evidence-led line of reasoning. Food security is genuinely contested, spatially variable and topical, so examiners reward named, up-to-date case studies used as real evidence rather than generic description, and answers that weigh physical and human causes against each other, across more than one topic and scale, before reaching a judgement.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Food security has three pillars and a variable pattern","t":"Food security means everyone, everywhere, having access to enough safe and nutritious food at all times, built on three pillars: food access (can people obtain and afford food), food availability (is enough food actually produced, stored or imported) and food use (can food be prepared and digested to give proper nutrition). Global data — undernourishment statistics (approximately 735 million people undernourished in 2022, FAO estimate) and the Global Food Security Index — show the pattern is dynamic, worsening in some regions while easing in others, and varies within countries as much as between them. A top-level answer names the pillar(s) at stake rather than writing generically about 'not having enough food'."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Global food production is one interconnected system","t":"Growing food depends on physical conditions — air, climate, soil and water — that vary hugely between regions, so the same crop needs different inputs in different places. Feeding the world is then a chain: growing, processing, transporting and disposing of consumer waste, with a weak link anywhere in that chain reducing food security downstream. Production methods sit on two spectrums, intensive to extensive and subsistence to commercial, and a place's position on both shapes how vulnerable its food supply is to shocks. A top-level answer traces the specific link in this system a question is really asking about, rather than describing farming in general."},{"code":"1.c","h":"Globalisation is reshaping the food industry","t":"Globalisation has increased demand for food and spread global tastes across income groups and continents, restructuring the food industry around long, international supply chains. This creates issues — food miles, inequality between powerful TNCs and small suppliers, rising obesity in some places and food price crises when global markets are disrupted — but also opportunities: technological innovation, faster short-term food relief when crises strike, and wider consumer choice. A top-level answer weighs issues against opportunities for a specific group, rather than treating globalisation as simply good or bad."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Interrelated factors combine to cause food insecurity","t":"Physical factors — geology and soil quality, climate and length of growing season — set a ceiling on what a place can produce, but human factors decide who gets access to what is produced: land ownership systems, competition for land and water, and land grabbing that can push production toward export crops rather than local food security. Malthusian theory warns population grows faster than food supply, causing crisis, while Boserupian theory argues population pressure drives the innovation that raises food supply to meet it. In Ethiopia, drought-prone rainfall combines with land tenure pressures and periodic conflict to produce chronic food insecurity, showing physical and human causes reinforcing each other. A top-level answer uses a named place to show this combination, not physical or human causes alone."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Risk mapping identifies the most vulnerable","t":"Food security risk is not evenly spread: it concentrates where poverty limits access even when food is available, and at geographical pinchpoints — narrow points in global distribution, such as the Suez Canal, where a blockage can delay shipments and raise prices worldwide. Desertification — land degradation in drylands from physical causes such as rainfall variability and human causes such as overgrazing and unsustainable irrigation — destroys the ecosystem services drylands depend on, raising risk further. In the Lake Chad Basin, a shrinking lake, a fast-growing population, land grabbing and worsening climate variability combine to threaten one of the world's most vulnerable dryland populations. A top-level answer identifies specifically who is at risk and why, using named places rather than generalising."},{"code":"3.b","h":"Shocks make the food system vulnerable","t":"Climate change is increasing the frequency of extreme weather events — wildfire, El Nino episodes, floods and drought — that can destroy a harvest in a single season, while water scarcity compounds the problem by limiting irrigation exactly when rain-fed farming fails; tectonic hazards can also destroy farmland, storage and transport links. Indigenous groups in extreme environments show how finely tuned traditional food systems are to a stable climate: Inuit communities in the Canadian Arctic rely on hunting and fishing timed to sea ice and animal migration, so melting sea ice threatens a food system with almost no buffer against change. A top-level answer treats 'shock' precisely — naming the hazard, its mechanism and who absorbs the impact."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Food production imbalance harms environment and people","t":"Producing more food can degrade the environment it depends on: irrigation without adequate drainage causes salinisation, deforestation for farmland destroys biodiversity and disrupts local water and carbon cycles, and agrochemical run-off pollutes water quality. The Aral Sea shows the scale of this: Soviet-era irrigation for cotton diverted its feeder rivers, shrinking the sea by approximately 90% and leaving salt- and pesticide-laden dust that damages farmland and health for miles around. Poor food security also harms people directly, through undernutrition where food is short and obesity or diet-related disease where surplus and poor-quality diets are common, and these impacts fall very differently in an AC such as the UK compared with an LIDC such as Tanzania. A top-level answer keeps physical and human impacts separate, evidencing each with the specific case."},{"code":"5.a","h":"Food is a geopolitical commodity","t":"Countries can cooperate to secure food supply through agricultural trading policies, the World Trade Organization's rules on agricultural trade, and appropriate aid that is targeted and need-led rather than aid that undercuts local producers. Non-state actors matter just as much: agribusiness and Trans-National Corporations such as Unilever control much of the world's processing and supply chains, food retailers such as Tesco shape what and how much is grown through their purchasing power, and fair trade organisations try to redress the imbalance of power between TNCs and small producers. A top-level answer names the specific player and its actual effect on food security, rather than referring vaguely to 'globalisation' or 'big companies'."},{"code":"5.b","h":"A spectrum of strategies can improve food security","t":"Strategies to improve food security range from short-term relief through capacity building to long-term system redesign, and effectiveness depends on matching the strategy to the problem rather than assuming bigger and more technological is always better. Israel's large-scale drip irrigation and treated-wastewater reuse show what capital-intensive technology can achieve in an arid climate, while Ghana's Kuapa Kokoo Fairtrade cocoa cooperative shows a small-scale, bottom-up approach raising smallholder incomes and funding local infrastructure directly. A top-level answer judges sustainability and appropriateness for the place in question — who can afford, maintain and benefit from a strategy — rather than simply describing what a strategy involves."}],"examples":[{"title":"Faded 6-mark answer: explaining a food security threat","setup":"Exam question (6 marks): 'Explain how drought threatens food security in dryland regions. Support your answer with a named example.' The dryland case you have studied is the Lake Chad Basin.","steps":["Drought hits dryland food production hardest because farming there depends on one short, unreliable rainy season with almost no irrigation to fall back on.","When those rains fail, soil moisture drops below what crops need at germination and grain-filling, so rain-fed millet and sorghum yields collapse and the pasture that feeds livestock dries out.","In the Lake Chad Basin, reduced rainfall since the 1960s has combined with rising abstraction of water for irrigation to shrink the lake by approximately 90%, cutting both the irrigated area and the fishery that supplied protein and income.","Availability and access therefore fail together — less food is grown locally and households have less income to buy what is left — so drought threatens dryland food security by degrading production and livelihoods at once, and around Lake Chad it has sharpened competition and conflict over the remaining land and water."],"cues":["Why are dryland farmers so exposed to a failed rainy season?","How does the lack of rain reach the crop itself?","What has happened to Lake Chad, and since when?","How do these effects combine to threaten food security?"]},{"title":"Faded 12-mark synoptic paragraph: linking desertification to the wider specification","setup":"12-mark synoptic question: 'Explain the physical and human causes of desertification in the Sahel, and show how this links to other parts of the specification.' You may use the FAO estimate that 735 million people worldwide were undernourished in 2022.","steps":["Physical cause: the Sahel lies at the northern limit of the tropical rain belt, so a weak or southward-shifted monsoon leaves rainfall both lower and far less reliable, reducing effective precipitation and leaving soils bare for longer each year.","Human cause: population growth and land grabbing by commercial investors push cultivation and grazing onto marginal land and shorten fallow periods, stripping the vegetation cover that had been holding the soil in place.","Link across the specification: once that cover is gone, Landscape Systems (dryland landscapes) explains the aeolian deflation that removes the fine topsoil, while Earth's Life Support Systems explains the water-cycle side — less infiltration and a smaller soil-moisture store, with more runoff when rain does arrive.","Scale the evidence: locally a single Sahel village loses its millet harvest; nationally Niger's cereal deficit widens; globally, when several Sahel states fail in the same year, their hungry households add to the FAO's 735 million undernourished in 2022.","Judgement: neither cause is sufficient alone — the rain belt's shift sets the physical limit, while land pressure removes the vegetation that buffered it — so Sahel desertification must be explained as a physical–human interaction working from village to global scale."],"cues":["Which rainfall shift makes Sahel rain so unreliable?","Which human pressures strip the protective vegetation?","Which other specification topics explain the soil and water losses?","How does this look at village, national and global scale?","So is the cause physical, human, or something else?"]},{"title":"Faded data calculation: reading a food-supply table","setup":"FAO estimates put the global prevalence of undernourishment at approximately 8.0% of the world's population in 2019 and approximately 9.2% in 2022. Calculate the percentage change (the relative increase) in prevalence between those two years.","steps":["Change in prevalence = 9.2 − 8.0 = 1.2, and because both figures are themselves percentages of the world's population, the 2022 value stands 1.2 percentage points above the 2019 one.","A percentage change is always measured against the starting value, so the divisor is the 2019 figure: percentage change = (1.2 ÷ 8.0) × 100.","1.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.15, and 0.15 × 100 = 15.","Prevalence of undernourishment rose by 1.2 percentage points between 2019 and 2022, which is a relative increase of approximately 15% — so the change should be reported as a rise of about 15%, not a rise of 1.2%."],"cues":["What do you subtract, and what unit is the difference in?","Which year's value is the base you divide by?","What does that division, times 100, come to?","How should the rise be worded in an exam answer?"]},{"title":"Faded 33-mark opening paragraph: defining terms and signalling the argument","setup":"33-mark question: \"'The greatest threat to global food security is physical rather than human.' Discuss, with reference to named examples on each side of the argument.\" Write the opening paragraph; the cases you have studied are the Lake Chad Basin and Ethiopia.","steps":["Food security rests on access, availability and use, so 'the greatest threat' means whichever pressure most often breaks one of those three pillars — a comparison to be judged, not a single hazard to be described.","On the physical side of the claim sit drought, desertification and climate-driven extremes; on the human side sit land grabbing, conflict, unequal trade and poverty, which decide who can actually obtain the food that exists.","This essay will argue that physical events are usually the trigger, but human factors decide who goes hungry, so the human threat is the greater of the two.","The evidence will be the Lake Chad Basin, where a physical loss of water cut what could be produced, set against Ethiopia, where land leasing and conflict turned poor harvests into famine risk — one named case for each side, weighed again in the conclusion."],"cues":["How should 'food security' and 'greatest' be defined here?","Which threats belong on each side of the claim?","Which side will the essay argue is more decisive?","Which named case will carry each side of the argument?"]},{"title":"Faded case-study paragraph: Ethiopia as combined evidence","setup":"Exam question: 'Using Ethiopia as a case study, explain how physical and human factors combine to cause food insecurity.' Write the case-study paragraph, using the drought and conflict years of 2020–2022 that you have studied.","steps":["Physical factor: most Ethiopian farming is rain-fed and depends on the kiremt rains of June to September, so when that season is late or short the harvest falls before any human factor is taken into account.","Human factor: farmland leased to foreign investors for export crops takes the best-watered, most reliable land out of local food production, leaving fewer households with the land or savings to absorb a bad season.","Second human factor: the Tigray conflict of 2020–2022 closed roads, markets and aid routes, so grain that existed elsewhere in the country could not reach the people who needed it — a failure of access rather than of availability.","The two sides act at once: drought cut what was grown while land leasing and conflict cut what could be bought or delivered, so Ethiopia's food insecurity is best explained by interacting physical and human causes rather than by either one alone."],"cues":["Which rainy season do Ethiopian harvests depend on?","What does leasing farmland to investors do to local food supply?","Which conflict cut distribution, and in which years?","How do the physical and human strands combine in the judgement?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What are the three pillars of food security?","a":"Food access, food availability and food use."},{"q":"Name one geographical pinchpoint for global food distribution.","a":"The Suez Canal (or another named strait or canal, such as the Bosphorus Strait)."},{"q":"Give one large-scale and one small-scale strategy to improve food security.","a":"Large-scale: Israeli drip irrigation and wastewater reuse. Small-scale: a Fairtrade cooperative such as Kuapa Kokoo."},{"q":"What does the Boserupian view argue, in contrast to the Malthusian view?","a":"That population pressure drives the innovation that raises food supply to meet demand, rather than population inevitably outrunning food supply."}]},"room":{"facts":["Food security rests on three pillars: food access, food availability and food use.","Approximately 735 million people were undernourished worldwide in 2022 (FAO estimate).","The Suez Canal is a named geographical pinchpoint for global food distribution; it was blocked by a grounded container ship in 2021.","Lake Chad has shrunk by approximately 90% in surface area since the 1960s.","Malthus (1798) argued population grows faster than food supply; Boserup argued population pressure drives the agricultural innovation that raises food supply.","Modern drip irrigation was commercialised by the Israeli company Netafim, founded in 1965."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify the three pillars that make up the concept of food security.","a":"Food access, food availability and food use.","sol":"Food security is built on three pillars: food access (whether people can obtain and afford food), food availability (whether enough food is actually produced, stored or imported) and food use (whether food can be prepared and digested to give proper nutrition).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about the definition the specification builds food security on, not about hunger statistics.","There are exactly three linked ideas, each about a different stage between growing food and being nourished by it.","List each pillar as a short, separate term."],"codes":["1.a"]},{"q":"Identify three physical conditions necessary for growing food.","a":"Any three of: air, climate, soil, water.","sol":"Growing food requires suitable air (for example CO2 and temperature for photosynthesis), a suitable climate, adequate water and suitable soil, which together set the physical conditions in which crops or livestock can be produced; any three of these four conditions meet the question.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This is about what a plant or animal physically needs to grow, not about how food is sold.","The specification names four physical conditions together — choose any three of them.","Name each condition as a single word or short phrase."],"codes":["1.b"]},{"q":"Identify three geographical pinchpoints, other than the Suez Canal, through which global food trade must pass.","a":"Any three of: the Strait of Hormuz, the Bosphorus Strait/Black Sea grain corridor, the Panama Canal, the Strait of Malacca.","sol":"Global food trade passes through several narrow pinchpoints besides the Suez Canal: the Bosphorus Strait, which carried the Black Sea grain corridor for Ukrainian and Russian exports; the Strait of Hormuz, a route used by Gulf states' shipping; the Panama Canal, a shortcut for grain moving between the Americas and Asia; and the Strait of Malacca, the main route for trade between the Indian Ocean and East Asia. Naming any three of these meets the question.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about narrow shipping straits or canals elsewhere in the world, not just the Suez Canal.","Consider routes used for grain or general trade between major world regions.","Name each strait or canal, one at a time."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"Identify three types of organisation, other than national governments, that influence the global food system.","a":"Any three of: agribusiness/TNCs (e.g. Unilever), food retailers (e.g. Tesco), the World Trade Organization, fair trade organisations.","sol":"The global food system is shaped by agribusiness and Trans-National Corporations such as Unilever, by food retailers such as Tesco whose buying power affects what is grown, by the World Trade Organization's rules on agricultural trade, and by fair trade organisations that try to guarantee small producers a fairer price.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think beyond governments to companies and international bodies named in this topic.","Look back at the section on food as a geopolitical commodity, which names several very different kinds of influential organisation — pick three that are clearly not the same kind as each other.","Name three of these, using the specification's own examples where you can."],"codes":["5.a"]},{"q":"Identify three ways that attempts to increase food production can damage the physical environment.","a":"Any three of: salinisation from irrigation, deforestation and biodiversity loss, changing landscapes, water pollution from agrochemicals.","sol":"Irrigation without adequate drainage can cause salinisation, the build-up of salts in soil that eventually reduces yields; clearing land for farming can cause deforestation, destroying habitats and biodiversity; large-scale conversion of land for farming directly changes landscapes; and agrochemical run-off can pollute water quality downstream. Naming any three of these meets the question.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what happens to soil, forests or water when farming is intensified, not about people's health.","The specification lists four ways farming can damage the physical environment — choose any three of them.","Name each impact as a short geographical term."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Table 1 shows average daily food (kcal) supply per person: UK approximately 3,440 kcal; Chad approximately 2,110 kcal. Using the data, analyse the difference in food availability this shows between the UK and Chad. [6]","a":"Calculates the percentage difference correctly (approximately 63% higher in the UK) and links this to unequal food availability between an AC and an LIDC.","sol":"Difference = 3,440 minus 2,110 = 1,330 kcal. Percentage = 1,330 divided by 2,110, multiplied by 100 = 63.0% (to 1 decimal place), so the UK's average daily food supply is approximately 63% higher than Chad's. This gap reflects the food availability pillar of food security rather than food access or use: the UK's intensive, high-input commercial agriculture and its ability to import food from across the world give it a far larger and more reliable food supply than Chad, where much farming remains rain-fed and subsistence-based and is more exposed to drought. The scale of the gap illustrates why data sources such as the Global Food Security Index treat availability as a distinct pillar from a country's wealth or trade position alone.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["This question asks you to use the figures given, not just describe causes of food insecurity in general.","First find the numerical difference between the two figures, then express that difference as a percentage of the smaller figure, showing each step.","Once you have the percentage, use it to say something about the difference in food availability between an AC and an LIDC."],"codes":["1.a","1.b"]},{"q":"Explain how physical and human factors have combined to cause food insecurity in one place you have studied. [6]","a":"A named place (e.g. Ethiopia) where a physical factor such as drought has combined with a human factor such as land grabbing or conflict to reduce food access and availability.","sol":"In Ethiopia, food insecurity results from physical and human causes acting together rather than separately. Physically, much of the country depends on rain-fed farming, so a failed kiremt rainy season directly cuts harvests and pushes up local food prices. Humanly, government leases of farmland to foreign investors for commercial crops have reduced the land available to smallholders, while periods of conflict, such as the Tigray conflict (2020-2022), have disrupted farming and blocked food distribution to affected regions. Because drought, land-use change and conflict have coincided in the same period, Ethiopia's food insecurity is best explained as an interaction between physical and human causes, not either acting alone.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Focus on one named place where you can show a physical cause and a human cause acting at the same time.","Explain each factor as a chain of cause and effect, then show how the two chains connect rather than listing them side by side.","Your answer should end with a clear judgement about how the two factors relate to each other, not just describe them side by side."],"codes":["2.a"]},{"q":"Explain how climate change is increasing threats to food production through extreme weather events. [6]","a":"Climate change is increasing the frequency/intensity of events such as drought, flood, wildfire and El Nino, each disrupting food production through its own mechanism.","sol":"Climate change is increasing the frequency of extreme weather events that disrupt food production through different mechanisms. Prolonged drought lowers soil moisture and river flow, reducing yields and limiting irrigation at the same time; more frequent flooding can destroy standing crops and spoil stored grain; wildfire, worsened by hotter, drier conditions, can destroy pasture and farm infrastructure outright; and stronger El Nino events disrupt normal rainfall patterns across large regions simultaneously, so several food-producing areas can fail in the same season. Because these events are becoming more frequent rather than one-off, they threaten not just a single harvest but the reliability of food production over time.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Focus on the weather-related hazards named in this topic, not on tectonic hazards.","Explain at least two different hazards, each through its own mechanism for damaging food production.","Your answer should link how these events are becoming more frequent to the reliability of food production over time, not just describe one event."],"codes":["3.b"]},{"q":"Explain two health impacts that poor food security can have on people. [6]","a":"Any two of: undernutrition-related disease from food shortage; obesity/diet-related disease from food surplus and poor diet; harm from pesticide/chemical exposure linked to intensive farming.","sol":"Poor food security harms health in more than one direction. Where food is short, undernutrition can cause stunted growth in children and weakened immunity to disease, as seen in many LIDCs with chronic food shortages. Where food is abundant but of poor nutritional quality, over-consumption of processed, energy-dense food contributes to obesity and related conditions such as type 2 diabetes, a growing problem in many ACs including the UK. These two impacts show poor food security harming health at opposite ends of the same spectrum: too little food and too much of the wrong kind.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about health impacts at both ends of the food-security spectrum, not just from having too little food.","Explain each impact as a cause-and-effect chain from a specific food-security problem to a specific health outcome.","Choose two contrasting impacts so your answer shows the range of ways food insecurity affects health."],"codes":["4.a"]},{"q":"Compare the effectiveness of two contrasting strategies used to improve food security in places you have studied. [6]","a":"A large-scale technological strategy (e.g. Israeli drip irrigation) compared with a small-scale bottom-up strategy (e.g. a Fairtrade cooperative such as Kuapa Kokoo), judged on effectiveness and sustainability.","sol":"Israel's large-scale drip irrigation, combined with treated-wastewater reuse, has raised agricultural output substantially in an arid climate, but requires capital, infrastructure and expertise that many LIDCs cannot easily replicate. In contrast, Ghana's Kuapa Kokoo Fairtrade cocoa cooperative improves food security more indirectly and on a smaller scale, by guaranteeing smallholder farmers a minimum price and premium that funds community wells, schools and healthcare, requiring far less capital and being controlled by the farmers themselves. Israel's approach is more effective at raising output quickly, but Kuapa Kokoo's approach is arguably more sustainable and appropriate for smallholders, because it builds local capacity rather than depending on imported technology and expensive infrastructure.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Choose one large-scale, technological strategy and one small-scale, bottom-up strategy to contrast.","Compare them on both how much they raise food security and how sustainable or appropriate they are for the place using them.","Your answer should end with a direct comparison, not two separate descriptions."],"codes":["5.b"]},{"q":"To what extent is desertification the greatest threat to food security in dryland regions? [12]","a":"A synoptic answer connecting desertification to landscape and water-cycle processes and to human threats such as land grabbing and conflict-driven migration, working across local, regional and global scales, and reaching a judgement.","sol":"Desertification is a serious threat to food security in drylands: as vegetation and soil structure break down under overgrazing, deforestation and unreliable rainfall, land loses the fertility and water-holding capacity food production depends on. This connects directly to content studied in Landscape Systems, where dryland landform processes such as wind erosion explain how degraded land loses its productive capacity once vegetation cover is stripped away, and to the water cycle studied in Earth's Life Support Systems, where a fall in evapotranspiration and soil-moisture storage helps explain the reduced effective precipitation recorded across the Sahel. At the local scale, in the Lake Chad Basin, shrinking water resources linked to this drying trend have combined with desertification of surrounding land to cut both farming and fishing livelihoods. However, desertification rarely acts as the sole threat: at the same local scale, rapid population growth increases pressure on the degrading land and land grabbing by outside investors reduces what remains for local farmers, while at a regional scale conflict linked to Boko Haram has displaced millions of people, a pattern of environmentally linked displacement that connects to the drivers of migration studied in Global Connections. At the global scale, this regional crisis feeds into international humanitarian aid flows and commodity markets, showing the threat is not confined to the dryland itself. Because human pressures — population, land ownership and conflict — often determine whether degraded land can still support people, and because the consequences reach well beyond the local scale, desertification is best understood as one interacting driver of dryland food insecurity, working alongside human and migratory pressures across scales, rather than the single greatest threat on its own.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Treat this as a genuine 'to what extent' question — you need reasons for and against desertification being the dominant threat.","Bring in the physical processes behind desertification from Landscape Systems or the water cycle, and connect the human consequences to migration at more than one scale.","Your conclusion should weigh desertification against the other pressures you have described, rather than simply listing them."],"codes":["3.a"]},{"q":"'Globalisation creates more opportunities than problems for global food security.' How far do you agree with this statement? [12]","a":"A synoptic answer weighing globalisation's problems (food miles, TNC-supplier inequality, obesity, price crises) against its opportunities (technological innovation, short-term relief, consumer choice), connecting the debate to trade governance, Changing Spaces; Making Places and diet-related disease, and working across local to global scale, reaching a judgement.","sol":"Globalisation has created real problems for food security: long supply chains increase food miles and carbon footprints, TNCs such as Unilever can hold far more bargaining power over price than small suppliers, and shocks to global markets can trigger sudden price crises that hit poorer food-importing countries hardest. The spread of globally marketed, processed diets has also contributed to rising obesity in many countries, a pattern that connects to the rise of diet-related non-communicable disease studied in Disease Dilemmas, showing globalisation's food effects reaching beyond food security into public health. At the local scale, this same process is visible in how supermarket chains and global brands have reshaped small towns and high streets, the kind of change studied in Changing Spaces; Making Places, altering what food is available and affordable within a single community. Against this, globalisation has also brought clear opportunities: technological innovation spreads faster between countries, short-term food relief can reach a crisis zone more quickly through global logistics networks, and consumers in many countries now have far more year-round choice than before. Fair trade organisations show globalisation can be redirected to reduce its own problems, guaranteeing small producers a fairer price within the same global trading system that is governed by bodies studied in Global Connections, such as the World Trade Organization. Because the problems tend to fall on producers and poorer consumers at a local scale while the opportunities more often benefit wealthier consumers and companies operating globally, whether opportunities outweigh problems depends heavily on who and which scale is being considered, so the claim only holds for parts of the global food system, not all of it.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["This needs both sides of the globalisation debate weighed against each other, not just a list of effects.","Connect at least one effect to content beyond this topic — for example diet-related disease, changing local places, or global trade governance — and consider more than one scale.","Your conclusion should say for whom, or at what scale, opportunities outweigh problems, rather than giving a single yes-or-no answer."],"codes":["1.c","5.a"]},{"q":"How far do you agree that indigenous communities are among the most vulnerable to threats to food security? [12]","a":"A synoptic answer using an indigenous case study (e.g. Inuit in the Arctic) linked to global climate processes, weighed against another highly vulnerable group (e.g. in the Lake Chad Basin) linked to conflict-driven migration, working across local and global scale, reaching a judgement about relative vulnerability.","sol":"Indigenous communities in extreme environments can be highly vulnerable to threats to food security: Inuit communities in the Canadian Arctic depend on hunting and fishing timed closely to sea ice and animal migration, so as climate change melts sea ice earlier and shifts caribou routes, their traditional food system has very little buffer. This local vulnerability is the product of a global-scale physical process, Arctic amplification, whereby the region warms faster than the global average, a mechanism examined through the carbon cycle and climate feedbacks studied in Earth's Life Support Systems; imported 'store' food is also far more expensive than in southern Canada, deepening the risk. However, other groups face comparably severe or more immediate threats through a different, human pathway: in the Lake Chad Basin, populations facing desertification, land grabbing and conflict linked to Boko Haram have been displaced in far larger numbers, a pattern of conflict-driven displacement that connects directly to the causes of migration studied in Global Connections, and with less access to any social safety net than Arctic indigenous communities, who at least have some government subsidy support such as Nutrition North Canada. Vulnerability therefore depends on more than cultural or environmental specialism alone: it also depends on whether the threat operates mainly through a slow physical process at a global scale, as in the Arctic, or through a faster human and migratory process at a regional scale, as around Lake Chad, and on how much support or alternative access to food a group has. Indigenous Arctic communities are highly vulnerable in a specific, environmentally dependent way, but not necessarily more vulnerable overall than populations facing conflict, land loss and displacement in the Global South.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Weigh vulnerability in your indigenous case study against vulnerability in a different, contrasting case study.","Connect one case to a physical process operating at a global scale and the other to a human process such as migration, and consider what each group can fall back on.","Your answer should reach a comparative judgement about relative vulnerability, not just describe one group's threats."],"codes":["3.b","3.a"]},{"q":"'The greatest threats to global food security are human, not physical.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? [33]","a":"A full 33-mark synoptic essay defining terms, weighing physical threats (drought, desertification, tectonic hazards, water scarcity) against human threats (land grabbing, conflict, unequal trade, land ownership) using at least two named case studies, with a synoptic link to the wider specification and a qualified conclusion.","sol":"This essay defines 'threat' as anything that reduces food access, availability or use, and judges 'greatest' by asking which category of cause, physical or human, most consistently decides whether a shock becomes a food-security crisis and who suffers from it. It argues that although physical processes frequently trigger food-security crises, human decisions about land, conflict, trade and corporate power are more consistently what decide their severity, so the statement holds to a large extent but not completely.\n\nPhysical processes clearly threaten food security in their own right. In the Lake Chad Basin, reduced and more variable Sahel rainfall, linked to shifts in the tropical rain belt, has combined with rising water abstraction to shrink the lake by approximately 90% since the 1960s, cutting the water available for both farming and fishing around its shores. This is a genuine physical trigger: even without any change in population or governance, a lake shrinking on this scale would reduce the food a fixed number of people could draw from it. Interim judgement: physical change here is a real and non-trivial threat to food security, not simply a backdrop to human causes.\n\nYet in the same basin, human decisions determine how severely that physical change turns into hunger. Rapid population growth has intensified pressure on the remaining land and water, foreign land grabbing has reduced what is available to local farmers, and conflict linked to Boko Haram has displaced millions of people independently of the lake itself, cutting off access to whatever food is still produced or traded. A smaller lake with a stable population and no conflict would strain food security; the same lake with growing population, land grabbing and insurgency produces a humanitarian crisis. Interim judgement: because removing the human pressures would do more to restore food access than removing the physical trigger alone, human factors are the more decisive threat in this case.\n\nThe same pattern holds in Ethiopia, a second and contrasting case study. Physically, much of the country depends on rain-fed farming tied to the kiremt wet season, so unreliable rainfall directly cuts harvests. Humanly, government leases of farmland to foreign investors for commercial crops have reduced smallholders' access to land, while the Tigray conflict (2020-2022) disrupted farming and distribution across the north of the country at the same time as drought. Interim judgement: because drought recurs in Ethiopia without always producing famine, but conflict and land-use change reliably worsen any drought that does occur, human decisions again appear to be the more consistent determinant of severity.\n\nBeyond individual regions, human governance of the food system operates as a persistent threat layered on top of physical shocks, and this connects directly to content studied in Global Connections: the World Trade Organization's rules on agricultural trade, and the purchasing power of TNCs such as Unilever and retailers such as Tesco, all shape which crops are grown, where food goes and who profits, regardless of the physical resource base beneath them. Displacement from crises such as Boko Haram's insurgency around Lake Chad and the Tigray conflict also connects to the drivers of migration studied under Global Connections, showing the same human governance failures operating across local, national and global scales at once, from a single displaced household to international commodity markets. Interim judgement: this global-scale evidence reinforces the pattern seen locally, that human governance choices, not the underlying physical resource base, most often decide whether a shock becomes a crisis.\n\nOverall, physical hazards are frequently the trigger for a food-security crisis, but it is human factors, land ownership, conflict, trade rules and corporate and political power, that most consistently decide its severity and who suffers, across scales from a single community to the global trading system. The statement therefore holds to a large extent, though not completely, since some genuinely physical constraints, such as the fixed water and land resources a region like the Lake Chad Basin depends on, set a limit that no amount of better governance can fully remove.","m":33,"d":3,"hints":["This is a genuine 'to what extent' essay — plan to weigh physical and human threats against each other using real evidence, rather than assuming one side is obviously right.","Use at least two named case studies from this topic, each showing physical and human factors acting together, and connect at least one part of your answer explicitly to another part of the specification and to more than one scale.","Structure your essay with a defining introduction, paragraphs that each reach a mini-judgement, and a conclusion that states how far you agree, not just that you agree."],"codes":["2.a","3.a","3.b","4.a","5.a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What are the three pillars of food security?","a":"Food access, food availability and food use."},{"q":"Name the physical factor and the human factor combining in the Lake Chad Basin's food insecurity.","a":"Physical: a shrinking lake and reduced/variable rainfall (desertification). Human: population growth, land grabbing and conflict such as Boko Haram."},{"q":"Name one food TNC and one food retailer named in this topic.","a":"Unilever (a Trans-National Corporation) and Tesco (a food retailer)."},{"q":"Which theorist argued population pressure drives agricultural innovation, in contrast to Malthus?","a":"Ester Boserup (the Boserupian view)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Food security — three pillars","back":"Food access (can people get/afford food), food availability (is enough produced/imported) and food use (can it be prepared and digested for proper nutrition).","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Global Food Security Index","back":"An index used to compare countries' food security across indicators such as affordability, availability, and quality and safety.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Physical conditions for growing food","back":"Air, climate, soil and water — the physical inputs food production depends on.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Intensive vs extensive, subsistence vs commercial","back":"Two spectrums describing food production methods: how much input per unit of land, and whether output is for the farmer's own use or for sale.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Food miles","back":"The distance food travels from producer to consumer; globalisation has lengthened supply chains and increased food miles and their carbon footprint.","code":"1.c"},{"front":"Malthus vs Boserup","back":"Malthus: population grows faster than food supply, causing crisis. Boserup: population pressure drives the innovation that raises food supply to meet it.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Land grabbing","back":"Foreign or corporate acquisition of farmland, often for export crops, that can displace local farmers and reduce local food access.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Geographical pinchpoint","back":"A narrow point in global food distribution, such as the Suez Canal or the Bosphorus Strait, where disruption can delay shipments worldwide.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Desertification","back":"Land degradation in drylands from physical causes (rainfall variability) and human causes (overgrazing, unsustainable irrigation), reducing food-producing capacity.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Indigenous Arctic food system","back":"Inuit communities rely on hunting and fishing (seal, caribou, Arctic char) timed to sea ice and migration; melting ice threatens this food system.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Salinisation","back":"Salt build-up in irrigated soil without adequate drainage, which eventually reduces crop yields.","code":"4.a"},{"front":"Fair trade organisations","back":"Organisations that guarantee small producers a minimum price and premium, redressing some of the power imbalance between TNCs and small suppliers.","code":"5.a"},{"front":"Capacity building vs system redesign","back":"Points on the food-security strategy spectrum: capacity building supports people to feed themselves; system redesign changes land, trade or production systems altogether.","code":"5.b"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (25–33 marks) on a 33-mark question","items":["Question: \"The greatest threats to global food security are human, not physical.\" To what extent do you agree with this statement? (33 marks)","Political decisions about trade, not physical scarcity, decided who went hungry during the 2022 global food price crisis. India banned wheat exports in May 2022 and Russia's Black Sea blockade disrupted Ukrainian grain shipments after its February 2022 invasion, at a time when global cereal stocks were not exceptionally low by historical standards. Because several major exporters withheld grain from the world market for political rather than agronomic reasons in the same period, prices rose sharply within weeks and hit import-dependent countries such as Egypt hardest, even though that year's physical harvest was not unusually poor. This mirrors the way trade rules and geopolitical decisions, not resource scarcity alone, are shown to shape who benefits from global markets in Global Connections. Because the crisis was triggered by policy choices rather than a harvest failure, this episode is best explained as evidence that human governance of trade, not a physical shortfall, decides who can access food — reinforcing the case that human factors are the more decisive threat."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (9–16 marks) on a 33-mark question","items":["Question: \"The greatest threats to global food security are human, not physical.\" To what extent do you agree with this statement? (33 marks)","Lake Chad has got a lot smaller because of climate change and this has caused problems for food in the area. Population has also grown a lot which makes it worse. There are also human problems like conflict which stop people getting food. This shows that both physical and human factors cause food insecurity in this region, so it is a mixture of the two."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Ethiopia — combined physical and human causes of food insecurity","items":["Ethiopia lies in the Horn of Africa and is landlocked; much of its farming depends on rain from the kiremt wet season (roughly June to September), so a failed kiremt season directly threatens harvests.","Ethiopia's population has grown rapidly, reaching approximately 126-130 million by the mid-2020s, intensifying demand on farmland and water.","The government has leased farmland to foreign and domestic investors for large-scale commercial agriculture, a practice widely described as land grabbing, reducing land available to smallholders.","The Tigray conflict (2020-2022) disrupted farming and food distribution in northern Ethiopia, contributing to a serious food crisis in the region.","Ethiopia experienced a major historic famine in 1984-85 and has continued to need substantial international humanitarian food assistance in the 2020s."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Lake Chad Basin — a dryland at risk","items":["Lake Chad lies at the boundary of Chad, Niger, Nigeria and Cameroon in the Sahel, the semi-arid belt south of the Sahara.","The lake's surface area has shrunk by approximately 90% since the 1960s, driven by reduced and more variable rainfall alongside rising water abstraction for irrigation.","Population around the basin has grown rapidly, increasing pressure on the shrinking supply of farmland, grazing land and water.","Conflict linked to Boko Haram in the region has displaced millions of people since the early 2010s, disrupting farming, fishing and trade routes.","Land grabbing by outside investors has reduced the land available to local farmers and herders around the basin."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Inuit food security in the Canadian Arctic (Nunavut)","items":["Nunavut lies largely above or near the Arctic Circle in northern Canada; its tundra ecosystem and short growing season make crop farming very limited.","Traditional 'country food' comes from hunting and fishing — seal, caribou, Arctic char and whale — timed to sea-ice conditions and seasonal animal migration.","Climate change is reducing Arctic sea-ice extent and duration, altering caribou migration routes and making traditional hunting routes less predictable and more dangerous.","Imported 'store' food in Nunavut is substantially more expensive than in southern Canada, so communities rely heavily on country food and on programmes such as Nutrition North Canada, a federal food subsidy."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the Aral Sea — a physical environment impacted by food production","items":["The Aral Sea lies between Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan in Central Asia and was once one of the world's largest inland seas.","From the 1960s, Soviet-era irrigation schemes diverted its feeder rivers, the Amu Darya and Syr Darya, to grow cotton and other crops.","The sea has shrunk by approximately 90% in area, collapsing the local fishing industry and leaving the once-major fishing port of Moynaq stranded far from the water.","Long-term impacts include salinisation of farmland, wind-blown salt and pesticide dust from the exposed seabed harming health, and a more extreme local climate as the sea's moderating effect on temperature has been lost."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the UK and Tanzania — contrasting impacts of poor food security","items":["In the UK, an AC, food-security problems are more linked to surplus and poor diet: obesity affects approximately one in four adults, and food banks such as those run by the Trussell Trust distribute several million emergency food parcels a year, showing access inequality despite national food abundance.","In Tanzania, an LIDC, most of the population depends on rain-fed subsistence agriculture, making food supply vulnerable to unreliable rainfall.","FAO estimates put undernourishment across parts of East Africa, including Tanzania, in the double digits as a percentage of the population in recent years.","Child stunting, impaired growth from chronic undernutrition, has been estimated at around a third of children under five in parts of Tanzania in recent surveys."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Israel and Ghana — contrasting strategies to improve food security","items":["Israel pioneered modern drip irrigation, commercialised by the company Netafim (founded 1965), delivering water precisely to crop roots in an arid climate.","Israel treats and reuses a very high proportion of its wastewater for agricultural irrigation, among the highest shares of any country, supplementing scarce freshwater.","Ghana's Kuapa Kokoo is a Fairtrade-certified cocoa farmers' cooperative, founded in 1993, representing tens of thousands of smallholder farmers.","The Fairtrade premium Kuapa Kokoo receives has funded community wells, schools and healthcare, and the cooperative holds a share in the chocolate company Divine Chocolate."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan: \"The greatest threats to global food security are human, not physical.\" To what extent do you agree? (33 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'greatest threats' means you must judge relative importance, not just list threats; 'human, not physical' is a claim to test against evidence for both, reaching a qualified position rather than a flat yes or no.","Introduction: define food security using its three pillars, state that both physical and human threats exist, and signal the line of argument — that human factors more often decide how severely a physical trigger becomes a food-security crisis.","Paragraph 1 (the physical case): use the Lake Chad Basin to show a genuine physical trigger — approximately 90% shrinkage of the lake since the 1960s, driven by rainfall change and abstraction — reaching a mini-judgement that physical change is a real, not trivial, threat.","Paragraph 2 (human factors compounding the same case): in the same basin, show population growth, land grabbing and Boko Haram-linked conflict deciding who actually loses access to food, reaching a mini-judgement that human factors determine the severity of the physical trigger.","Paragraph 3 (a second case study, Ethiopia): show drought combining with land leased to investors and conflict such as the Tigray conflict, reinforcing the pattern that human decisions about land and security shape the outcome as much as, or more than, the physical trigger.","Paragraph 4 (synoptic link beyond this topic): bring in global governance of the food system, WTO trade rules, and the purchasing power of TNCs such as Unilever and retailers such as Tesco, as a further human threat layered on top of physical shocks, and explicitly connect this, and the conflict-driven displacement around Lake Chad and Tigray, to content on trade governance and migration studied in Global Connections.","Evaluation thread running through every paragraph: in each case, ask what would happen to food security if only the physical element were removed, and what would happen if only the human element were removed, to justify which matters more.","Conclusion: state directly how far you agree — human factors most consistently decide severity and who suffers — but qualify it by acknowledging that fixed physical constraints, such as available land and water, still set a limit no governance can fully remove."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.944Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"All nine faults in the first verdict are genuinely fixed — tariffs are now 3/6/12/33 only, the 'Discuss' stems are replaced by 'To what extent'/'How far do you agree', the three 12-mark solutions cross into Landscape Systems, Earth's Life Support Systems, Disease Dilemmas and Global Connections across local-to-global scale, lesson.why states the 3-mark short answer correctly, room.questions[13].sol is now a structured essay with a defining introduction, interim judgements and a qualified conclusion, room.questions[3].hints[1] no longer enumerates the four organisation types, room.questions[8].sol gives exactly two impacts, and the strong model paragraph (2022 price crisis, India's May 2022 wheat ban, Black Sea disruption, Egypt) is independent of the essay answer key; every answer key re-solved correctly (3,440 − 2,110 = 1,330 kcal, 63.0% higher; 9.2 − 8.0 = 1.2 pp, a ~15% relative rise) and the six required fact files are exactly Ethiopia, the Lake Chad Basin/Sahel, the Inuit of Nunavut, the Aral Sea, the UK and Tanzania, and Israel and Kuapa Kokoo with figures true or properly hedged (735 million undernourished 2022, Lake Chad and Aral Sea ~90%, Netafim 1965, Kuapa Kokoo 1993, Tanzanian stunting ~a third, Trussell Trust parcels, Nutrition North Canada); minor polish only — the five 3-mark stems omit the '[3]' tariff that every other stem carries, room.questions[1].q and [4].q refer to what 'the specification lists' rather than reading as an exam question, room.questions[5].a insists on the percentage when an examiner would also credit the 1,330 kcal absolute gap, room.questions[7].hints[2] restates the sol's closing point about reliability over time, the Aral Sea fact file separates long-term impacts but never labels the short-term ones the 4.a case study asks for, and the essay plan covers the same question and evidence as room.questions[13] so adds structure rather than new material."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:49:50.411Z"}},"3.5":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"3.5","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Geographical debates (Component 03) sets short 3-mark items, 6-mark Explain items, 12-mark synoptic items and 33-mark essays on Hazardous Earth, always alongside a Resource Booklet. The 12-mark synoptic items deliberately connect tectonic hazards to physical and human content elsewhere in the specification — the carbon cycle, disease, migration, development — and to more than one scale, while the 33-mark essays reward a sustained, evidence-weighed argument built on named case studies of countries at contrasting levels of economic development. This room builds the physical mechanisms (plate tectonics, volcanic and seismic hazards) and the human debate (why people live at risk, the impacts they experience, and the strategies used to manage the event, vulnerability and losses) you need to argue at the top level.","idea":[{"code":"1.a","h":"Evidence for continental drift and plate tectonics","t":"Wegener's continental drift was dismissed until a plausible mechanism and supporting evidence emerged. The Earth's structure supplies the mechanism: the rigid lithosphere (crust and uppermost mantle) is broken into plates that move over the semi-molten asthenosphere, driven by convection currents in the mantle below. Independent evidence for that movement includes sea-floor spreading (new crust forming at ridges), paleomagnetism (symmetrical, reversing magnetic striping either side of a ridge) and the age of sea-floor rock increasing with distance from the ridge; the original evidence for a single supercontinent included matching ancient glaciations (striations and tillite deposits that align once continents are reassembled) and fossil records (species such as Mesosaurus and Glossopteris found on continents now oceans apart). A top-level answer names specific types of evidence rather than asserting 'the plates moved', and uses the lithosphere/asthenosphere structure to explain why they move at all."},{"code":"1.b","h":"Features and processes at plate boundaries","t":"The global pattern of plates and boundaries sets the stage for every hazard in this topic. At divergent (constructive) boundaries, plates move apart, magma rises to fill the gap, and features such as mid-ocean ridges and rift valleys form. At convergent boundaries the outcome depends on the crust involved: oceanic-continental convergence subducts the denser oceanic plate beneath the continental plate, forming a deep ocean trench, fold mountains and a volcanic arc; oceanic-oceanic (destructive) convergence subducts one oceanic plate beneath the other, forming an island arc; continental-continental (collision) convergence subducts neither plate, since both are too buoyant, and instead crumples the crust upward into fold mountains such as the Himalayas. At conservative boundaries plates slide past each other with no crust created or destroyed, building stress that releases as earthquakes. A top-level answer matches the correct boundary type to the correct hazard outcome rather than treating 'plate boundary' as one category."},{"code":"2.a","h":"Volcanic activity, landforms and the VEI","t":"Volcano type follows from magma viscosity and setting. At convergent (destructive) boundaries, subducted oceanic crust and sediment produce viscous, gas-rich, silica-rich magma that erupts explosively, building steep-sided composite (strato-) volcanoes. At divergent (constructive) boundaries, magma is less viscous, lower in silica and gas, so eruptions are effusive, building gently-sloping shield volcanoes and fissure flows. A third setting, hot spots, occurs away from any plate boundary where a stationary mantle plume melts through the moving plate above it, producing linear volcanic chains such as the Hawaiian Islands or extensional volcanism such as the East African Rift Valley. The Volcanic Explosivity Index (VEI) measures eruption size on a scale from 0 to 8, each step representing roughly a tenfold increase in the volume of material ejected, so it lets contrasting eruptions be compared on one scale. A top-level answer explains why a setting produces a particular magma type, rather than describing the volcano's shape in isolation."},{"code":"2.b","h":"The hazards volcanic eruptions generate","t":"A single eruption can generate several distinct hazards, and an answer should name the mechanism behind each rather than list them. Lava flows are slow-moving but destroy everything in their path; pyroclastic flows are fast-moving, superheated currents of gas, ash and rock that are the most lethal volcanic hazard; gas emissions (sulphur dioxide, carbon dioxide) can poison the air and, at a larger scale, cool the climate; tephra and ash disrupt agriculture, respiration and aviation. Lahars are volcanic mudflows formed when eruption material mixes with melting ice, snow or heavy rainfall, and can travel far beyond the volcano itself; melting ice can also cause flooding (a jökulhlaup). Explosive eruptions can trigger tsunamis, through flank collapse, pyroclastic flow entering the sea, or caldera collapse. A top-level answer links the hazard to the type of eruption that produces it, and to the exposure of the population nearby."},{"code":"3.a","h":"Earthquake characteristics and their effect on landscapes","t":"Earthquakes vary by focus depth and are measured on several scales. Shallow-focus earthquakes (0–70 km) occur at all boundary types and, being closer to the surface, tend to cause more severe surface damage; deep-focus earthquakes (down to about 700 km) occur only within subducting slabs at convergent boundaries. Magnitude is measured on the moment magnitude scale (Mw, which has largely replaced the Richter scale because it stays accurate for the largest events), a logarithmic scale where each whole number represents roughly ten times the ground movement and about 31.6 times the energy released; the modified Mercalli intensity scale instead measures the observed effects on people, buildings and the ground at a given location, so it varies with distance from the epicentre even for one earthquake. Over time, repeated movement at a boundary can produce escarpments (steep faces along a fault) and rift valleys (a block of crust that has dropped between parallel faults). A top-level answer picks the measure that fits the point being made — magnitude for comparing events, intensity for comparing impact within one event."},{"code":"3.b","h":"The hazards earthquakes generate","t":"An earthquake's hazards follow a chain from the initial rupture. Ground shaking and ground displacement are the direct effects of seismic waves and fault movement, and are usually the first cause of building and infrastructure failure. Liquefaction occurs where shaking causes water-saturated, loosely-packed sediment to behave temporarily like a liquid, so buildings can sink, tilt or collapse even away from the epicentre. Shaking on steep or saturated slopes triggers landslides and avalanches, which can dam rivers or bury settlements. Tsunamis form where the sea bed is displaced vertically — typically by rupture at an oceanic-continental or oceanic-oceanic convergent boundary — or by an underwater landslide triggered by shaking, sending a wave that grows in height as it reaches shallow water. Flooding can follow from a tsunami, from a landslide-dammed river bursting, or from damage to flood defences and water infrastructure. A top-level answer explains the mechanism connecting the earthquake to the named secondary hazard, not just that it 'caused' it."},{"code":"4.a","h":"Living with volcanic risk: reasons and impacts","t":"People choose to live near volcanoes despite the risk because the same processes that make them dangerous also make them valuable: volcanic soil weathers into some of the most fertile farmland on Earth, geothermal energy and tourism bring income, and eruptions are often infrequent relative to a human lifetime, so perceived risk is discounted against the certain, everyday benefit of the location. The impacts of an eruption then depend on the country's level of economic development as much as on the eruption itself: an AC with monitoring, evacuation planning and insurance can absorb an eruption as a large economic disruption, while an EDC or LIDC with denser rural populations near the volcano, weaker monitoring capacity and less insurance cover can see the same-sized eruption cause far greater loss of life and a larger relative hit to national income. Political impacts range from criticism of a government's evacuation and response, to the closure of contested land, to shifts in aid dependency. A top-level answer sets a named AC case study against a named EDC or LIDC case study directly, rather than describing each eruption in isolation."},{"code":"4.b","h":"Living with seismic risk: reasons and impacts","t":"Reasons for living in earthquake-prone locations mirror those for volcanoes — fertile land, coastal and resource access, established cities and economies that are costly to relocate — combined with the fact that a specific, damaging earthquake is infrequent and hard to predict, so risk is discounted against everyday opportunity. The impacts of a given earthquake again depend heavily on the country's level of economic development: an AC with enforced seismic building codes, early-warning systems and emergency capacity typically records a much lower death toll and a faster economic recovery than an EDC or LIDC facing an earthquake of similar or even lower magnitude, where poorer construction, denser informal settlement and weaker emergency services raise both the death toll and the share of national income lost. Economic impacts include rebuilding cost and lost output; environmental impacts include landslides, liquefaction and damaged infrastructure; political impacts include scrutiny of building regulation enforcement and the speed and fairness of the government's response. A top-level answer explains why magnitude alone does not predict the death toll, using two contrasting named countries as evidence."},{"code":"5.a","h":"Managing volcanic hazards","t":"Strategies to cope with volcanic hazards fall into three types, and a top-level answer names which type each strategy is. Mitigating against the event tries to control or redirect the hazard itself, such as lava diversion channels or barriers, or spraying water on an advancing flow to cool and slow it. Mitigating against vulnerability reduces exposure before an eruption happens, through hazard mapping and exclusion zones, monitoring (seismometers, gas sensors, ground-deformation surveys) that supports early warning, and community preparedness such as evacuation drills and education. Mitigating against losses acts once an eruption has struck, through rescue and emergency relief, temporary shelter, and insurance or international aid that spreads the financial cost. Which strategies are affordable and enforceable again varies with a country's level of economic development, so an AC and an EDC or LIDC facing a comparable eruption can rely on very different combinations of the three. A top-level answer evaluates which type of strategy did the most to reduce harm in a named case, rather than listing all three equally."},{"code":"5.b","h":"Managing seismic hazards","t":"Strategies to cope with earthquake hazards also split into the same three types. Mitigating against the event is largely impossible for earthquakes (they cannot be stopped or redirected), so land-use zoning that keeps critical infrastructure and dense settlement away from active faults and liquefaction-prone ground is the nearest equivalent, alongside tsunami sea walls. Mitigating against vulnerability centres on building design — seismic-resistant features such as base isolation, cross-bracing and flexible materials — enforced through building codes, plus early-warning systems that give seconds to minutes of notice, and public drills. Mitigating against losses includes insurance, emergency response planning and international aid that spreads the financial and human cost after the event. Enforcement and affordability again track economic development: strict codes only reduce deaths where they are actually enforced, which is harder in an EDC or LIDC with widespread informal construction. A top-level answer explains why the same category of strategy (for example building design) can succeed in one named country and fail in another."},{"code":"5.c","h":"How risk and resilience change over time","t":"Exposure to tectonic hazards is not fixed: population growth and urbanisation put more people in hazardous locations even where hazard frequency itself is unchanged, while better monitoring, forecasting and construction can reduce the impact of a given event over time. The disaster risk equation formalises this: risk is a function of the hazard itself, the vulnerability of the population exposed to it, and their capacity to cope, so risk can be reduced either by making the event less severe (rarely possible), reducing vulnerability, or raising capacity to cope — even without changing the hazard. The Park model (the disaster-response curve) describes how quality of life or level of functioning falls sharply at the moment of a disaster, then recovers through relief, rehabilitation and reconstruction phases; a resilient, well-prepared place has a shallower drop and a faster return to (or above) its pre-disaster level, while a vulnerable place drops further and recovers more slowly, if at all. A top-level answer uses the equation or the model to explain a named trend, not just to define them."}],"examples":[{"title":"Comparing seismic energy release: Tōhoku (2011) and Haiti (2010)","setup":"The moment magnitude scale is logarithmic: the energy released rises by roughly 31.6 times for every whole number of magnitude. The Tōhoku earthquake (Japan, 2011) measured approximately 9.0 Mw and killed approximately 15,900 people; the Haiti earthquake (2010) measured approximately 7.0 Mw and killed an estimated 100,000 to 316,000. Work out how many times more seismic energy Tōhoku released, then say what the comparison shows about what makes an earthquake deadly.","steps":["The magnitude difference between the two events is 9.0 − 7.0 = 2.0 whole numbers of magnitude.","Each whole number is worth a factor of 31.6, so two whole numbers means the energy ratio is 31.6 × 31.6, i.e. 31.6².","31.6² ≈ 1,000, so the Tōhoku rupture released roughly 1,000 times more seismic energy than the Haiti rupture.","The death tolls run the opposite way: Haiti's estimated 100,000 to 316,000 dead is many times Tōhoku's approximately 15,900, most of whom were drowned by the tsunami rather than crushed by shaking.","So Tōhoku released about 1,000 times the energy and still killed far fewer people: magnitude sets the size of the shaking, but vulnerability — building quality, warning systems and capacity to cope — decides how deadly an earthquake turns out to be."],"cues":["What is the gap between the two magnitudes?","How many times must you apply the 31.6 factor?","What does that multiplication come to, to the nearest thousand?","Which country lost more lives, and to which hazard?","If energy does not decide the death toll, what does?"]},{"title":"Why magnitude did not predict the death toll: Chile (2010) and Haiti (2010)","setup":"Chile's 2010 Maule earthquake measured approximately 8.8 Mw, with a focus approximately 35 km deep offshore, and killed approximately 525 people. Haiti's 2010 earthquake measured approximately 7.0 Mw, with a focus only approximately 13 km deep beside Port-au-Prince, and killed an estimated 100,000 to 316,000. Explain why the far weaker earthquake caused so many more deaths.","steps":["Chile has enforced seismic building codes since the 1960 Valdivia earthquake, so most blocks of flats in Concepción and Santiago swayed and stood, whereas Port-au-Prince was built largely of unreinforced concrete-block and poorly tied frames that pancaked within seconds.","Location multiplied Haiti's shaking: a shallow focus immediately beside a crowded capital put the worst ground motion directly under the buildings and people, while Chile's deeper, offshore focus spread its energy through more rock and sea before it reached the main settlements.","Capacity to cope then widened the gap: Chile's own army, hospitals and emergency services were functioning and mobilised within hours, whereas in Haiti the ministries, the port and much of the hospital stock had themselves collapsed, so search-and-rescue waited on foreign teams arriving through one damaged airport.","So the magnitude difference did not decide the outcome — the weaker earthquake was far deadlier because vulnerable buildings and a shallow focus beneath the capital magnified the hazard while low capacity to cope slowed the response, exactly as Risk = Hazard × Vulnerability ÷ Capacity to cope predicts."],"cues":["How did the two countries' buildings differ, and since when?","Why did a shallower, closer focus matter so much here?","Which country could run its own rescue, and why?","Which terms of the disaster risk equation explain the gap?"]},{"title":"Tracing the process chain from subduction to tsunami","setup":"A tsunami is generated at a subduction zone — an oceanic-continental or oceanic-oceanic convergent plate boundary. Explain the chain of processes, step by step, from the plates converging to the wave striking the coast.","steps":["The denser oceanic plate is forced down beneath the other plate along the subduction interface, descending at a few centimetres a year.","Friction locks that interface between earthquakes, so the leading edge of the overriding plate is dragged down with it and elastic strain builds up over decades or centuries.","When the locked patch finally fails, the overriding plate springs back up: the sea bed is displaced vertically and lifts the whole column of water above it.","That raised column collapses under gravity and spreads outward as a tsunami — in deep water only a low swell, but with an enormous wavelength, crossing the ocean at the speed of a jet aircraft.","In shallow coastal water friction slows the front, the wavelength shortens and the energy is forced upwards into a steep, destructive run-up: subduction → locked interface → sudden slip → sea-bed displacement → ocean-crossing wave → shoaling at the coast."],"cues":["Which plate goes down, and why that one?","What accumulates while the interface stays locked?","What happens to the sea bed the moment it slips?","What does the wave look like out in deep water?","Why does the wave grow as it nears the coast?"]},{"title":"Reading the evidence for continental drift in the rock and fossil record","setup":"Three lines of evidence are offered for continental drift: fossils of the reptile Mesosaurus, ancient glacial deposits, and the rocks of the sea floor. Explain what each reveals, and state the reconstruction they point to.","steps":["Mesosaurus fossils occur in matching Permian rock layers in eastern South America and southern Africa, and nowhere else, although the Atlantic Ocean now lies between those outcrops.","Mesosaurus was a small freshwater swimmer, far too weak to cross an ocean, and the beds containing it match bed for bed, so the two areas must once have been a single lake-and-river basin.","Glacial striations and tillite of the same late Palaeozoic age occur across South America, southern Africa, India and Australia, and the striation directions radiate from one centre — which only makes sense if those land masses were joined together near the South Pole.","The sea floor adds the mechanism: paleomagnetic striping is symmetrical either side of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the rock is youngest at the ridge and oldest at the continental margins, nowhere older than about 200 million years, so the Atlantic has been widening by sea-floor spreading.","Three independent records therefore agree: the southern land masses were once joined as Gondwana within the supercontinent Pangaea, and have since drifted apart as new ocean floor formed between them."],"cues":["Where do Mesosaurus fossils turn up, and what separates those places now?","Why could the animal not simply have swum across?","What glacial evidence appears on four continents at once?","What does the sea floor's magnetism and age add?","Which single reconstruction fits all three records?"]},{"title":"Why Eyjafjallajökull (2010) and Merapi (2010) produced very different hazards","setup":"In April 2010 Iceland's Eyjafjallajökull erupted beneath an ice cap at a divergent boundary setting, and in October–November 2010 Indonesia's Merapi, on a convergent plate boundary, erupted with far more viscous magma onto densely settled slopes. Explain why the two eruptions produced such different hazards and impacts.","steps":["At Eyjafjallajökull the rising magma met glacial meltwater, which chilled and shattered it into unusually fine, hard, glass-rich ash that the eruption column lifted into the jet stream and carried across Europe.","Because such ash melts inside jet engines, regulators closed European airspace for about six days, cancelling roughly 100,000 flights and stranding millions of passengers — an enormous economic cost thousands of kilometres from Iceland, but no direct deaths.","At Merapi the viscous, gas-rich andesitic magma of a convergent boundary could not degas quietly, so the eruption was explosive and sent pyroclastic flows — hot gas, ash and rock at hundreds of kilometres per hour — down the populated southern flank.","Those flows and the ash they carried killed an estimated 350 or more people and forced around 350,000 to evacuate, even though warnings and exclusion zones had been issued days beforehand.","So magma viscosity and setting decided the hazard: ice-contact fragmentation at Eyjafjallajökull spread a costly but non-lethal ash cloud over a continent, while Merapi's viscous convergent-boundary magma killed people within kilometres of the vent — the costliest hazard and the deadliest hazard need not be the same one."],"cues":["What did magma meeting meltwater do to the ash?","Why does that ash matter to aviation, and at what cost?","What does viscous, gas-rich magma produce instead?","What did those flows cost in lives and evacuations?","What decided the difference between the two eruptions?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Name a type of plate boundary where earthquakes occur but volcanoes typically do not.","a":"A conservative (transform) boundary — plates slide past each other with no magma generated, but built-up stress still releases as an earthquake."},{"q":"What is the key difference between a shallow-focus and a deep-focus earthquake?","a":"Shallow-focus earthquakes occur at depths of roughly 0–70 km and can occur at any boundary type; deep-focus earthquakes occur down to about 700 km and only within subducting slabs at convergent boundaries."},{"q":"Why does the modified Mercalli intensity scale give a different reading for the same earthquake in two different towns?","a":"Mercalli measures the observed effects on people, buildings and the ground at a specific location, so it falls with distance from the epicentre and varies with local ground conditions, unlike magnitude, which is a single value for the whole earthquake."},{"q":"What does the disaster risk equation say risk depends on, besides the hazard itself?","a":"The vulnerability of the population exposed to the hazard and their capacity to cope with it."}]},"room":{"facts":["The disaster risk equation: Risk = (Hazard × Vulnerability) ÷ Capacity to cope.","The moment magnitude scale (Mw) is logarithmic: each whole-number increase represents about ten times the ground movement (amplitude) and roughly 31.6 times more energy released.","The Volcanic Explosivity Index (VEI) runs from 0 (non-explosive) to 8 (mega-colossal); each whole step represents roughly a tenfold increase in the volume of material ejected.","Continental crust is thicker (roughly 30–70 km) but less dense (approximately 2.7 g/cm³) than oceanic crust (roughly 5–10 km thick, approximately 3.0 g/cm³) — this density contrast is why oceanic crust subducts beneath continental crust at a convergent boundary.","Shallow-focus earthquakes occur at depths of roughly 0–70 km; deep-focus earthquakes occur at depths down to about 700 km, and only within subducting slabs at convergent boundaries."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify two independent types of evidence, other than the shape of the continents, that support the theory of continental drift.","a":"Any two of: matching fossil records across continents (e.g. Mesosaurus, Glossopteris); matching evidence from ancient glaciations (striations/tillite); paleomagnetic striping of the sea floor; the age of sea-floor rock increasing with distance from a ridge.","sol":"Two creditable types of evidence are: (1) fossil records — species such as Mesosaurus (a freshwater reptile) and Glossopteris (a fern) are found in matching rock layers on continents now separated by ocean, which is only explicable if those continents were once joined; and (2) evidence from ancient glaciations — glacial striations and tillite deposits of the same age appear across South America, southern Africa, India and Australia in directions that only align once those land masses are reassembled near the South Pole. (Paleomagnetic striping and the age of sea-floor rock either side of a mid-ocean ridge would also be credited.)","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["1.a"],"hints":["Think about evidence that comes from rocks and living things, not just the outline of the coastlines.","Two different disciplines supply independent evidence for the same idea — consider what each one records.","Give two named types of evidence, each in one sentence."]},{"q":"Explain how a crustal feature forms where two oceanic plates converge.","a":"An island arc forms, with a deep ocean trench where the denser plate subducts.","sol":"Where two oceanic plates converge, the older, denser plate is subducted beneath the other. This subduction forms a deep ocean trench at the boundary, and as the subducted plate melts at depth, rising magma produces a curved chain of volcanic islands on the overriding plate — an island arc, such as the Mariana Islands or Tonga.","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["1.b"],"hints":["Think about what happens when two plates of similarly high density meet.","One of the two plates has to give way — consider which, and what happens to it.","Give two features in order: what forms directly at the boundary itself, then what forms further away as a result of it."]},{"q":"Explain why eruptions at divergent (constructive) plate boundaries are typically effusive rather than explosive.","a":"Magma at divergent boundaries is lower in silica and gas content, so it is less viscous and gas escapes easily rather than building up pressure.","sol":"Magma generated at divergent boundaries comes directly from the mantle, without melting subducted crust, so it is basaltic: low in silica and dissolved gas. This makes it low-viscosity, so it flows readily and gas escapes gradually rather than building up pressure. As a result, eruptions are gentle and effusive, producing lava flows rather than violent explosions.","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["2.a"],"hints":["Compare the source of the magma at this boundary with the source at a convergent boundary.","Think about what the magma's silica and gas content does to how easily it flows.","Answer in one cause-and-effect sentence: composition → viscosity → eruption style."]},{"q":"Identify two scales used to measure earthquakes, and state what each one measures.","a":"Any two of: the moment magnitude scale (energy released); the Richter scale (energy released, an earlier measure); the modified Mercalli intensity scale (observed effects at a location).","sol":"The moment magnitude scale (Mw) measures the energy released at the earthquake's source, giving a single logarithmic value for the whole event. The modified Mercalli intensity scale instead measures the observed effects on people, buildings and the ground at a specific location, so its value can differ across places affected by the same earthquake. (The Richter scale, an earlier magnitude measure, would also be credited.)","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["3.a"],"hints":["There is more than one recognised scale for measuring earthquakes — think about what you have already met when comparing the size of different earthquakes.","Consider that a single earthquake could be reported using more than one number, each capturing something different about it.","Name each scale, then say in a few words what it measures."]},{"q":"Explain how a lahar forms.","a":"Volcanic material (ash and debris) mixes with melting ice/snow or heavy rain to form a fast-moving mudflow down the volcano's slopes.","sol":"A lahar forms when loose volcanic material — ash, pumice and rock debris — mixes with water, most often from rapidly melting snow or ice on the volcano's summit during an eruption, or from heavy rainfall. This produces a dense, fast-moving flow of mud and debris that travels down river valleys on the volcano's flanks, often reaching much further than the eruption itself.","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["2.b"],"hints":["Think about what has to combine with erupted material for this hazard to form.","Consider where the water involved usually comes from on an active volcano.","Describe it as a two-step process: material plus water source, then the resulting flow."]},{"q":"Explain how an earthquake can generate a tsunami.","a":"Vertical displacement of the sea bed (directly, or via an underwater landslide triggered by shaking) displaces the water column above it, producing a wave that grows as it reaches shallow water.","sol":"Most tsunamis form at oceanic-continental or oceanic-oceanic convergent boundaries, where the subducting plate is locked against the overriding plate between earthquakes. When the interface finally slips, the sea bed above it is displaced vertically — either lifted or dropped — over a wide area. This sudden vertical movement displaces the entire water column above it, generating a wave, or series of waves, that spreads outward from the source. A tsunami can also form without direct sea-bed rupture, where shaking causes an underwater landslide, which displaces water in the same way. In deep water the wave travels fast but is low in height and hard to detect; as it reaches shallow coastal water it slows and the wave energy compresses into a much greater wave height, which is what causes damage on reaching the coast.","m":6,"d":2,"codes":["3.b"],"hints":["Think about what has to move for a very large volume of water to be displaced.","Consider two different ways that shaking near the sea bed can move that water — one direct, one indirect.","Structure your answer as a chain: what moves at the sea bed, what that does to the water above it, then what happens as the wave nears the coast."]},{"q":"Explain how convection currents in the mantle are thought to drive the movement of tectonic plates.","a":"Heat from the core causes mantle material to rise, move laterally beneath the lithosphere, cool and sink, dragging the rigid plates above it apart or together.","sol":"Heat from the Earth's core and from radioactive decay in the mantle warms mantle material near the core-mantle boundary, making it less dense so it rises slowly through the asthenosphere. On nearing the base of the lithosphere it spreads laterally, dragging the rigid plates above it in the direction of flow. As it moves away from the heat source it cools, becomes denser, and sinks back down elsewhere, completing a convection cell. Where currents diverge beneath the lithosphere, plates are pulled apart, forming divergent boundaries; where currents converge, plates are pushed together, forming convergent boundaries. This mechanism is what finally explained, decades after continental drift was first proposed, how continents could plausibly move.","m":6,"d":2,"codes":["1.a","1.b"],"hints":["Think about what happens to material when it is heated near a very hot core and then moves further away from it.","Consider what a rising-and-sinking cell of material beneath a rigid, floating layer would do to that layer.","Trace the chain: heating, rising, lateral flow, then cooling and sinking, and link each stage to plate movement."]},{"q":"Table 1 shows two earthquakes of contrasting magnitude.\nCountry | Year | Magnitude (Mw) | Estimated deaths\nJapan (Tōhoku) | 2011 | 9.0 | approximately 18,000\nHaiti | 2010 | 7.0 | about 100,000–316,000 (estimates vary by source)\nUsing Table 1, identify how many times greater the estimated death toll in Haiti was than in Japan, using the midpoint of Haiti's range, showing your working and giving your answer to one decimal place.","a":"Midpoint of Haiti's range = 208,000. 208,000 ÷ 18,000 ≈ 11.6 times greater.","sol":"Haiti's death toll is given as a range because estimates vary by source, so a single working figure is needed first: the midpoint is (100,000 + 316,000) ÷ 2 = 208,000. Dividing this by Japan's estimated deaths, 208,000 ÷ 18,000 = 11.555... ≈ 11.6. Using the midpoint of the commonly cited range, the estimated death toll in Haiti's 2010 earthquake was therefore approximately 11.6 times greater than in Japan's 2011 earthquake, despite Japan's earthquake being far stronger in magnitude.","m":3,"d":2,"codes":["4.b"],"hints":["Look at what each of the two death-toll figures in the table represents, and what kind of comparison 'how many times greater' needs.","This is a ratio between two same-type figures, not a difference between them.","Show the division, then round your answer to one decimal place, with the units 'times'."]},{"q":"Explain why Haiti's 2010 earthquake caused a much higher death toll than Japan's 2011 earthquake, despite recording a lower magnitude.","a":"Haiti's greater vulnerability (weak/unenforced building codes, dense informal settlement, weaker emergency capacity) and lower capacity to cope outweighed Japan's higher magnitude.","sol":"Magnitude measures the energy released, not the harm caused, so the death toll depends far more on vulnerability and capacity to cope. Japan is an economically advanced country with earthquake-resistant building codes strictly enforced since the 1980s, an established tsunami warning system, and well-funded emergency services, so even a magnitude 9.0 earthquake and the tsunami it triggered killed relatively few people directly from ground shaking. Haiti, by contrast, is a low-income country with widespread informal, unreinforced concrete construction, very limited building-code enforcement, a densely populated capital (Port-au-Prince) near the epicentre, and a state with limited emergency response capacity even before the earthquake. The same shaking that a well-built structure would survive caused widespread building collapse in Haiti, and the emergency response was slower and less resourced. The comparison shows that vulnerability and capacity to cope, not the size of the hazard, are usually the larger determinants of an earthquake's death toll.","m":6,"d":2,"codes":["4.b"],"hints":["Magnitude tells you about the size of the hazard — think about what else determines how many people are harmed by it.","Consider what differs between the two countries in how buildings are constructed, regulated and responded to.","Contrast the two countries directly on vulnerability and capacity to cope, not just on the earthquake itself."]},{"q":"Explain how community preparedness can reduce the impact of a volcanic eruption.","a":"Preparedness (evacuation planning, drills, education, hazard maps) reduces vulnerability by helping people recognise warning signs and evacuate quickly, reducing harm from delay or non-compliance.","sol":"Community preparedness mitigates against vulnerability rather than the eruption itself. Hazard mapping identifies which areas are most at risk from lava flows, pyroclastic flows or lahars, and this is combined with education so residents understand local warning signs (such as ground swelling, gas smell or increased seismic activity) and know their evacuation route in advance. Regular evacuation drills mean that when an official warning is issued, people can move to safety quickly and with less panic, and are less likely to ignore or delay acting on a warning. Preparedness also builds trust between authorities and communities, which matters because a poorly-prepared population is more likely to resist evacuation orders, and ignoring an order can itself cost lives. Well-prepared communities near an active volcano can therefore evacuate before an eruption's most lethal hazards, such as pyroclastic flows, arrive, even though the eruption itself cannot be prevented.","m":6,"d":2,"codes":["5.a"],"hints":["Preparedness cannot stop an eruption — think about what it changes instead.","Consider what a community needs to know and practise in advance for an evacuation to work when it matters.","Explain the chain: preparation leads to a faster, more compliant response, which leads to fewer people in the hazard's path when it strikes."]},{"q":"Explain how building design can reduce the risk earthquakes pose to people.","a":"Seismic-resistant design (base isolation, flexible/reinforced materials, cross-bracing) absorbs or dissipates shaking so structures deform rather than collapse, reducing deaths from building failure.","sol":"Building design mitigates against vulnerability by changing how a structure responds to ground shaking, rather than reducing the shaking itself. Seismic-resistant features include base isolation (flexible bearings between a building and its foundation that absorb ground movement before it reaches the structure), cross-bracing and reinforced concrete or steel frames that allow a building to flex and dissipate energy rather than fracture, and simple, symmetrical structural shapes that distribute stress evenly. Where these features are required by building codes and the codes are actually enforced, as in Japan, buildings can survive shaking that would otherwise cause collapse, which is the largest single cause of earthquake deaths. Design alone is not sufficient, however: it only reduces risk where codes are enforced across all construction, including informal building, which is harder to guarantee in lower-income countries.","m":6,"d":2,"codes":["5.b"],"hints":["Design does not stop the ground shaking — think about what it changes about the building instead.","Consider named structural features that let a building move without breaking.","Explain the chain: design feature, then how the building responds to shaking, then reduced risk of collapse, and note one limit."]},{"q":"Using a named example, evaluate the significance of volcanic activity within the carbon cycle, and the human responses it triggers at contrasting scales.","a":"Volcanic outgassing is a genuine but comparatively small store-to-atmosphere flux in the carbon cycle; the larger significance is at the human/economic scale (e.g. Eyjafjallajökull's ash disrupting European aviation, itself a carbon-emitting sector), so significance depends heavily on the scale considered.","sol":"Volcanic activity contributes to the carbon cycle through outgassing: magma rising at plate boundaries and hot spots releases carbon dioxide stored in the mantle and subducted crust directly into the atmosphere, a slow but continuous geological flux operating over thousands to millions of years. At the global scale, this flux is small relative to human fossil-fuel combustion, so on its own volcanic outgassing is not a significant driver of short-term atmospheric carbon change. Its significance is far greater, however, when a single large eruption is considered at a different scale. Eyjafjallajökull's 2010 eruption in Iceland, while volcanically modest (VEI approximately 4), produced a fine ash cloud that closed most of European airspace for about six days, grounding an estimated 100,000 flights. Because aviation is itself a significant source of anthropogenic carbon emissions, this grounding produced a measurable, if temporary and localised, reduction in emissions over Europe, illustrating that the eruption's carbon-cycle significance came less from its own outgassing and more from the human systems it disrupted. Overall, volcanic activity is a minor direct flux in the long-run global carbon cycle but can be locally and temporarily significant through its disruption of high-carbon human activity, so its significance depends heavily on the scale and timeframe being judged.","m":12,"d":3,"codes":["2.a","2.b"],"hints":["Think about volcanic activity as one of several natural fluxes in the carbon cycle, and consider what it disrupts beyond the atmosphere directly.","Consider two very different scales at which a single eruption could matter: the geological carbon budget, and a human sector that itself emits carbon.","Weigh the eruption's direct contribution to the carbon cycle against its indirect effect through disrupting a human system, and reach a judgement on which is more significant."]},{"q":"Using a named example, evaluate the extent to which the impacts of a seismic hazard event extend beyond the immediate physical damage it causes.","a":"The immediate physical damage (e.g. Haiti 2010) was compounded by a longer-running secondary impact — a major cholera outbreak caused by damaged water/sanitation infrastructure and displacement — showing impacts extending in time and scale beyond the event itself.","sol":"The immediate physical damage of an earthquake is usually the most visible impact, but it is rarely the whole story. Haiti's 2010 earthquake (Mw approximately 7.0) killed an estimated 100,000 to 316,000 people directly, through building collapse in a densely populated capital with widespread unreinforced construction. Beyond this immediate toll, however, the earthquake severely damaged water supply and sanitation infrastructure and displaced an estimated 1.5 million people into crowded temporary camps with poor sanitation. Ten months later, a cholera outbreak began, able to spread rapidly because the earthquake had already destroyed the infrastructure and concentrated the population needed to contain it, and went on to kill thousands more over the following years, considerably longer than the earthquake itself lasted. At the same time, the earthquake triggered a large international humanitarian response, which brought its own political and economic impacts, including long-running debate over how effectively aid was distributed. This shows that the impacts of a seismic hazard event extend well beyond its physical damage, in both time (a multi-year disease outbreak) and scale (national public health and international aid), largely because the event itself damages the very systems that would otherwise limit its knock-on effects.","m":12,"d":3,"codes":["3.b","4.b"],"hints":["Think about what an earthquake can damage besides buildings, and what depends on that damaged infrastructure afterwards.","Consider a secondary hazard that can follow an earthquake, on a much longer timescale than the event itself.","Trace a chain from the immediate physical damage to a longer-running, larger-scale consequence, and judge how far that consequence outweighs the immediate damage."]},{"q":"\"The greatest hazard from tectonic activity is not the physical event itself, but human vulnerability.\" To what extent do you agree with this statement?","a":"Vulnerability (construction, wealth, preparedness, governance) usually explains far more of the harm than an event's physical size, as the Haiti/Chile and Merapi/Eyjafjallajökull comparisons show, but an extreme-magnitude event such as Tōhoku can still overwhelm even a well-prepared AC, so the claim holds to a large but not unlimited extent.","sol":"Tectonic hazards vary enormously in physical size, yet the harm they cause does not track that size closely, which supports the statement that human vulnerability, not the physical event, is usually the greater hazard. The clearest evidence is the contrast between countries facing earthquakes of similar or even reversed magnitude. Haiti's 2010 earthquake (Mw approximately 7.0) killed an estimated 100,000 to 316,000 people, largely because its capital, Port-au-Prince, had dense, unreinforced informal construction, weak building-code enforcement and a state with limited emergency capacity even before the disaster. Chile's earthquake later that year (Mw approximately 8.8), releasing far more energy, killed approximately 525 people, because strict seismic building codes, introduced after its 1960 earthquake and rigorously enforced, meant buildings absorbed and dissipated the shaking rather than collapsing. The same pattern holds for volcanic hazards: Indonesia's Mount Merapi eruption in 2010 killed an estimated 350 or more people, in part because dense rural settlement on fertile volcanic slopes placed many people within reach of fast-moving pyroclastic flows, whereas Iceland's Eyjafjallajökull eruption in the same year, despite disrupting European aviation for about six days, caused no direct deaths, reflecting Iceland's low population density near the volcano and well-established monitoring. In each pairing, the country with lower vulnerability and greater capacity to cope suffered far less harm from a comparable or larger hazard, which is strong evidence for the statement. However, the statement should not be accepted without limit. Japan's 2011 Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami (Mw approximately 9.0) struck one of the best-prepared countries in the world, with enforced building codes, a tsunami warning system and extensive disaster planning, and still killed an estimated 18,000 people, mainly through the tsunami overwhelming coastal defences designed for smaller events, and triggered a nuclear meltdown at Fukushima. This shows that at the most extreme end of magnitude, the physical scale of the event can still overwhelm even low vulnerability and high capacity to cope, so the physical event is not wholly irrelevant. Overall, the evidence strongly supports the statement for the great majority of tectonic hazard events: vulnerability and capacity to cope explain far more of the variation in harm between countries than the magnitude of the event does, as the Haiti-Chile and Merapi-Eyjafjallajökull comparisons show. But the claim holds only to a large, not an absolute, extent, because an event of sufficiently extreme physical magnitude, as at Tōhoku, can still cause a major disaster even where vulnerability has been substantially reduced.","m":33,"d":3,"codes":["4.a","4.b","5.a","5.b"],"hints":["Think about what usually differs most between two countries hit by tectonic hazards of similar size.","Build your argument from named contrasting pairs, and consider whether there is any point at which the physical event itself starts to matter more.","Structure your answer as: evidence supporting the statement, then a limit or counter-example, then a conclusion on how far the statement holds overall."]},{"q":"\"Improvements in monitoring, prediction and building design mean the risk from tectonic hazards has fallen over time.\" How far do you agree?","a":"Monitoring/design have reduced harm per event where they have been adopted (e.g. Japan, post-1960 Chile), but rising exposure and uneven access to these improvements mean overall risk has not fallen everywhere, so agreement should be qualified.","sol":"The disaster risk equation frames risk as a function of the hazard, the vulnerability of the exposed population, and their capacity to cope, and there is good evidence that monitoring, prediction and design have reduced the second and raised the third of these factors over time, at least where they have been adopted. Seismometer networks, satellite ground-deformation monitoring and gas sensors now give volcanologists days to weeks of warning before many eruptions, and earthquake early-warning systems such as Japan's can give tens of seconds of notice before damaging shaking arrives, enough to stop trains, halt lifts and trigger automatic safety systems. Building design has also improved measurably: base isolation, cross-bracing and reinforced materials, required under codes tightened after past disasters (for example Chile's codes after 1960, tightened further and credited with limiting deaths in 2010), mean a modern, code-compliant building survives shaking that would previously have caused collapse. Applying the Park model, a well-prepared place now shows a shallower drop in functioning after an event and a faster recovery through the relief, rehabilitation and reconstruction phases than in the past. However, these improvements have not reduced risk evenly, so agreement with the statement must be qualified. First, exposure to hazard has risen alongside these improvements: global population growth and rapid, often unplanned urbanisation have put many more people in tectonically active locations, including the informal settlements around cities such as Port-au-Prince, than a century ago, so the same-sized hazard now threatens more people even before vulnerability is considered. Second, monitoring, prediction and design only reduce harm where they are actually implemented and enforced: Haiti's 2010 death toll shows that a country lacking the resources to build and enforce codes, or to fund comprehensive monitoring, gains little protection from advances that exist elsewhere in the world. Even within a well-prepared country, Tōhoku in 2011 shows that improved defences can still be overwhelmed by an event larger than they were designed for. Overall, improvements in monitoring, prediction and building design have reduced risk substantially where they are funded, implemented and enforced, as Japan and post-1960 Chile show, so the statement holds to a considerable extent for those places. But because rising exposure and unequal access to these improvements mean risk has not fallen for much of the world's tectonically active population, and because even the best-prepared places remain vulnerable to the largest events, the statement should be agreed with only partially, not accepted as a general global trend.","m":33,"d":3,"codes":["5.a","5.b","5.c"],"hints":["Think about each part of the disaster risk equation separately, and whether improvements have changed all three parts equally.","Consider whether any reduction in risk applies everywhere, or only where these improvements have actually been adopted and enforced.","Structure your answer as: evidence for the claim, then what limits or complicates it, then a qualified overall judgement on how far you agree."]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the asthenosphere?","a":"The semi-molten layer of the upper mantle beneath the rigid lithosphere, over which the tectonic plates move."},{"q":"Name one hazard produced by a volcanic eruption besides lava flows.","a":"Any one of: pyroclastic flow, gas emissions, tephra/ash, lahar, tsunami."},{"q":"What is liquefaction?","a":"Where earthquake shaking causes water-saturated, loosely-packed sediment to temporarily behave like a liquid, so buildings can sink or tilt."},{"q":"According to the Park model, what happens to a place's level of functioning immediately after a disaster?","a":"It falls sharply, then recovers over time through relief, rehabilitation and reconstruction phases."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Lithosphere","back":"The rigid outer layer of the Earth — crust plus uppermost mantle — broken into tectonic plates that move over the asthenosphere below.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Volcanic hazard management: three strategy types","back":"Mitigating the event (e.g. lava diversion channels, spraying water to slow flows); mitigating vulnerability (hazard mapping, exclusion zones, monitoring for early warning); mitigating losses (rescue, relief, insurance) — the affordable mix varies with economic development.","code":"5.a"},{"front":"Paleomagnetism as evidence","back":"Symmetrical, reversing bands of magnetism either side of a mid-ocean ridge, recorded as new sea floor cools, showing the sea floor is spreading apart.","code":"1.a"},{"front":"Divergent (constructive) boundary","back":"Plates move apart; magma rises to fill the gap, forming features such as mid-ocean ridges or rift valleys, with mostly effusive volcanism.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"Continental-continental (collision) boundary","back":"Neither plate subducts, since both are buoyant; crust crumples upward into fold mountains, e.g. the Himalayas, with earthquakes but little volcanism.","code":"1.b"},{"front":"VEI","back":"The Volcanic Explosivity Index, scaled 0 to 8, where each whole step is roughly a tenfold increase in the volume of ejected material.","code":"2.a"},{"front":"Pyroclastic flow","back":"A fast-moving, superheated current of gas, ash and rock from an explosive eruption; the most lethal volcanic hazard.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Lahar","back":"A volcanic mudflow formed when eruption material mixes with melting ice or snow, or heavy rain, able to travel far beyond the eruption itself.","code":"2.b"},{"front":"Moment magnitude scale (Mw)","back":"A logarithmic scale measuring the energy released at an earthquake's source; has largely replaced the Richter scale for large events.","code":"3.a"},{"front":"Liquefaction","back":"Shaking makes water-saturated, loosely-packed sediment behave like a liquid, so buildings can sink, tilt or collapse even away from the epicentre.","code":"3.b"},{"front":"Why live near a volcano or fault?","back":"Fertile volcanic soils allow dense farming, as on Merapi's flanks; geothermal power and tourism bring income, as in Iceland; established cities such as Naples or Tokyo hold jobs, family and infrastructure, and a damaging event is rare within one lifetime, so everyday benefits outweigh perceived risk.","code":"4.a","open":true},{"front":"Why death tolls differ at similar magnitude","back":"Vulnerability (construction quality, code enforcement, settlement density) and capacity to cope usually explain differences in death toll more than an earthquake's magnitude does.","code":"4.b"},{"front":"Mitigating the event, vulnerability and losses","back":"Three strategy types: against the event itself (e.g. land-use zoning), against vulnerability (e.g. building design, preparedness), and against losses (e.g. insurance, emergency relief).","code":"5.b"},{"front":"Disaster risk equation","back":"Risk = (Hazard × Vulnerability) ÷ Capacity to cope: risk can fall even if the hazard itself does not, by reducing vulnerability or raising capacity to cope.","code":"5.c"},{"front":"Park model (disaster-response curve)","back":"Level of functioning drops sharply at a disaster, then recovers through relief, rehabilitation and reconstruction; a resilient place drops less and recovers faster.","code":"5.c"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4","items":["Question: \"The greatest hazard from tectonic activity is not the physical event itself, but human vulnerability.\" To what extent do you agree with this statement? (33 marks)","Vulnerability, not the physical size of a tectonic event, is usually the larger determinant of harm, and the clearest evidence for this is the contrast between countries hit by hazards of similar or even reversed magnitude. Chile's 2010 earthquake (Mw approximately 8.8) released roughly 500 times more energy than Haiti's 2010 earthquake (Mw approximately 7.0) in the same year, yet Chile recorded approximately 525 deaths against Haiti's estimated 100,000 to 316,000. This gap is explained by vulnerability and capacity to cope rather than by the hazard: Chile's building codes, tightened and enforced after its 1960 earthquake, meant structures could flex and absorb the shaking rather than collapse, while Port-au-Prince's dense, largely unreinforced informal construction and Haiti's limited emergency capacity meant the same category of hazard translated directly into mass building collapse and a slow, under-resourced response. The direction of the comparison — the physically larger earthquake causing vastly fewer deaths — is strong evidence that the statement holds: in this pairing, human vulnerability, not the tectonic event, was the greater hazard."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: \"The greatest hazard from tectonic activity is not the physical event itself, but human vulnerability.\" To what extent do you agree with this statement? (33 marks)","Vulnerability can make earthquakes worse. Haiti had an earthquake in 2010 and lots of people died, about 100,000 to 316,000. Chile also had an earthquake in 2010 but not as many people died, only about 525. This is because Haiti was poorer than Chile and did not have good buildings. Chile has better buildings so fewer people died. This shows vulnerability is important, so human vulnerability is a big cause of the disaster, not just the earthquake. (This paragraph states the same comparison as the Level 4 version but does not explain the mechanism — why Chile's codes worked, why Haiti's did not — and reaches an assertion rather than a weighed judgement, so it sits at a lower level.)"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Eyjafjallajökull eruption, Iceland, 2010 (AC)","items":["Iceland sits directly on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, a divergent plate boundary, and also over a hot spot, giving it unusually frequent volcanic activity for an economically advanced country (AC).","The 2010 Eyjafjallajökull eruption sequence began on 20 March 2010 with an effusive phase; the explosive VEI approximately 4 phase followed from 14 April, when meltwater from the overlying ice cap mixed with fine ash to produce an unusually fine, glass-rich ash cloud.","The ash cloud closed most of European airspace for about six days, grounding an estimated 100,000 flights and costing the global airline industry an estimated several billion US dollars.","No deaths were directly attributed to the eruption; local evacuations were ordered as a precaution against flooding (a jökulhlaup) from the melting ice cap.","Iceland's continuous volcanic and seismic monitoring, run by the Icelandic Meteorological Office, meant the eruption and its early stages were closely tracked, supporting the evacuation and international aviation warnings."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Mount Merapi eruption, Indonesia, 2010 (EDC)","items":["Indonesia lies on the Pacific 'Ring of Fire', at a convergent (destructive) boundary where the Indo-Australian plate subducts beneath the Eurasian plate, producing explosive stratovolcanoes such as Merapi.","Merapi's fertile volcanic soil supports some of the highest rural population densities in Indonesia on its slopes, which is part of why people live so close to a frequently active volcano.","The 2010 eruption (VEI approximately 4) produced repeated pyroclastic flows and killed an estimated 350 or more people, many of whom had not evacuated despite official warnings.","An estimated 350,000 people were evacuated from the danger zone around the volcano during the eruption.","Indonesia's national volcanological survey (PVMBG) monitors Merapi continuously and had raised the alert level before the eruption, but incomplete local compliance with evacuation orders raised the death toll."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami, Japan, 2011 (AC)","items":["Japan sits at a convergent, oceanic-continental plate boundary where the Pacific Plate subducts beneath the Okhotsk microplate, one of the most seismically active settings on Earth.","The 11 March 2011 earthquake had a moment magnitude of approximately 9.0, one of the largest ever recorded, with a shallow, offshore focus.","The resulting tsunami, with wave heights reaching over 10 metres in places, caused most of the estimated 18,000 deaths, and triggered a meltdown at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant.","Japan's enforced seismic building codes and early-warning system gave many residents tens of seconds of warning before shaking arrived, and are credited with keeping deaths from shaking alone comparatively low relative to the earthquake's magnitude.","Economic losses were estimated at over US $200 billion, making it one of the costliest natural disasters on record, despite Japan's high income and preparedness."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Haiti earthquake, 2010 (LIDC/EDC)","items":["Haiti lies near the boundary between the Caribbean and North American plates, close to the conservative Enriquillo-Plantain Garden fault zone.","The 12 January 2010 earthquake had a moment magnitude of approximately 7.0, with a shallow focus close to the densely populated capital, Port-au-Prince.","Estimated deaths range from about 100,000 to 316,000 depending on the source, reflecting widespread unreinforced, informal construction and limited building-code enforcement.","An estimated 1.5 million people were displaced into temporary camps; damaged water and sanitation infrastructure contributed to a major cholera outbreak later that year.","International rescue and relief efforts followed, but were widely criticised for coordination problems, and Haiti's government struggled to lead reconstruction given its limited pre-existing capacity.","For contrast: Chile's earthquake later in 2010 (Mw approximately 8.8) released far more energy and triggered a tsunami, yet recorded only about 525 deaths — enforced seismic building codes, introduced after its 1960 earthquake, meant far fewer buildings collapsed than in Haiti's weaker, less-enforced construction."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan: \"The greatest hazard from tectonic activity is not the physical event itself, but human vulnerability.\" To what extent do you agree? (33 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'greatest hazard' asks you to weigh the relative importance of the physical event versus human vulnerability as causes of harm, not just describe both — the verdict must be a weighed judgement, not a list.","Define terms: the 'physical event' is the hazard's magnitude or scale (e.g. Mw, VEI); 'vulnerability' is the exposure of the population and its inability to cope (construction, wealth, governance, preparedness).","Paragraph 1: evidence supporting the statement — contrast a physically larger earthquake with a physically smaller one where the smaller caused far more deaths, using named building-design and governance differences to explain why.","Paragraph 2: a second, independent case in a different hazard type — contrast two volcanic eruptions of similar VEI where death toll and disruption differed for reasons of settlement density and monitoring, not eruption size.","Paragraph 3 (the limit, or counter-argument): use a case where an extreme-magnitude event overwhelmed even strong preparedness and low vulnerability, to show the physical event still matters at the extreme end.","Evaluation thread throughout: after each paragraph, state explicitly whether that evidence supports or limits the statement, so the argument builds rather than just describing cases in sequence.","Conclusion: state how far you agree — vulnerability explains most of the variation in harm across most events, but the claim is not absolute, since a sufficiently extreme physical event remains dangerous even where vulnerability is low."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.945Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Structurally this is a strong kit — all eleven key ideas are taught faithfully and each section closes with what a top-level answer does, tariffs are all valid Component 03 values (3/6/12/33), both 12-mark items are genuinely synoptic (carbon cycle and aviation; disease and international aid, each across two scales), the four fact files map exactly onto the specification's two contrasting-development pairs (Eyjafjallajokull/Merapi for volcanic, Tohoku/Haiti for seismic), and the facts, cards and worked examples check out (Mw 10x amplitude / 31.6x energy, VEI 0-8, crust thickness and density, 0-70 km and ~700 km focus depths, Chile ~525 deaths, Merapi ~350, Eyjafjallajokull VEI 4 with ~100,000 flights grounded, Tohoku ~US$200bn). Fix the Chile energy ratio and the Haiti death-toll inconsistency and it is publishable; minor polish beyond that: the Level 4 model paragraph and essay plan lean on Chile 2010, which has no fact file of its own; idea 4.a (impacts of eruptions on people) is only examined inside the 33-mark question; there is no card for 5.a; 'Describe' (Q1, Q4) and 'calculate' (Q7) are used as stems though neither appears in the board's command-word list supplied with the brief; and the Eyjafjallajokull fact file's 'began in April' is true of the VEI 4 explosive phase but the eruption sequence began on 20 March 2010."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:52:11.202Z"}},"2e":{"id":"OCR-H481","topic":"2e","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room exists because every OCR A level Geography paper carries marks for geographical skills, and Investigative geography (04/05) — worth 20% of the whole A level — is assessed almost entirely on them. Papers 01, 02 and 03 each include a Resource Booklet and skills-based questions worth real marks, since AO3 (skills) makes up 28% of the qualification, testing whether you can calculate and interpret statistics, read maps and graphs critically, and evaluate data sources and sampling methods — while the NEA rewards a justified, well-analysed and honestly evaluated independent investigation. Of that 28% AO3 weighting, most of the marks sit within the NEA itself, with the remaining skills marks spread across the short answer and data-response questions in Papers 01, 02 and 03. Mastering these transferable skills, rather than memorising one topic's content, is what separates a mid-range script from a top-level one across every component.","idea":[{"h":"Geographical information: what it is, and using it critically","t":"Geographical data becomes 'geographical' when it is linked to a place, scale or location, and it comes in many forms: qualitative and quantitative, primary and secondary, images, maps, diagrams, digital data, and newer sources such as crowd-sourced and 'big data'. Exam questions reward learners who can classify a source correctly, use appropriate analytical approaches for the type of information given, and question it critically — identifying possible errors, bias or misuse of data, and the ethical or socio-political implications of how it was collected, especially data involving human communities. A top-level answer never simply describes a map or graph; it interprets what the pattern shows, questions how reliable the source is, and links the evidence to a well-evidenced conclusion.","code":"4.1"},{"h":"Geo-located data and GIS","t":"Geo-located data is information tied to a precise location, often collected using geospatial technologies such as GPS-enabled smartphones or tablets in the field. Geographical Information Systems (GIS) let this data be layered, mapped and analysed — for example overlaying flood-risk zones onto population density — making spatial patterns and relationships far easier to see and interpret than raw figures alone. Exam questions may ask you to identify the benefits GIS offers over a single static map, or the opportunities and limits of collecting digital, geo-located data in fieldwork; a strong answer names a specific analytical benefit rather than describing GIS in vague terms.","code":"4.2"},{"h":"Qualitative skills: interviews, sources and coding","t":"Qualitative skills cover the collection and analysis of non-numerical evidence: interviews (structured, semi-structured or unstructured), and textual or visual source material such as newspaper reports, photographs or blog posts. Coding — sorting responses or text into themes or categories — makes large amounts of qualitative material easier to analyse and compare, but always involves some subjective judgement by the researcher. Exam questions test whether you understand when a qualitative method suits the research aim better than a quantitative one, and can weigh its opportunities (depth, richness, capturing reasons and feelings) against its limitations (small sample sizes, time to analyse, researcher bias in coding).","code":"4.3"},{"h":"Quantitative and statistical skills","t":"Quantitative skills fall into four groups you must be able to use, not just define: descriptive statistics (mean, median, mode, range, interquartile range, standard deviation); tests of association and significance (Chi-squared, Spearman's rank, Mann-Whitney U, T-test); graphical relationships (correlation and lines of best fit); and measurement, measurement error and sampling. Exam questions may ask you to calculate a statistic from given data, choose and justify the correct test for a described dataset, or interpret what a calculated value — such as a Spearman's rank coefficient compared against a critical value — actually shows about the geography. A top-level answer always shows full working, states units, and interprets the number geographically rather than stopping at the calculation.","code":"4.4"},{"h":"Fieldwork skills and the NEA","t":"Fieldwork skills run from defining a research question and reviewing relevant literature, through justifying and implementing a methodology and sampling framework, to analysing data, writing up results, and evaluating the investigation's limitations and ethical dimensions. These skills are examined only within the Investigative geography (04/05) non-exam assessment, not within the written papers, where marks associated with geographical skills 4.1–4.4 are assessed instead. At least four days of fieldwork covering both physical and human geography are required across the course. In the NEA, the top level always rewards methodologies that are justified, data analysis that is detailed and accurate, and a conclusion that evaluates limitations and draws a balanced, well-supported judgement rather than simply restating the results.","code":"5"}],"examples":[{"title":"Spearman's rank: pebble size and distance along a spit","setup":"Six sites are spaced evenly along a spit. The distance of each from the proximal end, with the mean pebble size measured there, is: 0 m, 68 mm; 200 m, 60 mm; 400 m, 51 mm; 600 m, 39 mm; 800 m, 30 mm; 1000 m, 22 mm. Calculate Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (rs) for distance against pebble size, and say what the value shows.","steps":["Rank the sites by distance, shortest first: 0 m = 1, 200 m = 2, 400 m = 3, 600 m = 4, 800 m = 5 and 1000 m = 6.","Rank pebble size on the same convention, smallest first: 22 mm = 1, 30 mm = 2, 39 mm = 3, 51 mm = 4, 60 mm = 5 and 68 mm = 6, so the size ranks read 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 as you move down the spit.","d (distance rank − size rank) for the six sites is −5, −3, −1, 1, 3, 5; squaring clears the signs to give 25, 9, 1, 1, 9, 25, so Σd² = 70.","Substituting n = 6 and Σd² = 70 into rs = 1 − (6Σd²)/(n(n²−1)) gives rs = 1 − (6×70)/(6×35) = 1 − 420/210 = 1 − 2 = −1.","rs = −1 is a perfect negative correlation: pebble size falls steadily as distance along the spit increases, which is what longshore drift predicts as waves carry the smallest, lightest material furthest towards the distal end."],"cues":["How do the six sites rank by distance?","Ranking smallest first, how do the size ranks run?","What are the rank differences, and their squares?","What goes into rs = 1 − (6Σd²)/(n(n²−1))?","What does this value of rs show about the spit?"]},{"title":"Standard deviation: wave heights at a beach","setup":"Five wave heights were recorded at a beach: 0.8 m, 1.0 m, 1.2 m, 0.9 m and 1.1 m. Calculate the standard deviation of these heights, dividing by n, and say what it shows about the sea state.","steps":["Mean = (0.8 + 1.0 + 1.2 + 0.9 + 1.1) ÷ 5 = 5.0 ÷ 5 = 1.0 m.","Taking that 1.0 m mean from each reading in turn gives deviations of −0.2, 0, 0.2, −0.1 and 0.1 m.","Squaring each deviation clears the minus signs, and the squares total 0.04 + 0 + 0.04 + 0.01 + 0.01 = 0.10.","Variance = 0.10 ÷ 5 = 0.02, and the standard deviation is its square root: √0.02 ≈ 0.14 m.","The standard deviation is approximately 0.14 m — only about a seventh of the 1.0 m mean — so these wave heights cluster tightly around the mean, indicating a steady swell rather than a mixed, variable sea."],"cues":["What do you add together, then divide by 5?","What is taken from each reading next?","Why square the deviations, and what do they total?","Which two operations turn that total into the SD?","How spread out are the waves, compared with the mean?"]},{"title":"Interquartile range: infiltration rates on a hillslope","setup":"Seven infiltration rates were recorded on a hillslope and placed in order: 4, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18 and 24 mm/hr. Calculate the interquartile range of these readings, and say why it is a safer measure of spread here than the full range.","steps":["With seven ordered readings, the lower quartile sits at position (7 + 1) ÷ 4 = 2, so Q1 is the 2nd reading, 6 mm/hr.","The upper quartile sits at position 3 × (7 + 1) ÷ 4 = 6, so Q3 is the 6th reading, 18 mm/hr — which leaves the largest reading, 24 mm/hr, outside the quartiles altogether.","Interquartile range = Q3 − Q1 = 18 − 6 = 12 mm/hr.","So the interquartile range is 12 mm/hr, the spread of the middle 50% of readings; it is safer than the full range of 24 − 4 = 20 mm/hr because that figure is set entirely by the two extremes, including the 24 mm/hr reading that may be an anomaly from a burrow or crack."],"cues":["Which position gives Q1 from seven readings?","Which position gives Q3, and which reading falls outside?","What do you subtract to find the IQR?","Why trust the IQR more than the full range here?"]},{"title":"Percentage change: coastal erosion at a cliff-top marker","setup":"The distance from a fixed cliff-top marker to the cliff edge was measured as 14.5 m in 2015 and 9.8 m in 2023. Calculate the percentage change in that distance, and comment on the rate of recession it implies.","steps":["Change = new − old = 9.8 m (2023) − 14.5 m (2015) = −4.7 m, the minus sign showing the gap has shortened as the cliff edge retreated towards the marker.","Percentage change is measured against the starting value, so divide by the 2015 distance: −4.7 ÷ 14.5 = −0.324.","Multiplying by 100 turns that proportion into a percentage: −0.324 × 100 = −32.4%.","Spread over the eight years from 2015 to 2023, the 4.7 m lost is an average of 4.7 ÷ 8 ≈ 0.59 m of cliff retreat per year.","The marker-to-edge distance therefore fell by 32.4% — almost a third of the buffer gone in eight years at roughly 0.59 m a year — which is rapid recession, and at that rate the marker itself would be lost in under 20 more years."],"cues":["What do you subtract, and what does the sign mean?","Which year's distance is the divisor?","What converts that decimal into a percentage?","How many metres a year is that, over the eight years?","How fast is this recession, and what follows from it?"]},{"title":"Reading a scatter graph: soil pH and moorland vegetation cover","setup":"A scatter graph plots vegetation cover (%) against soil pH for ten moorland sites, with a line of best fit drawn through the points. The ten readings, as (pH, cover), are (4.0, 30), (4.3, 38), (4.7, 45), (5.0, 50), (5.5, 35), (5.8, 62), (6.1, 70), (6.4, 75), (6.7, 82) and (7.0, 90). Describe the relationship shown by the graph, including any anomaly.","steps":["Taking the two end sites, cover climbs from 30% at pH 4.0 to 90% at pH 7.0, so the points as a whole run from bottom-left to top-right of the graph.","The line of best fit through them therefore has a positive gradient, rising by about 20% of cover for each whole unit of pH, so more acidic soils carry least vegetation.","Nine of the ten points sit within a few percentage points of that line, but the site at pH 5.5 has only 35% cover where the line predicts roughly 60% — about 25 percentage points below the trend.","The graph therefore shows a strong positive relationship between soil pH and vegetation cover across the moorland, with one clear anomaly at pH 5.5 whose 35% cover sits well below the line and would be worth rechecking for a measurement error or a local cause such as heavy trampling or waterlogging."],"cues":["Compare the two end sites — what do they show?","What gradient does the line of best fit have?","Which site lies furthest from the line, and by how much?","What relationship, and what exception, does the graph show?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between primary and secondary data?","a":"Primary data is collected first-hand by the researcher; secondary data was collected and published by someone else beforehand."},{"q":"Which test would you use to compare two independent samples of continuous data that are not normally distributed?","a":"The Mann-Whitney U test."},{"q":"How many days of fieldwork must be completed across the A level course?","a":"At least four days, covering both physical and human geography."},{"q":"What proportion of the A level is the NEA (04/05) worth?","a":"20% of the A level (60 marks)."}]},"room":{"facts":["Spearman's rank: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) / (n(n² − 1)) — tests the strength and direction of correlation between two ranked datasets.","Standard deviation: σ = √(Σ(x − x̄)²/n) — measures how spread out data values are around the mean.","Interquartile range: IQR = Q3 − Q1, the spread of the middle 50% of ordered data.","Percentage change: (new value − old value) ÷ old value × 100.","Chi-squared: χ² = Σ((O − E)²/E) — tests association between two categorical (frequency-count) variables.","The NEA (04/05) is 60 marks across six sections: planning, purpose and introduction (8); data collection methods and sampling framework (7); data presentation techniques (9); data analysis and explanation (14); conclusions and investigation evaluation (12); overall quality and communication (10).","A minimum of four days of fieldwork must be undertaken across the A level course, covering both physical and human geography processes.","Sampling method (how items are selected) within a sampling framework (the population, the unit and the size decided first): choosing how items, people or points are selected always trades representativeness against practicality — a chance-based selection avoids selection bias but can under-sample small sub-groups; a fixed-interval selection is quick but can coincide with a hidden pattern; a proportional, sub-group selection improves representativeness but needs accurate prior knowledge of the sub-groups and their sizes.","Data ethics: collecting and representing geographical data, especially about people, raises ethical and socio-political questions — whether those studied gave informed consent and had their privacy respected, and whether the sample or source under-represents some groups, which can bias the conclusions drawn.","NEA 'best fit' principle: work does not have to meet every criterion of a level before it can be placed in that level — the examiner judges which level's descriptors the work as a whole matches most closely, then how fully it meets those requirements fixes where within the level it is placed.","Primary data is collected first-hand by the investigator in the field (counts, measurements, questionnaires); secondary data was collected by someone else for another purpose (Census, ONS, IMD, OS maps, published research) and must be checked for date, scale and reliability."],"questions":[{"q":"A student records rainfall at their fieldwork site using a rain gauge on the day of the survey. They also download 30-year average rainfall statistics for the same location from the Met Office website. Identify which source is primary data and which is secondary data.","a":"Rain gauge reading = primary data; Met Office statistics = secondary data.","sol":"The rain gauge reading is primary data because the student collected it first-hand, specifically for their own investigation. The Met Office statistics are secondary data because they were collected and published by another organisation before the student used them.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about who actually went out and collected each set of figures.","Recall the definitions of primary and secondary data from your facts sheet and decide which each source is.","Give one classification per source, each followed by a short reason."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"Six students record the width of a river channel, in metres, at a meander bend: 4.2, 5.8, 6.1, 4.9, 5.5, 5.0. Calculate the mean channel width and the range.","a":"Mean = 5.25 m; range = 1.9 m.","sol":"Sum = 4.2+5.8+6.1+4.9+5.5+5.0 = 31.5. Mean = 31.5 ÷ 6 = 5.25 m. Range = highest − lowest = 6.1 − 4.2 = 1.9 m.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Two different descriptive statistics are being asked for here — one about central tendency, one about spread.","Recall which formula finds a 'typical' value and which one compares the extremes of a dataset.","Give two separate numerical answers, each with the correct unit."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"A geographer numbers every shop on a high street and uses a random number generator to select 15 shops to survey. Identify the sampling method used.","a":"Random sampling.","sol":"Every shop has an equal and independent chance of selection because the shops are chosen using a random number generator applied to the numbered list, so this is random sampling (not systematic, which would use a fixed interval, or stratified, which would sample from sub-groups in proportion).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about how the selection was actually made — was there a pattern, a proportion, or pure chance?","Recall the different ways a sample can be selected and what distinguishes each one from the others.","Name the sampling method in one word, then add a short justification."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"A choropleth map of a city shows average household income by ward, shaded in five classes from under £20,000 (lightest) to over £60,000 (darkest). The wards nearest the city centre are shaded darkest; the wards on the eastern edge of the city are shaded lightest. Describe the spatial pattern shown.","a":"Income is highest near the centre and lowest on the eastern edge, i.e. a general decrease in income with distance from the centre, most marked towards the east.","sol":"The map shows a spatial pattern in which household income decreases with distance from the city centre: the darkest shading (over £60,000) is concentrated in the central wards, while the lightest shading (under £20,000) is concentrated in the eastern wards, suggesting income is not evenly distributed across the city and is lowest in the east.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look at where the darkest and lightest shading classes are located relative to each other.","Recall how to describe a spatial pattern on a map: direction, distance and comparison between areas.","Write one sentence naming the trend, then one sentence naming where it is strongest or weakest."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"Five consecutive daily maximum temperatures at a fieldwork site were 18°C, 20°C, 22°C, 19°C and 21°C. Calculate the standard deviation of these temperatures, showing your working.","a":"Standard deviation ≈ 1.41°C.","sol":"Mean = (18+20+22+19+21)/5 = 100/5 = 20°C. Deviations from mean: −2, 0, 2, −1, 1. Squared deviations: 4, 0, 4, 1, 1; sum = 10. Variance = 10/5 = 2. Standard deviation = √2 ≈ 1.41°C.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This is about how spread out a set of values is around their average, not just how big the values are.","Recall the standard deviation formula from your facts sheet and apply it in order, step by step.","State the standard deviation as a single value with two decimal places and the correct unit."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"Nine pedestrian counts recorded along a transect, arranged in order, were: 12, 15, 18, 20, 24, 27, 30, 33, 40. Calculate the interquartile range of this dataset.","a":"IQR = 15 pedestrians (Q1 = 16.5, Q3 = 31.5).","sol":"With n = 9, Q1 lies at position (9+1)/4 = 2.5, between the 2nd value (15) and 3rd value (18): Q1 = 15 + 0.5×(18−15) = 16.5. Q3 lies at position 3×(9+1)/4 = 7.5, between the 7th value (30) and 8th value (33): Q3 = 30 + 0.5×(33−30) = 31.5. IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 31.5 − 16.5 = 15 pedestrians.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This asks about the spread of the middle portion of the data, not the whole range.","Recall how to locate Q1 and Q3 within an ordered dataset before subtracting.","Give Q1, Q3 and the final interquartile range as three separate values."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"A student investigating land use in a town divides the town into residential, commercial and industrial zones and samples in proportion to the area of each zone. Explain one strength and one weakness of this sampling method.","a":"Strength: the sample proportionally represents each land-use zone, reducing bias from any zone being over- or under-represented. Weakness: it requires the zone boundaries and proportions to be known accurately in advance, which takes time and can be inaccurate.","sol":"One strength of this stratified approach is that, because sampling points are allocated in proportion to the area of each zone, the resulting sample reflects the true balance of residential, commercial and industrial land use in the town; this means the findings are less likely to be skewed towards whichever zone happens to be easiest to access, improving the representativeness of the results. One weakness is that stratified sampling depends on the researcher first dividing the town accurately into zones and calculating correct proportions before any sampling can begin; if the zone boundaries are drawn incorrectly, or the land-use data used to define them is out of date, the proportions allocated to each zone — and therefore the whole sample — will be unrepresentative, and identifying the boundaries in the field can also be time-consuming.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what proportional sampling is designed to achieve, and what it depends on to work correctly.","Recall from your facts sheet the general trade-off this kind of proportional, sub-group sampling involves, then apply it to this scenario.","Write two separate developed points, each following a cause leading to an effect on the sample's quality."],"codes":["4.4","5"]},{"q":"A scatter graph plots pebble roundness (0–1 scale) on the y-axis against distance downstream (km) on the x-axis for 12 samples along a river. The points rise from around 0.2 at 0 km to around 0.85 at 10 km, and a line of best fit has a positive gradient with most points close to the line, though the point at 6 km (roundness 0.35) lies well below it. Using the data, describe the pattern shown.","a":"Roundness increases with distance downstream (positive correlation), rising from about 0.2 to 0.85 over 10 km, with one anomaly at 6 km.","sol":"The scatter graph shows a positive correlation between distance downstream and pebble roundness: roundness rises from approximately 0.2 at the source (0 km) to approximately 0.85 by 10 km downstream, and the line of best fit has a positive gradient with most points lying close to it, indicating a fairly strong relationship. One anomalous result occurs at 6 km, where roundness is only 0.35, well below the value the line of best fit predicts, which may reflect a local input of angular material such as a tributary or a recent rockfall at that point.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the overall direction of the relationship before considering any points that don't fit it.","Recall how to describe a scatter graph: direction of correlation, strength, and any anomalies relative to the line of best fit.","Describe the overall trend in one sentence, then identify and quantify the anomaly in a second sentence."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"A glacier's length was measured as 8.4 km in 1990 and 6.7 km in 2020. Calculate the percentage change in the glacier's length over this period.","a":"≈ −20.2% (a decrease of about 20.2%).","sol":"Percentage change = (new value − old value) ÷ old value × 100 = (6.7 − 8.4) ÷ 8.4 × 100 = (−1.7 ÷ 8.4) × 100 ≈ −20.2%, i.e. the glacier decreased in length by about 20.2%.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This is about how much a value has changed relative to where it started.","Recall the percentage change formula and identify which value is 'old' and which is 'new'.","Give a single percentage, with a sign or word to show whether it is an increase or decrease."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"A student wants to understand in depth how long-term residents feel about a regeneration scheme in their neighbourhood. Explain why a semi-structured interview might be more appropriate than a closed questionnaire for this purpose.","a":"Interviews allow follow-up questions so residents can explain feelings and reasons in their own words, gathering richer qualitative detail than fixed questionnaire responses allow.","sol":"A semi-structured interview allows the researcher to ask open, follow-up questions in response to what a resident says, so complex or personal feelings about the regeneration scheme can be explored and explained in the resident's own words; a closed questionnaire restricts responses to pre-set categories, which would not capture this depth of opinion or the reasons behind it.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Think about how much freedom each method gives the person responding to shape their own answer.","Recall the general opportunities of qualitative methods such as interviews compared with structured questionnaires.","Write one sentence on what the interview allows, and one on why that suits this research aim."],"codes":["4.3"]},{"q":"The table shows distance from the CBD (km) and pedestrian counts (per hour) at eight sites along a transect: 1 km/240; 2 km/210; 3 km/150; 4 km/160; 5 km/130; 6 km/90; 7 km/70; 8 km/50. (a) Calculate Spearman's rank correlation coefficient for this data. (b) Using a critical value of 0.738 (n = 8, 5% significance level), justify whether the correlation is significant.","a":"rs ≈ −0.976; since 0.976 > 0.738, the correlation is significant at the 5% level (reject the null hypothesis of no correlation).","sol":"Ranking distance 1–8 gives ranks 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8 (already in order). Ranking the counts (1 = lowest count) gives, for sites 1–8: 8,7,5,6,4,3,2,1. d (distance rank − count rank) = −7,−5,−2,−2,1,3,5,7; d² = 49,25,4,4,1,9,25,49; Σd² = 166. rs = 1 − (6×166)/(8×(8²−1)) = 1 − 996/504 ≈ −0.976. Since |−0.976| = 0.976 is greater than the critical value of 0.738 for n = 8 at the 5% significance level, the correlation is statistically significant: there is a strong, significant negative relationship between distance from the CBD and pedestrian counts, so the null hypothesis of no correlation can be rejected.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["This tests whether two ranked variables are related, and how confident you can be in that relationship.","Recall the Spearman's rank procedure and formula from your facts sheet, and apply it to this data.","Give the coefficient to 3 significant figures, then a one-sentence comparison with the critical value."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"'Big data' and crowd-sourced sources, such as social media posts geotagged during a flood event, are increasingly used in geographical research. Evaluate the ethical and socio-political implications of using this type of data.","a":"Benefits: large, real-time volumes of otherwise inaccessible information. Concerns: informed consent and privacy, and uneven social-media use meaning some groups are under-represented, which can bias conclusions.","sol":"Crowd-sourced and big data sources such as geotagged social media posts can provide large volumes of real-time information from places and communities a researcher could not otherwise reach quickly, which is valuable when evaluating rapidly changing events such as floods. However, their use raises ethical concerns: individuals posting content are often unaware their data will be used for research, raising questions of informed consent and privacy, particularly where posts can be linked to identifiable people or exact locations. There are also socio-political implications, since social media use is not evenly distributed across a population — it tends to under-represent older people, poorer households and some ethnic or language groups — so conclusions drawn from this data may misrepresent the experience of a whole community and reinforce existing inequalities in whose voice is heard. On balance, such data is a valuable supplement to primary fieldwork but should not be used uncritically or in place of informed consent and reflection on whose perspectives are missing.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Weigh what this kind of data makes possible against who it excludes and how it was obtained.","Recall from your facts sheet the ethical and socio-political questions raised by geographical data, and think about who provided it and who might be missing from it.","Structure your answer as one paragraph on the benefits, one on the concerns, and a final sentence weighing them."],"codes":["4.1","4.2"]},{"q":"A student has collected pebble long-axis lengths (mm) from two different beaches, with 15 pebbles sampled from each beach, and wants to test whether the two beaches have significantly different pebble sizes. Justify which of Chi-squared and Mann-Whitney U is the more appropriate test to use, and explain one reason the alternative would not be suitable.","a":"Mann-Whitney U is appropriate because it compares two independent samples of ranked/continuous data; Chi-squared is unsuitable because it tests association between categorical (frequency-count) data, not continuous measurements from two samples.","sol":"Mann-Whitney U is the more appropriate test here because it is designed to compare two independent samples of data (here, pebble lengths from Beach A and Beach B) to see whether they come from populations with different medians, and it works with data, such as continuous pebble-length measurements, that can be ranked. Chi-squared would not be suitable because it tests for association between two categorical variables using frequency counts in a contingency table; pebble length in millimetres is continuous, ranked data comparing two samples, not a set of categories with observed and expected frequencies, so a Chi-squared test does not fit the design of this investigation.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Think about why these two named tests suit different kinds of geographical data, and which one this dataset matches.","Recall what each named test is designed to compare, then match that to the pebble-length data described here.","Name the correct test with one reason it fits, then name the other test with one reason it does not."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"The NEA (Investigative geography, 04/05) is marked using a 'best fit' approach across six sections, including 'conclusions and investigation evaluation'. Explain how this marking approach rewards a learner who critically evaluates the limitations of their own data collection.","a":"Best-fit marking places the work in the level its overall qualities best match, so a learner whose evaluation shows sustained, well-supported critical reflection on their methodology's limitations meets more of a higher level's descriptors and is placed in that higher level, even if other parts of the report are weaker.","sol":"Under best-fit marking, an examiner does not require every single criterion of a level to be met before that level can be awarded; instead they judge which level's descriptors the work as a whole most closely matches, then decide where within that level's mark range it sits based on how fully it meets the requirements. A learner who evaluates their data collection critically and in depth — for example, identifying specific sources of sampling bias or measurement error and discussing how they affected their conclusions — demonstrates the sustained, well-supported evaluation that the top level of the 'conclusions and investigation evaluation' section describes, so their work is placed in that higher level even if another section of the report is comparatively weaker.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about what 'best fit' means, compared with needing to tick off every single criterion first.","Recall the best-fit marking principle from your facts sheet, and connect it to what a strong evaluation section looks like.","Explain the marking principle in one sentence, then apply it to a critical, well-evidenced evaluation in a second."],"codes":["5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What formula finds the interquartile range?","a":"IQR = Q3 − Q1."},{"q":"Name three sampling methods used in geographical investigations.","a":"Random, systematic and stratified sampling."},{"q":"Which OCR component assesses fieldwork skills?","a":"Investigative geography (04/05), the NEA."},{"q":"What does a Spearman's rank coefficient close to 0 indicate?","a":"Little or no correlation between the two ranked variables."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Spearman's rank formula","back":"rs = 1 − (6Σd²) / (n(n² − 1)); measures the strength and direction of correlation between two ranked datasets.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Standard deviation formula","back":"σ = √(Σ(x − x̄)²/n); measures how spread out data values are around the mean — a larger SD means more spread.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Interquartile range","back":"IQR = Q3 − Q1, the range of the middle 50% of ordered data; less affected by extreme outliers than the full range.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Percentage change formula","back":"% change = (new value − old value) ÷ old value × 100; a negative result shows a decrease.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Random sampling","back":"Every item in the population has an equal, independent chance of selection, e.g. using random number generators; avoids bias but may miss small sub-groups.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Systematic sampling","back":"Samples are taken at regular, fixed intervals (e.g. every 10th house, every 50 m); quick and easy but can align with a hidden pattern and cause bias.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Stratified sampling","back":"The population is divided into sub-groups (strata) and sampled in proportion to their size; representative but needs accurate prior knowledge of the strata.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Primary vs secondary data","back":"Primary data is collected first-hand by the researcher; secondary data was collected and published by someone else before the researcher used it.","code":"4.1"},{"front":"Chi-squared test","back":"χ² = Σ((O − E)²/E); tests whether an association between two categorical variables (frequency counts) is statistically significant.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Mann-Whitney U test","back":"Tests whether two independent samples of ranked or continuous data come from populations with different medians.","code":"4.4"},{"front":"Fieldwork requirement","back":"At least four days of fieldwork across the A level course, covering both physical and human geography processes.","code":"5"},{"front":"NEA sections and marks","back":"60 marks across six sections: planning/introduction (8), data collection/sampling (7), data presentation (9), data analysis (14), conclusions/evaluation (12), quality of writing (10).","code":"5"},{"front":"GIS","back":"Geographical Information Systems: software that layers, presents and analyses spatially referenced (geo-located) data, e.g. from GPS-enabled devices.","code":"4.2"},{"front":"Qualitative technique: coding","back":"Assigning categories or themes to open-ended qualitative data, such as interview transcripts, so patterns across responses can be identified and compared.","code":"4.3"}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model answer — 6 marks (full marks)","items":["Question: Figure 1 shows a proportional flow-line map of daily commuter flows into a city centre from four surrounding towns — Elmswood (3,200 commuters, 8 km from the centre), Barham (1,800 commuters, 15 km), Cowley (900 commuters, 22 km) and Drayford (450 commuters, 30 km). Using the data, describe the pattern of commuter flows shown. [6 marks]","Model answer (full marks): The flow-line map shows that the volume of commuter flow into the city centre generally decreases as distance from the centre increases: Elmswood, the closest town at 8 km, sends the largest flow at 3,200 commuters per day, while Drayford, the furthest town at 30 km, sends the smallest flow at only 450 commuters per day, less than a seventh of Elmswood's total. This general trend is not perfectly linear, however — Barham (15 km, 1,800 commuters) sends roughly double the commuters of Cowley (22 km, 900 commuters) despite the distance between them being only 7 km, a smaller gap in distance than between Cowley and Drayford, which suggests that factors other than distance alone, such as public transport connections or the size of each town's working population, are also shaping the flows. Overall, the map shows a broadly negative relationship between distance from the city centre and commuter flow volume, quantified across all four towns, with the rate of decline uneven between town pairs."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model answer — 6 marks (partial)","items":["Question: Figure 1 shows a proportional flow-line map of daily commuter flows into a city centre from four surrounding towns — Elmswood (3,200 commuters, 8 km from the centre), Barham (1,800 commuters, 15 km), Cowley (900 commuters, 22 km) and Drayford (450 commuters, 30 km). Using the data, describe the pattern of commuter flows shown. [6 marks]","Partial answer: The map shows that the town closest to the city centre has the most commuters and the town furthest away has the fewest. This means that flow decreases with distance.","What this partial answer lacks: it does not quote any of the four figures given (3,200; 1,800; 900; 450) or the distances, so the pattern is not supported by the data as the question requires; it only compares the two extreme towns and ignores Barham and Cowley, so it misses the uneven, non-linear part of the pattern between them; and it does not suggest what else, besides distance, might explain the flows, so the description stays generic rather than fully using the map."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Statistical tests: formulae and when to use each","items":["Spearman's rank correlation coefficient: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) / (n(n² − 1)). Use when testing whether there is a significant correlation between two ranked (or ranked-from-continuous) variables, e.g. distance and land value; a result close to +1 or −1 shows a strong relationship, compared against a critical value table for the sample size and significance level chosen. Spearman's rank normally needs a sample size of at least 10 for the significance test to be considered reliable.","Chi-squared test: χ² = Σ((O − E)² / E), where O is the observed frequency and E is the expected frequency in each category of a contingency table. Use when testing whether there is a significant association between two categorical variables (frequency counts, not measurements), such as land-use type against distance-from-centre zone. As with Spearman's rank, the calculated value must exceed the critical value for the result to be significant.","Mann-Whitney U test: ranks all values from two independent samples together, then compares the sum of ranks in each sample. Use when comparing whether two independent samples of ranked or continuous data (e.g. pebble size on two beaches) come from populations with different medians, especially when the data is not normally distributed. Unlike Spearman's rank and Chi-squared, the calculated U value must be below (not above) the critical value for the result to be significant.","T-test: compares the means of two samples relative to the variability within them. Use when comparing the means of two independent samples of continuous, normally distributed data to see whether the difference between the means is statistically significant.","For any of these tests, state a null hypothesis (H0: no significant difference/association/correlation) before testing, and compare the calculated statistic against a critical value for the sample size (or, for Chi-squared and the t-test, for the degrees of freedom) and chosen significance level (usually 0.05) to accept or reject it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Descriptive statistics and graphical techniques","items":["Mean, median and mode each describe a 'typical' value in a dataset; mean uses every value so is sensitive to outliers, median is the middle value of ordered data so resists outliers, and mode is the most frequent value, useful for categorical data.","Range (highest − lowest) and interquartile range (Q3 − Q1) both describe spread; IQR uses only the middle 50% of ordered data so is less distorted by extreme values than the range.","Standard deviation, σ = √(Σ(x − x̄)²/n), measures how far, on average, values lie from the mean; a small SD shows the data is clustered close to the mean, a large SD shows it is more dispersed.","A scatter graph with a line of best fit is used to show the relationship between two continuous variables; the gradient and closeness of points to the line indicate the direction and strength of any correlation, and points far from the line are anomalies worth investigating.","Choose a bar chart or pie chart for comparing categories or showing proportions, a line graph for change over time, and a scatter graph for the relationship between two continuous variables."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Cartographic techniques","items":["Choropleth maps shade areas (e.g. wards, countries) by class to show how a variable, such as income or population density, varies between defined zones; they show spatial pattern but hide variation within each zone.","Isoline maps join points of equal value (e.g. rainfall, pressure, journey time) with a continuous line, showing gradual spatial change rather than the abrupt zone boundaries of a choropleth map.","Flow-line maps use arrows or lines of varying width, drawn proportional to the quantity moving, to show the direction and volume of movement between places, such as migration or trade flows.","Proportional symbol maps draw a symbol (often a circle or bar) at a point, sized in proportion to the value it represents, to compare a quantity, such as city population, between locations.","Desire-line maps draw straight lines between an origin and a destination, proportional to the number of movements, to show the pattern of flows, such as commuting, without following the actual route taken."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fieldwork methods and sampling: strengths and weaknesses","items":["Random sampling selects items using chance (e.g. random number generators) so every item has an equal probability of selection, avoiding bias; a weakness is that it can, by chance, under-sample small but important sub-groups or areas.","Systematic sampling selects items at a regular, fixed interval (e.g. every 20 metres along a transect); it is quick and straightforward to apply but can produce biased results if the interval happens to coincide with an underlying pattern in what is being measured.","Stratified sampling divides the population into sub-groups (strata, e.g. land-use zones) and samples from each in proportion to its size, improving representativeness; its weakness is that it requires accurate prior knowledge of the strata and their proportions, which takes time to establish.","Semi-structured interviews use a flexible set of open questions, allowing follow-up questions; this gathers rich, in-depth qualitative data but is more time-consuming to conduct and analyse, and the sample of respondents is usually small.","Questionnaires with closed questions are quick to administer to large samples and produce data that is easy to compare and quantify, but they limit respondents to pre-set answers and so can miss the reasons behind opinions."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"The NEA (Investigative geography, 04/05): stages and how marks are awarded","items":["The NEA is a written report, recommended at 3,000–4,000 words, marked internally by the centre and externally moderated by OCR, worth 20% of the A level (60 marks).","The 60 marks are broken into six sections: planning, purpose and introduction (8 marks); data, information collection methods and sampling framework (7 marks); data presentation techniques (9 marks); data analysis and explanation (14 marks); conclusions and investigation evaluation (12 marks); overall quality and communication of written work (10 marks).","Marking uses a 'best fit' principle: if the work meets all the criteria of a level it is awarded the top mark of that level; it does not have to meet every requirement of a level before being placed in it, and how fully it meets the level's requirements determines where within the level it is placed.","Learners must undertake at least four days of fieldwork across the course, covering processes in both physical and human geography; fieldwork carried out for the independent investigation can count towards this requirement.","The topic of the investigation may relate to any part of the specification, including options not studied as part of the taught course, and teachers may give only general guidance, not feedback that improves a specific learner's work."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"NEA investigation plan: Does pedestrian footfall decline with distance from a town centre?","items":["Research question: Does pedestrian footfall decline with distance from the town centre, and what factors explain any variation?","Hypothesis: Pedestrian footfall will be significantly negatively correlated with distance from the town centre, as retail and service provision (and therefore attractiveness to pedestrians) decreases with distance.","Data: primary pedestrian counts (number of people passing a fixed point in 5 minutes) at 10 sites at regular intervals along a transect from the town centre; secondary land-use and retail-frontage data from a council or Ordnance Survey map for context.","Sampling: systematic sampling of count sites at fixed 100 m intervals along the transect, with counts repeated at the same time of day at each site to control for the effect of time on footfall.","Analysis: Spearman's rank correlation coefficient calculated between distance from the centre and mean pedestrian count, compared against a critical value for the sample size (or, for Chi-squared and the t-test, for the degrees of freedom) at the 5% significance level; a scatter graph with a line of best fit to show the pattern visually.","Evaluation: consider sources of error such as different weather or times of day between count sessions, the risk that a systematic interval coincides with a hidden pattern (e.g. always sampling outside a specific shop), and how representative a single day's counts are of typical footfall."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T07:38:16.946Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Every calculation re-solves correctly and independently — mean 5.25 m and range 1.9 m; standard deviation √(10/5) = 1.41 degC, with the population n divisor used consistently across facts, card, worked example and question; IQR with n = 9 giving Q1 = 16.5, Q3 = 31.5 and 15 pedestrians (now with units, and the (n+1)/4 rule agrees with the median-of-halves method here); percentage change −20.2%; and Spearman with count ranks 8,7,5,6,4,3,2,1, Σd² = 166, rs = 1 − 996/504 = −0.976 to 3 s.f., correctly beaten against 0.738, which is indeed the two-tailed 5% critical value for n = 8 — while the lesson's five worked examples (rs = −1, σ = 0.14 m, IQR = 12 mm/hr, −32.4%) and the full-marks model answer's arithmetic (450 < 3200/7; Barham–Cowley 7 km < Cowley–Drayford 8 km) also check out, and every NEA claim matches the specification verbatim (60 marks in six sections of 8/7/9/14/12/10 from pages 64–69, the best-fit wording on page 64, 3000–4000 words and free choice of topic including untaught options on page 60, the teacher \"must not\" guidance on page 61, 20% from the AO table on page 58, four days and the NEA counting towards them on page 55, and a Resource Booklet with each of the three papers); all seven faults in the first verdict are substantively fixed — the giveaway second hints on questions 6, 9, 11 and 12 are now rewritten to name only the dimension of thought rather than the point, the invented claim that the specification names three sampling methods is gone from both exit[1] and questions[2].hints[1], room.facts now genuinely carries the sampling trade-off, data-ethics and best-fit items the hints cite, and questions[10] now reads \"justify whether the correlation is significant\" with no \"State\"; the statistical-tests sheet is sound, including the added warning that Mann-Whitney U is significant when the calculated value falls below the critical value and that Spearman normally wants n ≥ 10 (the NEA plan's 10 sites respect this, though questions[10] still uses n = 8, which is fine for an exam-style item but worth a word in class), and lesson.why now correctly qualifies the 28% AO3 figure by noting most of it sits in the NEA; before publishing, a teacher might still want to (i) watch that questions[6].hints[1] and questions[11].hints[1] route students to facts items that already contain the substance, leaving mainly the application and development to earn the 6 marks, (ii) retitle room.facts[8] (\"Sampling method vs sampling framework\"), whose body never actually distinguishes the two terms it names, (iii) add a question that squarely targets 4.2 (GIS/geo-located data), which currently appears only as a secondary code on questions[11], and (iv) note that the statistical sheet's \"critical value for the sample size\" is loose for Chi-squared and the T-test, which use degrees of freedom."},"refined":"2026-09-11T14:55:05.318Z"}}} };
